COLLDATA("ibnkhuzaymah","en",[[1,"I said—meaning to ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿUmar: O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, there are people who claim that there is no decree (qadar). He said: Is there anyone of them with us? I said: Yes. He said: Convey to them from me when you meet them that Ibn ʿUmar declares himself innocent before Allah of you, and you are innocent of him. Then he said…"],[2,"I saw ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān call for water for ablution and perform ablution on the paved area. He said: I will tell you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever performs ablution and perfects the ablution, and then prays, his sins between that prayer and the next prayer are forgiven."],[3,"ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān one day called for water for ablution, and he performed ablution. He washed his palms three times and rinsed his nose, then washed his face three times, then washed his right hand up to the elbow three times, then washed his left hand likewise, then wiped his head, then washed his right foot up to the ankles three times, then washed his left foot likewise. Then he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution like this ablution of mine. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever performs ablution like this ablution of mine, then stands and prays two rakʿahs without letting his mind wander in them, his previous sins will be forgiven."],[4,"When a Muslim (or a believer) servant performs ablution and washes his face, every sin that his eyes looked upon comes out from his face with the water (or with the last drop of water). When he washes his hands, every sin that his hands committed comes out from his hands with the water or with the last drop of water. When he washes his feet, every sin that his feet walked toward comes out with the water (or with the last drop of water), until he emerges cleansed of sins."],[5,"Should I not guide you to that by which Allah erases sins and raises ranks? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Perfecting ablution despite difficulties, taking many steps to the mosques, and waiting for the next prayer after the previous prayer. That is the steadfast guarding (ribāṭ), that is the steadfast guarding (ribāṭ). He said it once, except that ʿAlī b. Ḥujr said: [“That is the steadfast guarding” once]. And Yūnus said in his hadith: “Should I not inform you of what erases sins?”"],[6,"Ibn Wahb informed us that Mālik b. Anas narrated to him, from al-ʿAlāʾ b. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah."],[7,"I saw Abū Hurayrah performing ablution, and he would bring the ablution close to his armpit. I said to him about it, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The adornment will reach the places of purification."],[8,"Ibn ʿĀmir fell ill, and they began praising him, while Ibn ʿUmar remained silent. Then he said: Indeed, I am not the most deceitful of them, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Allah does not accept prayer without purification, nor charity from stolen wealth (ghulūl)."],[9,"Prayer is not accepted without purification, nor charity from stolen wealth (ghulūl)."],[10,"Allah does not accept a prayer without purification, nor a charity from fraud."],[11,"The prayer of any of you is not accepted if he breaks his wudu until he performs ablution."],[12,"He used to perform ablution for every prayer. When it was the day of the conquest, he performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks, and prayed all the prayers with a single ablution. 'Umar said to him: O Messenger of Allah! You have done something you did not used to do. He said: \"I did it deliberately, O 'Umar.\""],[13,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution for every prayer except on the day of the conquest of Makkah, for he was busy; so he combined Dhuhr and 'Asr with a single ablution."],[14,"He used to perform ablution for every prayer, but when it was the day of the conquest of Makkah, he prayed all the prayers with a single ablution."],[15,"I said to him: What do you think about 'Abdullah b. 'Umar's ablution for every prayer, whether he was in a state of purity or not? Who is it from? He said: Asma' bt. Zayd b. al-Khattab narrated to him that 'Abdullah b. Hanzalah b. Abi 'Amir al-Ghasil narrated to her that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to perform ablution for every prayer, whether he was in a state of purity or not. When that became difficult for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he commanded the use of the toothstick (siwak) for every prayer, and the obligation of ablution was lifted from him except from a ritual impurity. 'Abdullah considered that he had the strength for that, so he did it until he died. This is the hadith of Ya'qub b. Ibrahim, except that Muhammad b. Mansur said: And he used to do it until he died."],[16,"He witnessed 'Ali pray Dhuhr, then he sat in the open area attending to people's needs. When 'Asr time came, he called for a vessel of water and wiped his forearms, face, head, and feet, then drank the leftover water of his ablution while standing, and said: Some people dislike drinking while standing. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did the same as I have done, and said: \"This is the ablution of one who has not broken his wudu.\""],[17,"I came to Safwan b. 'Assal al-Muradi to ask him about wiping over the leather socks. He said: What brings you, O Zirr? I said: Seeking knowledge. He said: O Zirr! The angels lower their wings for the seeker of knowledge, pleased with what he seeks. He said: I said: Something occurred to me about wiping over the leather socks after defecation, and you are one of the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Have you heard the Messenger of Allah mention anything about that? He said: Yes. He used to command us, when we were traveling – or he said: travelers – not to remove our leather socks for three days and their nights, except from major ritual impurity (janabah), but not from defecation, urination, or sleep."],[18,"I was a man who emitted a lot of pre-ejaculatory fluid (madhy), so I was too shy to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ because his daughter was with me. I ordered a man to ask him, and he said: \"From it, ablution is required.\""],[19,"I was too shy to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about pre-ejaculatory fluid (madhy) because of Fatimah. So I ordered al-Miqdad b. al-Aswad to ask the Prophet ﷺ about that, and he said: \"In it, ablution is required.\""],[20,"I was a man who emitted pre-ejaculatory fluid frequently, so I used to perform a full bath in the winter until my back became cracked. I mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, or it was mentioned to him, and he said to me: \"Do not do that. When you see pre-ejaculatory fluid, wash your penis and perform ablution as you do for prayer. When you ejaculate water (semen), then perform a full bath.\""],[21,"‘Ali b. Abi Talib ordered him to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who draws near to his wife and pre-ejaculatory fluid comes out of him: what is upon him? ‘Ali said: \"For I have the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ (as my wife), and I am too shy to ask him.\" Al-Miqdad said: \"So I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that, and he said: 'When any of you finds that, let him sprinkle his private part with water and perform ablution as he does for prayer.'\""],[22,"I sent al-Miqdad b. al-Aswad to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he asked him about pre-ejaculatory fluid that comes out of a person: how should he act? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Perform ablution and sprinkle your private part.\""],[23,"I was a man who emitted pre-ejaculatory fluid frequently, so the Prophet ﷺ was asked on my behalf about that, and he said: \"Ablution is sufficient for you for it.\""],[24,"When one of you feels something in his stomach and is uncertain whether something came out of him or not, let him not leave (the prayer) until he hears a sound or smells an odor."],[25,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who feels something while he is in prayer. He said: \"He should not leave until he hears a sound or smells an odor.\""],[26,"The servant remains in prayer as long as the prayer is holding him back, as long as he does not break his ablution."],[27,"There is no ablution except from a sound or an odor."],[28,"When one of you feels something in his stomach and is uncertain whether something came out of him or not, let him not leave until he hears a sound or smells an odor."],[29,"Satan comes to one of you in his prayer and says: 'You have broken your ablution.' So let him say: 'You have lied,' unless he smells its odor with his nose or hears its sound with his ear."],[30,"Every son of Adam has his share of adultery inevitably. The eye's adultery is looking, the hand's adultery is touching, and the soul longs or whispers, and the private part confirms it or denies it."],[31,"A man asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, should I perform ablution after eating sheep meat?' He said: 'If you wish, perform ablution, and if you wish, do not perform ablution.' He said: 'Should I perform ablution after eating camel meat?' He said: 'Yes, perform ablution after eating camel meat.' He said: 'May I pray in sheep pens?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'May I pray in the resting places of camels?' He said: 'No.'"],[32,"A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'May I pray in the resting places of camels?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'Should I perform ablution after eating their meat?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'May I pray in the sheep pens?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Should I perform ablution after eating their meat?' He said: 'No.'"],[33,"When one of you touches his penis, let him perform ablution."],[34,"We regard ablution after touching the penis as recommended, not obligatory, based on the hadith of Abdullah b. Badr, from Qays b. Talq, from his father, from the Prophet."],[35,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out from the privy, and food was brought close to him. They said: 'Shall we not bring you water for ablution?' He said: 'I was only commanded to perform ablution when I stand for prayer.' And al-Dawraqi said: 'for prayer.'"],[36,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the expedition of Dhat al-Riqa' from Nakhl. A man from the Muslims captured a woman of a man from the polytheists. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned homeward, her husband came, having been absent. When he was told the news, he swore that he would not cease until he shed blood among the companions of Muhammad. He went out following the trail of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped at a place and said: 'Who is the man who will guard us this night of ours?' A man from the Muhajirun and a man from the Ansar volunteered, saying: 'We will, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Be at the mouth of the mountain pass.' He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his companions had descended to the pass from the valley. When the two men went out to the mouth of the pass, the Ansari said to the Muhajir: 'Which part of the night do you prefer me to take care of for you, its beginning or its end?' He said: 'Take care of its beginning for me.' He said: So the Muhajir lay down and slept, and the Ansari stood praying. He said: And the husband of the woman came. When he saw the figure of the man, he knew that he was the watchman of the people. He said: So he shot him with an arrow and placed it in him. He said: He pulled it out and placed it, and remained standing praying. Then he shot him with another arrow and placed it in him. He said: He pulled it out and placed it, and remained standing praying. Then he returned to him a third time and placed it in him. He pulled it out and placed it, then he bowed and prostrated, then he woke his companion, saying: 'Sit up, for I have been wounded.' He jumped up. When the man saw the two of them, he knew that he had been noticed and fled. When the Muhajir saw the blood on the Ansari, he said: 'Glory be to Allah! Why did you not wake me when he first shot you?' He said: 'I was in a surah I was reciting, and I did not like to cut it off until I completed it. When he kept shooting at me, I bowed and then alerted you. By Allah, had it not been for fear of neglecting the gap I was commanded by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to guard, my soul would have departed before I cut it off or completed it.'"],[37,"We used to pray with the Prophet ﷺ and not perform ablution from stepping on impurities."],[38,"The Prophet ﷺ ate a bone—or he said meat—then prayed and did not perform ablution."],[39,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate bread and meat—or a bone with meat—then prayed and did not perform ablution."],[40,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate a bone with meat, then prayed and did not perform ablution."],[41,"The Prophet ﷺ ate a shoulder of mutton, then prayed and did not perform ablution."],[42,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ perform ablution after eating a piece of dried curd, then he saw him eat a shoulder of mutton, then pray and not perform ablution."],[43,"The last of the two matters from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was abandoning ablution from what fire has touched."],[44,"The Prophet ﷺ ate a shoulder, then prayed and did not touch water."],[45,"Whoever swears and says in his oath, 'By al-Lat,' let him say, 'There is no god but Allah,' and whoever says to his companion, 'Come, let me gamble with you,' let him give charity with something."],[46,"The Prophet ﷺ drank milk, then rinsed his mouth."],[47,"The Prophet ﷺ drank milk and rinsed his mouth, and said: 'It has fat.' Al-San'ani said in his hadith: 'Or it is fat,' and Bundar said: 'It is fat.'"],[48,"My eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep."],[49,"He asked Aishah: How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? She said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not exceed eleven rak'ahs in Ramadan or outside it. He would pray four, so do not ask about their excellence and length, then pray four, so do not ask about their excellence and length, then pray three.' Aishah said: I said: O Messenger of Allah! Do you sleep before you perform witr? He said: 'O Aishah, my eyes sleep but my heart does not sleep.'"],[50,"When the Prophet ﷺ went to relieve himself, he would go far away."],[51,"I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saw him come out from the toilet. When he wanted to answer the call of nature, he would go far away."],[52,"I saw myself walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he came to a rubbish dump of a people. He stood and urinated as one of you urinates. I went to distance myself from him, but he said, \"Come close.\" So I came close to him, until I stood at his heels, until he finished."],[53,"The most beloved thing for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to screen himself with when answering the call of nature was a target or a wall of palm trees."],[54,"Sawdah bt. Zam'ah was a big woman. When she went out at night to answer the call of nature, she would be conspicuous among the women. 'Umar b. al-Khattab saw her and said, \"Consider how you go out, for by Allah, you do not go unnoticed by us when you go out.\" Sawdah mentioned that to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ while a bone was in his hand, and he did not put down the bone from his hand until the revelation ended. Then he said, \"Allah has granted you a concession to go out for your needs.\""],[55,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a wall from the walls of Makkah or Madinah and heard the voices of two persons being tormented in their graves. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"They are being tormented, and they are not tormented for a major sin.\" Then he said, \"Yes, one of them did not screen himself from his urine, and the other used to walk around spreading malicious gossip.\" Then he called for a leaf-stalk and broke it into two pieces, and he placed a piece on each of their graves. It was said to him, \"Why did you do this?\" He said, \"Perhaps it will be lightened for them as long as they do not dry up — or until they dry up.\""],[56,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by two graves, with the like of it."],[57,"Do not face the qiblah with defecation or urination, nor turn your backs to it, but face east or west.\" Abu Ayyub said, \"So we came to Syria and found toilets built facing the qiblah. We would turn away from them and seek forgiveness from Allah.\""],[58,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from facing the qiblah when urinating. Then I saw him a year before he passed away facing it."],[59,"I entered upon Hafsah bt. 'Umar and climbed onto the roof of the house, and I looked down upon the Prophet ﷺ while he was on his toilet, with his back to the qiblah, facing towards Syria. This is the wording of the hadith of 'Abd al-A'la. In the report of Abu Hisham: facing the qiblah."],[60,"I saw Ibn 'Umar make his mount kneel down facing the qiblah, then he sat and urinated towards it. I said, \"O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, was this not forbidden?\" He said, \"Yes, but it was forbidden only in open country. If there is something between you and the qiblah that screens you, then there is no harm.\""],[61,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to a people's waste dump and urinated while standing, then performed ablution and wiped over his leather socks."],[62,"I saw Sahl b. Sa'd urinating while standing; that was mentioned to him, and he said: 'I saw someone better than me do it.'"],[63,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to the waste dump of Banu so-and-so, spread his legs, and urinated while standing."],[64,"The Prophet ﷺ urinated in the mountain pass on the night of Muzdalifah."],[65,"I was supporting the Prophet ﷺ against my chest, and he called for a basin and urinated into it, then he leaned and died."],[66,"Sufyan b. 'Uyaynah narrated to us from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Muhammad b. Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, and from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of God said:"],[67,"Avoid the two cursings - or the two cursed ones. It was asked: What are they? He said: The one who relieves himself on people's paths or in their shade."],[68,"When one of you urinates, let him not touch his penis with his right hand."],[69,"These privies are attended [by jinn], so when one of you enters them, let him say: O God, I seek refuge in You from male and female devils."],[70,"The Prophet ﷺ wanted to relieve himself, and he said: 'Bring me three stones.' I found for him two stones and some donkey dung. He took the two stones and threw away the dung, saying: 'It is filth.'"],[71,"Let not two men go out to defecate, exposing their private parts, conversing; for God, Mighty and Majestic, abhors that."],[72,"A man should not look at the nakedness of a man, and a woman should not look at the nakedness of a woman. A man should not lie skin-to-skin with another man under a single garment, and a woman should not lie skin-to-skin with another woman under a single garment."],[73,"A man passed by the Prophet ﷺ while he was urinating and greeted him, but he did not return the greeting."],[74,"Some of the polytheists—who used to mock him—said to him: 'I see your companion teaches you even how to defecate.' Salman said: 'Yes indeed. He commanded us not to face the qiblah, not to cleanse ourselves with our right hands, and not to be content with fewer than three stones, containing neither dung nor bone.' Except that al-Dawraqi said: 'Some of the polytheists said to Salman.'"],[75,"Whoever performs ablution should rinse his nose, and whoever cleanses himself with stones should use an odd number."],[76,"When one of you cleanses himself with stones, let him use three stones."],[77,"When one of you cleanses himself with stones, let him use an odd number, for Allah is odd and loves the odd. Do you not see the heavens are seven, the earths are seven, and the circumambulation is seven?\" And he mentioned other things."],[78,"When one of you drinks, he should not breathe into the vessel. When he goes to the lavatory, he should not touch his penis with his right hand, and when he wipes himself, he should not wipe with his right hand."],[79,"When one of you urinates, he should not touch his penis with his right hand, nor cleanse himself with his right hand, nor breathe into the vessel."],[80,"I am to you only like a father to his child. None of you should face the qiblah or turn his back to it—meaning while defecating—and he should not cleanse himself with fewer than three stones, among which there is no dung or decayed bone."],[81,"The polytheists said: 'Your companion has taught you so much that it is almost as if he will teach you how to defecate.' He said: 'Yes indeed. He forbade us to face the qiblah, or to cleanse ourselves with our right hands, or with bone, or with dung. And he said: None of you should be content with fewer than three stones.'"],[82,"Was Ibn Mas'ud present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the night of the jinn? 'Alqamah said: I asked Ibn Mas'ud, saying: 'Did any of you witness with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the night of the jinn?' He said: 'No, but we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night and we lost him. We searched for him in the valleys and mountain passes, and we said: \"He has been taken by the jinn or assassinated.\"' He said: 'We spent the worst night that any people have ever spent. When morning came, behold, he came from the direction of Hira'. He said: 'We said: \"O Messenger of Allah! We lost you, so we searched for you but did not find you, and we spent the worst night that any people have ever spent.\" He said: \"A caller from the jinn came to me, so I went with him and recited the Quran to them.\"' He said: 'He took us and showed us their fires.' He said: 'They asked him for provision, and he said: \"For you is every bone upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned—it will come into your hands with more meat than it ever had—and every piece of dung is fodder for your riding beasts.\" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"So do not cleanse yourselves with these two, for they are the food of your brothers.\"' This is the hadith of 'Abd al-A'la. In the hadith of Ibn Abi Za'idah, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Do not cleanse yourselves with bone or dung, for it is the provision of your brothers among the jinn.'"],[83,"The Prophet ﷺ said to the people of Quba': Allah has praised you well regarding purification, and he recited: \"In it are men who love to purify themselves\" until the verse ended. Then he said to them: \"What is this purification?\" They said: We know nothing except that we had Jewish neighbors who used to wash their behinds after defecation, so we washed as they washed."],[84,"Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to relieve himself, I would bring him water, and he would cleanse himself with it."],[85,"Whenever he went out for his need, I would go with him carrying a staff and a water skin. When he came out, he would cleanse himself with water and perform ablution from the water skin."],[86,"Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out for his need, I and another boy would follow him with a water skin."],[87,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would enter the privy, and I and a boy of my age would carry a water skin and other things, and he would cleanse himself with water."],[88,"Ten are from the natural practices: trimming the mustache, sniffing water into the nose, using the toothstick, letting the beard grow, plucking the armpit hair, shaving the pubic hair, washing with water after relieving oneself, trimming the nails, and washing the knuckles.\" 'Abdah said in his narration: \"And the tenth, I do not know what it is except that it may be rinsing the mouth.\" And in the narration of Waki', Mus'ab said: \"And I forgot the tenth except that it may be rinsing the mouth.\""],[89,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ entered a thicket and relieved himself. Jarir brought him a water skin, and he cleansed himself with it. He said: \"And he wiped his hand on the ground.\""],[90,"'Ubayd Allah b. Musa narrated to us, from Isra'il, something similar to this."],[91,"The Prophet ﷺ intended to perform ablution. One of his wives said: O Messenger of Allah, I performed ablution from this. The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution and said: \"Water is not made impure by anything.\""],[92,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about water and what animals and predators come to it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"If the water is two qullahs, it does not carry impurity.\" This is the narration of Hawtharah. Musa b. 'Abd al-Rahman said, from 'Abd Allah b. 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar, from his father: \"Nothing makes it impure.\" As for al-Mukharrami, he..."],[93,"\"None of you should bathe in stagnant water while he is junub.\" He said: How should he do it, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: He should take water from it by cupping his hands."],[94,"None of you should urinate in stagnant water, then perform ablution from it or drink."],[95,"The purification of the vessel of one of you, if a dog licks in it, is to wash it seven times, the first of them with dust.\" Al-Dawraqī said: \"The first of them with dust.\" Al-Qutaʿī said: \"The first of them with dust.\""],[96,"The purification of the vessel of one of you, if a dog licks in it, is to wash it seven times."],[97,"If a dog drinks from a vessel, its purification is to wash it seven times, the first of them with dust."],[98,"If a dog licks in the vessel of one of you, let him pour out [the contents] and wash it seven times. And if the strap of one of you breaks, he should not walk in it until he repairs it."],[99,"When one of you wakes from sleep, he should not dip his hand into the vessel until he washes it three times; for he does not know where his hand spent the night."],[100,"When one of you wakes from sleep, he should not dip his hand into his vessel or into his ablution water until he washes it; for he does not know where his hand came from."],[101,"It was said to ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb: 'Tell us about the affair of the hour of hardship.' ʿUmar said: 'We set out to Tabūk in intense heat. We stopped at a place where thirst afflicted us until we thought our necks would snap. A man would go searching for water and not return until we thought his neck would snap. A man would slaughter his camel, squeeze its stomach contents, drink it, and put what remained on his liver. Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq said: \"O Messenger of God, God has accustomed you to good in supplication, so supplicate for us.\" He said: \"Would you like that?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He raised his hands and did not lower them until the sky spoke and darkened, then poured down, and they filled what they had with them. Then we went looking and did not find it beyond the army.'"],[102,"It is not impure; it is like one of the household."],[103,"Abū Qatādah used to perform ablution from a vessel while a cat was drinking from it. ʿIkrimah said: Abū Hurayrah said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"The cat is part of the household belongings.\""],[104,"Abū Qatādah entered upon her, and she poured ablution water for him. A cat came to drink from it, so Abū Qatādah tilted the vessel for it until it drank. Kabshah said: 'He saw me looking at him and said: \"Are you surprised, O daughter of my brother?\"' She said: 'I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'It is not impure; it is only one of those that go around among you.'\"'"],[105,"If a fly falls into the vessel of one of you, then in one of its wings there is disease and in the other a cure, and it protects itself with the wing that has the disease. So let him immerse it completely, then remove it."],[106,"I fell ill, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to visit me, and Abu Bakr, both walking. He found me unconscious, so he performed ablution and poured the water on me, and I regained consciousness. I said, \"O Messenger of Allah, what shall I do with my wealth? How shall I dispose of my wealth?\" He did not answer me at all until the verse of inheritance was revealed: \"If a man dies having no child, but has a sister, she shall receive half of what he leaves\" (4:176), and he said once: until the verse of kalalah was revealed."],[107,"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the time for prayer came. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Is there any water for purification among the people?\" A man came with leftover water in a leather water-skin. He poured it into a bowl, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution. Then the people brought the rest of the purification water, and he said, \"Wipe yourselves with it.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard them and said, \"Take it easy.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand in the bowl in the midst of the water and said, \"Perform ablution thoroughly.\" Jabir b. Abdullah said, \"By the One who took away my sight,\" and his sight had indeed gone, \"I saw water gushing from between the fingers of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not lift his hand until they had all performed ablution.\" Ubaydah said: Al-Aswad said: I think he said, \"We were two hundred or more.\""],[108,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution with the leftover water of Maymunah."],[109,"One of the Prophet's ﷺ wives performed ghusl from janabah, and the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution, or took a bath, from her leftover water. This is the hadith of Waki'. Ahmad b. Mani' said: The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution from her leftover water. Abu Musa and Utbah b. Abdullah said: The Prophet ﷺ came to perform ablution from her leftover water, and she said to him... He said: Water is not made impure by anything."],[110,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be brought a vessel, and I would start and drink from it while I was menstruating. Then he would take the vessel and place his mouth where my mouth had been, and I would take a bone with meat on it and bite it, then he would place his mouth where my mouth had been."],[111,"A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, \"O Messenger of Allah, we travel by sea and carry a small amount of water. If we perform ablution with it, we become thirsty. May we perform ablution with seawater?\" He said, \"Its water is purifying, its dead animals are lawful.\" This is the hadith of Yunus. Yahya b. Hakim said: from Safwan b. Sulaym, and he did not say \"from the family of Ibn al-Azraq\" or \"from Banu Abd al-Dar\". He said: \"We ride the sea for long periods.\""],[112,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the sea. He said, \"Its water is purifying, its dead animals are lawful.\""],[113,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey. He called so-and-so and Ali b. Abi Talib and said, \"Go and search for water for us.\" They went and met a woman between two large water skins - or between two leather water bags - on a camel. They said to her, \"Where is water?\" She said, \"My last sight of water was yesterday at this hour, and our men are away.\" They said to her, \"Come with us.\" She said, \"Where to?\" They said, \"To the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" She said, \"The one called the Sabian?\" They said, \"He is the one you mean.\" They went and brought her to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and told him the story. He said, \"Bring her down from her camel.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a vessel and put the mouths of the two water skins - or the two large skins - into it. He rinsed his mouth and returned the water into the mouths of the two water skins - or the two large skins. Then he closed their mouths and called out to the people, \"Give water and take water.\""],[114,"The Prophet ﷺ wanted to perform ablution from a waterskin, and it was said to him, \"It is from a dead animal.\" He said, \"Its tanning removes its impurity, or filth, or defilement.\""],[116,"Some people - or men - from Ukl and Uraynah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah and spoke words of Islam. They said, \"O Messenger of Allah, we are people of livestock, not people of settled cultivation,\" and they found Madinah unhealthy. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered for them a herd of camels and a shepherd, and ordered them to go out and drink from their urine and their milk."],[117,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution with a mudd of water and take a bath with five mudds."],[118,"\"A mudd suffices for wudu' and a sa' for janabah.\" A man said to him, \"That is not enough for us, O Jabir.\" He said, \"It was enough for one who was better than you and had more hair.\""],[119,"The Prophet ﷺ was brought two-thirds of a mudd, so he performed wudu' and began rubbing his forearms."],[120,"I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform wudu' from a single vessel."],[121,"We used to perform wudu', men and women, and wash our hands in a single vessel during the time of the Messenger of Allah."],[122,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ and his companions purifying themselves while the women were with them - men and women from a single vessel, all of them purifying themselves from it."],[123,"Indeed, for wudu' there is a devil called Walahan, so beware of the whispering about water."],[124,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during the illness in which he died: \"Pour over me from seven water-skins whose ties have not been untied, so that I may rest and give instructions to the people.\" Aisha said: \"We seated him in a copper washtub belonging to Hafsa and poured water over him from them until he began gesturing to us that we had done enough. Then he went out.\""],[125,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for water for wudu', and a vessel with water was brought - I think he said a glass vessel - and he placed his fingers in it. The people began performing wudu', one after another, and I estimated them to be between seventy and eighty. I looked at the water as if it was gushing from between his fingers."],[126,"The people became thirsty on the day of Hudaybiyyah while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a small leather water container in front of him from which he was performing wudu'. The people rushed toward him. He said, \"What is the matter with you?\" They said, \"We have no water to perform wudu' or drink except what is in front of you.\" He placed his hands in the container and supplicated as Allah willed him to supplicate. The water began to gush from between his fingers like springs. We drank and performed wudu'. I asked Jabir, \"How many were you?\" He said, \"We were fifteen hundred, and if we had been a hundred thousand it would have sufficed us.\""],[127,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was brought a small wooden bowl and performed wudu' from it. I asked Anas, \"Did the Prophet ﷺ perform wudu' for every prayer?\" He said, \"Yes.\" I said, \"What about you?\" He said, \"We used to pray the prayers with one wudu'.\""],[128,"I spent the night in the house of my maternal aunt Maymunah, and I observed how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray at night. He urinated, then washed his face and hands, then slept. Then he got up, untied the strap of the water skin, and poured into the large bowl — or the platter — and performed wudu' in a manner between two wudu's. Then he stood to pray, so I got up and performed wudu', then I came and stood on his left, and he took me and placed me on his right."],[129,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to cover the water vessel used for wudu', to tie the mouth of the water skin, and to turn over the vessel."],[130,"I brought the Prophet ﷺ a cup of milk in al-Baqi' that was not covered, and he said: \"Why did you not cover it, even if you placed a stick across it?\" Abu Humayd said: The doors were only commanded to be closed at night, and the water skins were only commanded to be covered at night."],[131,"The Prophet ﷺ only commanded that water skins be tied at night, and that doors be closed at night."],[132,"Close your door and mention the name of Allah, for Shaytan does not open what is closed. Extinguish your lamp and mention the name of Allah. Tie your water skin and mention the name of Allah. Cover your vessel and mention the name of Allah, even if with a stick that you place across it."],[133,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us: \"Close your doors, tie your water skins, cover your vessels, and extinguish your lamps, for Shaytan does not open a closed door, nor untie a tied strap, nor uncover a cover. And the little mischief-maker (the mouse) may set fire to a household's house over them. And restrain your livestock and your families at sunset until the darkness of 'Isha' passes.\""],[134,"He used to say: \"Tie the water skins, close the doors when you go to sleep at night, and cover food and drink, for Shaytan comes: if he does not find the door closed, he enters; if he does not find the water skin tied, he drinks from it; and if he finds the door closed and the water skin tied, he does not untie a strap nor open a closed door. And if one of you finds nothing to cover his vessel with, let him place a stick across it.\""],[135,"\"With what did the Prophet ﷺ begin when he entered the house?\" She said: \"With the siwak.\" And Yusuf said: \"When he entered his house.\""],[136,"The siwak is a purification for the mouth and a means of earning the Lord's pleasure."],[137,"When the Prophet ﷺ got up at night for tahajjud, he would rub his mouth with the siwak."],[138,"The virtue of the prayer for which the siwak is used over the prayer for which the siwak is not used is seventy-fold."],[139,"I said: \"Ibn 'Umar's performing wudu' for every prayer, whether he was in a state of purity or not — from whom did he learn that?\" He said: \"Asma' bt. Zayd b. al-Khattab narrated to him that 'Abdullah b. Hanzalah b. Abi 'Amir narrated to her that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to perform wudu' for every prayer, whether pure or impure. When that became difficult for him, he was commanded to use the siwak for every prayer. Ibn 'Umar considered himself able to do that, so he did not abandon wudu' for every prayer.\""],[140,"Were it not that I would impose hardship on my community, I would have commanded them to delay the 'Isha' prayer, and to use the siwak at every prayer."],[141,"Were it not that I would impose hardship on my community, I would have commanded them to use the siwak with every ablution."],[142,"I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was using the siwak, with the end of the siwak on his tongue, and he was saying: \"A' A'.\""],[143,"Actions are but by intention, and each person will have only what he intended. So whoever's emigration was to Allah and His Messenger, his emigration was to Allah and His Messenger. And whoever's emigration was to a worldly gain he could attain or a woman he could marry, his emigration was to what he emigrated for."],[144,"Actions are by intention, and each person will have only what he intended."],[145,"Some of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ looked for water for ablution but did not find any. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Here is water.\" I saw the Prophet ﷺ place his hand in the vessel containing the water, then he said: \"Perform ablution in the name of Allah.\" I saw the water gushing from between his fingers, and the people performed ablution until they had all done so, down to the last of them. Thabit said: I said to Anas: \"How many do you think they were?\" He said: \"About seventy.\""],[146,"When one of you wakes from sleep, let him not dip his hand into the vessel until he washes it three times, for he does not know where his hand spent the night."],[147,"When one of you wakes from sleep, let him not put his hand into the vessel until he washes it three times, for he does not know where his hand spent the night or where his hand wandered.\" A man said to him: \"What if it is a large basin?\" Ibn 'Umar threw pebbles at him and said: \"I narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and you say: 'What if it is a large basin!'"],[148,"Ali entered the open area after he had prayed Fajr, then he said to a servant of his: \"Bring me water for purification.\" The servant brought him a vessel containing water and a basin. 'Abd Khayr said: \"We were sitting watching him. He took the vessel with his right hand and poured water over his left hand, then he washed his palms. Then he took the vessel with his right hand and poured water over his left hand, doing that three times.\" 'Abd Khayr said: \"Each time he did not put his hand into the vessel until he had washed it several times. Then he put his right hand into the vessel, filled his mouth, rinsed his mouth and sniffed water into his nose, and blew it out with his left hand three times. Then he washed his face three times. Then he washed his right hand three times up to the elbow. Then he washed his left hand three times up to the elbow. Then he put his right hand into the vessel so that the water covered it, then he lifted it with the water it carried, then he wiped it with his left hand, then he wiped his head with both hands, or all of them. Then he put his right hand into the vessel, then he poured water over his right foot and washed it three times with his left hand. Then he poured water with his right hand over his left foot and washed it three times with his left hand. Then he put his right hand in, filled it with water, and drank from it. Then he said: \"This is the purification of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. Whoever would like to see the purification of the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, this is his purification.\""],[149,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ perform ablution. He scooped a handful and rinsed his mouth and sniffed water into his nose. Then he scooped a handful and washed his face. Then he scooped a handful and washed his right hand. Then he scooped a handful and washed his left hand. Then he scooped a handful and wiped his head and the inside and outside of his ears, inserting his two fingers into them. Then he scooped a handful and washed his right foot, and a handful and washed his left foot."],[150,"When one of you wakes from sleep and performs ablution, let him sniff water into his nose and blow it out three times, for Satan spends the night on his nostrils."],[151,"I said: O Messenger of God, tell me about ablution. He said: Perform ablution thoroughly, run water between the fingers, and exaggerate in sniffing water into the nose, unless you are fasting."],[152,"He performed ablution and washed his face three times, sniffed water into his nose three times, rinsed his mouth three times, wiped his head and his ears, their outsides and insides, and his feet three times, and ran his fingers through his beard and the toes of his feet. And he said: This is how I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ perform ablution."],[153,"I saw Uthman b. Affan perform ablution. He washed his palms three times, rinsed his mouth, sniffed water into his nose, washed his face three times, wiped his ears, their outsides and insides, washed his feet three times three times, ran his fingers between his fingers, and ran his fingers through his beard when he washed his face three times. And he said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do as you saw me do."],[154,"Ali entered my house after he had urinated. He called for water for ablution, so we brought him a bowl that held a mudd or close to it, until it was placed before him. He said: O Ibn Abbas, shall I not perform for you the ablution of the Messenger of God ﷺ? I said: Yes, may my father and mother be your ransom. He said: Then a vessel was placed for him, and he washed his hands, then rinsed his mouth, sniffed water into his nose, and blew it out, then he took water with his right hand and struck his face with it."],[155,"He saw the Messenger of God ﷺ perform ablution. He rinsed his mouth, then blew water out of his nose, then washed his face three times, and his right hand three times, and the other three times, and wiped his head with water other than the leftover from his hand, and washed his feet until he cleaned them."],[156,"The Messenger of God ﷺ wiped his head with both hands, moving them forward and backward. He began with the front of his head, then moved them to the back of his neck, then returned them until they came back to the place where he began."],[157,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed ablution: he washed his face three times, washed his hands twice, then wiped his head, beginning with the front, then washed his feet."],[158,"The Messenger of God ﷺ wiped his head in his ablution from his forelock to the back of his neck, then returned his hands to his forelock and wiped his entire head."],[159,"Uthman one day called for water for ablution, and he mentioned the hadith describing the ablution of the Prophet ﷺ, and said: Then he washed his right foot up to the ankles three times, and the left similarly."],[160,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ pass through the market of Dhu al-Majaz wearing a red hulla, saying: O people, say: There is no god but God, and you will prosper. A man was following him, throwing stones at him, and he had made his ankles and Achilles tendons bleed, and he was saying: O people, do not obey him, for he is a liar. I said: Who is this? They said: A youth of Banu Abd al-Muttalib. I said: Who is this who is following him and throwing stones at him? They said: This is Abd al-Uzza Abu Lahab."],[161,"Abu al-Qasim al-Jadali narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Nu'man b. Bashir."],[162,"We returned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Makkah to Madinah until when we were at some water on the way, some people hastened at the time of 'Asr and performed ablution while they were in a hurry. We reached them while their heels were white, shining, not touched by water. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Woe to the heels from the Fire! Perform ablution thoroughly.'"],[163,"Woe to the heels from the Fire."],[164,"Woe to the heels and the soles of the feet from the Fire."],[165,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ having performed ablution and left on the back of his foot a spot the size of a fingernail. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Go back and perfect your ablution.'"],[166,"'Amr b. 'Abasah narrated to us, and he mentioned the hadith in full regarding the description of his embracing Islam, and he said: I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, tell me about ablution.' And he mentioned the hadith in full, and he said: 'Then he washes his feet up to the ankles as Allah commanded him, except that the sins of his feet come out from the tips of his toes with the water.'"],[167,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell behind us on a journey we traveled, and he caught up with us when the prayer – the 'Asr prayer – had overtaken us and we were performing ablution. We began wiping our feet, and he called out at the top of his voice two or three times: 'Woe to the heels from the Fire!'"],[168,"I saw 'Uthman b. 'Affan perform ablution three times each, wipe his head and his ears, their outer and inner sides, wash his feet three times each, wash his fingertips, run his fingers through his beard, and wash his face. And he said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do as you saw me do.'"],[169,"I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, tell me about ablution.' He said: 'Perform ablution thoroughly, run your fingers between the fingers, and exaggerate in sniffing water unless you are fasting.'"],[171,"The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution twice each."],[172,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution once each."],[173,"The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution: he washed his face three times, his hands twice, his feet twice, and wiped his head. And I think he said: 'And he sniffed water.'"],[174,"Can you show me how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution? 'Abd Allah b. Zayd said: Yes. He called for water for ablution and poured it over his hands, washing his hands twice, then rinsed his mouth and nose three times, then washed his face three times, then washed his hands twice up to the elbows, then wiped his head with his hands, bringing them forward and back, starting from the front of his head, then moving them to the nape of his neck, then returning them until they came back to the place where he had started, then washed his feet."],[175,"A bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about ablution. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution three times for each limb, then said: \"Whoever adds more has done wrong and acted unjustly, or transgressed and acted unjustly.\""],[176,"By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not single us out for anything apart from the people except three things: he commanded us to perform ablution thoroughly, not to consume charity, and not to mate donkeys with horses."],[177,"A transaction coupled with two transactions is usury."],[178,"\"Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah expiates sins and increases good deeds?\" They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah.\" He said: \"Performing ablution thoroughly despite difficulties, and waiting for the next prayer after the prayer.\""],[179,"When you put on clothes and when you perform ablution, begin with your right sides."],[180,"He used to love starting with the right whenever he could, in his purification, putting on his sandals, and combing his hair."],[181,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiping over his leather socks and the turban. In the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiped over his leather socks and the turban."],[182,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution and wipe over his leather socks and his turban."],[183,"He wiped over his leather socks."],[184,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wipe over the khuffayn."],[185,"He saw Saʿd b. Mālik while he was wiping over the khuffayn, and he said, “Do you do that?!” They then both came together before ʿUmar, and Saʿd said to ʿUmar, “Give a ruling to the son of my brother about wiping over the khuffayn.” ʿUmar said, “We used to wipe over our khifāf when we were with our Prophet ﷺ, and we did not consider that problematic.” Ibn ʿUmar said, “Even if he came from defecation?” He said, “Yes.”"],[186,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Bilāl entered al-Aswāf and he went to relieve himself. He said: Then they came out. Usāma said: I asked Bilāl, “What did he do?” Bilāl said, “The Prophet ﷺ went to relieve himself, then performed wuḍūʾ, washing his face and hands, wiping his head, and wiping over the khuffayn.” Yūnus added in his ḥadīth: “Then he prayed.”"],[187,"I saw Jarīr urinate, then call for water and perform wuḍūʾ and wipe over his khuffayn, then stand and pray. He was asked about that, and he said, “I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do the like of this.”"],[188,"Jarīr urinated, performed wuḍūʾ, and wiped over his khuffayn, and they criticized him for it. He said, “I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wiping over the khuffayn.” It was said to him, “That was before [the revelation of] al-Māʾidah?” He said, “My Islam only came after al-Māʾidah.”"],[189,"I accepted Islam forty days before the death of the Prophet ﷺ."],[190,"He wiped over the mūqayn and the khimār."],[191,"I said, “O Messenger of Allah, do you wipe over your khuffayn?” He said, “Yes, for I put them on while they were pure.”"],[192,"I said, “O Messenger of Allah, do you wipe over the khuffayn?” He said, “I put my feet in them while they were pure.”"],[193,"He granted a concession to the traveler for three days and their nights, and to the resident for a day and a night, if he purified himself and then put on his khuffayn, to wipe over them."],[194,"I came to Ṣafwān b. ʿAssāl al-Murādī, and he said, “What brings you?” I said, “I came to seek knowledge.” He said, “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘No one goes out of his house in search of knowledge but the angels lower their wings for him, pleased with what he is doing.’” He said, “I have come to ask you about wiping over the khuffayn.” He said, “Yes, we were in the army that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent, and he commanded us to wipe over the khuffayn when we put them on in a state of purity—for three [days] when we traveled, and for a night when we resided—and not to remove them because of defecation or urination, and not to remove them except because of janābah.” And he said, “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘In the west there is an open gate for repentance, whose distance is seventy years; it will not be closed until the sun rises from its west.’”"],[195,"I asked ʿĀʾishah about wiping over the khuffayn, and she said, “Go to ʿAlī and ask him, for he knows more about that than I.” So he went to ʿAlī and asked him about wiping over the khuffayn. He said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command that: the resident wipes for a day and a night, and the traveler for three.”"],[196,"The Messenger of God ﷺ granted us a concession for three days for the traveler and a day and a night for the resident."],[197,"I went to Safwan b. 'Assal al-Muradi and asked him about wiping over the khuffayn. He said: We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ and he commanded us not to remove our khuffs for three days—meaning during travel—except for major impurity, but not for defecation, urination, or sleep."],[198,"Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me."],[199,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed wudu and wiped over the jawrabayn and the na'layn."],[200,"It was said to Ibn 'Umar: We have seen you do something we have not seen anyone else do. He said: What is it? They said: We have seen you wear these sibti sandals. He said: Indeed, I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ wearing them and performing wudu in them, and wiping over them."],[201,"He called for a jug of water, then performed a light wudu, then wiped over his sandals, then said: This is how the Messenger of God ﷺ performed wudu for one who is pure, as long as he has not broken his wudu."],[202,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ performing wudu and wiping the water over his feet."],[203,"We prayed Zuhr with 'Ali, then we went out to the Rahbah. He called for a vessel containing a drink, took it, and rinsed his mouth—Mansur said: I think he said: and sniffed water into his nose—and wiped his face, his forearms, his head, and his feet. Then he drank the remainder while standing. Then he said: Some people dislike drinking while standing, but the Messenger of God ﷺ did the same as I did, and said: This is the wudu of one who has not broken his wudu."],[204,"I poured water for the Messenger of God ﷺ when he performed wudu during the campaign of Tabuk, and he wiped over the khuffayn."],[205,"Indeed, you consider the signs as punishment, but we used to consider them a blessing during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. We used to eat with the Messenger of God ﷺ while we heard the food glorifying God. A vessel was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and he placed his hand in it, and water began to spring from between his fingers. The Prophet ﷺ said: Come to the blessed purification, and the blessing is from God, until we all performed wudu."],[206,"I saw men and women performing wudu during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ from a single vessel."],[207,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was performing wudu and greeted him, but the Messenger of God ﷺ did not return the greeting until he had completed wudu. Then he apologized to him, saying: I disliked mentioning God except upon purity—or he said: upon purification."],[208,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to remember Allah in all his states."],[209,"I entered upon Ali b. Abi Talib with two men: a man from among us, and a man from Banu Asad, I think. He sent them on an errand and said: 'You are two sturdy men, so act strenuously for your religion.' Then he entered the privy, then came out, took a handful of water, wiped himself with it, then came and recited the Qur'an. We found that objectionable. Ali said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go to the privy, relieve himself, then come out and eat bread and meat with us, and recite the Qur'an. Nothing would prevent him from the Qur'an except janabah' — or he said, 'except janabah.'"],[210,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until when we were at al-Harrah at al-Suqya, which belonged to Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Bring me water for ablution.' When he performed ablution, he stood and faced the qiblah, then said the takbir, then said: 'O Allah, Ibrahim was Your servant and intimate friend; he called upon You for the people of [Makkah]. And I, Muhammad, Your servant and messenger, call upon You for the people of Madinah: bless for them their mudd and their sa' just as You blessed the people of Makkah, with the blessing doubled.'"],[211,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution, then prayed in the land of Sa'd."],[212,"He asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'May one of us sleep while he is junub?' He said: 'He may sleep and perform ablution if he wishes.'"],[213,"Umar b. al-Khattab asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'May one of us sleep while he is junub?' He said: 'If he wants to sleep, let him perform ablution.'"],[214,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wanted to sleep while he was junub, he would perform ablution like his ablution for prayer."],[215,"Umar asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'Janabah affects me at night; what should I do?' He said: 'Wash your male organ, perform ablution, then sleep.'"],[216,"When he wanted to eat or sleep while he was junub, he would perform ablution."],[217,"When you come to your bed, perform ablution as for prayer, then lie down on your right side."],[218,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about a junub person: may he eat or sleep? He said: 'If he performs ablution like his ablution for prayer.'"],[219,"Whenever he wanted to eat while he was junub, he would wash his hands and then eat."],[220,"When one of you has intercourse with his wife, then wants to resume, let him perform wudu."],[221,"When one of you wants to resume, let him perform wudu as he does for prayer — meaning the one who has intercourse — then resume before performing ghusl."],[222,"When one of you wants to resume, let him perform wudu, for it is more invigorating for him in resuming."],[223,"Mu'awiyah said: And Rabi'ah b. Yazid reported to me, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from 'Uqbah b. 'Amir, the like of the hadith of Abu 'Uthman, from Jubayr b. Nufayr, from 'Uqbah:"],[224,"There is none of you who performs wudu, completes the wudu, then says: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that Muhammad is the servant of Allah and His Messenger,' except that the gates of Paradise are opened for him, and he may enter through whichever he wishes.\""],[225,"He asked 'Uthman b. 'Affan about a man who has intercourse but does not ejaculate. He said: \"He does not have to perform ghusl.\""],[226,"The fatwa they used to give — 'water is due from water' — was a concession granted by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the early days of Islam; then he commanded ghusl after that."],[227,"The statement of the Anṣār was only: “Water from water.” It was a concession in the beginning of Islam, then we were commanded to perform ghusl."],[228,"That they were sitting and mentioned what makes ghusl obligatory. Those present from the Muhājirūn said: “When the circumcised parts meet the circumcised parts, ghusl becomes obligatory.” And those present from the Anṣār said: “No, not until he ejaculates.” Abū Mūsā said: “I will bring you the report.” So he arose and went to ʿĀʾishah ؓ and greeted, then said: “I want to ask you about something, and I am shy of it.” She said: “Do not be shy to ask about something you would ask your mother who bore you, for I am only your mother.” He said: “I said: What makes ghusl obligatory?” She said: “You have fallen upon the well-informed one. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ‘When he sits between her four limbs and the circumcised parts meet the circumcised parts, ghusl becomes obligatory.’”"],[229,"Actions are but by intention, and each person will have only what he intended. So whoever's emigration was to Allah and His Messenger, his emigration was to Allah and His Messenger. And whoever's emigration was to a worldly gain he could attain or a woman he could marry, his emigration was to what he emigrated for."],[230,"He used to go around to his wives with a single ghusl."],[231,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go around to his wives with a single ghusl, except that al-Ribāṭī said: from Maʿmar, and he said: “He goes around.”"],[232,"He used to go around to his wives in an hour of the night or day with a single ghusl, and they were eleven. He said: I said to Anas: “And was he able to do that?” He said: “We used to say that he was given the strength of thirty men.”"],[233,"I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a rabbi from among the Jewish rabbis came to him and said: “Peace be upon you, O Muhammad.” I pushed him with a push that nearly knocked him down. He said: “Why did you push me?” I said: “Will you not say: O Messenger of Allah?” The Jew said: “We call him by the name his family gave him.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “My name is Muhammad, which my family gave me.” The Jew said: “I have come to ask you.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Will it benefit you if I tell you?” He said: “I will listen with my ears.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made a mark with a stick he had and said: “Ask.” The Jew said: “Where will the people be on the day when the earth is changed to other than the earth, and the heavens too?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “In the darkness before the bridge.” He said: “Who will be the first of the people to cross?” He said: “The poor among the Muhājirūn.” He said: “What is their gift when they enter Paradise?” He said: “An extra lobe of the liver of the whale.” He said: “What will be their food after that?” He said: “The ox of Paradise that used to eat from its edges will be slaughtered for them.” He said: “What will be their drink upon it?” He said: “From a spring in it called Salsabīl.” He said: “You have spoken the truth. And I have come to ask you about something that no one among the people of the earth knows except a prophet or a man or two men.” He said: “Will it benefit you if I tell you?” He said: “I will listen with my ears.” He said: “I have come to ask you about the child.” He said: “The fluid of the man is white, and the fluid of the woman is yellow. When they meet and the man’s semen overcomes the woman’s semen, the child is male by Allah’s permission, and when the woman’s semen overcomes the man’s semen, the child is female by Allah’s permission.” The Jew said: “You have spoken the truth, and you are indeed a prophet.” Then he left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “This one asked me about what he asked me, and I had no knowledge of any of it until Allah informed me.”"],[234,"Water is only from water."],[235,"Water from water."],[236,"Umm Sulaym came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about a woman who sees in her dream what a man sees. He said: “If she sees fluid, she should perform ghusl.” She said: I said: “You have disgraced women. Does a woman have wet dreams?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “May your right hand be in the dust. Then how does her child resemble her?”"],[237,"The Prophet ﷺ and I used to perform ghusl from a single vessel, and I would say: “Leave some for me, leave some for me.”"],[238,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at the upper part of Makkah on the day of the conquest. I came to him, and Abū Dharr brought a bowl with water in it. I said: “I see traces of dough in it.” She said: Abū Dharr screened him and he performed ghusl, then the Prophet ﷺ screened Abū Dharr and he performed ghusl, then the Prophet ﷺ prayed eight rakʿahs, and that was in the forenoon."],[239,"We used to take turns with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ [using] the same large basin, washing from it."],[240,"A washing vessel would be placed for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and me, and we would both put our hands into it together."],[241,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Maymunah taking a bath from one vessel containing traces of dough."],[242,"I brought water for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to take a bath from janabah. She said: He washed his palms twice or thrice, then he put his right hand into the vessel and poured water over his private parts with it and washed them with his left hand. Then he struck the ground with his left hand and rubbed it vigorously. Then he performed wudu as for prayer. Then he poured three handfuls of water over his head, filling both his hands. Then he washed the rest of his body. Then he moved away from that spot and washed his feet. Then I brought him a towel, but he returned it. This is the wording of the hadith of 'Isa b. Yunus. In the report of Ibn Fudayl, she said: He began shaking the water off himself. Likewise, Ibn Idris said: A towel was brought to him, but he refused to accept it and began shaking the water off himself."],[243,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took a bath from janabah, he would pour from the vessel onto his right hand and wash it. Then he would pour onto his left hand and wash his private parts. Then he would perform wudu as for prayer. Then he would put his hand into the vessel and run it through his hair like this, working his fingers through it until he saw that the water had reached his skin. Then he would scoop water over his head three times, and there was some water left in the vessel which he poured over himself after finishing."],[244,"Your cousin al-Hasan b. Muhammad asked me about taking a bath from janabah. I said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pour water over his head three times. He said: My hair is thick. I said: The hair of the Messenger of Allah was thicker than yours and better."],[245,"He loved beginning with the right side in his affairs, even in combing his hair, putting on his sandals, and his purification."],[246,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would take a bath from a milk vessel. He would take water with his hands and put it on his right side, then take water with his hands and put it on his left side, then take water with his hands and pour it over the middle of his head."],[247,"O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman who ties my braided hair tightly; should I undo it for the bath from janabah? He said: It is enough for you to scoop three handfuls of water over your head, then pour water over yourself and you will be purified.\" Or he said: \"Then you will have become purified.\" This is the hadith of al-Makhzumi. 'Abd al-Jabbar said: \"Then you will have become purified,\" and he did not say: \"you will be purified.\""],[248,"It reached 'A'ishah that 'Abd Allah b. 'Amr b. al-'As commanded his women to undo their hair when they took a bath from janabah. She said: How astonishing for this Ibn 'Amr! He has burdened them with toil. Why does he not command them to shave their heads? I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to take a bath from one vessel, putting our hands into it together, and I never added more than three handfuls. Or she said: Three scoops. This is the hadith of 'Abd al-Warith. In the report of Ibn 'Ulayyah, the words \"putting our hands into it together\" are not present. She said in it: I never added more than three pourings of water over my head."],[249,"Asma' asked the Prophet ﷺ about taking a bath after menstruation. He mentioned part of the hadith. She also asked him about taking a bath from janabah. He said: One of you should take her water and purify herself, making her purification thorough. Then she should pour water over her head and rub it until it reaches the roots of her hair. Then she should pour water over her head. 'A'ishah said: What excellent women are the women of the Ansar! Their modesty did not prevent them from understanding the religion."],[250,"He forbade entering the water without a waist-wrapper."],[251,"I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ghusl from a single vessel on account of janabah. Bundar said: \"From a single vessel on account of janabah.\""],[252,"I asked 'A'ishah: Does a woman perform ghusl with her husband from a single vessel together on account of janabah? She said: \"Yes; water is purifying, and nothing makes water junub. I and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ghusl from a single vessel. She said: I would begin and pour water over his hands before he immersed them in the water.\""],[253,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched a cavalry, and it brought a man from Banu Hanifah called Thumamah b. Uthal, the chief of the people of Yamamah. They tied him to one of the pillars of the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to him—and he mentioned a long hadith—and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Release Thumamah. So he went to some palm trees near the mosque, performed ghusl, then entered the mosque and said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is [the Messenger of Allah]."],[254,"Thumamah al-Hanafi was taken captive. The Prophet ﷺ used to come to him in the morning and say: \"What do you have, O Thumamah?\" He would say: If you kill, you kill one who has blood; if you bestow a favor, you bestow it on one who is grateful; if you want wealth, we will give you from it whatever you wish. The companions of the Prophet ﷺ loved ransom and said: What is to be gained by killing this man? One day the Prophet ﷺ bestowed a favor on him and he accepted Islam. He released him and sent him to the garden of Abu Talhah, and ordered him to perform ghusl. So he performed ghusl and prayed two rak'ahs. The Prophet ﷺ said: Your brother's Islam has become good."],[255,"He accepted Islam, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to perform ghusl with water and sidr."],[256,"He came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked to be alone with him, and he accepted Islam. So he ordered him to perform ghusl with water and sidr."],[257,"Ghusl is performed for four things: janabah, Friday, washing the deceased, and cupping."],[258,"I entered upon 'A'ishah and said to her: Will you not tell me about the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? She said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became heavy. He said: \"Have the people prayed?\" We said: No; they are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: \"Put water for me in the tub.\" She said: We did so, and he performed ghusl. Then he tried to get up and fainted. Then he regained consciousness and said: \"Have the people prayed?\" We said: No; they are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: \"Put water for me in the tub.\" She said: We did so, and he performed ghusl. Then he tried to get up and fainted. Then he regained consciousness and said: \"Have the people prayed?\" We said: No; they are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Put water for me in the tub. We did so. She said: He performed ghusl, then tried to get up and fainted. Then he regained consciousness and said: \"Have the people prayed?\" We said: No; they are waiting for you, O Messenger of Allah. She said: The people were staying in the mosque waiting for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the last 'Isha' prayer."],[259,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said during his illness in which he died: Pour over me from seven waterskins whose ties have not been untied, so that I may find relief and give my final instructions to the people.\" 'A'ishah said: We seated him in a tub belonging to Hafsah made of copper, and we poured water on him from them until he began to signal to us that you have done enough. Then he went out."],[260,"I asked 'A'ishah ؓ: How was the sleep of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in janabah? She said: He used to do all of that; sometimes he performed ghusl and slept, and sometimes he performed wudu' and slept."],[262,"She borrowed a necklace from Asma' and it was lost, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent some of his companions in search of it. The prayer time overtook them and they prayed without wudu'. When they came to the Prophet ﷺ they complained about that to him, and the verse of tayammum was revealed. Usayd b. Hudayr said: \"May Allah reward you well, for by Allah, no matter ever befalls you but Allah makes a way out of it for you and places in it a blessing for the Muslims.\""],[263,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys, and when we were at al-Bayda'—or at Dhat al-Jaysh—a necklace of mine broke, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped to search for it, and the people stopped with him. They were not at a water source and had no water with them, so the people came to Abu Bakr al-Siddiq ؓ and said: Do you not see what 'A'ishah has done? She has made the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people stop, and they are not at a water source and have no water with them. Abu Bakr came while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had placed his head on my thigh and had fallen asleep."],[264,"This nation has been favored over the people with three things: the earth has been made a place of prostration and a means of purification for us, our rows have been made like the rows of the angels, and I was given these verses from the end of Surat al-Baqarah from a treasure house beneath the Throne, which no one before me or after me has been given."],[265,"We have been favored over the people with three things: the entire earth has been made a place of prostration for us, its dust has been made a means of purification for us when we do not find water, our rows have been made like the rows of the angels, and I was given these verses from the end of Surat al-Baqarah, from a treasure house beneath the Throne, which no one before me or after me has been given.\""],[266,"I never remember my parents except that they followed the religion, and no day passed but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would come to us at the two ends of the day, morning and evening. Then he mentioned the hadith in full. And he said in the report: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the Muslims: \"I have been shown the land of your emigration, shown a salty ground with palm trees between two lava tracts, which are the two harrahs.\""],[267,"He said regarding tayammum: \"One strike for the face and the palms.\""],[268,"One strike for the face and the palms."],[269,"A man came to 'Umar b. al-Khattab and said: I became junub and did not find water. 'Umar said: Do not pray. 'Ammar said: Do you not remember, O Commander of the Believers, when you and I were on a military expedition and we became junub and did not find water? As for you, you did not pray, but as for me, I rolled in the dust and prayed. When we came to the Prophet ﷺ and I mentioned that to him, he said: \"It would have been sufficient for you to do this,\" and the Prophet ﷺ struck his hand on the ground, then blew into it, and wiped his face and palms with them."],[270,"A man came to 'Umar and said: We become junub and have no water—and he mentioned his story with 'Ammar b. Yasir, and he said—and he said: meaning 'Ammar—so I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, and he said: \"It would have been sufficient for you to do with your hands like this and like this,\" and he struck his hands on the dust, then shook them, then blew into them, and wiped his face and hands with them."],[271,"I was sitting with 'Abdullah and Abu Musa, and Abu Musa said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what do you think if a man becomes junub and does not find water for a month—should he perform tayammum? 'Abdullah said: He should not perform tayammum. Abu Musa said: Have you not heard what 'Ammar said to 'Umar: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me on an errand, and I became junub and did not find water, so I rolled in the dust as an animal rolls. I mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"It would have been sufficient for you to strike your palms on the ground, then wipe them, then wipe your face and palms with them."],[272,"We were on a journey with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and we traveled one night until, when it was the pre-dawn before morning, we fell into that sleep—and no sleep is sweeter to a traveler than it—and nothing woke us except the heat of the sun. He mentioned part of the hadith—and he said: Then he called for prayer and prayed with the people. Then he turned from his prayer, and there was a man who had set himself apart and had not prayed with the group. He said to him: “What prevented you, O so-and-so, from praying with the group?” He said: “O Messenger of God, I became junub and there is no water.” He said: “You must use the earth, for it suffices you.” Then he traveled on, and the people complained to him of thirst. He called so-and-so—Abu Raja’ named him but ‘Awf forgot—and he called ‘Ali b. Abi Talib, and said to them: “Go and seek water for us.” They departed and met a woman between two water-skins on a camel—he mentioned the hadith. And he said: Then an announcement was made among the people: “Give water to drink and take water for yourselves.” So whoever wished gave water to drink and whoever wished took water. He said: The last of that was that he gave the one who had been afflicted with janabah a vessel of water and said: “Go and pour it over yourself.”"],[273,"“And if you are ill or on a journey…” [the verse]. He said: When a man has a wound in the path of God, or sores, or smallpox, and then becomes junub, and he fears that if he washes he will die, then let him perform tayammum."],[274,"A man became junub in winter, so he asked and was ordered to wash. He washed and died. That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: “What is wrong with them? They killed him—may God kill them—three times. God has made the earth—or tayammum—a means of purification.”"],[275,"I came with ‘Abd Allah b. Yasar, the freedman of Maymunah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, until we entered upon Abu al-Juhaym b. al-Harith b. al-Simmah al-Ansari. Abu Juhaym said: The Messenger of God ﷺ came from the direction of Bi’r Jamal, and a man met him and greeted him, but the Messenger of God ﷺ did not return the greeting until he faced the wall, then he wiped his face and hands and returned the greeting."],[276,"A woman asked the Prophet ﷺ about menstrual blood that gets on a garment. He said: “Scrape it, then rub it with water, then sprinkle it.” This is the hadith of Hammad. In the report of Ibn ‘Uyaynah: “Then sprinkle it and pray in it.” In the report of Yahya: “Then you sprinkle it and pray in it.”"],[277,"She heard a woman asking the Prophet ﷺ: “When one of us becomes pure, what should she do with the garments she used to wear?” The Prophet ﷺ said: “If she sees something on it, let her scrape it, then rub it with some water, and sprinkle water over the rest of the garment, and pray in it.”"],[278,"I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about menstrual blood that gets on a garment. He said: “Wash it with water and lote-tree leaves, and rub it with a rib.”"],[279,"How did you women use to do with your garments when you menstruated in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ? She said: “We used to menstruate in our garments and our shifts, and we would wash nothing from them except the trace of what the blood had touched. Yet the servant among your servants today finishes on the day of her purity only with washing her garments.”"],[280,"I asked ‘A’ishah about a man who has intercourse with his wife and then wears the garment and sweats in it—is that impure? She said: “The woman used to prepare a rag or rags, and when that happened the man would wipe the filth from himself with it, and he did not consider that it made him impure.”"],[281,"The woman would take a rag, and when her husband finished, she would hand it to him, and he would wipe the filth from himself, and she would wipe herself, then they would pray in their garments."],[282,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to enter upon Umm So-and-so, and she would spread a leather mat for him, and he would take a midday nap on it. She would take some of his sweat and put it into her perfume."],[283,"Al-Husayn urinated in the lap of the Prophet ﷺ, so I said: 'Give me your garment so I can wash it.' He said: 'Only the urine of a female is washed; the urine of a male is sprinkled.' In the hadith of Asad b. Musa: Al-Husayn was in the lap of the Prophet ﷺ and urinated on him, so I said: 'Put on another garment and give me your garment so I can wash it.'"],[284,"I was the servant of the Prophet ﷺ. Al-Hasan — or al-Husayn — was brought and he urinated on his chest. They wanted to wash it, but he said: 'Sprinkle it; for the urine of a girl is washed and the urine of a boy is sprinkled.'"],[285,"He said concerning the urine of a nursing infant: 'The urine of a boy is sprinkled, and the urine of a girl is washed.'"],[286,"I brought a small son of mine who had not yet eaten food to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He urinated on him, so he called for water and sprinkled it."],[287,"She came to the Prophet ﷺ with a small son of hers who had not yet eaten food. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ seated him in his lap, and he urinated on him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for water and sprinkled it, and did not wash it."],[288,"When the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was affected by semen, he would wash it, then go out to prayer while I would look at a spot from the trace of washing on his garment. This is the wording of al-San'ani. In the hadith of Ibn al-Mubarak, she said: 'I used to wash the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from semen, and he would go out with the trace of water on his garment.'"],[289,"She used to scrape semen from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Some of them abridged the hadith, and some mentioned the arrival of a guest to her and her washing her blanket, and her saying: 'I saw myself scraping it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"],[290,"I used to rub janabah from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ like dried mucus."],[291,"She used to scrape semen from the garment of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying."],[292,"I used to suffer greatly and with difficulty from madhy, and I used to perform ghusl frequently because of it. So I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that, and he said: \"Wudu suffices you for that.\" I said: Then what about what gets on my garment from it? He said: \"It suffices you to take a handful of water and sprinkle with it on your garment wherever you think it reached.\""],[293,"When one of you treads on impurity with his khuff or his sandal, their purification is by dust."],[294,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the mosque with his Companions when a bedouin came and urinated in the mosque. His Companions said: \"Stop, stop.\" The Prophet ﷺ said to his Companions: \"Do not interrupt him; leave him.\" Then he called him and said: \"This mosque is not suitable for any filth or urine\" – or as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"It is only for reciting the Quran, remembering Allah, and prayer.\" Then the Prophet ﷺ said to a man among the people: \"Get up and bring us a bucket of water and pour it over it.\" So he brought a bucket of water and poured it over it."],[295,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wipe semen from his garment with a stalk of idhkhir, then pray in it, and scrape it off his garment when dry, then pray in it."],[296,"When the Prophet ﷺ saw janabah on his garment dry, he would crumble it off."],[297,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, and some of the people jumped up to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Do not interrupt him.\" Then he called for a bucket of water and poured it over it."],[298,"A bedouin urinated in the mosque, and the people rushed to him to stop him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: \"Leave him, and pour a bucket of water over his urine\" – or a pail of water – \"for you were sent to make things easy, and you were not sent to make things difficult.\""],[299,"Sufyan – and he is Ibn Husayn – narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id b. al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, h"],[300,"One day he awoke troubled, and what was seen from him was unrecognizable. She asked him about what she had found objectionable in him, and he said to her: \"Jibril promised me that he would meet me tonight, but I did not see him. By Allah, he did not break his promise to me.\" Maymunah said: There was a puppy in my house under a couch of ours, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it out, and then sprinkled its place with water with his hand. When night came, Jibril met him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: \"You promised me, but I did not see you.\" Jibril said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"We do not enter a house in which there is an image or a dog.\""],[301,"ʿUmar used to say in the mosque at the top of his voice: Avoid idle talk in the mosque."],[302,"While I was at the House, between the sleeping and the waking, I heard a speaker saying: Take the middle of the three. I was brought a basin of gold in it was water from Zamzam. He said: \"My chest was split open to such and such.\" Qatādah said: I said: What does he mean by that? He said: To the bottom of his belly. \"My heart was extracted, washed with Zamzam water, then returned to its place, then filled with faith and wisdom. Then I was brought a white beast called al-Burāq, above a donkey and below a mule, whose stride reaches as far as its eye can see. I was mounted on it. Then we set off until we came to the lowest heaven. Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: Who is this? He said: Jibrīl. It was said: And who is with you? He said: Muḥammad. [It was said]: And has he been sent to? He said: Yes. So it was opened for us. He said: Welcome to him, and what an excellent coming has come. I came to Ādam and said: O Jibrīl, who is this? He said: This is your father Ādam. I greeted him and he said: Welcome to the righteous son and the righteous prophet.\" He said: \"Then we set off until we reached the second heaven. Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: Who is this? He said: Jibrīl. It was said: And who is with you? He said: Muḥammad. It was said: And has he been sent to? He said: Yes. So it was opened for us. He said: Welcome to him, and what an excellent coming has come. I came to Yaḥyā and ʿĪsā and said: O Jibrīl, who are these two? He said: Yaḥyā and ʿĪsā. Saʿīd said: I believe he said in his ḥadīth: the two maternal cousins. I greeted them and they said: Welcome to the righteous brother and the righteous prophet.\" He said: \"Then we set off until we reached the third heaven. Jibrīl asked for it to be opened. It was said: Who is this? He said: Jibrīl. It was said: And who is with you? He said: Muḥammad. He said: And has he been sent to? He said: Yes. He said: So it was opened for us, and he said: Welcome to him, and what an excellent coming has come. He said: I came to Yūsuf and greeted him, and he said: Welcome to the righteous prophet and the righteous brother. Then they set off to the fourth heaven, and it was something like Jibrīl's speech and their speech. I came to Idrīs and greeted him, and he said: Welcome to the righteous brother and the righteous prophet. Then we reached the fifth heaven, and I came to Hārūn and greeted him, and he said: Welcome to the righteous brother and the righteous prophet. Then we set off to the sixth heaven, and I came to Mūsā – may God bless [him and] all of them – and greeted him, and he said: Welcome to the righteous brother and the righteous prophet. When I passed him, he wept. He said: Then I returned to the Lote Tree of the Utmost Boundary. The Prophet of God ﷺ related that its fruit is like the jars of Hajar, and its leaves like the ears of elephants. The Prophet of God ﷺ related that he saw four rivers emerging from its root: two apparent rivers and two hidden rivers. I said: O Jibrīl, what are these rivers? He said: As for the two hidden rivers, they are two rivers in Paradise. As for the two apparent ones, they are the Nile and the Euphrates. Then the Much-Frequented House was raised for us. I said: O Jibrīl, what is this? He said: This is the Much-Frequented House, into which seventy thousand angels enter every day; when they leave it, they never return to it, as the last of them. He said: Then I was brought two vessels, one of them wine and the other milk. They were presented to me, and I chose the milk. It was said: You have chosen rightly; may God keep your community upon the natural disposition. Then fifty prayers every day were made obligatory upon me. I returned with them until I came to Mūsā, and he said: What have you been commanded? I said: Fifty prayers every day. He said: Your community cannot bear that. I have tested the Children of Israel before you, and I have struggled with the Children of Israel the hardest struggle. So go back to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it for your community. I went back, and He reduced five from me. I kept going back and forth between my Lord and Mūsā, He reducing from me and he saying to me the like of his statement, until I returned with five prayers every day. I came to Mūsā, and he said: What have you been commanded? I said: Five prayers every day. He said: Your community cannot bear that. I have tested the people before you, and I have struggled with the Children of Israel the hardest struggle. So go back to your Lord and ask Him to lighten it for your community. He said: I have gone back to my Lord until I felt ashamed, but I accept and submit. Then it was called out: I have decreed – or I have finalized – My obligation and have lightened it for My servants, and I have made every good deed ten times its like.\""],[303,"He narrated to them about the night he was taken on the Night Journey."],[304,"The prayer, when it was first made obligatory, was two rakʿahs. The prayer of travel was kept as it was, and the prayer of residence was completed. I said to ʿUrwah: What was the matter with her? She used to complete it. He said: She interpreted as ʿUthmān interpreted."],[305,"God made prayer obligatory upon the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ: four in residence, two in travel, and one in fear."],[306,"The prayer of travel and residence was made obligatory as two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs. When the Messenger of God ﷺ settled in Madinah, two rakʿahs were added to the prayer of residence, two and two. The dawn prayer was left as it was because of the length of the recitation, and the Maghrib prayer because it is the witr of the day."],[307,"A bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ with disheveled hair and said: O Messenger of God, tell me what God has made obligatory upon me of prayer. He said: The five prayers, unless you wish to do something voluntary. He said: Tell me what God has made obligatory upon me of zakāh. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ informed him of the laws of Islam. He said: By the One who has honored you, I will not do anything voluntary, nor will I reduce anything of what God has made obligatory upon me. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"He has succeeded, by his father, if he is truthful – or he has entered Paradise, by his father, if he is truthful.\""],[308,"A jar of mine in which I would steep nabīdh and drink from it. If I prolonged sitting with the people, I feared I would be disgraced by its sweetness. He said: The delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came to the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he said: Welcome to the delegation, without disgrace and without regret. They said: O Messenger of God ﷺ, between us and you are the polytheists of Muḍar, and we can only reach you in the sacred months. So tell us comprehensive matters which, if one of us acts upon them, he will enter Paradise by them, and we call to it those behind us. He said: \"I command you with four and I forbid you from four: faith in God. Do you know what faith in God is?\" They said: God and His Messenger know best. He said: \"The testimony that there is no god but God, the establishment of prayer, the giving of zakāh, the fast of Ramadan, and that you give a fifth of the spoils. And I forbid you from nabīdh in the gourd, the hollowed stump, the green jar, and the pitch-sealed vessel.\""],[309,"A man said to ʿAbdallāh b. ʿUmar: Will you not go forth in battle? ʿAbdallāh b. ʿUmar said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"Islam is built on five: the testimony that there is no god but God, the establishment of prayer, the giving of zakāh, the fast of Ramadan, and the pilgrimage to the House.\""],[310,"Islam is built on five: the testimony that there is no god but God and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of God, the establishment of prayer, the giving of zakāh, the pilgrimage to the House, and the fast of Ramadan."],[311,"There were two brothers in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one of them was more virtuous than the other. The more virtuous one died, then the other lived forty nights after him and then died. The virtue of the first over the other was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said: \"Was he not praying?\" They said: \"Yes, O Messenger of Allah, and he was fine.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"So what makes you know how far his prayer took him? The likeness of prayer is only like a flowing river of abundant sweet water at a man's door, into which he plunges five times every day. What do you think that leaves of his dirt? You do not know how far his prayer took him.\""],[312,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed punishment, so carry it out on me.\" He turned away from him, and the prayer was commenced. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, and when he said the salutation, he said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a prescribed punishment, so carry it out on me.\" He said: \"Did you perform ablution when you came?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Go, for Allah has pardoned you.\""],[313,"He did something with a woman: a kiss."],[314,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I met a woman in the garden and embraced her, touched her, and kissed her, and did everything with her except that I did not have intercourse with her.\" The Prophet ﷺ fell silent, and this verse was revealed: \"Indeed, good deeds remove evil deeds. That is a reminder for those who remember.\" So the Prophet ﷺ called him and recited it to him. Umar said: \"O Messenger of Allah, is this for him specifically or for all people?\" He said: \"No, rather for all people.\""],[315,"The five prayers, and Friday to Friday, are expiations for what is between them as long as major sins are not committed."],[316,"By the One in whose hand is my soul,\" three times, then he fell silent. Every man among us bowed his head, weeping sadly because of the oath of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he said: \"There is no servant who performs the five prayers, fasts Ramadan, and avoids the seven major sins, except that the gates of Paradise will be opened for him on the Day of Resurrection until they clatter.\" Then he recited: \"If you avoid the major sins which you are forbidden, We will remove from you your lesser sins.\""],[317,"I met Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and said to him: \"Guide me to a deed by which Allah will benefit me – or admit me to Paradise.\" He said: He remained silent for a while, then turned to me and said: \"You should prostrate, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'There is no servant who prostrates to Allah a single prostration except that Allah raises him a degree thereby and wipes away a sin thereby.'\""],[318,"We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ. He said: \"So if you are able not to be overcome regarding a prayer before sunrise and before sunset.\""],[319,"Whoever prays before sunrise and before sunset, Allah will make him forbidden to the Fire."],[320,"He will never enter the Fire who prays before sunrise and before sunset."],[321,"No one will enter the Fire who prayed before sunrise or before sunset. A man from the people of Basra came to him and said: Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: Yes. He said: And I testify that you heard it."],[322,"Allah has angels who take turns among you. When it is the Fajr prayer, the angels of the day descend and witness the prayer with you all together. Then the angels of the night ascend, and the angels of the day remain with you. Their Lord asks them—and He knows them best—: How did you leave My servants doing? So they say: We came to them while they were praying, and we left them while they were praying. When it is the Asr prayer, the angels of the night descend and witness the prayer with you all together. Then the angels of the day ascend, and the angels of the night remain with you. Their Lord asks them, and He knows them best, saying: How did you leave My servants doing? So they say: We came to them while they were praying, and we left them while they were praying. He said: I think they say: So forgive them on the Day of Judgment."],[323,"The angels of the night and the angels of the day gather at the Fajr prayer and the Asr prayer. They gather at the Fajr prayer, then the angels of the night ascend and the angels of the day remain. They gather at the Asr prayer, then the angels of the day ascend and the angels of the night remain. Their Lord asks them: How did you leave My servants? They say: We came to them while they were praying, and we left them while they were praying, so forgive them on the Day of Judgment."],[324,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about the times of the prayers. He said: Pray with us. When the sun declined, [the Messenger of Allah] ﷺ prayed Zuhr. He said: And he prayed Asr while the sun was high, white and pure, and he prayed Maghrib when the sun had set, and he prayed Isha when the twilight had disappeared, and he prayed Fajr in the darkness. The next day, he ordered Bilal and he called the adhan for Zuhr, and he delayed it and cooled it, and he made it very cool. He ordered him and he called the iqamah for Asr while the sun was alive, but he delayed it more than before. He ordered him and he called the iqamah for Maghrib before the twilight disappeared, and he ordered him and he called the iqamah for Isha after a third of the night had passed, and he ordered him and he called the iqamah for Fajr and he made it bright. Then he said: Where is the one who asked about the time of prayer? He said: I am here, O Messenger of Allah. He said: The time of your prayer is between what you have seen."],[325,"The transmitter of this hadith deserves to have the takbir pronounced over him. Bundar said: So I erased it from my book. Abu Bakr said: Abu Dawud deserves to have the takbir pronounced over him because he erred, and Bundar deserves to be struck ten times because he erased this hadith from his book. It is a sound hadith as also narrated by al-Thawri from Alqamah. Abu Dawud erred and Bundar changed it. This is a sound hadith narrated by al-Thawri also from Alqamah."],[326,"Jibril led me in prayer at the House twice. He prayed Zuhr with me when the sun had declined the amount of a sandal strap, and he prayed Asr with me when the shadow of every object was equal to its length, and he prayed Maghrib with me when the fasting person breaks his fast, and he prayed Isha with me when the twilight had disappeared, and he prayed Fajr with me when food and drink become forbidden for the fasting person. The next day, he prayed Zuhr with me when the shadow of every object was equal to its length, and he prayed Asr with me when the shadow of every object was twice its length, and he prayed Maghrib with me when the fasting person breaks his fast, and he prayed Isha with me when a third of the night had passed, and he prayed the morning prayer with me after it had become bright. Then he turned to me and said: O Muhammad, the time is between these two times. This is your time and the time of the prophets before you."],[327,"When you pray Fajr, it is a time until the first horn of the sun rises. When you pray Zuhr, it is a time until you pray Asr. When you pray Asr, it is a time until the sun turns yellow. When the sun sets, it is a time until the twilight disappears. When the twilight disappears, it is a time until midnight."],[328,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Which deed is most excellent? He said: \"Prayer at the beginning of its time.\""],[329,"The mu'adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called the adhan for Zuhr, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Delay, delay\"—or he said: \"Wait, wait\"—and he said: The intense heat is from the blaze of Hell, so when the heat becomes intense, delay the prayer. Abu Dharr said: Until we saw the shadows of the hillocks."],[330,"When the heat becomes intense, delay the prayer, for the intense heat is from the blaze of Hell."],[331,"The intense heat is from the blaze of Hell, so delay the prayer in intense heat."],[332,"Make the noon prayer cool in the heat."],[333,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray the afternoon prayer while the sun was shining in my room and the shadow had not yet appeared. Ahmad said: in her room."],[334,"He entered upon Anas b. Malik in his house in al-Basrah after he had finished the noon prayer. He said: his house was next to the mosque. When we entered upon him he said: Have you prayed the afternoon prayer? We said to him: We have just finished the noon prayer. He said: Then pray the afternoon prayer. So we got up and prayed. When we finished he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: That is the prayer of the hypocrite: he sits watching the sun until when it is between the two horns of the Shaytan, he gets up and pecks it four times, not remembering Allah in it except a little."],[335,"Shu'bah narrated to us; he said: al-'Ala' b. 'Abd al-Rahman – meaning Ibn Ya'qub – narrated to us from Anas b. Malik that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"],[336,"The one who misses the afternoon prayer is as if he has been bereft of his family and his wealth."],[337,"We were with Buraydah al-Aslami on a military expedition on a cloudy day. He said: Pray early in the day, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever abandons the afternoon prayer, his deeds are nullified."],[338,"We used to pray the sunset prayer with the Prophet ﷺ, then we would go to Banu Salimah and we could see the places where the arrows fell."],[339,"They used to pray the sunset prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ, then they would return and one of them could see the places where his arrows fell."],[340,"Did you not hear the Messenger of God ﷺ say: My community will remain in good—or upon the natural disposition—as long as they do not delay the sunset prayer until the stars appear intertwined? He said: Yes."],[341,"My community will remain upon the natural dispositions as long as they do not delay the sunset prayer until the stars appear intertwined."],[342,"Do not let the Bedouins overpower you regarding the name of the sunset prayer. He said: The Bedouins say: It is the evening prayer (al-ʿisha)."],[343,"Sufyan b. Uyaynah informed us, from Amr b. Dinar and Ibn Jurayj, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas."],[344,"The Messenger of God ﷺ delayed the night prayer (al-ʿisha) one night, so Umar called out to him and said: The women and children have slept. He came out to them and said: No one on earth is waiting for this prayer except you."],[345,"We stayed one night waiting for the Messenger of God ﷺ for the later night prayer. He came out to us when a third of the night had passed, and we did not know what had kept him—something with his family or otherwise. When he came out, he said: You are waiting for a prayer that no people of a religion other than you wait for. Were it not that it would be heavy upon my community, I would pray with them at this hour. Then he ordered the muezzin, and he commenced the prayer, and he prayed."],[346,"We waited for the Messenger of God ﷺ for the night prayer until half the night had passed. Then he came and prayed with us, then said: Take your seats, for the people have taken to their beds. You will remain in prayer since you began waiting for it. Were it not for the weakness of the weak, the illness of the sick, and the need of the needy, I would delay this prayer until half the night."],[347,"The Messenger of God ﷺ disliked sleeping before the night prayer and talking after it. This is the hadith of Ahmad b. Mani. In the hadith of Yahya b. Said, he said:"],[348,"He was occupied one night from the 'Atama prayer until we slept, then we woke, then we slept, then we woke, then he came out and said: \"No one on earth is waiting for this prayer except you.\""],[349,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the 'Isha' one night until most of the night had passed, and until the people of the mosque had slept. Then he came out and prayed, and said: \"This is its time, were it not that I would burden my community.\""],[350,"\"Do not let the Bedouins prevail over you concerning the name of your prayer. They call it 'Atama because they delay milking the camels until dark. It is the 'Isha' prayer.\""],[351,"\"The believing women used to pray the Morning Prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then they would leave wrapped in their cloaks, unrecognizable.\" Ahmad added: \"Then he mentioned the darkness (ghalas).\""],[352,"He went on the expedition to Khaybar. He said: \"We prayed the Dawn Prayer there in the dark (ghalas).\""],[353,"'Umar b. 'Abd al-'Aziz was sitting on the pulpit and delayed the prayer a little. 'Urwa b. al-Zubayr said: \"Indeed, Jibril informed Muhammad ﷺ of the time of the prayer.\" 'Umar said to him: \"Know what you are saying.\" 'Urwa said: \"I heard Bashir b. Abi Mas'ud say: I heard Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Jibril descended and informed me of the time of the prayer, so I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him, then I prayed with him,\" and he counted with his fingers five prayers. \"And I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying Zuhr when the sun declines, and sometimes he would delay it when the heat became intense. And I saw him praying 'Asr while the sun was high and white before yellowness entered it, so that a man would leave after the prayer and reach Dhu al-Hulayfa before sunset. And he would pray Maghrib when the sun set, and he would pray 'Isha' when the horizon darkened, and sometimes he would delay it until the people gathered. And he prayed the Morning Prayer once in the dark (ghalas), then he prayed another time and it became bright, then his prayer after that was in the dark until he died ﷺ, and he did not return to making it bright.\""],[354,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about the time of the prayer. He mentioned the hadith in full about the times of the prayer over two days and two nights, and he said regarding the first night: \"Then Bilal called the adhan for 'Isha' when the whiteness of the day had gone, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded him, so he performed the iqama and prayed.\" And he said regarding the second night: \"Then Bilal called the adhan for 'Isha' when the whiteness of the day had gone, but the Prophet ﷺ delayed it, so we slept, then we slept many times. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and said: 'The people have prayed and slept, and you have been in prayer since you have been waiting for the prayer.'\""],[355,"\"The time of Zuhr is until 'Asr, the time of 'Asr is until the sun turns yellow, the time of Maghrib is until the redness of the twilight disappears, the time of 'Isha' is until midnight, and the time of the Morning Prayer is until sunrise.\""],[356,"\"Until the twilight disappears.\""],[357,"Dawn is of two kinds: a dawn in which food is forbidden and prayer is permitted, and a dawn in which prayer is forbidden and food is permitted."],[358,"“Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah erases sins and increases good deeds?” They said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Performing ablution thoroughly despite difficulties, and waiting for the prayer after the prayer. There is no man among you who leaves his house and prays with the imam, then sits waiting for the next prayer, but the angels say, ‘O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him.’”"],[359,"Seven whom Allah will shade in His shade on the day when there is no shade but His: the just imam, a young man who grew up in the worship of Allah, a man whose heart is attached to the mosques, two men who loved each other for the sake of Allah, meeting and parting for that, a man whom a woman of status and beauty sought, and he said, “Indeed I fear Allah,” a man who gave charity secretly, so that his left hand did not know what his right hand spent, and a man who remembered Allah in solitude and his eyes overflowed."],[360,"There is no man who frequented the mosques and then something or an illness occupied him, and then he returned to what he used to do, but Allah rejoices at him as the family of an absent person rejoices at their absent one when he arrives."],[361,"The servant remains in prayer as long as he is in his place of prayer waiting for the prayer. The angels say, ‘O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him,’ as long as he does not leave or pass wind.” They said, “What is passing wind?” He said, “Breaking wind silently or audibly.”"],[362,"When the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to gather and estimate the time of prayer, and no one called to it. One day they spoke about this, and some of them said, “Use a bell like the bell of the Christians.” Others said, “Rather, a horn like the horn of the Jews.” Umar said, “Why not send a man to call to prayer?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Stand, O Bilal, and call to prayer.”"],[363,"Bilal used to say at the beginning of his call: “I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, come to prayer.” Umar said to him, “Say after it: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Say as Umar ordered you.”"],[364,"When morning came, we went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and I told him of the dream. He said, “This dream is true. Stand with Bilal, for he has a louder or clearer voice than you, and cast upon him what was said to you, and let him call with that.” He said, “So I did.” When Umar b. al-Khattab heard Bilal’s call to prayer, he went out to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dragging his cloak, saying, “O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, I saw the like of what he said.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Praise be to Allah.”"],[365,"When the Muslims came to Madinah, they used to gather and estimate the time of prayer, and no one called to it. One day they spoke about this, and some of them said, “Use a bell like the bell of the Christians.” Others said, “Rather, a horn like the horn of the Jews.” Umar said, “Why not send a man to call to prayer?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Stand, O Bilal, and call to prayer.”"],[366,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ came to it, the people used to assemble for prayer at the times of prayer without any call. The Messenger of God ﷺ intended to have a horn like the horn of the Jews, which they use to call to their prayers, but he disliked it. Then he ordered a bell to be made so that it could be struck to call the Muslims to prayer. While they were in that situation, Abdullah b. Zayd b. Abd Rabbih, brother of al-Harith b. al-Khazraj, was shown the call in a dream. He came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said to him, 'O Messenger of God, a visitor came to me this night; a man passed by me wearing two green garments, carrying a bell in his hand. I said, O servant of God, will you sell this bell? He said, And what will you do with it? I said, We will call the people to prayer with it. He said, Shall I direct you to something better than that? I said, What is it? He said: Say: God is most great, God is most great, God is most great, God is most great. I bear witness that there is no god but God, I bear witness that there is no god but God. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God. Come to prayer, come to prayer. Come to success, come to success. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God. Then he paused a little, then he said the same as before, but made it odd, except that he said: The prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God. When I told this to the Messenger of God ﷺ, he said: It is a true vision, God willing. Go with Bilal and teach it to him, for he has a louder voice than you. When Bilal called the adhan with it, Umar b. al-Khattab heard it while he was in his house, and he came out to the Messenger of God ﷺ dragging his cloak, saying: O Prophet of God, by the One who sent you with the truth, I saw the like of what he saw. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: To God be the praise; that is firmer."],[367,"Bilal was ordered to make the call to prayer even and the commencement single."],[368,"They sought something to call with as a sign for prayer. He said, “Bilal was ordered to make the call to prayer even and the commencement single.”"],[369,"When the people increased, they mentioned that they should know the time of prayer by something they recognize. They mentioned lighting a fire or striking a bell. Then Bilal was ordered to make the call to prayer even and the commencement single."],[370,"At the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when prayer came, a man would walk in the road and call, “Prayer, prayer.” That became difficult for the people, so they said, “O Messenger of Allah, why not use a bell?” He said, “That is for the Christians.” They said, “Then why not use a horn?” He said, “That is for the Jews.” He said, “So Bilal was ordered to make the call to prayer even and the commencement single.”"],[371,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ came to it, the people used to assemble for prayer at the times of prayer without any call. The Messenger of God ﷺ intended to have a horn like the horn of the Jews, which they use to call to their prayers, but he disliked it. Then he ordered a bell to be made so that it could be struck to call the Muslims to prayer. While they were in that situation, Abdullah b. Zayd b. Abd Rabbih, brother of al-Harith b. al-Khazraj, was shown the call in a dream. He came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said to him, 'O Messenger of God, a visitor came to me this night; a man passed by me wearing two green garments, carrying a bell in his hand. I said, O servant of God, will you sell this bell? He said, And what will you do with it? I said, We will call the people to prayer with it. He said, Shall I direct you to something better than that? I said, What is it? He said: Say: God is most great, God is most great, God is most great, God is most great. I bear witness that there is no god but God, I bear witness that there is no god but God. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God. Come to prayer, come to prayer. Come to success, come to success. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God. Then he paused a little, then he said the same as before, but made it odd, except that he said: The prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God. When I told this to the Messenger of God ﷺ, he said: It is a true vision, God willing. Go with Bilal and teach it to him, for he has a louder voice than you. When Bilal called the adhan with it, Umar b. al-Khattab heard it while he was in his house, and he came out to the Messenger of God ﷺ dragging his cloak, saying: O Prophet of God, by the One who sent you with the truth, I saw the like of what he saw. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: To God be the praise; that is firmer."],[372,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ ordered the bell, it was made to be struck for the people when gathering for prayer."],[374,"\"This is indeed a true vision, God willing.\" Then he ordered the call to prayer, and Bilal, the freedman of Abu Bakr, used to call the adhan with it."],[375,"The adhan in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ was twice twice and the iqamah once, except that he used to say: The prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced. When we heard that, we performed ablution and went out.\""],[376,"Bilal used to say the adhan in pairs and the iqamah singly, except for his saying: The prayer has commenced, the prayer has commenced.\""],[377,"Bilal was ordered to \"make the adhan even and the iqamah odd, except the iqamah,\" meaning: The prayer has commenced."],[378,"He ordered about twenty men, and they called the adhan. He was pleased with the voice of Abu Mahdhurah, so he taught him the adhan: \"God is most great, God is most great, God is most great, God is most great. I bear witness that there is no god but God, I bear witness that there is no god but God. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God. I bear witness that there is no god but God, I bear witness that there is no god but God. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God, I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God. Come to prayer, come to prayer. Come to success, come to success. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God.\" And he taught him the iqamah in pairs.\""],[379,"He seated him and dictated the adhan to him word by word. Bishr said: Ibrahim said to me: It is like this adhan of ours. I said to him: Repeat it to me. He said: \"God is most great, God is most great. I bear witness that there is no god but God twice. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God twice.\" He said in a voice; that voice was audible to those around him: I bear witness that there is no god but God twice. I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of God twice. Then he raised his voice and said: Come to prayer twice. Come to success twice. God is most great, God is most great. There is no god but God.\""],[380,"Abd al-Aziz b. Abd al-Malik b. Abi Mahdhurah informed me, from Abdullah b. Muhayriz: , : , :"],[381,"Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, and Ibn Abi Layla narrated it, from Amr b. Murrah, from Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, from Abdullah b. Zayd. Abdullah b. Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated it to us, saying: Uqbah - meaning Ibn Khalid - narrated to us. 381 (h) And al-Hasan b. Qaza'ah narrated to us, saying: Husayn b. Numayr narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us."],[382,"Asim—meaning Ibn 'Ali—narrated to us, saying: Al-Mas'udi narrated to us. (H) And al-Hasan b. Yunus b. Mihran al-Zayyat narrated to us the report of Abu Bakr b. 'Ayyash, saying: Al-Aswad b. 'Amir narrated to us, saying: Abu Bakr b. 'Ayyash narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from 'Amr b. Murrah, from 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla, from Mu'adh."],[383,"Abu Bakr narrated to us, saying: Al-Makhzumi narrated it to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, from 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Layla."],[384,"The prayer was changed through three states, and the fasting through three states. Our companions narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'It has pleased me that the prayer of the believers—or the Muslims—be one. Until I resolved to send men to the dwellings to announce to the people the time of prayer.'"],[385,"The prayer was changed through three states, and the fasting was changed through three states."],[386,"'Uthman b. al-Sa'ib, their client, informed me, from his father, the client of Abu Mahdhurah, and from Umm 'Abd al-Malik b. Abi Mahdhurah, that they both heard that from Abu Mahdhurah."],[387,"It is from the Sunnah that when the muezzin says in the dawn call: 'Come to success,' he says: 'Prayer is better than sleep.'"],[388,"He began to say in his call to prayer like this, turning his head right and left with “Come to success.”"],[389,"I saw Bilāl giving the call to prayer, and he had placed his fingers in his ears while he was turning in his call right and left."],[390,"When you are in the desert, raise your voice with the call, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: “No tree, clod, stone, jinn, or human hears his voice except that it will testify for him.”"],[391,"The muezzin is forgiven as far as his voice reaches, and every moist and dry thing testifies for him, and the one who attends the prayer is credited with twenty-five good deeds, and what is between them is expiated.”"],[392,"If the people knew what is in the call to prayer and the first row, and then found no way except to draw lots for it, they would draw lots for it."],[393,"When Satan hears the call to prayer, he turns his back and breaks wind so that he will not hear it."],[394,"When Satan hears the call to prayer, he goes away until he is at the distance of al-Rawḥāʼ."],[395,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and the muezzin wanted to give the call to prayer, so he said: “Cool it down.” Then he wanted to give the call, so he said: “Cool it down.” Shuʿbah said: the third time—to the best of my knowledge—until we saw the shade of the mounds, meaning the mountains. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “The intensity of the heat is from the blast of Hell, so cool down the prayer.”"],[396,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ with a man, and he bade us farewell, then said: “When you two travel and the prayer time comes, give the call to prayer and the iqāmah, and let the elder of you lead you.””"],[397,"We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, myself and a cousin of mine, and he said: “When you two travel, give the call to prayer and the iqāmah, and let the elder of you lead you.”"],[398,"We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while we were young men of similar age, and we stayed with him twenty nights. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was compassionate and gentle. When he thought that we had missed our families—or longed for them—he asked us about those we had left behind, and we told him. He said: 'Return to your families, stay with them, teach them, and command them.' He mentioned things I remember and things I do not remember, and he said: 'Pray as you have seen me praying. When the time for prayer comes, let one of you call the adhan, and let the eldest of you lead you in prayer.'"],[399,"Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Malik b. al-Huwayrith, and he mentioned the hadith in full."],[400,"The Prophet ﷺ heard a man while he was on a journey saying: 'Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar.' The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: 'He is upon the fitrah.' The man said: 'Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah.' He said: 'He has come out of the Fire.' The people rushed to the man, and found him to be a shepherd who had been overtaken by the time for prayer, and he stood up to give the call to prayer."],[401,"He used to raid at the time of the Morning Prayer. If he heard an adhan, he would restrain himself; otherwise, he would raid. One day he listened and heard a man saying: 'Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar.' He said: 'He is upon the fitrah.' Then the man said: 'Ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah.' He said: 'You have come out of the Fire.'"],[402,"Bilal gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until you hear the call to prayer of Ibn Umm Maktum."],[403,"Let not Bilal's call to prayer prevent any of you from his pre-dawn meal; for he gives the call to prayer—or he calls out—so that your standing one may return and your sleeping one may wake up. It is not that he says it like this and like this, until he says it like this and like this."],[404,"Bilal gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer. Between them there was only the time it takes for one to ascend and the other to descend."],[405,"When Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer, then eat and drink; and when Bilal gives the call to prayer, then do not eat or drink. If any of us women still had some of her pre-dawn meal left, she would say to Bilal: 'Wait until I finish my pre-dawn meal.'"],[406,"Ibn Umm Maktum—or Bilal—used to call out at night, so eat and drink until Bilal—or Ibn Umm Maktum—called out. There was nothing between them except that one would descend and the other would ascend. Then she would take hold of his garment and say: 'Stay as you are until I have my pre-dawn meal.'"],[407,"Indeed Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until Bilal gives the call to prayer; and Bilal would not give the call to prayer until he saw the dawn."],[408,"I said to 'A'ishah: At what hour do you perform Witr? She said: I do not perform Witr until they give the call to prayer, and they do not give the call to prayer until the dawn breaks. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two muezzins, so-and-so and 'Amr b. Umm Maktum. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"When 'Amr gives the call to prayer, eat and drink, for he is a man blind of sight, and when Bilal gives the call to prayer, raise your hands, for Bilal does not give the call to prayer until it is morning.\""],[409,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had three muezzins: Bilal, Abu Mahdhurah, and 'Amr b. Umm Maktum. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When 'Amr gives the call to prayer, he is blind of sight, so let him not deceive you, and when Bilal gives the call to prayer, let no one eat."],[410,"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night. Some of the people said: Would that you halt with us, O Messenger of Allah! He said: \"I fear that you will sleep past the prayer.\" He mentioned the hadith in full, and said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up, then said: \"O Bilal, get up and call the people to prayer.\""],[411,"'Abdullah b. Rabah narrated to the people in the congregational mosque, and among the people was 'Imran b. Husayn. 'Imran said: Who is the young man? He said: A man from the Ansar. 'Imran said: The people are more knowledgeable of their hadith. Watch how you narrate, for I was the seventh of seven that night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. 'Imran said: I did not think anyone besides me remained who memorized this hadith. He said: I heard Abu Qatadah say: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey. He said: \"If you do not reach water by tomorrow, you will be thirsty.\" So the swift ones among the people went ahead. Abu Qatadah said: I stayed close to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that night. He dozed and slept, so I propped him up. Then he dozed again and leaned, so I propped him up. Then he dozed and leaned the other way until he was about to fall off, so he woke up and said: \"Who is the man?\" I said: Abu Qatadah. [He said]: \"How long has your journey been?\" I said: Since the night. He said: \"May Allah preserve you as you preserved His Prophet.\" Then he said: \"Would that we halts.\" He leaned toward a tree and I leaned with him. He said: \"Do you see anyone?\" I said: Yes, this is a rider, [this is a rider,] these are two riders, these are three, until we became seven. He said: \"Guard for us our prayer so that we do not sleep past the Fajr prayer.\" Then sleep fell upon their ears until the heat of the sun woke them. They got up and led their mounts a little, then dismounted. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Do you have water with you?\" I said: Yes, I have a small water vessel with water in it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Bring it.\" So I brought it to him. He said: \"Pour from it, pour from it.\" So we performed ablution, and a gulp remained in it. He said: \"Keep it safe, O Abu Qatadah, for there is news regarding this.\" Then Bilal gave the call to prayer, and they prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then they prayed Fajr, then they mounted. Some of them said to others: We missed our prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"What are you saying? If it is something of your worldly affair, then it is your concern, and if it is something of your religious affair, then it is to me.\" We said: O Messenger of Allah, we missed our prayer. He said: \"There is no negligence in sleep; negligence is only in wakefulness. If one of you forgets his prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, and the next day at its time.\""],[412,"When you hear the caller, say the like of what he says."],[413,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with her on her day and heard the muezzin giving the call to prayer, he would say as the muezzin says until he finished."],[414,"He would say as the muezzin says until the muezzin fell silent."],[415,"We entered upon Mu'awiyah, and the caller called to prayer. He said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar. Mu'awiyah said: \"Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar.\" Then he said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. Mu'awiyah said: \"And I bear witness.\" Then he said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Mu'awiyah said: \"And I bear witness.\" Then he said: Come to prayer. Mu'awiyah said: \"There is no might and no power except with Allah.\" Then he said: Come to success. Mu'awiyah said: \"There is no might and no power except with Allah.\" Then [he said]: \"Thus I heard your Prophet ﷺ say.\""],[416,"The muezzin gave the call to prayer. He said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar. Mu'awiyah b. Abi Sufyan said: \"Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar.\" Then he said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. Mu'awiyah said: \"I bear witness that there is no god but Allah.\" He said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Mu'awiyah said: \"I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" Then Mu'awiyah said: \"Thus I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say.\""],[417,"I was with Mu'awiyah b. Abi Sufyan, and the muezzin said: \"Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar.\" Mu'awiyah said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar. He said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. Mu'awiyah said: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah. He said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Mu'awiyah said: I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. He said: Come to prayer. Mu'awiyah said: There is no might and no power except with Allah. He said: Come to success. Mu'awiyah said: There is no might and no power except with Allah. He said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, there is no god but Allah. Mu'awiyah said: Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, there is no god but Allah. Then he said: \"Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say.\""],[418,"When the mu'adhdhin says: 'Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar', and one of you says: 'Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar'; then he says: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah', and he says: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah'; then he says: 'I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah', and he says: 'I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah'; then he says: 'Come to prayer', and he says: 'There is no might nor power except with Allah'; then he says: 'Come to success', and he says: 'There is no might nor power except with Allah'; then he says: 'Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar', and he says: 'Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar'; then he says: 'There is no god but Allah', and he says: 'There is no god but Allah' from his heart, he will enter Paradise."],[419,"Sa'id b. Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Ka'b b. 'Alqamah, from 'Abd al-Rahman b. Jubayr, from 'Abdullah b. 'Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \""],[420,"Two things are not rejected, or rarely rejected: supplication at the time of the call to prayer, and at the time of battle when the fighting intensifies."],[421,"Whoever says when he hears the call to prayer: 'O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and the established prayer, grant Muhammad the wasilah and the virtue, and raise him to the praiseworthy station which You have promised him', intercession will be permissible for him on the Day of Resurrection."],[422,"Whoever says when he hears the mu'adhdhin: 'And I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Muhammad as Messenger, and with Islam as religion', his sins will be forgiven."],[423,"Whoever hears the mu'adhdhin pronouncing the testimony of faith, and turns his face toward him, and says: 'I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. I am pleased with Allah as Lord, and with Islam as religion', his past sins will be forgiven."],[424,"I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, teach me the Qur'an and make me the imam of my people.' He said: 'Follow the weakest of them, and appoint a mu'adhdhin who does not take payment for his adhan.'"],[425,"The only difference between them was the time it took for one to descend and the other to ascend."],[426,"Supplication between the adhan and the iqamah is not rejected."],[427,"Supplication between the adhan and the iqamah is not rejected."],[428,"The supplication between the adhan and the iqamah is not rejected, so supplicate."],[429,"We prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ toward Bayt al-Maqdis for sixteen or seventeen months, then we were turned toward the Ka'bah."],[430,"And the report of Ka'b b. Malik about the departure of the Ansar from Madinah to Makkah for the pledge at al-ʿAqabah. And it was mentioned in the report that al-Bara' b. Ma'rur said to the Prophet ﷺ: I set out on this journey of mine, and Allah had guided me to Islam. I saw that I should not place this house (the Ka'bah) behind my back, so I prayed toward it. My companions opposed me in that until something of that entered my heart. What do you think? He said: You were upon a qiblah if you had remained patient on it. He said: So al-Bara' returned to the qiblah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and prayed with us toward Syria."],[431,"The Prophet ﷺ and his companions used to pray toward Bayt al-Maqdis. When this verse was revealed: “So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque,” a man from Banu Salimah passed by and called out to them while they were bowing in the Fajr prayer: “Indeed the qiblah has been changed to the Ka'bah.” So they turned while bowing."],[432,"They used to pray toward Bayt al-Maqdis. He mentioned something similar, and added: And they counted what had passed of their prayer."],[433,"When the Prophet ﷺ entered the House (the Ka'bah), he supplicated in all its corners and did not pray in it until he left it. When he left, he prayed two rak'ahs in front of the Ka'bah and said: “This is the qiblah.”"],[434,"Then we were turned toward the Ka'bah. And Isra'il said, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara': Then he was directed to the Ka'bah, and he loved to be directed to the Ka'bah."],[435,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed toward Bayt al-Maqdis for months. While one day he was praying Dhuhr, he had prayed two rak'ahs when he was turned to the Ka'bah. The foolish ones said: What has turned them away from their qiblah upon which they used to be?"],[436,"The people of Quba' used to pray toward Bayt al-Maqdis. A messenger came to them and said: Indeed the Quran has been revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he has turned toward the Ka'bah, so face it. So they turned around as they were."],[438,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ toward Bayt al-Maqdis for sixteen months."],[439,"Ibn Abbas recited: \"Shall we compel you to accept it from the direction of ourselves?\"—from the direction of yourselves."],[440,"When one of you performs ablution in his house, then comes to the mosque, he is in prayer until he returns, so let him not do thus,\" and he interlocked his fingers."],[441,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said to Ka'b b. Ujrah: \"When you perform ablution, then enter the mosque, do not interlock your fingers.\""],[442,"When one of you performs ablution, then goes out to the mosque, let him not interlock his fingers, for he is in prayer."],[443,"I met Ka'b b. Ujrah while I was heading to Friday prayer, and I had interlocked my fingers. When I drew near, he struck my hands and separated my fingers, saying: \"We were forbidden for anyone to interlock his fingers in prayer.\" I said: \"I am not in prayer.\" He said: \"Have you not performed ablution and are you not intending Friday prayer?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then you are in prayer.\""],[444,"Muhammad b. Rafi' narrated it to us, saying: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, saying: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us. Abu Bakr said: Sa'd b. Ishaq b. Ka'b is from Banu Salim."],[445,"Abu Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated it to us, saying: Abu Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn 'Ajlan."],[446,"From Sa'id, from Ka'b. And Ibn Abi Dhi'b had made clear that al-Maqburi, Sa'id b. Abi Sa'id, only narrated it from a man of Banu Salim, and in my view that was Sa'd b. Ishaq. However, he erred [regarding the chain above] Sa'd b. Ishaq, saying: from his father, from his grandfather Ka'b. But Dawud b. Qays and Anas b. Iyad both agreed that the report is only from Abu Thumamah: ."],[447,"Whoever performs ablution, then goes out intending prayer, he is in prayer until he returns to his house, and he should not say this."],[448,"When one of you performs ablution in his house, then comes to the mosque, he is in prayer until he returns, so let him not do thus,\" and he interlocked his fingers."],[449,"He slept in the presence of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: Then the muezzin came to him, and he went out to prayer saying: \"O God, place light in my heart, and place light on my tongue, and place light in my hearing, and place light in my sight, and place light behind me, and light in front of me, and place light above me, and light below me. O God, magnify light for me."],[450,"I spent the night at my maternal aunt Maymunah's."],[451,"There was a man from the Ansar whose house was the farthest house in Madinah, and he never missed praying with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I felt sorry for him and said: O so-and-so, if only you would buy a donkey to protect you from the scorching heat, to lift you from the rugged ground, and to protect you from the vermin of the earth. He said: By Allah, I do not like that my house be adjacent to the house of Muhammad ﷺ. He (the narrator) said: I carried this as a burden until I came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He said: He called him and asked him, and he mentioned the same thing to him, and he mentioned that he hopes for reward in his footsteps. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'You shall have what you hoped for.'"],[452,"The vacant lands around the mosque became empty, so Banu Salimah wanted to be near the mosque. That reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'O Banu Salimah, do you intend to move near the mosque?' They said: Yes. He said: 'O Banu Salimah, remain in your homes, for your footsteps are recorded.' He said it three times."],[453,"When one of you enters the mosque, let him greet the Prophet and say: O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy. And when he leaves, let him greet the Prophet and say: O Allah, protect me from the accursed devil."],[454,"A man came to the prayer while the Prophet ﷺ was praying with us, and he said when he reached the row: O Allah, give me the best of what You give Your righteous servants. When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, he said: 'Who spoke just now?' The man said: I, O Messenger of Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Then your horse will be hamstrung and you will be martyred in the path of Allah.'"],[455,"A man entered the mosque and prayed, then came and greeted the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'When you stand for prayer, perform ablution thoroughly, then face the qiblah and say the takbir.'"],[456,"Actions are but by intention."],[457,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for prayer, he raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then said the takbir. When he wanted to bow, he did the same. When he raised from bowing, he did the same, but he did not do that when raising his head from prostration."],[458,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and his companions, and I saw them raising their hands in their burnouses."],[459,"He used to spread his fingers in prayer, spreading them."],[460,"Abu Hurayrah entered upon us the mosque of Banu Zurayq and said: Three things the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do which people have abandoned. When he stood for prayer, he did this - and Abu 'Amir gestured with his hand without spreading his fingers or closing them - and he said: This is how Ibn Abi Dhi'b showed us. Abu Bakr said: Yahya b. Hakim gestured to us and raised his hands, spreading his fingers moderately, neither wide nor closed, and he raised his hands above his head, stretching them. He would pause briefly before the recitation, asking Allah, the Exalted, from His bounty, and he would say the takbir in prayer whenever he prostrated and raised."],[461,"He raised his hands, stretching them."],[462,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque, and a man entered and prayed, then greeted the Prophet ﷺ. He returned the greeting and said: 'Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.' He did that three times, until the man said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I do not know any other than this. He said: 'When you stand for prayer, say the takbir, then recite what is easy with you from the Qur'an, then bow until you are at ease in bowing, then rise until you are standing straight, then prostrate until you are at ease in prostration, then rise until you are at ease sitting, and do that in all of your prayer.'"],[463,"He used to, when he began the prayer, say the takbir, then say: 'I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth, as a hanif, and I am not of the polytheists. Indeed, my prayer, my rites, my living, and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds. He has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims. O Allah, You are the Sovereign, there is no god but You. You are my Lord and I am Your servant. I have wronged myself and I confess my sin, so forgive me all my sins; indeed, none forgives sins but You. Guide me to the best of character; none guides to the best of it but You. Turn away from me its evil; none turns away its evil but You. Here I am at Your service, and in Your aid, and all good is in Your hands, and evil is not to be attributed to You. I am through You and to You. Blessed and exalted are You. I seek Your forgiveness and turn to You in repentance.' Abu Salih said: 'There is no god for me but You.'"],[464,"Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Abd Allah b. al-Fadl, and from his uncle al-Majishun, from al-A'raj with this chain of transmission, the like of it. Muhammad b. Yahya said: 'One of them adds a word or a thing to his companion.' Abu Bakr said: 'His saying \"and evil is not to be attributed to You\": meaning, it is not from what one draws near to You with.'"],[465,"He used to, when he stood for the obligatory prayer, say the takbir, and say when beginning the prayer after the takbir: 'I have turned my face to the One who originated the heavens and the earth,' and he mentioned the hadith at length, and he said: 'and I am of the Muslims.'"],[466,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he said the takbir in prayer, remained silent for a little while. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be your ransom, what do you say in your silence between the takbir and the recitation?' He said: 'I say: O Allah, distance me from my sins as You distanced between the east and the west. O Allah, purify me from my sins as a white garment is purified from dirt. O Allah, wash me from my sins with snow, water, and hail.'"],[467,"Qatadah narrated to us, from Anas."],[468,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he stood at night for prayer, said the takbir three times, then said: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise. Blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You.' Then he said: 'There is no god but Allah' three times, then he said: 'Allah is greatest' three times, then he said: 'I seek refuge in Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from Satan the accursed; from his madness, his blowing, and his spitting.' Then he recited."],[469,"When he began the prayer, he said: 'Allah is greatest, greatly' three times, 'Praise be to Allah, abundantly' three times, 'Glory be to Allah, morning and evening' three times."],[470,"Ibn Idris narrated to us, (h) and Harun b. Ishaq narrated to us, [from Ibn] Fudayl, all of them from Husayn b. Abd al-Rahman. Abu Bakr said: 'Asim al-Anazi and Abbad b. Asim are unknown; we do not know who they are, and we do not know [them]. The correct version is what Husayn or Shu'bah reported.'"],[471,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he began the prayer, raised his hands level with his shoulders and said the takbir, then said: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, blessed is Your name, exalted is Your majesty, and there is no god but You.'"],[473,"He used to say: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from Satan the accursed, and from his blowing, his madness, and his spitting.'"],[474,"Three things the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do, which the people have abandoned: when he stood for prayer, he raised his hands fully; and he used to pause before the recitation for a little while, asking Allah of His bounty; and he used to say the takbir whenever he lowered and raised. Bundar said in his hadith: 'Three things he used to do, which the people have abandoned: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he stood for prayer, raised his hands fully; and he used to pause before the recitation for a little while, saying: I ask Allah of His bounty; and he used to say the takbir whenever he bowed and placed [his hands].'"],[475,"ﷺ - the noon prayer, and when he said the taslim, he called out to a man who was in the last row, saying: O so-and-so, will you not fear Allah? Will you not look at how you pray? When one of you stands to pray, he is only standing to confide in his Lord, so let him look at how he confides in Him. You think that I do not see you? By Allah, I certainly see from behind my back just as I see from in front of me."],[476,"What is the matter with people who raise their eyes to the sky during their prayer? His words about that became severe, until he said: Let them stop doing that, or their eyesight will be snatched away."],[477,"The Prophet’s ﷺ words about that became severe."],[478,"I came to Madinah and said: I will certainly look at the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I saw him when he began the prayer, he said the takbir and raised—meaning his hands—and I saw his thumbs level with his ears. Then he took his left hand with his right hand, then he recited."],[479,"I was among those who came to the Prophet ﷺ, and I said: I will certainly look at the prayer of the Messenger of Allah, how he prays. I saw him when he said the takbir, he raised his hands until they were level with his ears. Then he struck his right hand over his left and held it."],[480,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he placed his right hand over his left hand on his chest."],[481,"I said: I will certainly look at the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, how he prays. He said: So I looked at him. He stood, said the takbir and raised his hands until they were level with his ears. Then he placed his right hand on the back of his left palm, and the wrist and the forearm."],[482,"Yunus reported to me, from al-Zuhri who said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas—the freed slave of Banu Layth—telling Sa'id b. al-Musayyib that Abu Dharr said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: the like of it."],[483,"Allah continues to face the servant as long as he does not turn away. When he turns his face away, He turns away from him."],[484,"Allah commanded Yahya b. Zakariyya with five words to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon them. He preached to the people, then said: Allah has commanded you with prayer. When you set your faces, do not turn away, for Allah sets His face to the face of His servant when he prays to Him, and He does not turn His face away from him until the servant himself turns away."],[485,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about turning around during prayer. He said: It is a snatching away that Satan snatches from the servant’s prayer. And in the report of Abu al-Ahwas: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man’s turning around during prayer."],[486,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to turn to his right and left during his prayer, but he did not twist his neck behind his back."],[487,"The Prophet ﷺ kept turning toward the mountain pass until, when he finished his prayer and said the salam, he said to me: \"Rejoice, for your horseman has come to you.\""],[488,"Mu'awiyah b. Sallam narrated to us in a long hadith: :, :"],[489,"There is no prayer for one who does not recite the Opening of the Book."],[490,"Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Mother of the Qur'an, it is deficient, it is deficient, it is deficient, not complete.\" I said: \"O Abu Hurayrah, sometimes I am behind the imam.\" He said: Then he pinched my forearm and said: \"O Persian, recite it to yourself.\""],[491,"No prayer is sufficient in which the Opening of the Book is not recited. I said: \"What if I am behind the imam?\" He took my hand and said: \"Recite it to yourself, O Persian.\""],[492,"That the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman used to begin the recitation with 'All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds.'"],[493,"That the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman used to begin the recitation with 'All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds.'"],[494,"That the Prophet ﷺ recited in prayer 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful' and counted it as one verse, 'All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds' as two verses, and 'You alone we ask for help' and he joined his five fingers."],[495,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with Abu Bakr, and 'Umar, and I did not hear any of them recite: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.'"],[496,"I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman, and they did not say aloud: 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.'"],[497,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not say aloud 'In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful,' nor did Abu Bakr, nor 'Umar, nor 'Uthman."],[498,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and with Abu Bakr, and Umar, and they did not recite aloud “In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.”"],[499,"He used to recite “In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful” silently in prayer, as did Abu Bakr and Umar."],[500,"I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah, and he recited “In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful,” then he recited the Mother of the Qur’an until he reached “nor of those who go astray,” and he said, “Amin,” and the people said, “Amin.” And he would say, every time he prostrated, “Allah is most great,” and when he stood up from sitting he said, “Allah is most great,” and he would say when he gave the salutation: “By Him in whose hand is my soul, I am the closest of you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,” all of it in one wording, except that Ibn Abd al-Hakam said: “And when he stood up from sitting in the second [rak’ah] he said, ‘Allah is most great.’”"],[501,"“Shall I not teach you a surah the like of which has not been sent down in the Torah, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Criterion?” I said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Perhaps you will not leave through that door until I tell you about it.” So I stood up with him, and he began to talk to me while my hand was in his hand. I began to walk slowly, disliking that he should go out before he told me about it. When I drew near the door, I said, “O Messenger of Allah, the surah you promised me.” He said, “How do you begin when you stand for prayer?” He said, “So I recited the Opening of the Book.” He said, “It is that, it is that, and it is the seven oft-repeated verses about which Allah said: ‘And We have certainly given you seven of the oft-repeated and the great Qur’an’ — it is that which I have been given [and granted].”"],[502,"Allah has not sent down in the Torah, nor in the Gospel, nor in the Qur’an anything like the Mother of the Book, and it is the seven oft-repeated verses."],[503,"“Whoever prays a prayer without reciting the Mother of the Qur’an, it is deficient, it is deficient, it is deficient, not complete.” I said, “O Abu Hurayrah, sometimes I am behind the imam.” He pressed my forearm and said, “Recite it to yourself, O Persian, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: I have divided the prayer between Me and My servant into two halves; half of it is for Me and half of it is for My servant. The servant says: “Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds.” Allah says: “My servant has praised Me.” The servant says: “The Most Gracious, the Most Merciful.” Allah says: “My servant has extolled Me.” The servant says: “Master of the Day of Judgment.” Allah says: “My servant has glorified Me.” And this verse is between Me and My servant. The servant says: “You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help.” This is between Me and My servant, and for My servant is what he asks. The servant says: “Guide us to the straight path, the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor, not of those who incurred anger nor of those who went astray.” This is for My servant, and for My servant is what he asks.’”"],[504,"He used to recite in the first two rak’ahs of the noon and afternoon prayers the Opening of the Book and a surah, and sometimes he would let us hear a verse, and he would recite in the last two rak’ahs the Opening of the Book."],[505,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to lead us in the noon and afternoon prayers, and he would recite in the first two rak’ahs the Opening of the Book and a surah with it, and in the last two rak’ahs the Opening of the Book. He would lengthen the first rak’ah and sometimes let us hear a verse."],[506,"We asked Khabbab, “Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite in the noon and afternoon prayers?” He said, “Yes.” We said, “How did you know?” He said, “By the movement of his beard.”"],[507,"By the movement of his beard."],[508,"He used to recite the Mother of the Qur’an and two surahs with it in the first two rak’ahs of the noon prayer and the afternoon prayer, and sometimes he would let us hear a verse, and he would lengthen the first rak’ah of the noon prayer. Ali b. Sahl said: from his father. He also said: he would lengthen the first rak’ah of the noon prayer."],[509,"The people of Kufa complained about Sa'd to 'Umar, and they mentioned [something] about his prayer. So 'Umar sent for him, and he came to him. He mentioned to him what they had criticized about his prayer. He said: \"I lead them in prayer with the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I do not deviate from it. I prolong the first two [units] for them, and I shorten the last two for them.\" 'Umar said to him: \"That is what was expected of you, O Abu Ishaq.\" This is the hadith of al-Dawraqi. Al-Makhzumi said: \"And I lighten the last two.\""],[510,"We used to estimate the standing of the Messenger of God ﷺ in the noon prayer in the first two units as the length of recitation of thirty verses, the length of the recitation of Alif Lam Mim Tanzil al-Sajdah. He said: And we estimated his standing in the last two at half of that. He said: And we estimated his standing in the first two of the afternoon prayer at half of that."],[511,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite in the noon and afternoon prayers 'By the night when it covers' and 'By the sun and its brightness' and the like of them, and he used to recite in the morning prayer with longer than that."],[512,"He used to recite in the noon prayer with 'When the sky is split asunder' and the like of it."],[513,"They used to hear from him the tone in the noon prayer with 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High' and 'Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming?'"],[514,"I said to 'Ikrimah: Sometimes I recite in the sunset prayer with 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak' and 'Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind,' and some people find fault with that. He said: 'Glory be to God, what is wrong with that? Recite with them, for they are from the Quran.'"],[515,"Ali b. Khashram and Sa'id b. 'Abd al-Rahman al-Makhzumi narrated to us, both said: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Muhammad b. Jubayr b. Mut'im, from his father."],[516,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the sunset prayer [with the longer of the two long surahs]."],[517,"Why do you recite in the sunset prayer with the short ones of the Mufassal? The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite in the sunset prayer [with the longer of the two long surahs]."],[518,"He used to recite Sūrat al-Aʿrāf in both units of the Maghrib prayer."],[519,"Abū Ayyūb, or Zayd b. Thābit—Hishām was uncertain—said to Marwān, while he was the governor of Madinah: 'You make the recitation light in the two units of Maghrib. By God, the Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite Sūrat al-Aʿrāf in both of those two units together.'"],[520,"She heard the Messenger of God ﷺ reciting al-Mursalāt in the Maghrib prayer."],[521,"I have not seen anyone whose prayer more closely resembled that of the Messenger of God ﷺ than so-and-so—an amir who was in Madinah. Sulaymān said: 'I prayed behind him, and he would lengthen the first two units and lighten the last two, and lighten the ʿAṣr prayer. In the first two units of Maghrib he would recite the short mufaṣṣal sūrahs, in the first two of ʿIshāʾ the medium mufaṣṣal sūrahs, and in the morning prayer the long mufaṣṣal sūrahs.'"],[522,"Muʿādh used to pray with the Messenger of God ﷺ, then return to his people and lead them in prayer. One night the Prophet ﷺ delayed the prayer, so Muʿādh returned and led them, and he recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. When a man among the people saw that, he turned aside to a corner of the mosque and prayed alone. They said: 'Have you become a hypocrite?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'I will certainly go to the Messenger of God ﷺ and inform him.' He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'Muʿādh prays with you, then returns and leads us. You delayed the prayer last night, so he came and led us, and he recited Sūrat al-Baqarah. I fell behind him and prayed alone, O Messenger of God. We are people who work with water-drawing camels, and we work with our own hands.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'O Muʿādh, are you a troublemaker? Recite \"By the night when it covers\" and \"Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High\" and \"By the sky containing constellations.\"'"],[523,"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ reciting al-Tīn wa-l-Zaytūn in the late evening prayer, and I have never heard a more beautiful recitation than his."],[524,"I complained or fell ill, and mentioned it to the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: 'Perform the circumambulation from behind the people while riding.' She said: 'So I circumambulated on a camel while the Messenger of God ﷺ was praying toward the side of the House, and I heard him reciting in the late evening prayer—while he was leading the people—\"By Mount Ṭūr and an inscribed book.\"' Ibn Lahīʿah said: 'Abū al-Aswad said: He recited slowly and distinctly when he recited, except that Mālik said: He was praying beside the House.'"],[525,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was on a journey and prayed the late evening prayer, reciting in one of the two units al-Tīn wa-l-Zaytūn."],[526,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed while traveling, and he prayed the late evening prayer, reciting in it al-Tīn wa-l-Zaytūn."],[527,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite Qāf in the morning prayer, and his prayer afterwards was light."],[528,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting Sūrat Qāf in the morning prayer, [and] I heard him recite: 'and the tall palm trees.'"],[529,"He used to recite in the morning prayer between one hundred and sixty, or between sixty and one hundred verses."],[530,"Between sixty and one hundred."],[531,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite in the dawn prayer between sixty and one hundred verses."],[532,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray something like your prayer, but he would lighten the prayer. He used to recite in the dawn prayer al-Wāqiʿah and similar sūrahs."],[533,"Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated to us, from Anas, from the Messenger of God ﷺ, this. This is a gross mistake. The report is only from Sulaymān, from Abū al-Minhāl Sayyār b. Salāmah, from Abū Barzah. Thus narrated those ḥuffāẓ whose craft is ḥadīth."],[534,"He used to recite in the morning prayer on Friday: Alif Lām Mīm Tanzīl and Hal Atā ʿalā al-Insān."],[535,"I led the Messenger of God ﷺ's mount through one of those mountain passes. He said, \"Will you not ride, O ʿUqayb?\" I deemed it too grave to ride the Messenger of God ﷺ's mount. Then he said, \"Will you not ride, O ʿUqayb?\" I feared it might be disobedience, so the Messenger of God ﷺ dismounted, and I rode for a short while, then I dismounted and the Messenger of God ﷺ rode. Then he said, \"O ʿUqayb, shall I not teach you two sūrahs of the best two sūrahs that people recite?\" I said, \"Yes, O Messenger of God.\" He made me recite Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Falaq and Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Nās. Then the prayer was commenced, and he prayed and recited them. Then he passed by me and said, \"How did you find it, O ʿUqayb? Recite them whenever you sleep and whenever you rise.\""],[536,"I was leading the Messenger of God ﷺ's riding camel on a journey. He said, \"O ʿUqbah, shall I not teach you the best two sūrahs that are recited?\" I said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Falaq and Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Nās.\" When he dismounted, he prayed the dawn prayer with them. He said, \"How did you find it, O ʿUqbah?\" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. ʿAbdah did not say \"on a journey,\" and he said: \"He did not see me pleased with them, so he led the people in the dawn prayer, reciting them, then he said to me, 'O ʿUqbah, how did you find it?'\""],[537,"He used to recite in the dawn prayer Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Falaq and Qul Aʿūdhu bi-Rabb al-Nās. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Yazīd b. Abī al-Zarqāʾ. In the ḥadīth of Abū Usāmah he said: I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about the Muʿawwidhatayn, whether they are part of the Qurʾān. So the Messenger of God ﷺ led us with them in the dawn prayer."],[538,"A man from the Ansar used to lead them in the mosque of Quba. He said: Whenever he began a surah to recite to them in the prayer out of what he recited, he began with “Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad” until he finished it, then he recited another surah with it. He used to do that in every rak'ah. When the Prophet ﷺ came to them, they informed him of the matter. He said: “O so-and-so, what makes you adhere to this surah in every rak'ah?” He said: I love it. The Prophet ﷺ said: Its love will admit you to Paradise."],[539,"Nahik b. Sinan came to Abdullah and said: How do you find this reading: “min ma'in ghayri asin” or “yasin”? He said: “Have you counted all of the Qur'an except this?” He said: I recite the Mufassal in one rak'ah. Abdullah said: Hasty like the recitation of poetry. There are people who recite the Qur'an with their tongues but it does not go beyond their collarbones. But when it enters the heart and takes root, it benefits. Indeed, the best part of the prayer is the bowing and the prostration."],[540,"I said to 'A'ishah: Did the Messenger of Allah combine surahs in a rak'ah? She said: The Mufassal. This is the hadith of Waki'. And al-Dawraqi said in his hadith: I said to 'A'ishah: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray the Duha? She said: When he came from his journey? I said: Did he join surahs? She said: The Mufassal. I said: Did he pray sitting? She said: After the people wore him down."],[541,"Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, that Abu Ayyub or Zayd b. Thabit — then he mentioned the hadith."],[542,"Zayd b. Thabit said to Marwan b. al-Hakam: O Abu 'Abd al-Malik, do you recite in Maghrib “Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad” and “Inna A'taynaka al-Kawthar”? He said: [Yes]. Zayd b. Thabit said: By an oath, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite, and he began with the longer of the two long ones: Alif Lam Mim."],[543,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ one night and he began al-Baqarah. He recited until he reached one hundred, and I said: He will bow. Then he continued until he reached two hundred, and I said: He will bow. Then he recited until he completed it, and I said: He will bow. Then he began al-Nisa' and recited, then he bowed. His bowing was like his standing, and he said in his bowing: “Subhana Rabbiyal-'Azim.” Then he prostrated, and his prostration was like his bowing, and he said in his prostration: “Subhana Rabbiyal-A'la.” When he passed by a verse of mercy, he asked; when he passed by a verse of punishment, he sought refuge; and when he passed by a verse containing glorification of Allah, he glorified."],[544,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night. He did not pass by a verse of mercy except that he stopped at it and asked, nor did he pass by a verse of punishment except that he stopped at it and sought refuge."],[545,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, teach me something that will suffice me from the Qur'an, for I cannot recite. He said: “Say: Subhan Allah, wal-hamdu lillah, wa la ilaha illa Allah, wa Allahu Akbar, wa la ilaha illa Allah, wa la hawla wa la quwwata illa billah.” He said: The man clenched his hand over them and said: This is for my Lord, so what is for me? He said: “Say: Allahumma ighfir li, warhamni, wahdini, warzuqni, wa 'afini.” He said: He clenched his other hand over them and stood up. This is the hadith of al-Makhzumi. And Harun said in his hadith: He said: Teach me something that will suffice me from the Qur'an, and he did not say: The man clenched his hand over them. And he said at the end of the hadith: Mis'ar said: I was with Ibrahim while he was narrating this hadith and I sought confirmation from him."],[546,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the mosque one day — Rifa'ah said: and we were with him — a man like a bedouin came and prayed, and he made his prayer brief. Then he left and greeted the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ said: “And upon you. Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.” He went back and prayed, then came and greeted the Prophet ﷺ. He responded to him and said: “Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.” He did that two or three times, each time coming to the Prophet ﷺ and greeting him, and he would say: “And upon you. Go back and pray, for you have not prayed.” The people became afraid and it weighed heavily on them that whoever made his prayer brief had not prayed. The man said at the end of that: Show me or teach me, for I am only human and I err and make mistakes. The Prophet ﷺ said: “Yes. When you stand for prayer, perform wudu' as Allah commanded you, then say the tashahhud, then stand, then say takbir. If you have Qur'an with you, recite it; otherwise, praise Allah, magnify Him, and declare His oneness. Then bow and remain calm in bowing, then straighten up standing, then prostrate and remain calm in prostration, then sit and remain calm sitting, then stand. If you do that, your prayer is complete, and if you diminish anything from it, you diminish from your prayer.” He said: This was easier for them than the first, that whoever diminished anything from that diminished from his prayer but it did not go away entirely."],[547,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the morning prayer in Makkah and began Surah al-Mu'minun. When he reached the mention of Musa and Harun, or the mention of 'Isa — Muhammad b. 'Abbad was uncertain or they differed about it — the Prophet ﷺ was seized by a cough. He said: So he bowed. He said: And Ibn al-Sa'ib was present at that."],[548,"In every prayer there is recitation. Whatever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made us hear, we made you hear, and whatever he concealed from us, we concealed from you."],[549,"The Prophet ﷺ drew aside the curtain while the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr. He said: O people, nothing remains of the glad tidings of prophethood except the true dream, which a Muslim sees or is seen for him. Indeed, I was forbidden to recite while bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, magnify the Lord in it; and as for prostration, strive earnestly in supplication, for it is likely to be answered for you."],[550,"When the son of Adam recites a verse of prostration and prostrates, Satan withdraws weeping and says: Woe to him! The son of Adam was commanded to prostrate and he prostrated, so for him is Paradise; and I was commanded to prostrate but I refused, so for me is the Fire. In Jarir's hadith he said: I disobeyed Him."],[551,"Sad is not one of the obligatory prostrations, but I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrate in it."],[552,"He used to prostrate in Sad. It was said to him, and he said: Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance. And he said: David prostrated it, and the Messenger of Allah prostrated it."],[553,"I said to Ibn Abbas: The prostration of Sad, from where did you take it? He said: He recited to me: And of his progeny, David and Solomon and Job, until he reached His saying: Those are they whom Allah guided, so follow their guidance. He said: David prostrated in it, and for that the Messenger of Allah prostrated."],[554,"He recited al-Najm and prostrated in it, and those with him prostrated, except that an old man took a handful of pebbles or dust and raised it to his forehead and said: This suffices me. Abdullah said: I later saw him killed as a disbeliever."],[555,"We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in: Read in the name of your Lord who created and: When the sky is split asunder."],[556,"I prostrated with the Prophet ﷺ in: When the sky is split asunder, and in: Read in the name of your Lord who created."],[557,"In the year of the conquest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited a verse of prostration and all the people prostrated, some of them mounted and some prostrating on the ground, until even the rider prostrated on his hand."],[558,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite the Qur'an to us. He would recite a surah containing a sajdah (prostration verse), then he would prostrate and we would prostrate with him, until one of us could not find a place for his forehead."],[559,"We used to recite the sajdah (verse) in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, and he would prostrate, and we would prostrate with him until some of us crowded against others."],[560,"I prayed with Abu Hurayrah above this mosque. He recited, “When the sky is split open” (al-Inshiqaq 84:1) and prostrated in it, and said, “I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ prostrate in it.”"],[561,"Abu Qudamah – and he is al-Harith b. Ubayd – narrated to us:"],[562,"I prayed the evening prayer (salat al-atamah) with Abu Hurayrah. He recited, “When the sky is split open” (al-Inshiqaq 84:1) and prostrated. I said to him, “What is this prostration?” He said, “I prostrated in it behind Abu al-Qasim ﷺ.” And al-San'ani said: from his father, and he added at the end of the report: “I will not cease to prostrate in it until I meet him.” And Abu al-Ash'ath said: from his father, from Bakr b. Abdullah, who said: “I prayed behind Abu al-Qasim ﷺ, and he prostrated in it, so I will not cease to prostrate in it until I meet Abu al-Qasim ﷺ.”"],[563,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of God, I saw in my sleep last night – as one sees in a dream – as if I was praying behind a tree. I saw as if I recited a sajdah verse and prostrated. Then I saw the tree as if it prostrated along with my prostration. I heard it, while it was prostrating, saying: ‘O God, write for me a reward with You through it, and make it a treasure for me with You, and remove from me a burden through it, and accept it from me as You accepted from Your servant Dawud.’” Ibn Abbas said: “Then I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ recite a sajdah verse, then prostrate. I heard him, while he was prostrating, say the like of what the man reported from the words of the tree.”"],[564,"He used to say in the prostration of the Qur'an at night: “My face prostrated to the One who created it, and split open its hearing and its sight, by His might and His power.”"],[565,"He would say that three times."],[566,"He would say in the sajdah (prostration) repeatedly."],[567,"I recited al-Najm to the Messenger of God ﷺ, and none of us prostrated."],[569,"He recited to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'By the star when it descends,' and he did not prostrate."],[570,"When the reciter says Amin, say Amin, for the angels say Amin, and whoever's Amin coincides with the Amin of the angels will have his previous sins forgiven."],[571,"When the imam says Amin, say Amin, for whoever's statement coincides with the statement of the angels will have his previous sins forgiven."],[572,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished reciting Umm al-Qur'an, he raised his voice and said: 'Amin.'"],[573,"When he was with the imam reciting Umm al-Qur'an and the people said Amin, Ibn Umar would say Amin, and he considered that the Sunnah."],[574,"Do not precede me in saying Amin."],[575,"A Jew entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'Al-samu 'alayka, O Muhammad.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'And upon you.' Aisha said: I wanted to speak, but I knew the Prophet's ﷺ dislike of that, so I remained silent. Then another entered and said: 'Al-samu 'alayka.' He said: 'Upon you.' I wanted to speak, but I knew the Prophet's ﷺ dislike of that. Then the third entered and said: 'Al-samu 'alayka.' I could not restrain myself until I said: 'And upon you be sam and the wrath of Allah and His curse, O brothers of apes and pigs! Do you greet the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with what Allah has not greeted him?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Allah does not love obscenity or obscene behavior. They said something, so we responded to them. The Jews are an envious people, and they do not envy us for anything as they envy us for the salam and for Amin.'"],[576,"When the imam says: 'Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,' say: 'Amin,' for the angels say: 'Amin,' and the imam says: 'Amin,' and whoever's Amin coincides with the Amin of the angels will have his previous sins forgiven."],[577,"He asked Ibn Umar about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'Allahu akbar whenever he lowered, Allahu akbar whenever he raised.' This is the wording of the hadith of al-Hasan b. Muhammad. Ibn Mani' said: From Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: 'Allahu akbar' whenever he raised and lowered, and he added: Then he would say: 'Al-salamu 'alaykum wa rahmatullah' to his right, and 'Al-salamu 'alaykum wa rahmatullah' to his left."],[578,"I saw a man near the Maqam saying takbir in every raising and lowering. I went to Ibn Abbas and said: 'I saw a man praying, saying takbir in every raising and lowering.' He said: 'Is that not the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may you be bereft of your mother?'"],[579,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for prayer, he would say the takbir upon standing. Then he would say the takbir when bowing. Then he would say, “Allah hears the one who praises Him,” when he raised his back from the bow. Then, while standing, he would say, “Our Lord, and to You is all praise.” [Then he would say the takbir] when going down into prostration, then he would say the takbir when he raised his head, then he would say the takbir when he prostrated, then he would say the takbir when he raised his head, then he would do the same throughout the whole prayer until he completed it. And he would say the takbir when he stood up from the two rak‘ahs after sitting. Then Abu Hurayrah said: “Indeed, I am the closest of you in prayer to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[580,"Abu Hurayrah used to lead us in prayer and would say the takbir when he stood, and when he bowed, and when he intended to prostrate, and after rising from bowing, and when he intended to prostrate after rising from prostration, and when he sat, and when he intended to stand for the two rak‘ahs he said the takbir, and he would say the takbir likewise in the other two rak‘ahs. When he said the salam, he said: “By the One in whose hand is my soul, I am the closest of you in resemblance to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—meaning his prayer. This remained his prayer until he departed from this world.”"],[581,"Abu Hurayrah fell ill—or was absent—so Abu Sa‘id al-Khudri led us in prayer. He said the takbir aloud when he began, and when he bowed, and when he said, “Allah hears the one who praises Him,” and when he raised his head from prostration, and when he prostrated, and when he raised, and when he stood up from the two rak‘ahs until he completed his prayer in that manner. It was said to him: “The people have differed about your prayer.” He came out and stood on the pulpit and said: “O people, by Allah, I do not care whether your prayers differ or not. This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray.”"],[582,"I prayed behind ‘Ali, and he would say the takbir when he prostrated and when he raised his head. When he finished, ‘Imran b. Husayn said to me: “This one led us in prayer like the prayer of the Messenger of Allah.”"],[583,"I said to Ibn ‘Abbas: “I prayed Zuhr at al-Batha’ behind a foolish old man, and he said twenty-two takbirs: when he prostrated, when he bowed, and when he raised his head.” Ibn ‘Abbas said: “That is the sunnah of Abu al-Qasim ﷺ.” This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Musa. Ibn Khashram said: “That is the sunnah of Abu al-Qasim,” or “the prayer of Abu al-Qasim ﷺ”—Sa‘id was uncertain. Nasr said: “That is the prayer of Abu al-Qasim.”"],[584,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raise his hands when he began the prayer until they were level with his shoulders, and when he intended to bow, and after he raised from bowing, and he did not raise them between the two prostrations."],[585,"When he stood for the obligatory prayer, he said the takbir and raised his hands level with his shoulders. He did the same when he finished his recitation and intended to bow, and he did it when he raised from bowing. He did not raise his hands in any part of his prayer [75 - a] while sitting. And when he stood up from the two prostrations, he raised his hands likewise and said the takbir."],[586,"He saw Malik b. al-Huwayrith, when he prayed, say the takbir and raise his hands; and when he intended to bow, he raised his hands; and when he raised his head from bowing, he raised his hands. And he reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray like that."],[587,"We came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as young men close in age, and we stayed with him for twenty nights. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was merciful and kind. When he thought that we had missed our families and longed for them, he asked us about what we had left behind, and we told him. He said: “Return to your families, stay among them, teach them, and command them.” He mentioned things, some of which I remember and some I do not. “And pray as you have seen me pray. When the prayer time comes, let one of you call the adhan, and let the eldest of you lead you.”"],[588,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for prayer, he stood upright. He then mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then he said, “Allahu akbar,” and bowed. Then he straightened up, neither lowering his head nor raising it, and placed his hands on his knees. Then he said, “Allah hears the one who praises Him,” and raised his hands. He stood straight until every bone returned to its place. Then he went down to the ground in prostration. Then he said, “Allahu akbar.” Then he kept his upper arms away from his sides and spread the toes of his feet. Then he folded his left leg and sat on it. Then he stood straight until every bone returned to its place. Then he went down in prostration. Then he said, “Allahu akbar.” Then he folded his leg and sat, and stood straight until every bone returned to its place. Then he rose. Then he did the same in the second rak‘ah, until when he stood up from the two prostrations, he said the takbir and raised his hands until they were level with his shoulders, just as he did when he began the prayer. Then he did the same, and when it was the rak‘ah in which the prayer would end, he drew back his left leg and sat on his left side, mutawarrikan, then he said the salam."],[589,"Indeed I know best among you the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ. And they mentioned the hadith in full, and at the end of the hadith they said: You have spoken the truth; thus did the Prophet ﷺ pray."],[590,"Some of the Ansar gathered, among them Sahl b. Sa'd al-Sa'idi, Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi, and Abu Usayd al-Sa'idi, and they mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Abu Humayd said: Let me narrate to you, for I am the most knowledgeable of you about this. They said: Then narrate. He said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ perform the ablution well, then he entered the prayer and said the takbir, raising his hands level with his shoulders. Then he bowed and placed his hands on his knees, grasping them, and he did not lower his head nor raise it, and he kept his arms away from his sides. Then he raised his head and stood upright until every bone of his returned to its place. Then Bundar mentioned the rest of the hadith, and at the end he said: Then all the people said: Thus was the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[591,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered the mosque, then a man entered and prayed, then he greeted the Prophet ﷺ, and he returned the greeting. The Prophet ﷺ said: Go back and pray, for you have not prayed—until he did that three times. The man said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I do not know any other than this. He said: When you stand for prayer, say the takbir, then recite what is easy for you of the Quran, then bow until you are at ease in bowing, then rise until you are standing straight, then prostrate until you are at ease in prostration, then rise until you are sitting straight, and do that throughout your prayer."],[592,"The prayer of one who does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration is not sufficient."],[593,"No prayer is sufficient for anyone—or for a man—who does not straighten his back in bowing, nor in prostration."],[594,"We prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and he glanced with the corner of his eye at a man who was not straightening his back in bowing and prostration. When the Prophet of God finished the prayer, he said: O assembly of Muslims, there is no prayer for one who does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration."],[595,"When he bowed, he spread his fingers."],[596,"The Messenger of God ﷺ taught us the prayer. He said: He said the takbir, and when he wanted to bow, he placed his hands together between his knees and bowed. That reached Sa'd, and he said: My brother spoke the truth; we used to do this, then we were commanded with this—meaning holding the knees."],[597,"When I bowed, I placed my hands between my knees, and my father Sa'd saw me and forbade me, saying: We used to do it, then we were forbidden, then we were commanded to raise them to the knees."],[598,"A man entered the mosque and prayed, and he mentioned the hadith in full, and he said: The Prophet ﷺ said: Then when you bow, fix your hands on your knees until every bone of yours is at ease."],[599,"We came to ʿUqbah b. ʿAmr, Abū Masʿūd, and said: “Relate to us about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He stood before us in the mosque and said the takbīr. When he bowed, he said the takbīr and placed his palms on his knees, positioning his fingers below that, then he spread his elbows outward. Then he said: “This is how we saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray.”"],[600,"As for bowing, magnify the Lord therein."],[601,"When “So glorify the name of your Lord, the Almighty” was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us: “Place it in your bowing.”"],[602,"Muḥammad b. ʿĪsā narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allah b. al-Mubārak, from Mūsā b. Ayyūb, from his paternal uncle, from ʿUqbah b. ʿĀmir, something similar."],[603,"The Prophet ﷺ drew back the curtain and saw the people standing behind Abū Bakr praying. He said: “O Allah, have I conveyed? No glad tidings of prophethood remain except the righteous dream which a Muslim sees for himself or is seen for him. I have been forbidden to recite while bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, magnify the Lord therein; as for prostration, increase supplication therein, for it is worthy of being answered for you.”"],[604,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ one night, and his bowing was like his standing. He said in his bowing: “Glory be to my Lord, the Almighty.”"],[605,"He would say in his bowing: “Glory be to my Lord, the Almighty” three times."],[606,"Among what he would often say: “Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise.”"],[607,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say in his bowing: “All-Glorious, All-Holy, Lord of the angels and the Spirit.”"],[608,"When he bowed, he said: “O Allah, to You I have bowed, in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted. You are my Lord. My hearing, my sight, my brain, my bones, my sinews, and what my feet carry have humbled themselves to Allah, Lord of the worlds.” Both of them have the same wording, except that Muhammad said: he said."],[609,"Some people from the Ansar gathered, among them Sahl b. Sa‘d al-Sa‘idi, Abu Humayd al-Sa‘idi, and Abu Usayd al-Sa‘idi, and they mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Humayd said: “Let me tell you, for I am the most knowledgeable of you about this.” They said: “Then speak.” He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform the ablution excellently, then enter into prayer and say the takbir, raising his hands level with his shoulders. Then he bowed and placed his hands on his knees like one gripping them, not lowering his head nor raising it, and he kept his hands away from his sides. Then he raised his head and stood upright until every bone of his returned to its place. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith, and all the people said: “Thus was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.”"],[610,"Anas b. Malik said to us: “I do not fall short in praying with you as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray.” Thabit said: Anas used to do something I do not see you do: when he raised his head from bowing, he would stand upright until we would say he had forgotten."],[611,"The bowing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his raising his head after bowing and prostration, and his sitting between the two prostrations were close to being equal."],[612,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: “Allah hears the one who praises Him” when he raised his back from bowing, then he would say while standing: “Our Lord, and to You belongs all praise.”"],[613,"When he began the prayer, he said the takbir—they mentioned part of the hadith—and they said: When he raised his head—meaning from bowing—he said: “Allah hears the one who praises Him. Our Lord, and to You belongs all praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will after that.”"],[614,"When he said: “Allah hears the one who praises Him,” he would say: “O Allah, our Lord, and to You belongs all praise, filling the heavens, filling the earth, and filling whatever You will after that. You are the One worthy of praise and glory, the truest thing a servant has said, and all of us are Your servants. None can withhold what You give, and no possessor of greatness can benefit from his greatness against You.” They have the same wording, except that Ahmad said: “Our Lord, to You belongs all praise.”"],[615,"One day we were praying behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he raised his head from bowing he said: “Allah hears the one who praises Him.” A man behind him said: “Our Lord, to You belongs all praise, abundant, good, blessed praise therein.” When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said: “Who spoke just now?” A man said: “I did.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “I saw thirty-some angels rushing to it, which of them would write it down first.”"],[616,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head from the last rak'ah, he said: \"O Allah, save al-Walid b. al-Walid, Salamah b. Hisham, 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah, and the weak ones in Makkah.\" Ahmad added: \"among the Muslims.\" They said: \"O Allah, make Your punishment severe on Mudar, and send upon them years like the years of Yusuf.\""],[617,"He used to perform qunut in Maghrib and Fajr."],[618,"When he prayed the last 'Isha' and raised his head from bowing, saying: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' he performed qunut and said: \"O Allah, save 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah. O Allah, save Salamah b. Hisham. O Allah, save al-Walid b. al-Walid. O Allah, save the weak believers among the people of Makkah. O Allah, make Your punishment severe on Mudar. O Allah, make it upon them years like the years of Yusuf.\""],[619,"The Prophet ﷺ performed qunut for a month consecutively in Zuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib, 'Isha', and Fajr, at the end of every prayer when he said: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' in the last rak'ah, supplicating against a tribe of Banu Sulaym—against Ri'l, Dhakwan, and 'Usayyah—and those behind him said Amin. He said: He had sent to them calling them to Islam, but they killed them."],[620,"He did not perform qunut except when supplicating for someone or against someone. When he said: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' he said: 'Our Lord, to You be praise. O Allah, save…'"],[621,"He did not perform qunut except when he supplicated for a people or against a people."],[622,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed qunut in prayer for a month, saying in his qunut: \"O Allah, save al-Walid b. al-Walid. O Allah, save Salamah b. Hisham. O Allah, save 'Ayyash b. Abi Rabi'ah. O Allah, save the weak believers. O Allah, make Your punishment severe on Mudar. O Allah, make it upon them years like the years of Yusuf.\" Abu Hurayrah said: Then one day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up and did not supplicate for them. I mentioned that to him, and he said: \"Do you not see that they have arrived?\""],[623,"He heard the Prophet ﷺ say in the Fajr prayer when he raised his head from bowing: 'Our Lord, to You be praise' in the last rak'ah. Then he said: \"O Allah, curse so-and-so and so-and-so,\" supplicating against some people among the hypocrites. Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, revealed: 'You have no part in the matter; whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers.'"],[624,"He used to supplicate against four individuals. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'You have no part in the matter; whether He turns to them or punishes them, for they are wrongdoers.' He said: Then Allah guided them to Islam."],[625,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say the takbir when he went down for prostration."],[626,"I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for prayer—and he mentioned part of the hadith—he said: Then he says: 'Allahu Akbar,' then he goes down to the ground, keeping his arms away from his sides. Muhammad b. Yahya said: He goes down to the ground, keeping his arms away from his sides. Muhammad b. Yahya added: Then he prostrates. They all said: You have spoken the truth; this is how the Prophet ﷺ used to pray."],[627,"He would place his knees before his hands when he prostrated. Ahmad and Raja' said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ, when he prostrated, place his knees before his hands."],[628,"That he would place his hands before his knees, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that."],[629,"We used to place the hands before the knees, then we were commanded to place the knees before the hands."],[630,"He would place his knees before his hands, and raise his hands before his knees when he raised [his head]."],[631,"Indeed the two hands prostrate as the face prostrates, so when one of you places his face, let him place his hands, and when he raises it, let him raise them."],[632,"When the servant prostrates, seven body parts prostrate with him: his face, his two palms, his two knees, and his two feet."],[633,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven, and not to tuck back hair or clothing."],[634,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven bones, and not to tuck back hair or clothing."],[635,"The Prophet ﷺ was commanded to prostrate on seven: on his face, his two palms, his two knees, and his two feet, and he was forbidden to tuck back hair or clothing."],[636,"or tuck back his clothes or his hair."],[637,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven, and not to tuck back hair or clothes: the forehead, the nose, the two hands, the two knees, and the two feet."],[638,"A group of people from the Ansar gathered, among them Sahl b. Saʿd al-Sāʿidī, Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī, and Abū Usayd al-Sāʿidī. They mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abū Ḥumayd said: Let me tell you, for I am the most knowledgeable of you about this. They said: Then narrate. He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform the ablution excellently, then he entered the prayer. He mentioned part of the hadith, and said: Then he prostrated and enabled his forehead and nose to touch the ground, and he moved his hands away from his sides. Then he raised his head. All the people said: This is how the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was."],[639,"That the Prophet ﷺ said to the man who prayed and whom the Prophet ﷺ commanded to repeat the prayer: “Then when you prostrate, make your face and your hands firm until every bone of you settles in its place.”"],[640,"The Prophet ﷺ used to prostrate on the heels of the palms."],[641,"Abu Humayd al-Saidi, Abu Usayd al-Saidi, Sahl b. Sa'd, and Muhammad b. Maslama gathered. Abu Humayd said: “I know best among you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He stood and said the takbir, and he mentioned part of the hadith, saying: “Then he prostrated and placed his nose and forehead firmly, moved his hands away from his sides, placed his palms level with his shoulders, then raised his head until every bone returned to its place, until he finished.”"],[642,"I came to Madinah and said: “I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” I saw him when he began the prayer say the takbir and raise—meaning his hands—and I saw his thumbs level with his ears. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: “Then he descended and prostrated, and his head was between his palms for the amount of time he began the prayer.”"],[643,"He used to join his fingers when he prostrated."],[644,"That he was sitting with a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, and they mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Humayd al-Saidi said: “I was the best of you at memorizing the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I saw him when he said the takbir place his hands level with his shoulders, and when he bowed he placed his hands firmly on his knees, then bent his back, and when he raised his head he stood straight until every vertebra returned to its place, and when he prostrated he placed his hands not spread out nor clenched, and he faced the tips of his fingers toward the Qiblah, and when he sat in the first two rak'ahs he sat on his left foot, and when he sat in the last rak'ah he pushed his left foot forward and sat on his buttocks.”"],[645,"When one of you prostrates, let him be moderate and not spread his forearms like a wild animal spreads."],[646,"Do not spread your forearms like the spreading of a wild animal, but support yourself on your palms and keep your upper arms away from your sides, for if you do that, every limb of yours prostrates."],[647,"Al-Bara' b. 'Azib described prostration to us, so he placed his hands on the ground and raised his buttocks, and said: “This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.”"],[648,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he prayed, kept his chest up."],[649,"When he prayed, he kept his arms away from his sides until his armpits appeared."],[650,"When he prostrated, he kept his arms away until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."],[651,"When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits would be seen."],[652,"When the Prophet ﷺ stood for prayer, he would stand straight, and he mentioned part of the hadith, and said: \"Then he descended to the ground in prostration, then said: Allahu Akbar, then he kept his upper arms away from his armpits and spread the toes of his feet.\""],[653,"He was sitting with a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, and they mentioned the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi said: \"I was the most knowledgeable of you regarding the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I saw him when he said the takbir, he placed his hands level with his shoulders; when he bowed, he placed his hands firmly on his knees, then bent his back; when he raised his head, he stood straight until every vertebra returned to its place; and when he prostrated, he placed his hands on the ground, neither spreading them out nor clenching them, and he faced the qiblah with the tips of his toes.\""],[654,"When one of you prostrates, he should not spread his forearms on the ground like a dog spreads its forearms, and he should keep his thighs together."],[655,"I missed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who had been with me in my bed. I found him prostrating, with his heels together, facing the qiblah with the tips of his toes. I heard him saying: \"I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and in Your pardon from Your punishment, and in You from You. I praise You, but I cannot fully praise You as You deserve.\" When he finished, he said: \"O 'A'ishah, has your devil taken hold of you?\" She said: \"Do you not have a devil?\" He said: \"There is no human being but has a devil.\" I said: \"Even you, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Even me, but I called upon Allah against him and he submitted.\""],[656,"I missed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night in bed. I searched for him with my hand, and my hand fell upon the soles of his feet while they were upright. I heard him saying: \"O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and in Your pardon from Your punishment, and in You from You. I cannot enumerate Your praise nor adequately extol You. You are as You have praised Yourself.\""],[657,"When you prostrate, place your palms on the ground and raise your elbows."],[658,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, if a lamb had wanted to pass beneath his arm, it could have passed. And 'Umar b. Hafs said: He said."],[659,"\"There is none among you but has been assigned a companion from the jinn and a companion from the angels.\" They said: \"And you too, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"And me too, but Allah helped me against him until he submitted, so he only commands me to do good.\""],[660,"The bowing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his raising his head after bowing, his prostration, and his sitting between the two prostrations were all nearly equal in length."],[661,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night, and he mentioned the hadith and mentioned that he recited in one rak'ah al-Baqarah and al-Nisa', then he bowed, and his bowing was like his standing, then he prostrated, and his prostration was like his bowing."],[662,"The standing of the Prophet ﷺ, his bowing, his prostration, and his sitting were such that one could not tell which was longer."],[663,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the pecking of the crow. Salm b. Junadah said: in the obligatory prayers, and they both said: \"and the spreading of the forearms like a beast of prey, and that a man should fix his place as a camel fixes it.\""],[664,"\"The worst of people in theft is the one who steals from his prayer.\" They said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal from his prayer? He said: \"He does not complete its bowing and its prostration.\""],[665,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the afternoon prayer and observed a man praying. He said: \"O so-and-so, fear Allah and perfect your prayer. Do you think that I do not see you?! Indeed I see from behind me just as I see from in front of me. Perfect your prayers and complete your bowing and prostration.\""],[666,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with his companions, then sat with a group of them. A man entered and stood to pray, and he began to bow and peck in his prostration. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Do you see this one? Whoever dies upon this dies upon other than the religion of Muhammad. He pecks in his prayer as a crow pecks at blood. The example of the one who bows and pecks in his prostration is like the hungry one who eats only one or two dates; what do they avail him? So perform ablution thoroughly, and woe to the heels from the Fire. Complete the bowing and the prostration.\""],[667,"A prayer is not sufficient in which a man does not straighten his back in the bowing and prostration."],[668,"I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he glanced with the corner of his eye at a man who did not straighten his back in the bowing and prostration. When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ finished the prayer, he said: \"O assembly of Muslims, there is no prayer for the one who does not straighten his back in the bowing and prostration.\""],[669,"He used to say in his bowing: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Magnificent\" three times, and in his prostration: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Most High\" three times."],[670,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned the hadith, and he said: Then he prostrated, and he said in his prostration: \"Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.\""],[671,"When the verse \"Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High\" was revealed, the Prophet ﷺ said to us: \"Place it in your prostrations.\""],[672,"I missed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night in bed, so I began searching for him with my hand. My hand fell upon the bottoms of his feet while they were erected, and I heard him saying: \"O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and I seek refuge in Your pardon from Your punishment. I seek refuge in You from You. I cannot enumerate praise of You; You are as You have praised Yourself.\" This is the hadith of al-Dawraqi. 'Ali b. Shu'ayb said: from 'Ubayd Allah, and he said: \"I cannot enumerate praise of You nor extol You enough.\""],[673,"He used to say in his prostration: “O Allah, forgive me all my sin, the small of it and the great of it, the first of it and the last of it, its open and its secret.”"],[674,"When he stood for the obligatory prayer, he said the takbir. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then when he prostrated, he said in his prostration: “O Allah, to You I have prostrated, in You I have believed, and to You I have submitted. You are my Lord. My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned its hearing and sight. Blessed is Allah, the best of creators.”"],[675,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew aside the curtain while the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, and he said: “As for prostration, strive in supplication, for it is likely that you will be answered.”"],[676,"We used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in intense heat. When one of us wanted to prostrate, he would spread his garment because of the intense heat and prostrate on it.” Al-San'ani said: “When one of us could not place his face on the ground, he would spread his garment and prostrate on it.”"],[677,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the mosque of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, wearing a cloak wrapped around him, placing his hands, and the cloak protected him from the cold of the pebbles."],[678,"I heard Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi, in the presence of ten of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, say: “I am the most knowledgeable of you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” They said: “You are not the earliest of us in his company nor the longest of us in following him.” He said: “Yes, I am.” They said: “Then show us.” He said: “When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for prayer, he would raise his hands until they were level with his shoulders, then say the takbir and stand upright until every bone settled in its place, straight. Then he would recite. Then he would raise his hands, say the takbir, and bow, placing his palms on his knees, neither hanging his head nor raising it. Then he would say: ‘Allah hears the one who praises Him,’ and raise his hands until they were level with his shoulders, standing upright until every bone settled in its place, straight. Then he would say the takbir and prostrate, keeping his upper arms away from his sides. Then he would raise his head, fold his left leg, and sit on it, spreading the toes of his right foot. Then he would stand and do the same in the other rak'ah. Then he would rise from the two prostrations and do as he did when he began the prayer.”"],[679,"It is part of the Sunnah in prayer to lay your left leg down and stand the right one up when you sit in prayer."],[680,"It is part of the Sunnah of prayer to lay your left leg down and stand the right one up. He said: The Prophet ﷺ, when he sat in prayer, would lay down his left leg and stand up his right leg."],[681,"We said to Ibn 'Abbas about sitting on the feet between the two prostrations, and he said: “It is the Sunnah.” We said: “We consider it rough for a man.” He said: “Rather, it is the Sunnah of your Prophet.”"],[682,"I sat in the market of Madinah in the forenoon with Abu Usayd Malik b. Rabi'ah and Abu Humayd, the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, both from his clan of Banu Sa'idah, and with Abu Qatadah al-Harith b. Rib'i. Some of them said to the others, while I was listening: “I know more about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than you two.” Each said it to his companion. They said to one of them: “Stand and lead us in prayer so that we may see whether you get the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ right or not.” One of them stood facing the qiblah, then said the takbir, then recited some of the Qur'an, then bowed, placing his hands on his knees firmly until every bone of his was at rest. Then he raised his head and stood upright until every bone of his returned to its place. Then he said: “Allah hears the one who praises Him.” Then he fell prostrate on his forehead, his palms, his knees, and the balls of his feet, keeping his hands away from his sides until I saw the whiteness of his armpits beneath his shoulders. Then he remained until every bone of his was at rest. Then he raised his head and sat upright on his heels and the balls of his feet until every bone of his returned to its place. Then he repeated that. He said: Then he stood and performed another rak'ah like it. He said: Then he said the salam and turned to his two companions, saying to them: “How did you see it?” They said: “You got the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ right. This is how he used to pray.”"],[683,"Anas b. Malik said to us: “I do my best to lead you in prayer as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lead us in prayer.” Thabit said: “Anas used to do something that I do not see you doing. When he raised his head from prostration, he would sit between the two prostrations until someone would say: ‘He has forgotten.’”"],[684,"The Prophet's ﷺ prostration, his bowing, and his sitting between the two prostrations were close to equal in length."],[685,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up at night to pray, so I came and stood beside him. He began [reciting] Al-Baqarah, and I thought: he intends the hundred [verses], but he went beyond it. I thought: he intends the two hundred, but he went beyond it. I thought: he will finish it, and he finished it. Then he began An-Nisa' and recited it. Then he recited Al 'Imran. Then he bowed for nearly as long as he recited. Then he raised [his head] and said: \"Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah, Rabbana laka al-hamd\" for nearly as long as he bowed. Then he prostrated for about as long as he had raised. Then he raised [his head] and said: \"Rabbi ighfir li\" for about as long as he prostrated. Then he prostrated for about as long as he had raised. Then he stood up for the second [rak'ah].\" Al-A'mash said: \"He would not pass by a verse of warning without seeking refuge or protection, nor a verse of mercy without asking, nor a verse of – meaning exaltation – without glorifying.\""],[686,"When the Prophet ﷺ stood for prayer, he stood upright. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: \"Then he went down to the ground prostrating, saying: 'Allahu Akbar.' Then he kept his upper arms away from his armpits and spread the toes of his feet. Then he folded his left leg and sat on it, and he remained upright until every bone returned to its place. Then he went down prostrating, saying: 'Allahu Akbar.' Then he folded his leg and sat, and he remained upright until every bone returned to its place. Then he rose.\""],[687,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ praying, and when he was in an odd rak'ah of his prayer, he did not rise until he sat up straight."],[688,"\"Shall I not tell you about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" He then prayed at a time other than the time of prayer. When he raised his head from the second prostration in the first rak'ah, he sat up straight, then he stood up, leaning on the ground."],[689,"I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah, and he said: \"Bismillah ar-Rahman ar-Rahim,\" then he recited Umm Al-Qur'an until he reached \"wa la ad-dallin,\" and he said: \"Amin,\" and the people said: \"Amin.\" When he bowed, he said: \"Allahu Akbar.\" When he raised his head, he said: \"Sami'a Allahu liman hamidah.\" Then he said: \"Allahu Akbar\" and prostrated. When he raised [his head], he said: \"Allahu Akbar.\" When he prostrated, he said: \"Allahu Akbar.\" Then he stood up straight with the takbir. When he stood up from the two [rak'ahs], he said: \"Allahu Akbar.\" When he said the salam, he said: \"By Him in Whose hand is my soul, I am the one among you whose prayer most resembles that of the Messenger of Allah.\""],[690,"Abu Humayd As-Sa'idi, Abu Usayd As-Sa'idi, Sahl b. Sa'd, and Muhammad b. Maslamah gathered, and Abu Humayd said: \"I know best among you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He mentioned the hadith in full and said: \"He sat and spread his left leg, turned the sole of his right foot toward his qiblah, placed his right palm on his right knee and his left palm on his left knee, and pointed with his index finger.\""],[691,"I came to Madinah and said: \"I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" He mentioned the hadith and said: \"He folded his left leg and stood upright on his right [foot].\""],[692,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he sat in prayer: he spread his left leg and stood up his right foot."],[693,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade a man, when sitting in prayer, from leaning on his left hand. And Al-Husayn b. Mahdi said: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from leaning on his hands in prayer.\""],[694,"He used to raise his hands when he entered prayer, when he intended to bow, when he raised his head from bowing, and when he stood up from the two rak'ahs. He raised his hands in all of that to the level of his shoulders."],[695,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began prayer, he said the takbir, then placed his hands level with his shoulders. When he bowed, he did the same. When he prostrated, he did the same, but he did not do it when he raised his head from prostration. When he stood up from the two rak'ahs, he did the same."],[696,"He said the takbir and raised his hands level with his shoulders."],[697,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat in the prayer, he would place his left foot between his thigh and his shin, put his left hand on his left knee, put his right hand on his right thigh, and point with his finger."],[698,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ. He said the takbir when he entered the prayer and raised his hands; when he intended to bow, he raised his hands; and when he raised his head from bowing, he raised his hands. He placed his palms and kept them apart—meaning in prostration—spread his left thigh, and pointed with his index finger—meaning when sitting in the tashahhud."],[699,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands when he said the takbir, when he bowed, and when he raised his head from bowing. And he said when he prostrated, 'Like this,' and he kept his hands away from his armpits. He placed his right thigh on his left thigh and said, 'Like this.' Wahb raised the index finger and formed a ring with the middle finger."],[700,"He used to say the tashahhud in the two rak'ahs, and he used to spread his left foot under his right."],[701,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the rak'ah in which the prayer ends, he would draw back his left foot, sit on his left hip, in the tawarruk posture, then give the salutation."],[702,"He used to sit in the last part of his prayer on his left hip."],[703,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him the tashahhud in prayer. He said: We used to memorize it from 'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud just as we memorized the letters of the Qur'an, the waw and the alif. When he sat on his left hip, he said: 'All greetings, prayers, and good words are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger.' Then he would supplicate for himself, then give the salutation and leave."],[704,"When we sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the tashahhud, we used to say: 'Peace be upon Allah from His servants, peace be upon so-and-so and so-and-so.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Do not say, \"Peace be upon Allah,\" for Allah is the Peace. But when one of you sits, let him say: \"All greetings, prayers, and good words are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.\" For when you say that, it reaches every righteous servant in the heavens and the earth. \"I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger.\" Then let one of you choose whatever supplication he likes best and call upon Allah with it.'"],[705,"Then he may choose whatever request he wishes."],[706,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us the tashahhud just as he taught us the Qur'an, and he would say: 'All blessed greetings, prayers, and good words are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.'"],[707,"It is part of the Sunnah to conceal the tashahhud."],[708,"This verse was revealed concerning the tashahhud: “And do not recite your prayer aloud, nor be silent in it.”"],[709,"And he narrated to me about the tashahhud of the Messenger of God ﷺ in the middle of the prayer and at its end: [‘Abd al-Rahman b. al-Aswad b. Yazid al-Nakha‘i, from his father, said:] We used to memorize it from ‘Abd Allah b. Mas‘ud just as we memorized the letters of the Qur’an."],[710,"The Messenger of God ﷺ heard a man supplicating in prayer without praising God and without invoking blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, so the Messenger of God ﷺ said, “You have been hasty, O praying person.” Then the Messenger of God ﷺ taught them. And he heard a man invoking blessings upon the Prophet, so the Messenger of God ﷺ said, “O praying person, supplicate and you will be answered; ask and you will be given.”"],[711,"The Messenger of God ﷺ saw a man praying who did not praise God or glorify Him, and did not invoke blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and then he finished. The Messenger of God ﷺ said, “This one has been hasty.” Then he called him and said to him and to others, “When one of you prays, let him begin by glorifying his Lord and praising Him, and let him invoke blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then let him supplicate for whatever he wishes.”"],[712,"A man came forward and sat before the Messenger of God ﷺ while we were with him, and said, “O Messenger of God, we know how to greet you with peace, but how should we invoke blessings upon you when we pray in our prayer—may God bless you?” He said: He remained silent until we wished that the man had not asked him. Then he said, “When you invoke blessings upon me, say: O God, bless Muhammad, the unlettered Prophet, and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. And bless Muhammad, the unlettered Prophet, and the family of Muhammad, as You blessed Ibrahim and the family of Ibrahim. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy and Glorious.”"],[713,"I prayed Dhuhr next to Ibn ‘Umar."],[714,"I was among those who came to the Prophet ﷺ, and I said, “I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ to see how he prays.” When he sat, he spread his left leg, then placed his left hand on his left thigh, then placed the edge of his right elbow on his right thigh, then made a knot—meaning two fingers—then made a circle, and began pointing with his index finger while supplicating. Ibn Khashram said: He made a circle with the middle finger and thumb, and raised the one between them to supplicate with it."],[715,"I said, “I will certainly observe the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ to see how he prays.” He said: So I observed him praying. He said the takbir, and he mentioned part of the hadith. He said, “Then he sat and spread his left leg, placed his left palm on his left thigh and knee, and placed the edge of his right elbow on his right thigh. Then he closed two of his fingers and made a circle, then raised his finger, and I saw him moving it, supplicating with it.”"],[716,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ during prayer placing his right hand on his right thigh and pointing with his finger."],[717,"That he saw the Messenger of God ﷺ sitting in prayer, placing his right forearm on his right thigh, raising his index finger, having bent it slightly while supplicating."],[718,"When he sat in prayer, he placed his hands on his knees, raised his finger next to the right thumb and supplicated with it, while his left hand was spread on his left knee."],[719,"When he performed the tashahhud, he placed his left hand on his left thigh and his right hand on his right thigh, and pointed with his index finger, his gaze not going beyond his pointing."],[720,"He saw a man moving pebbles with his hand while in prayer. When he finished, 'Abdullah said to him: 'Do not move the pebbles while you are in prayer, for that is from Satan. But do as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do.' He said: He placed his right hand on his thigh and pointed with his finger next to the thumb toward the qiblah, and fixed his gaze upon it or thereabouts, then said: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.'"],[721,"Indeed we did not know what to say in every two rak'ahs except to glorify, magnify, and praise our Lord. Muhammad was taught the openings of good and its comprehensive words. He said: 'When you sit in every two rak'ahs, say: All greetings, prayers, and good things are due to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. Then one of you may choose whatever supplication pleases him and supplicate with it.'"],[722,"When one of you performs the tashahhud, let him seek refuge in Allah from four things. He says: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of Hell, from the punishment of the grave, from the evil of the trial of the false messiah, and from the evil of the trial of life and death."],[723,"He used to say after the tashahhud words that he greatly emphasized. I said: In both tashahhuds? He said: Rather in the final tashahhud after the tashahhud. I said: What is it? He said: I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of Hell, and I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of the false messiah, and I seek refuge in Allah from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in Allah from the trial of life and death. He said: He greatly emphasized them."],[724,"Among the last things he said between the tashahhud and the salutation was: 'O Allah, forgive me what I have sent ahead and what I have delayed, what I have concealed and what I have made public, what I have committed in excess and what You know better than I. You are the One who brings forward and You are the One who delays; there is no god but You.'"],[725,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque and found a man who had finished his prayer, performing tashahhud and saying: 'O Allah, I ask You by Allah, the One, the Eternal, who begets not and is not begotten, and there is none equal to Him, to forgive me my sins. Indeed You are the Forgiving, the Merciful.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'He has been forgiven, he has been forgiven,' three times."],[726,"What do you say in prayer? He said: I perform tashahhud, then I say: O Allah, I ask You for Paradise, and I seek refuge in You from the Fire. By Allah, I do not understand your murmuring nor the murmuring of Mu'adh. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Around these two we murmur.'"],[727,"He used to greet to his right until the whiteness of his cheek was seen, and to his left until the whiteness of his cheek was seen."],[728,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ greet to his right and to his left until the whiteness of his cheek was seen."],[729,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give the salutation to his right until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen, [saying], “Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings,” and to his left until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen, [saying], “Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings.”"],[730,"He used to give one salutation in prayer, facing forward, inclining a little to the right side."],[731,"She used to give one salutation facing forward, [saying], “Peace be upon you.”"],[732,"He used to give one salutation: “Peace be upon you.”"],[733,"And she would not turn to her right nor to her left."],[734,"When we prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, we said with our hands, “Peace be upon you” to the right and left. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “What is the matter with me that I see your hands as if they were the tails of restive horses? Let one of you be still in prayer.” This is the hadith of Bundar. Others said, “Is it not enough for one of you to place his hand on his thigh, then give the salutation to his right and to his left?” except that Ibn Khashram said in his hadith: “then he gives the salutation from his right and from his left.” In the hadith of Waki‘: “to his brother from his right and from his left.” Al-Hasan b. Muhammad said in the hadith of Yazid: “When we prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we said, ‘Peace be upon Allah, peace be upon Gabriel, peace be upon Michael.’”"],[735,"Omitting the salutation is a sunnah."],[736,"Omitting the salutation is a sunnah."],[737,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the salutation in prayer, he would not remain seated except for the amount of time to say, “O Allah, You are Peace, and from You is peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.”"],[738,"This supplication was mentioned before the salutation: “...,” ..., ..., ..., ..., ..., ..."],[739,"When he wanted to say the salutation from the prayer, he would seek forgiveness three times, then say: 'O Allah, You are Peace, and from You is peace. Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honor.' Then he would say the salutation."],[740,"I heard 'Abd Allah b. al-Zubayr delivering a sermon on this pulpit, saying: 'When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the salutation at the end of the prayer, he would say: \"There is no god but Allah; we worship none but Him, the Possessor of blessing, grace, and good praise. There is no god but Allah, making the religion sincerely His, even if the disbelievers dislike it.\"'"],[741,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say at the conclusion of his prayer, before standing up: 'There is no god but Allah alone, with no partner. His is the dominion and His is the praise, and He is over all things capable. There is no might except with Allah. We worship none but Him. His is the blessing, grace, and good praise. There is no god but Allah, making the religion sincerely His, even if the disbelievers dislike it.'"],[742,"Mu'awiyah wrote to al-Mughirah: 'Inform me of something you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said: 'When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished the prayer...'"],[743,"When he finished his prayer and said the salutation, he would say: 'O Allah, forgive me what I have sent before and what I have delayed, what I have concealed and what I have declared, what I have been extravagant in, and what You know better than I. You are the Advancer and the Delayer; there is no god but You.'"],[744,"We used to go early to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a man and a woman would come and say: 'O Messenger of Allah, what should I say when I pray?' He said: 'Say: O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, guide me, grant me well-being, and provide for me; for these have gathered for you your worldly life and your Hereafter.'"],[745,"Ka'b swore to him by the One who parted the sea for Musa: 'We find in the Torah that Dawud, the Prophet of Allah, when he turned away from his prayer, said: \"O Allah, set right for me my religion, which You have made a protection for me, and set right for me my worldly life, in which You have placed my livelihood. O Allah, I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, and I seek refuge in Your pardon from Your punishment, and I seek refuge in You from You. O Allah, none can withhold what You give, and none can give what You withhold, and no possessor of fortune can benefit from You his fortune.\"' He said: And Ka'b told me that Suhayb, the Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated to him that Muhammad ﷺ used to say these at the conclusion of his prayer."],[746,"Sa'd used to teach his sons these words as a teacher teaches children to write, saying: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek refuge with these after the prayer: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from miserliness, and I seek refuge in You from cowardice, and I seek refuge in You from being returned to the most abject age, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of this world, and I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave.'"],[747,"He used to say after the prayer: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from disbelief, poverty, and the punishment of the grave.'"],[748,"He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the wealthy people have taken the rewards. They say as we say and they spend, but we do not spend.' He said: 'Shall I not inform you of an action by which, if you do it, you will catch up with those before you and surpass those after you, except for one who says the like of what you say? You say after every prayer: glorify Allah thirty-three times, praise Him and magnify Him the like of that, and when you retire to your bed...'"],[749,"The poor came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'The wealthy possessors of property have taken the highest degrees and everlasting bliss. They pray as we pray, and they fast as we fast, but they have surplus wealth with which they perform Hajj and 'Umrah, and they engage in jihad and give charity.' He said: 'Shall I not inform you of a matter which, if you take hold of it, you will catch up with those who preceded you, and no one after you will catch up with you, and you will be the best of those among whom you are, except one who does the like of your deeds? You should glorify Allah, praise Him, and magnify Him after every prayer thirty-three times.' He said: 'So we differed among ourselves. Some of us said: We should glorify thirty-three times, praise thirty-three times, and magnify thirty-four times. So I returned to him and he said: \"Say: Subhan Allah, wal-hamdu lillah, wa Allahu akbar, until you complete all of them thirty-three times.\"'"],[750,"Whoever glorifies Allah after every prayer thirty-three times, magnifies Allah thirty-three times, and praises Allah thirty-three times, that is ninety-nine. Then he says, completing the hundred: 'La ilaha illa Allah wahdahu la sharika lahu, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-hamdu wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in qadir,' his sins will be forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea."],[751,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took me by the hand one day and said to me: 'O Mu'adh, by Allah, I love you.' I said: 'May my father and mother be sacrificed for you, by Allah, I love you.' He said: 'O Mu'adh, I instruct you: do not fail to say after every prayer: Allahumma a'inni 'ala dhikrika wa shukrika wa husni 'ibadatika.'"],[752,"We were commanded to glorify Allah after every prayer thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times. Then a man from the Ansar was approached in his sleep and it was said to him: 'Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command you to glorify Allah after every prayer such and such?' He said: 'Yes.' It was said: 'Make them twenty-five, and include the tahlil in them.' When morning came, he went to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Do so.' This is the hadith of al-Thaqafi. Abu Qudamah said: A man was approached in his sleep and it was said to him: 'Did Muhammad ﷺ command you to glorify Allah after every prayer thirty-three times, praise Him thirty-three times, and magnify Him thirty-four times?' He said: 'Yes.'"],[753,"Juwayriyah bint al-Harith said – and her name was Barrah, but the Prophet ﷺ changed her name and called her Juwayriyah, and he disliked that it be said: 'He left from Barrah' – she said: The Prophet ﷺ left while I was in my prayer place, and he returned when the day had advanced and I was still there. He said: 'Have you been in your prayer place since I left?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'I have said four phrases three times; if they were weighed against what you have said, they would outweigh them: Subhan Allah wa bi-hamdihi, 'adada khalqihi, wa rida nafsihi, wa zinata 'arshihi, wa midada kalimatihi.' This is the hadith of Yahya b. Hakim. 'Abd al-Jabbar said: From Ibn 'Abbas, that the Prophet ﷺ, when he went out to the Fajr prayer, Juwayriyah was sitting in the mosque."],[754,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by him while he was moving his lips, and said: 'What are you saying, O Abu Umamah?' He said: 'I am remembering my Lord.' He said: 'Shall I not inform you of more – or better – than your remembrance throughout the night with the day, and the day with the night? That you say: Subhan Allah 'adada ma khalaqa, wa Subhan Allah mil'a ma khalaqa, wa Subhan Allah 'adada ma fi al-ardi wa al-sama'i, wa Subhan Allah mil'a ma fi al-ardi wa al-sama'i, wa Subhan Allah 'adada ma ahsa kitabuhu, wa Subhan Allah 'adada kulli shay'in, wa Subhan Allah mil'a kulli shay'in, and that you say the praise in the like manner.'"],[755,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'Recite the Mu'awwidhat after every prayer.'"],[756,"When one of you prays and then sits in the place where he prayed, the angels continue to pray for him: 'O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him,' as long as he does not break his ablution. This is the hadith of Ibn Fudayl. In the report of Ibn Wahb, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When a Muslim prays and then sits in his prayer place, the angels continue to supplicate for him: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him, as long as he does not break his ablution or get up.'"],[757,"He asked Jabir b. Samurah: How did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ act when he prayed the Fajr prayer? He said: 'He would remain sitting in his prayer place after praying Fajr until the sun rose.'"],[758,"A man stood up to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'May one of us pray in a single garment?' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'Does each of you have two garments?' Abu Hurayrah said to the one who asked him: 'Do you know Abu Hurayrah? He prays in a single garment while his clothes are placed on the peg.'"],[759,"By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Abu Hurayrah, I have seen myself in the mosque, hardly seeing a man praying in two garments, while today you pray in two and three."],[760,"He was asked about a man praying in a single shirt without his lower garment, and he said: 'There is no harm in that if it covers him.'"],[761,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in the house of Umm Salamah in a single garment, having wrapped its two ends in opposite directions."],[762,"He saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in a single garment, having wrapped its two ends in opposite directions over his shoulders, while his other garments were on the clothes rack."],[763,"Men would pray with the Prophet ﷺ having tied their waist-wrappers around their necks like the manner of young boys, and it was said to the women, 'Do not raise your heads until the men are sitting upright.'"],[764,"I was among seventy of the people of the Suffah, none of whom had a cloak—only a striped sheet or a blanket—which they had tied around their necks. Some of them reached the shin, and some reached the ankles, and he would gather it with his hand for fear that his private area would be seen."],[765,"None of you should pray in a single garment with nothing of it on his shoulders."],[766,"Ibn Umar saw me while I was praying in a single garment, and he said, 'Did I not give you two garments to wear?' I said, 'Yes.' He said, 'What do you think: if I sent you on an errand, would you go wearing a single garment?' I said, 'No.' He said, 'Then Allah is more deserving that you adorn yourself for Him.' Then he said, 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say, \"If one of you has only a single garment, let him tie it around his waist, and let him not wrap himself in it as the Jews do.\"'"],[767,"He came to Jabir b. Abd Allah, he and a group of people whom he named. When they entered upon him, they found him praying in a single garment, wrapped in it with its two ends crossed, and his cloak was near him such that if he reached for it, he would have gotten it. When he said the salam, they asked him about his praying in a single garment. He said, 'I do this so that fools like you may see me and spread from Jabir a concession that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted. I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys, and I came to him one night for some need of mine, and I found him praying while I had on a single garment that I had wrapped myself in, and I prayed beside him. When he finished, he said, \"What is this night travel, O Jabir?\" I told him of my need. When I finished, he said, \"O Jabir, what is this wrapping that I saw?\" I said, \"It was a single, narrow garment.\" He said, \"When you pray and you have on a single garment: if it is wide, wrap yourself in it; and if it is narrow, use it as a waist-wrapper.\"'"],[768,"The Prophet ﷺ would pray while I had on a woolen garment, part of it on me and part of it on him, and I was menstruating."],[769,"When one of you prays in a single garment, let him tie it around his waist, and do not wrap yourselves as the Jews wrap."],[770,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in the house of Umm Salamah in a single garment, wrapped in it."],[771,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in a single garment, wrapped in it, in the house of Umm Salamah, placing its two ends on his shoulders."],[772,"He forbade letting the garment hang down during prayer, and that a man cover his mouth."],[773,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray in a silk overgarment, then he did not wait long before he took it off."],[774,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."],[775,"Allah does not accept the prayer of a woman who has menstruated except with a head covering."],[776,"I asked Umm Habibah, 'Did the Prophet ﷺ pray in the garment in which he had intercourse with her?' She said, 'Yes, if he did not see any impurity on it.'"],[777,"I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am out hunting and the prayer time comes while I am wearing a shirt.' He said, 'Fasten it, even with a thorn.'"],[778,"I asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying, 'I am out hunting and I have on only a single shirt or a single jubbah; should I button it?' He said, 'Yes, even with a thorn.' Ahmad said once: He said, 'Button it, even with a thorn.'"],[779,"I saw Ibn 'Umar praying with his buttons undone, so I asked him about that, and he said, 'I saw the Prophet ﷺ do it.'"],[780,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying with his buttons undone."],[781,"Allah does not look at the prayer of a man who lets his lower garment hang down out of pride."],[782,"I was commanded to prostrate on seven (bones), and not to tuck up the hair or the garment."],[783,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to carry the daughter of Abū al-ʿĀṣ on his neck during prayer; when he prostrated he would put her down, and when he stood he would carry her."],[784,"He was carrying the daughter of Zaynab on his neck while leading the people in prayer; when he bowed he put her down, and when he stood he carried her."],[785,"While the Messenger of God ﷺ was prostrating and around him were people from Quraysh, ʿUqbah b. Abī Muʿayṭ came with the placenta of a slaughtered camel and threw it on the Messenger of God ﷺ, but he did not raise his head. Then Fāṭimah came, removed it from his back, and supplicated against those who did that. He said: \"O God, against you are the chiefs of Quraysh: Abū Jahl b. Hishām, ʿUtbah b. Rabīʿah, Shaybah b. Rabīʿah, ʿUqbah b. Abī Muʿayṭ, and Umayyah b. Khalaf or Ubayy b. Khalaf\" – Shuʿbah was the one uncertain – he said: \"I saw them killed on the Day of Badr and thrown into a well, except that Umayyah or Ubayy had his limbs torn apart, so he was not thrown into the well.\""],[786,"The Messenger of God ﷺ led us in prayer one day, and he removed his sandals and placed them to his left. When the people saw that the Messenger of God ﷺ had removed his sandals, they removed their sandals. When he finished, he said to them: \"What is the matter with you that you removed your sandals?\" They said: \"O Messenger of God, we saw you remove your sandals, so we removed our sandals.\" He said: \"A messenger came to me and told me that there was something harmful in my sandals, so I removed them. When one of you enters the mosque, let him look, and if he sees filth on his sandals, let him wipe them on the ground and then pray in them.\""],[787,"I said: \"O Messenger of God, which mosque was placed on earth first?\" He said: \"The Sacred Mosque.\" I said: \"Then which?\" He said: \"The Farthest Mosque.\" I said: \"How much time was between them?\" He said: \"Forty years. Then wherever the prayer overtakes you, pray, for it is a mosque.\""],[788,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ came [to Madinah], he used to pray wherever the prayer overtook him, so he prayed in sheep pens. Then he was ordered to build a mosque. He sent for a group of the Banū al-Najjār, and they came. He said: \"O Banū al-Najjār, sell me this walled garden of yours.\" They said: \"No, by God, we seek its price only from God.\" Anas said: In it were graves of the polytheists, ruins, and date palms. The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered the graves of the polytheists to be dug up, the ruins to be leveled, and the date palms to be cut down. He said: They lined up the date palms as the qiblah of the mosque, and he said: \"Make its two doorposts of stone.\""],[789,"Indeed among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are alive, and those who take graves as places of worship."],[790,"Umm Salamah and Umm Ḥabībah mentioned a church they had seen in Abyssinia in which there were images. They mentioned that to the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he said: \"Those people, when a righteous man among them died, they built a place of worship over his grave and made those images in it. Those are the worst of creation in the sight of God.\""],[791,"The entire earth is a mosque except a bathhouse and a cemetery."],[792,"ʿUmārah b. Ghaziyyah narrated to us, from Yaḥyā b. ʿUmārah al-Anṣārī, from Abū Saʿīd, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like of it."],[793,"Do not sit on graves, and do not pray toward them."],[794,"Busr b. ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to me, saying: I heard Abū Idrīs saying: I heard Wāthilah b. al-Asqaʿ saying: I heard Abū al-Marthad al-Ghanawī saying: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ saying the like of it."],[795,"If you find only sheep folds and camel resting places, pray in the sheep folds and do not pray in the camel resting places.” Muhammad b. al-ʿAlāʾ said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “Do not pray in camel resting places, but pray in sheep folds.”"],[796,"Yaḥyā narrated to us, from Abū Bakr, from Abū Ḥaṣīn, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it.”"],[797,"The Prophet ﷺ would sometimes pray on the place where he had had sexual intercourse."],[798,"He planted a spear and prayed towards it. Al-Ashajj said: He used to plant the spear in front of him, and he did not add anything to this."],[799,"A spear used to be planted for him, and he would pray towards it on the day of ʿĪd."],[800,"Do not pray except towards a sutrah, and do not let anyone pass in front of you. If he refuses, then fight him, for with him is a companion (qarīn)."],[801,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying towards his riding camel."],[802,"He used to pray. Hārūn said: towards his riding camel, and Abū Saʿīd said: towards his camel."],[803,"When one of you prays, let him pray towards a sutrah and draw near to it, so that Satan may not interrupt his prayer."],[804,"Between the prayer place of the Messenger of God ﷺ and the wall there was about the space of a sheep’s passage."],[805,"We used to pray while animals passed in front of us, so we asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: “Something like the back of a saddle placed in front of one of you is sufficient, and whatever passes in front of him will not harm him.”"],[806,"When one of you stands to pray, it will screen him if there is in front of him something like the back of a saddle."],[807,"I said to 'Ata': How long is the back of a saddle that you have heard serves as a sutrah for one praying? He said: The length of a forearm."],[808,"Something like the back of a saddle suffices as a sutrah, even if it is as thin as a hair."],[809,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying toward it at the prayer place."],[810,"Use a sutrah in your prayer, even if it is an arrow."],[811,"When one of you prays, he should place something in front of him. And one time he said: \"Something in front of his face. If he does not find something, then let him set up a staff. If he does not find a staff, then let him draw a line, then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him.\" And al-Jawwaz said: \"Let him place something in front of his face.\""],[812,"Isma'il b. Umayyah narrated to us, from Abu 'Amr [b. Muhammad] b. Hurayth, that he heard his grandfather narrating from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. Abu Bakr said: What is correct is what Bishr b. al-Mufaddal said, and likewise Ma'mar and al-Thawri said, from Abu 'Amr b. Hurayth, except that the two of them said: from his father, from Abu Hurayrah. Muhammad b. Rafi' narrated it to us, he said."],[813,"Zayd b. Khalid sent me to Abu Juhaym to ask him about the one passing in front of one praying: What is upon him? [He] said: If he were to stand for forty, that would be better for him than passing in front of him."],[814,"My uncle 'Ubaydullah informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah."],[815,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ when he finished his circumambulation, he came to the edge of the area of circumambulation and prayed two rak'ahs, and there was no one between him and those performing circumambulation."],[816,"When one of you is praying, he should not let anyone pass in front of him. If he refuses, then let him fight him, for he is only a devil."],[817,"He used to pray towards a pillar. A man from Banu Umayyah went to pass in front of him, so he prevented him. He went to return, so he struck him a blow on his chest. He was a man from Banu Umayyah. That was mentioned to Marwan. Marwan met him and said, \"What made you strike your nephew?\" He said, \"The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'When one of you prays towards something that screens him, and someone goes to pass in front of him, let him prevent him. If he refuses, let him fight him, for he is only a devil.' Indeed, I only struck the devil.\""],[818,"While Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was praying on Friday, he mentioned the hadith like the hadith of Sulayman b. al-Mughirah, which comes after it in the second chapter, except that he added: \"And I had forbidden him, but he refused to stop.\" He said: \"Marwan was then governor of Madinah, so he complained to him. Marwan mentioned that to Abu Sa'id. Abu Sa'id said, 'The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"When something passes in front of one of you while he is praying, let him prevent it twice. If it refuses, let him fight it, for it is only a devil.\"'\""],[819,"While Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was praying on Friday towards something that screened him from the people, a young man from Banu Abi Mu'ayt came and wanted to pass in front of him. He pushed him on the throat. He looked and found no way except in front of Abu Sa'id, so he returned. He pushed him on the throat harder than the first push. He said: He stood up straight, then he insulted Abu Sa'id, then he went out and entered upon Marwan and complained to him of what he had encountered from Abu Sa'id. He said: Abu Sa'id entered upon Marwan, and he said, \"What is the matter with you and your nephew? He came complaining about you.\" Abu Sa'id said, \"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'When one of you prays and someone wants to pass in front of him, let him push him on the throat. If he refuses, let him fight him, for he is only a devil.'\""],[820,"Do not pray except towards a sutrah, and do not let anyone pass in front of you. If he refuses, fight him, for with him is the qarin (companion)."],[821,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray at night while 'A'ishah was lying across between him and the qiblah."],[822,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray his prayer at night while I was lying across between him and the qiblah, like the lying across of a funeral bier.\" Al-Makhzumi added once: \"When he wanted to pray Witr, he moved me with his foot.\""],[824,"He used to pray at night while I was sleeping between him and the qiblah. When it was time for Witr, he woke me up."],[825,"While I was lying across between him and the qiblah, when he wanted to pray Witr, he woke me up, and I prayed Witr. In the hadith of Bundar: 'He used to pray at night while our bed was between him and the qiblah. When he wanted to pray Witr, he made me stand up, and I prayed Witr.'"],[826,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray while I was lying across in front of him. When I wanted to get up, I slipped away from the direction of his feet."],[827,"Sometimes I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying at night in the middle of the bed, while I was on the bed between him and the qiblah. I would need something, so I would slip away from the direction of the foot of the bed, disliking to face him."],[828,"He was praying, and a sheep passed in front of him. He raced it towards the qiblah until he pressed his belly against the qiblah."],[829,"A cat does not cut off the prayer; it belongs to the household goods."],[830,"Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Abi al-Zinad, this hadith as mawquf, not marfu'. Abu Bakr said: Ibn Wahb is more knowledgeable about the hadith of the people of Madinah than 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Abd al-Majid."],[831,"The donkey, the woman, and the black dog cut off the prayer. I said: O Abu Dharr, what is the difference between the black dog and the white, the yellow, the red? He said: O son of my brother, I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ just as you asked me, and he said: The black dog is a devil."],[832,"The prayer is repeated due to the passing of a donkey, a woman, or a black dog. I said: What is the difference between the black dog and the yellow, the red? He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ just as you asked me, and he said: The black dog is a devil."],[833,"The dog and the menstruating woman cut off the prayer."],[834,"I and al-Fadl came while we were on a female donkey, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying with the people at 'Arafah. We passed by some of the rows, dismounted, and left the donkey to graze, and he did not say to us — Abu Musa said: meaning anything, and 'Abd al-Jabbar said: The Prophet ﷺ did not forbid us, and al-Makhzumi said: he did not say anything to us."],[835,"And the female donkey passed in front of the people, and it did not cut off their prayer."],[836,"I came with a boy from Banu Hashim on a donkey, or two donkeys, and I passed in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying, and he did not turn away. Then two slave girls from Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib came and grabbed the knees of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he separated them—or parted them—and he did not turn away."],[839,"I and al-Fadl came on a female donkey, and we passed in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at 'Arafah while he was praying the obligatory prayer, with nothing screening him, no barrier between us and him."],[841,"The spear was planted in front of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at 'Arafat, and he prayed towards it, with the donkey behind the spear."],[842,"The Prophet ﷺ planted a spear and prayed towards it, while a dog, a woman, and a donkey passed behind it."],[843,"We used to pray while riding animals passed in front of us, so we asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'A thing like the rear part of a saddle should be in front of one of you, then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him.'"],[844,"Let one of you place in front of him something like the rear part of a saddle, then whatever passes in front of him will not harm him."],[845,"She had a cloth with images stretched out to a shelf, and the Prophet ﷺ used to pray facing it. He said: 'Move it away from me.' So I took it and made it into cushions."],[846,"Teach me a supplication that I can invoke in my prayer."],[847,"Teach me, O Messenger of Allah, a supplication that I can invoke in my prayer and in my house. He said: 'Say: O Allah, I have wronged myself with much wrong, and none forgives sins except You, so forgive me with forgiveness from You and have mercy on me. Indeed, You are the Forgiving, the Merciful.'"],[848,"When {When the victory of Allah and the conquest comes} was revealed until its end, I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray a prayer except that he said: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise. O Allah, forgive me.'"],[849,"We used to go early to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and a man would come and a woman would come and say: 'O Messenger of Allah, what should I say when I pray?' He said: 'Say: O Allah, forgive me and have mercy on me, guide me, grant me well-being, and provide for me.' For these have gathered for you your worldly life and your hereafter."],[850,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in one of his prayers: 'O Allah, call me to account with an easy reckoning.' When he finished, I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the easy reckoning?' He said: 'He looks into his record and overlooks it for him. Indeed, whoever is disputed about the account on that day, O Aisha, will perish. And whatever afflicts the believer, Allah expiates from him thereby, even a thorn that pricks him.'"],[851,"Umm Sulaym came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, teach me some words that I can supplicate with in my prayer.' He said: 'Glorify Allah ten times, praise Him ten times, and magnify Him ten times, then ask for your need; He will say: Yes, yes.'"],[852,"I was shown that you will be tried in the graves like the trial of the Dajjal. Amrah said: Aisha said: So I used to hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in his prayer: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the Fire and from the punishment of the grave.'"],[853,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate in his prayer: 'O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of the Dajjal, and I seek refuge in You from the trial of life and death. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from sin and debt.' Aisha said: A speaker said: 'How often you seek refuge from debt, O Messenger of Allah!' He said: 'Indeed, when a man incurs debt, he speaks and lies, and promises and breaks his promise.'"],[854,"There was fighting among the Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf, and that reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he prayed Zuhr, then went to them to make peace between them. Then he said to Bilal, \"O Bilal, if 'Asr prayer comes and I have not come, tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\" When 'Asr prayer came, Bilal called the adhan, then the iqamah, then said to Abu Bakr, \"Go forward.\" Abu Bakr went forward and entered the prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and began making his way through the people until he stood behind Abu Bakr. He said: The people clapped, and Abu Bakr, when he entered the prayer, did not turn around. When Abu Bakr saw the clapping not stopping, he turned around, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to him, meaning \"continue.\" When he said that, Abu Bakr paused briefly, praising Allah for the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"continue.\" Then Abu Bakr walked backward on his heels and stepped back. When the Prophet ﷺ saw that, he came forward and led the people in prayer. When he finished his prayer, he said, \"O Abu Bakr, what prevented you, when I gestured to you, from continuing?\" He said, \"It is not for the son of Abu Quhafah to lead the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" And the Prophet ﷺ said to the people, \"If something happens to you in your prayer, let the men say subhan Allah and let the women clap.\" Ibn Abi Hazim said in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to him like this, commanding him to pray. Abu Bakr raised his hand and praised Allah, then stepped back behind him. 'Abd al-A'la said in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to him, meaning \"stay as you are.\" Abu Bakr raised his hands and praised Allah and extolled Him for the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then stepped back."],[855,"Whoever something happens to him in his prayer, let him say: subhan Allah. Indeed this is for women."],[856,"We used to greet the Prophet ﷺ while he was in prayer, and he would respond to us. When we returned from being with the Negus, we greeted him and he did not respond. We said, \"O Messenger of Allah, we used to greet you in prayer and you would respond.\" He ﷺ said, \"Indeed in prayer there is occupation.\""],[857,"One of us used to speak to the man beside him in prayer, until the verse was revealed: 'And stand before Allah, devoutly obedient.' Added in the hadith of Hushaym: 'So we were commanded to be silent and forbidden from speaking.'"],[858,"A man used to speak to his companion in prayer about a need, during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, until the verse was revealed: 'And stand before Allah, devoutly obedient.' So we were commanded to be silent."],[859,"We used to greet the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying, with the like of it, and he said: 'So he responded to us,' then he said, 'Indeed in prayer there is occupation.'"],[861,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed one of the two evening prayers - and my strongest thought is that it was Zuhr - two rak'ahs. Then he came to a piece of wood in the qiblah of the mosque and placed his hands on it, one over the other. The quick people left and said, \"The prayer has been shortened.\" Among the people were Abu Bakr and 'Umar, and they were afraid to speak to him. A man - short in hands or long - called Dhu al-Yadayn said, \"Has the prayer been shortened or did you forget?\" He said, \"It has not been shortened and I did not forget.\" He said, \"Rather you forgot.\" He said, \"Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth?\" He said, \"Yes.\" So he prayed two rak'ahs, then said the salam, then said takbir and prostrated like his prostration or longer, then raised."],[862,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to Ubayy b. Ka'b while he was praying."],[863,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by me while I was in the mosque. He called me, but I did not come to him. He said, 'What prevented you from coming to me?' I said, 'I was praying.' He said, 'Did not Allah, Mighty and Majestic, say: \"O you who believe, respond to Allah and to the Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life\"?' Then he said, 'Shall I not teach you the greatest surah in the Quran before I leave?' When he turned to leave, I reminded him of that. He said, 'Al-hamdu lillahi rabbi l-alamin (Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds). It is the Seven Oft-Repeated Verses and the Magnificent Quran that I have been given.'"],[864,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by me while I was praying and called me, with the like of it, except that he said, 'the greatest surah.'"],[865,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began the prayer and we stood with him. A bedouin said during the prayer: 'O Allah, have mercy on me and Muhammad, and do not have mercy on anyone else with us.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the salam, he said to the bedouin: 'You have indeed restricted something vast.'"],[866,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed at Mina and a word slipped from him. The hypocrites heard it and made much of it, saying, 'He has two hearts. Do you not hear his saying and his speech in prayer? He has a heart with you and a heart with his companions.' Then it was revealed: 'O Prophet, fear Allah and do not obey the disbelievers and the hypocrites.' He recited until His saying: 'Allah has not made for any man two hearts within him.'"],[867,"He saw Abu Barzah al-Aslami praying while the rein of his mount was in his hand. When he bowed, the rein slipped from his hand and the animal started to move. Abu Barzah stepped back on his heels without turning around until he caught up with the animal, took it, then walked as he was and returned to the place where he had been praying, completed his prayer, and then said the salam. He said: 'I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in many expeditions'—until he counted several expeditions—'and I observed his concessions and his facilitation, and I acted accordingly. If I had left my mount until it reached the desert and then gone after it as an old man stumbling through the darkness, that would have been harder for me.'"],[868,"The Muslims were in the dawn prayer on Monday, with Abu Bakr leading them, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ suddenly lifted the curtain of A'ishah's chamber and looked at them while they were in rows in prayer. He smiled and laughed. Abu Bakr stepped back on his heels to join the row, thinking that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wanted to come out to the prayer, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to them with his hand, saying, 'Complete your prayer.'"],[869,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ leading the people in prayer with Umamah bint Zaynab on his shoulder. When he bowed he put her down, and when he rose from prostration he put her back."],[870,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the killing of the two black creatures in prayer: the scorpion and the snake. In the hadith of Ghundar, Ma'mar said: I said to him, and he said: 'The scorpion and the snake.' In the hadith of Abd al-A'la, Yahya said: 'Meaning the snake and the scorpion.'"],[872,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to turn right and left during his prayer, but he did not turn his neck behind his back."],[873,"I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ and he glanced out of the corner of his eye at a man who did not straighten his back in bowing and prostration. When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ finished, he said: 'O assembly of Muslims, there is no prayer for the one who does not straighten his back in bowing and prostration.'"],[874,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ill, so we prayed behind him while he was seated. He turned to us and saw us standing, so he gestured to us, and we sat down."],[875,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque, so he scraped it with a pebble and forbade a man from spitting in front of him or to his right, and he said: 'Let him spit to his left or under his left foot.'"],[876,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ once saw some mucus in the qiblah, so he took a pebble and scraped it off. Then he said: None of you should clear his throat in the qiblah, nor to his right; rather, he should spit to his left or beneath his left foot."],[877,"When you are in prayer, do not spit to your right, but rather behind you, or to your left, or beneath your left foot."],[878,"When you are in prayer, do not spit in front of you nor to your right, but spit to your left; and if the spot is not empty, then beneath your left foot, and then rub it with it."],[879,"He prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he cleared his throat and then rubbed it with his left sandal. Khalid added in his narration: It was on hard ground."],[880,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying, and he spat beneath his left foot. Al-ʿAlāʾ added: Then he rubbed it."],[881,"He used to like date-palm stalks and would hold them in his hand. One day he entered the mosque with one of them in his hand and saw some mucus in the qiblah of the mosque. He scraped them off until he had cleaned them all. Then he turned to the people angrily and said: Would any of you like a man to face him and spit in his face? When one of you stands for prayer, he is facing his Lord, and the angel is on his right, so he should not spit in front of him nor to his right, but he may spit beneath his left foot or to his left. If he cannot help it, then let him do like this in the edge of his garment, and he folded part of it over another part."],[882,"When one of you is in prayer, he should not spit in front of him, for his Lord is in front of him. He should spit to his left or beneath his foot, and if he finds no place to spit, then into his garment or sandal, until he gets rid of it."],[883,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was once leading the people in prayer when two slave girls from Banū ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib came and began fighting with each other. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took hold of them and pulled one away from the other, and he did not mind that."],[884,"When one of you is praying, he should not let anyone pass in front of him. If he refuses, then let him fight him, for he is only a devil."],[885,"I spent the night at my aunt Maymūnah's. When part of the night had passed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up to pray... then I stood on his left and he moved me to his right."],[886,"The Prophet ﷺ used to gesture during prayer."],[887,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ill, so we prayed behind him while he was sitting, and he gestured to us, so we sat down."],[888,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, and when he prostrated, al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn would jump on his back. When some people tried to stop them, he gestured to them: Leave them. When he finished the prayer, he placed them in his lap and said: Whoever loves me, let him love these two."],[889,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque of Quba, and men from the Ansar came in to him greeting him. I asked Suhayb: How did the Prophet ﷺ act when he was greeted while praying? He said: He would gesture with his hand."],[890,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Banu al-Mustaliq. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was on a donkey of his and was praying. I spoke to him, and he gestured to me with his hand."],[891,"We prayed with the Prophet ﷺ one day, and he prolonged the standing. Then I saw him reach with his hand to take something. When he said the salam, he said: There is nothing that you were promised except that it was shown to me in this standing of mine, until the Fire was shown to me, and sparks from it came toward me until they came level with this place of mine, and I feared that it would overwhelm you."],[892,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood praying, then he stretched out his hand as if taking something. When he finished the prayer, we said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you stretch out your hand. He said: The enemy of Allah, Iblis, came with a flame of fire to put it in my face. I said: I seek refuge in Allah from you, three times, but he did not retreat. Then I wanted to seize him. Were it not for the supplication of our brother Sulayman ﷺ, he would have been bound by morning, and the children of the people of Madinah would have played with him."],[893,"We prayed the morning prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: While he was in prayer, he stretched out his hand, then withdrew it. When he finished the prayer, we said: O Messenger of Allah, you did in this prayer of yours what you did not do in any previous prayer. He said: I saw Paradise displayed before me, and I saw in it a grapevine whose clusters were near, its berries like gourds. I wanted to take from it, but it was revealed to it to withdraw, and it withdrew. Then the Fire was displayed to me, between me and you, until I saw my shadow and your shadows. I gestured to you to move back, but it was revealed to me to settle them, for you have submitted and they have submitted, you have emigrated and they have emigrated, you have striven and they have striven. I saw no virtue for me over you except prophethood."],[895,"Saying subhan Allah is for men, and clapping is for women."],[896,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when asked about wiping in the mosque, said: If you must do it, then once."],[897,"From Muʿayqib: \"\""],[898,"I asked the Prophet ﷺ about wiping pebbles during prayer. He said: Once, and if you refrain, it is better for you than a hundred camels, all with black eyes."],[899,"Indeed Allah has overlooked for my community what their souls whisper, as long as it is not uttered or acted upon."],[900,"There was no horseman among us on the day of Badr except al-Miqdad. I saw us while there was none among us but sleeping, except the Messenger of Allah ﷺ under a tree, praying and weeping until morning."],[901,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying while his chest made a sound like the boiling of a cauldron."],[902,"The sun eclipsed one day during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray, then he prostrated. He hardly raised his head, and he began to blow and weep.\" And he mentioned the hadith. And he said: \"Then he stood, praised Allah, and extolled Him, and said: 'The Fire was presented to me, and I began to blow it away, fearing that it might overwhelm you.'\""],[903,"I had a standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that no one else among creation had: I would come to him and greet him until he cleared his throat, then I would leave to my family."],[904,"He says subhan Allah."],[908,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered into prayer, he raised his hands, then said the takbir, then wrapped himself in his garment, then inserted his hands into his garment, then grasped his left hand with his right."],[909,"Muhammad b. Yahya narrated to us, he said: 'Affan b. Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, except that in 'Affan's hadith there is no mention of: 'then he inserted his hands into his garment.'\""],[910,"If one of you becomes drowsy during his prayer, let him lie down until sleep leaves him, for if one of you prays while drowsy, he may intend to seek forgiveness but instead curse himself."],[911,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man from praying with his hands on his hips. And Isma'il said in his hadith: \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade placing the hands on the hips during prayer.\""],[912,"Placing the hands on the hips during prayer is the rest of the people of the Fire."],[913,"Abdullah b. 'Abbas saw 'Abdullah b. al-Harith praying with his hair braided behind him, so he stood and began to undo it, while the other let him. When he finished, he turned to Ibn 'Abbas and said: \"What do you have against my head?\" He said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'The likeness of this is the likeness of one who prays while his hands are bound.'\" Yunus said: \"And it was tied back, so he stood behind him and untied it, and the other let him.\""],[914,"He saw Abu Rafi', the freed slave of the Prophet ﷺ, pass by Hasan b. 'Ali while Hasan was praying, having stuck his two braids into the back of his neck. Abu Rafi' untied them, so Hasan turned to him angrily. Abu Rafi' said: \"Face your prayer and do not be angry, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'That is the seat of the devil.'\""],[915,"Aban b. Yazid narrated to us. Abu Bakr said: Abu Hurayrah informs that he witnessed this prayer with the Prophet ﷺ in which this story occurred. So how could this story of Dhu al-Yadayn be before the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of speaking during prayer? Ibn Mas'ud informs that the Prophet ﷺ informed him upon his return from the land of Abyssinia, when he greeted the Prophet ﷺ, that among what Allah had newly ordained was that they should not speak during prayer. Ibn Mas'ud's return from the land of Abyssinia was before the Battle of Badr, since Ibn Mas'ud had witnessed Badr and claimed that he killed Abu Jahl b. Hisham on that day. I have dictated this story in the book of Jihad. Abu Hurayrah only came to Madinah years after Badr; he came to Madinah while the Prophet ﷺ was at Khaybar, and he had appointed Siba' b. Urfutah al-Ghifari as his deputy over Madinah."],[916,"When one of you stands in prayer, mercy faces him, so he should not wipe away the pebbles."],[917,"When one of you stands in prayer, mercy faces him, so do not move the pebbles."],[919,"I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ about everything, until I asked him about wiping away pebbles in prayer, and he said: \"[Once] or leave it.\""],[921,"He forbade letting the garment hang down in prayer, and that a man cover his mouth."],[922,"When one of you yawns, let him cover his mouth with his hand, for Satan enters."],[923,"Yawning in prayer is from Satan, so when one of you yawns, let him suppress it as much as he can."],[924,"Sneezing is from God, and yawning is from Satan. When one of you yawns, let him not say, 'Hah,' for Satan laughs inside him."],[925,"God loves sneezing and dislikes yawning. When one of you yawns, let him not say, 'Ah, ah,' for Satan laughs at him,\" or he said, \"plays with him."],[926,"The Prophet ﷺ saw phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque and scraped it off, or he said: he removed it with his hand. Then he turned to the people and became angry with them, and said: \"God, Mighty and Majestic, is before the face of one of you in his prayer, so let none of you spit before his face in his prayer.\""],[927,"Shabath b. Rib'i prayed beside Hudhayfah and spat in front of him. Hudhayfah said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade us from that. When a man enters his prayer, God turns His face toward him and does not turn away from him until he turns away or breaks his ablution.\""],[928,"Whoever spits toward the qiblah will come on the Day of Resurrection with his spit between his eyes."],[929,"The Messenger of God ﷺ saw phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque and removed it with a stick he had. Then he turned to the people, who recognized anger in his face, and said: \"Which of you is the owner of this phlegm?\" They remained silent. He said: \"Would any of you like, when he stands to pray, for a man to face him and spit in his face?\" They said: \"No.\" He said: \"God, Mighty and Majestic, is before you in your prayer, so do not direct anything harmful before you, but to the left of one of you or under his foot.\""],[931,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in a khamīṣah (a black square garment) with markings, and he said: “These markings distracted me. Take it to Abū Jaḥm and bring me an anbijāniyyah (a heavy woollen cloak).”"],[932,"When the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah, he entered it from its upper side and left from its lower side."],[933,"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded Yaḥyā b. Zakariyyā with five statements to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon. He said: He was slow in acting upon them, so ʿĪsā said to him: “You were commanded with five statements to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon. Either you command them or I will stand and command them.” Yaḥyā said: “If you precede me in them, I fear that I will be punished or the earth will swallow me.” So he gathered the Children of Israel in Jerusalem until the mosque was filled, to the point that people sat on the balconies. He admonished the people, then said: “Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded me with five statements to act upon and to command you to act upon. The first of them: that you do not associate anything with Allah. The likeness of one who associates partners with Allah is like a man who buys a slave with his own pure wealth, of gold or silver, then says to him: ‘This is my house and my work, so work for me and deliver to me your labor.’ But the slave works and delivers his labor to someone other than his master. Which of you would like to have a slave like that, who delivers his labor to someone other than his master? Allah is the one who created you and provided for you, so do not associate anything with Allah. And he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded you with prayer. When you set your faces (toward the qiblah), do not turn away, for Allah sets His face toward the face of His servant when he prays to Him, and He does not turn His face away from him until the servant himself turns away.”"],[934,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man's turning around during prayer. He said: “It is a snatching that Satan snatches from the servant’s prayer.”"],[935,"He used to lead his people in prayer. He came once when the prayer had already been established and said: “Let one of you pray, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘When prayer becomes due and the need to defecate is present, begin with relieving the need to defecate.’”"],[936,"We were with ʿĀʾishah when food was brought. Al-Qāsim stood up to pray, and ʿĀʾishah said: “I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ‘No prayer should be performed in the presence of food, nor while he is resisting the two impure things (urine and feces).’”"],[937,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell from a horse, and his right side was grazed. We entered to visit him, and when the prayer time came, he prayed with us seated."],[938,"When dinner is placed and the call to prayer is made, begin with dinner. He said: Ibn ʿUmar ate dinner one night while he heard the recitation of the imam."],[939,"If one of you is at food, let him not rush until he fulfills his need of it, even if the prayer is established."],[940,"'Isa informed us, from Sa'id, with this chain, the like of it:"],[941,"“I am the best of partners.” Bundār said: “I am the most self-sufficient of partners from shirk. Whoever performs a deed and associates someone else with Me in it, I am free of it, and it belongs to the one he associated.” Bundār said: He said: “I am free of it, so let him seek his reward from him.”"],[942,"He stayed in Madinah seeking ʿAbdullāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ, and he asked about him. They said: “He has traveled to Makkah.” So he followed him and found that he had traveled to al-Ṭāʾif. He followed him and found him in his field walking arm-in-arm with a man from Quraysh, and the Qurayshi was suspected of wine. When I met him, I greeted him, and he greeted me. He said: “What brought you today, and from where have you come?” I told him, then I asked him: “O ʿAbdullāh b. ʿAmr, did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention anything about drinking wine?” He said: “Yes.” The Qurayshi pulled his hand away and left. He said: “I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: ‘No man of my ummah drinks wine but that his prayer will not be accepted for forty mornings.’”"],[943,"Three whose prayer Allah does not accept, and whose good deeds do not ascend: the runaway slave until he returns to his masters and places his hand in their hands, the woman with whom her husband is angry until he is pleased, and the drunkard until he becomes sober."],[944,"When a slave runs away, his prayer is not accepted until he returns to his masters."],[945,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to his companions: \"Has any of you seen a dream?\" So whoever Allah willed would narrate it to him. And indeed, one morning he said to us: \"Two visitors came to me last night, and they sent me forth and said to me: 'Go, go.' So we came upon a man lying down, and another man standing at his head with a rock, and he was raising the rock and crushing his head. Then he rolled the stone here and followed it and took it, and did not return to the man until his head healed as it was. Then he returned to him and did to him as he had done the first time.\" Then he mentioned the hadith in full, and said: \"They said: 'Now we shall inform you. As for the man whose head is crushed, he is a man who learns the Qur'an but then abandons it and sleeps through the obligatory prayer.'\""],[946,"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made obligatory upon the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ four rak'ahs in residence, two rak'ahs in travel, and one rak'ah in fear."],[947,"The prayer of travel and residence was made obligatory as two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ settled in Madinah, two rak'ahs were added to the prayer of residence, two rak'ahs. And the Fajr prayer was left as it was due to the length of the recitation, and the Maghrib prayer because it is the witr of the day."],[948,"'Isa informed us, from Sa'id, with this chain, the like of it:"],[949,"He said to Abdullah b. Umar: We find in the Qur'an the prayer of residence and the prayer of fear, but we do not find the prayer of travel in the Qur'an. Abdullah said: O son of my brother, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent Muhammad ﷺ to us while we knew nothing, so we only do as we saw Muhammad ﷺ do."],[950,"I traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman, and they would pray Zuhr and Asr as two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, not praying before or after them. Abdullah b. Umar said: If I had prayed before or after them, I would have completed them."],[953,"Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves that His concessions be taken, just as He hates that His disobedience be committed."],[954,"I asked Ibn Abbas: How should I pray in Makkah if I do not pray in congregation? He said: Two rak'ahs, the Sunnah of Abu al-Qasim."],[955,"Regarding a traveler praying behind a resident, he said: He prays according to his prayer."],[957,"When Ibn Umar was in Makkah, he would pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, unless an imam gathered the people, in which case he would pray with the imam. If the imam gathered the people, he would pray with him."],[958,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ traveled on a journey and stayed for nineteen days praying two rak'ahs."],[959,"I asked Anas b. Malik about shortening the prayer, and he said: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah to Makkah, praying two rak'ahs until we returned. I asked him: Did he stay in Makkah? He said: Yes, he stayed there for ten days."],[960,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived on the morning of the fourth day that had passed of Dhu al-Hijjah."],[961,"I asked Anas b. Malik, saying: Tell me something you understood from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Where did he pray Zuhr on the day of Tarwiyah? He said: At Mina."],[962,"When the Prophet ﷺ entered Makkah, he entered it from its upper side and left from its lower side."],[963,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered in the year of the conquest from Kada', from the upper part of Makkah."],[964,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah from the upper mountain pass which is near al-Batha' and left from the lower mountain pass."],[965,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha, and slept a short sleep at al-Muhassab, then rode to the House and circumambulated it."],[966,"From A'ishah, she mentioned some of the description of the Prophet's ﷺ Hajj and said: He ordered his companions to depart, so the people departed. He passed by the House before the Fajr prayer, circumambulated it, then left and mounted, then departed heading to Madinah."],[967,"When the Prophet ﷺ was in a hurry to travel, he would combine Maghrib and Isha."],[968,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ when he was in a hurry to travel, he would combine Maghrib and Isha."],[969,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined prayers on a journey he undertook, that being the expedition of Tabuk: he combined Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha. He said: I said, \"What caused him to do that?\" He said, \"He wanted not to burden his community.\""],[970,"Qurra narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Sa'id b. Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, similar to that."],[971,"They went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ in the year of Tabuk, and the Messenger of God ﷺ used to combine Zuhr and Asr, and Maghrib and Isha. He said: One day he delayed the prayer, then came out and prayed Zuhr and Asr together. Then he entered, then came out and prayed Maghrib and Isha together. Then he said: 'You will come tomorrow, God willing, to the spring of Tabuk, and you will not reach it until the day is bright. Whoever reaches it should not touch any of its water until I come.' He said: We reached it while two men had already preceded us to it, and the spring was like a shoelace, trickling with a little water. The Messenger of God ﷺ asked them: 'Have you touched any of its water?' They said: 'Yes.' He rebuked them, and said to them whatever God willed to say. Then they scooped water from the spring with their hands, little by little, until it gathered in something. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ washed his face and hands in it, then poured it back into it, and the spring flowed with abundant water, and the people drew water. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Soon, O Muadh, if you live long, you will see this place filled with gardens.'"],[972,"When the journey was pressing on a day, he would combine Zuhr and Asr; and when he intended to travel at night, he would combine Maghrib and Isha. He would delay Zuhr to the beginning of the time of Asr and combine them, and delay Maghrib until he combined it with Isha when the twilight disappeared."],[973,"I was with Abdullah b. Umar, Hafs b. Asim, and Musahiq b. Amr. He said: The sun set, and it was said to Ibn Umar, 'The prayer.' He said: He continued traveling, and it was said to him, 'The prayer.' He replied: 'The Messenger of God ﷺ, when the journey was pressing, would delay this prayer; and I intend to delay it.' He said: We traveled until midnight—or near midnight. He said: Then he dismounted and prayed it."],[974,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ in Madinah eight and seven together. I said, 'Why did he do that?' He said, 'He wanted not to burden his community.' He said: He was resident, without travel or fear."],[975,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed Zuhr and Asr together, and Maghrib and Isha together, without fear or travel."],[976,"I departed with the Messenger of God ﷺ from Arafat, and when he reached Muzdalifah, he called the adhan and the iqama, then prayed Maghrib. Then the last of the people had not dismounted before he called the iqama and prayed Isha."],[977,"We were detained on the Day of the Trench from prayer until after sunset, with a portion of the night having passed, until we were relieved. That is His saying: 'And Allah sufficed the believers in battle; and Allah is Strong, Mighty.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Bilal, and he gave the iqamah for prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr as beautifully as he used to pray it. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed 'Asr likewise. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed Maghrib likewise. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed 'Isha' likewise, before the prayer of fear was revealed: either on foot or mounted."],[978,"When he alighted at a stopping place, he would not depart until he prayed Zuhr. I said, 'Even if it was midday?' He said, 'Even if it was midday.'"],[979,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ on an expedition, and he used to pray voluntary prayers on his mount, facing east; but when he wanted to pray an obligatory prayer, he would dismount and face the qibla."],[980,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell from a horse, and his right side was grazed. We entered to visit him, and when the prayer time came, he prayed with us seated."],[981,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying cross-legged."],[982,"I had hemorrhoids, so I asked the Prophet ﷺ about prayer, and he said: Pray standing; if you cannot, then sitting; and if you cannot, then on your side."],[983,"When asked about the prayer of fear, he said: The imam stands with a group of people and prays one rak'ah with them, while another group remains between him and the enemy, not yet praying. When those with him have prayed one rak'ah, they move back to the place of those who have not prayed, without saying the taslim, and those who have not prayed come forward and pray one rak'ah with him. Then the imam leaves, having prayed two rak'ahs, and each of the two groups stands and prays one rak'ah for themselves. If the fear is more severe than that, they pray on foot standing, and mounted, facing the Qibla and not facing it. Nafi' said: I do not think Ibn 'Umar mentioned it except from the Messenger of Allah."],[984,"Nafi' said: Ibn 'Umar narrated that from the Messenger of Allah."],[985,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Khalid b. Sufyan b. Nubayh al-Hudhali, and it had reached him that he was gathering [forces] against him. He was [heading toward] 'Uranah and 'Arafat. He said to me: \"Go and kill him.\" I said: O Messenger of Allah, describe him to me. He said: \"When you see him, you will get goosebumps. It is enough for you that I describe nothing else of him to you.\" He was a hairy, shaggy man. I set out until when I drew near him, the prayer time came, the 'Asr prayer. I said to myself: I fear that if I delay the prayer, something may happen between me and him, so I prayed while walking, gesturing toward him. Then I reached him, and by Allah, as soon as I saw him I got goosebumps. He was among his womenfolk in his howdahs. I walked with him, and he said: Who are you? I said: A man from the Arabs; I heard that you are gathering [forces] against this man, so I came to you about that. He said: I am indeed doing that. I said to myself: You will soon know. I walked with him for a while until I could overpower him, then I struck him with my sword until he became still. Then I came to Madinah to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of the news, and he gave me a mikhsar—meaning a staff. I left his presence with it, and my companions said to me: What is this that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave you? I said: A mikhsar. They said: What will you do with it? Why didn't you ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ why he gave it to you and what you should do with it? Go back to him and ask him. So I returned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, why did you give me the mikhsar? He said: \"It will be between me and you on the Day of Resurrection, and the fewest people on that day will be those who carry such staffs.\" He hung it on his sword and it never left him as long as he lived. When death came to him, he instructed us to bury it with him, and by Allah, it was placed in his shroud."],[986,"Muhammad b. Ja'far b. al-Zubayr narrated to me, from Ibn 'Abd Allah b. Unays, from his father, and he mentioned the hadith in full. Abu Bakr said: I have included the chapters on the descriptions of the prayer of fear at the end of the Book of Prayer."],[987,"Whoever catches two rak'ahs of 'Asr before the sun sets, or one rak'ah of the Fajr prayer before sunrise, has caught it."],[989,"Whoever prays one rak'ah of the Fajr prayer, then the sun rises, let him pray another to complete it."],[990,"We were on a journey with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith about their sleeping through the prayer until the sun rose, and he said: Then he called for prayer and prayed with the people."],[991,"We stopped to rest at night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we did not wake until the sun rose. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Let each person take hold of his mount's head, for this is a place where Satan came upon us. So we did that. He called for water and performed ablution, then prayed two prostrations. Then the prayer was established, and he prayed the dawn prayer."],[992,"They mentioned their negligence in sleeping, and he said: 'They slept until the sun rose.' The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'There is no negligence in sleep; negligence is only in wakefulness. So if one of you forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, and at its time the next day.' 'Abd Allah b. Rabah said: 'Imran heard me narrating the hadith and said: O young man, look how you narrate, for I was present for the hadith with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he did not deny anything of his narration.'"],[993,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ and his companions slept through the prayer, the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Pray it the next day at its time.'"],[995,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was asked about a man who sleeps through the prayer or is heedless of it. He said: 'Its expiation is that he prays it when he remembers it.'"],[996,"Whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps through it, its expiation is that he prays it when he remembers it."],[997,"Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it. There is no expiation for it except that."],[998,"We traveled at night with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and when it was the last part of the night we halted to sleep, and our eyes overcame us, and nothing woke us except the heat of the sun. A man would get up to his ablution in a daze. The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered them, so they performed ablution, then he ordered Bilal, who gave the adhan, then they prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. Then he ordered him and he gave the iqamah, and he prayed Fajr. They said: 'O Messenger of God, we have been negligent. Should we not repeat it at its time tomorrow?' He said: 'Your Lord forbids you from riba.'"],[999,"Abu al-Qasim led us in prayer."],[1000,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah ؓ ;"],[1001,"We were on a journey with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith about their sleeping through the prayer until the sun rose, and he said: Then he called for prayer and prayed with the people."],[1002,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and he slept until the sun rose. He commanded Bilal, and he gave the adhan. They performed ablution, then prayed the two rak'ahs, then prayed the dawn prayer."],[1003,"We stopped for a night's rest with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and did not wake until the sun had risen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Let each person take hold of his mount's head, for this is a stopping place where Satan came to us.\" So we did that. Then he called for water, performed wudu', then prayed two prostrations when the prayer was called, and prayed the morning prayer."],[1004,"The Prophet ﷺ addressed the people and admonished them, then said: O company of women, you are the majority of the people of the Fire. A wise woman said: And why is that? He said: Because of your frequent cursing and your ingratitude to your husbands, and I have not seen any among those deficient in intellect and religion more overpowering to men of understanding and men of opinion than you. A woman said: What is the deficiency in our intellect and our religion? He said: The testimony of two of you equals the testimony of one man, and the deficiency of your religion is menstruation—one of you remains three or four [days] without praying."],[1005,"A woman asked Aisha: Does a menstruating woman make up the prayer? She said: Are you a Haruri? One of us used to menstruate and was not commanded to make up. She said: And she mentioned that she asked the Prophet."],[1006,"Teach the child to pray at seven years of age, and strike him for it at ten."],[1007,"Ali b. Abi Talib passed by a madwoman of the Banu so-and-so who had committed adultery. Umar had ordered her to be stoned, so Ali turned her back and said to Umar: O Commander of the Believers, would you stone this one? He said: Yes. He said: Do you not remember that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The pen is lifted from three: from the madman whose intellect is overcome, from the sleeper until he wakes, and from the child until he reaches puberty? He said: You have spoken the truth. So he let her go."],[1008,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed on a straw mat."],[1009,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed on a carpet."],[1010,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray on a straw mat and tanned leather."],[1011,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah ؓ ;"],[1012,"He used to pray on a khumrah."],[1013,"When one of you prays, let him wear his sandals or take them off and place them between his feet, and not harm others with them."],[1014,"Did the Prophet ﷺ pray in sandals? He said: Yes."],[1015,"He used to pray on a khumrah, and he said: O 'A'ishah, lift your mat from us, for I feared it might cause fitnah for the people."],[1016,"I kept hearing that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed on a khumrah. And he said: On the authority of Anas b. Malik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray on the khumrah and prostrate on it."],[1017,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray on the khumrah, not leaving it in travel or at home."],[1018,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed on the Day of the Conquest, placing his sandals to his left."],[1019,"I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of the Conquest. He prayed on the Day of the Conquest, then took off his sandals and placed them to his left."],[1020,"When one of you prays, let him not place his sandals to his right or to his left, unless there is no one to his left, but let him place them between his feet."],[1021,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, then he took off his sandals, so the people took off their sandals. When he finished, he said: \"Why did you take off your sandals?\" They said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you take them off, so we took them off. He said: \"Jibril came to me and informed me that there was some impurity on them. So when one of you comes to the mosque, let him turn over his sandal and look at it. If there is impurity, let him wipe them on the ground, then pray in them.\""],[1022,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who feels something while he is in prayer. He said: \"He should not leave until he hears a sound or smells an odor.\""],[1023,"If one of you breaks his ablution while he is in prayer, let him place his hand on his nose and leave."],[1024,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah ؓ ;"],[1027,"If one of you doubts in his prayer, let him disregard the doubt and build upon certainty. Then if he becomes certain of completion, he prostrates two prostrations. If his prayer was complete, the rak'ah and the two prostrations are supererogatory. If it was deficient, the rak'ah completes his prayer and the two prostrations humiliate Satan."],[1028,"If one of you doubts in his prayer and does not know whether he has prayed one, two, three, or four [rak'ahs], let him complete what he doubts, then prostrate two prostrations while sitting. If his prayer was deficient, he has completed it, and the two prostrations are a humiliation for Satan. If he had completed his prayer, the rak'ah and the two prostrations are supererogatory for him."],[1029,"If one of you doubts in his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or four [rak'ahs], let him pray one rak'ah and prostrate two prostrations before the salutation."],[1030,"If one of you prays and does not know how many he has prayed, three or four, let him bow one rak'ah perfecting its bowing and prostration, and prostrate two prostrations."],[1031,"While al-Hajjaj was giving a sermon and Ibn 'Umar was present, with him two of his sons, one on his right and the other on his left, al-Hajjaj said: 'Ibn al-Zubayr has altered the Book of Allah; may Allah alter his heart.' He said: And Ibn 'Umar was facing him, so Ibn 'Umar said: 'That is neither in your hand nor in his.' He said: So al-Hajjaj fell silent, then said: 'Indeed Allah has taught us and every Muslim, and you, O old man, to be rational.' So Ibn 'Umar began to laugh. And he narrated it from 'Asim from Habib, who said: Then he jumped up, and his two sons sat him down. He said: 'Leave me, for I left the one which has virtue, that I should say to him: You lied.'"],[1033,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in a prayer which we thought was the 'Asr prayer. When he was in the second [rak'ah], he stood up and did not sit. Then when it was before the salutation, he prostrated two prostrations of forgetfulness while sitting."],[1034,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one of the prayers, and he stood up from two [rak'ahs] and was glorified [by the people]. He continued until he finished his prayer and nothing remained except the salutation, then he prostrated two prostrations while sitting before he said the salutation."],[1035,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer... and he mentioned the hadith. Yahya b. Hakim said in his hadith: 'We glorified him, but when he stood straight he continued and did not return.' Al-Fadl said: 'They glorified him, but he continued and did not return.'"],[1036,"He stood up in the two rak'ahs, and they glorified him, so he completed [the standing], then prostrated two prostrations of forgetfulness when he finished. Then he said: 'Did you see me sit? I only did as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.'"],[1037,"Whoever forgets something of his prayer, let him prostrate twice while sitting."],[1038,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed, and he forgot, so he said the salutation after two rak'ahs. Dhu al-Yadayn said to him: “Has the prayer been shortened, or did you forget?” He said: “The prayer has not been shortened, and I did not forget.” He said: “Is it as Dhu al-Yadayn says?” Then he stood and prayed, then prostrated twice."],[1039,"Abu Hurayrah ؓ said; H"],[1040,"Qatadah b. Di'amah narrated to me, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, similar to it, meaning that he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness on the day Dhu al-Yadayn came to him, after the salutation. Abu Bakr said: The report of Ibn Sirin from Abu Hurayrah indicates the heedlessness of those who claim that this story occurred before the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of speaking during prayer. Whoever understands knowledge and reflects on the reports of the Prophet ﷺ and the wording of the transmitters of this report knows that such a statement is ignorance on the part of its utterer. In the report of Ibn Sirin from Abu Hurayrah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer. Thus Malik b. Anas narrated it, from Dawud b. al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan, the client of Ibn Abi Ahmad, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer."],[1041,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the Asr prayer and said the salutation after two rak'ahs. Dhu al-Yadayn stood and said: “Has the prayer been shortened, O Messenger of Allah, or did you forget?” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Neither of that happened.” He said: “Some of that did happen, O Messenger of Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to the people and said: “Has Dhu al-Yadayn spoken the truth?” They said: “Yes.” So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed the remainder of the prayer, then prostrated twice while sitting after the salutation."],[1042,"Aban b. Yazid narrated to us. Abu Bakr said: Abu Hurayrah informs that he witnessed this prayer with the Prophet ﷺ in which this story occurred. So how could this story of Dhu al-Yadayn be before the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of speaking during prayer? Ibn Mas'ud informs that the Prophet ﷺ informed him upon his return from the land of Abyssinia, when he greeted the Prophet ﷺ, that among what Allah had newly ordained was that they should not speak during prayer. Ibn Mas'ud's return from the land of Abyssinia was before the Battle of Badr, since Ibn Mas'ud had witnessed Badr and claimed that he killed Abu Jahl b. Hisham on that day. I have dictated this story in the book of Jihad. Abu Hurayrah only came to Madinah years after Badr; he came to Madinah while the Prophet ﷺ was at Khaybar, and he had appointed Siba' b. Urfutah al-Ghifari as his deputy over Madinah."],[1043,"I came to Madinah while the Prophet ﷺ was at Khaybar, and Siba' b. Urfutah had been appointed as deputy over Madinah."],[1044,"I accompanied the Prophet ﷺ for three years. Bundar narrated to us, saying: Yahya b. Said narrated to us, from Ismail b. Abi Khalid. And Abu Hurayrah only accompanied the Prophet ﷺ at Khaybar and afterwards, and he is reporting that he witnessed this prayer with the Prophet ﷺ. So whoever claims that the report of Ibn Mas'ud abrogates the story of Dhul-Yadayn, if he were to reflect on the knowledge and abandon obstinacy and not let his intellect contend, he would know the impossibility of this claim, since it is impossible for the later one to be abrogated and the earlier one to be the abrogator. And the story of Dhul-Yadayn was years after the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of speaking during prayer, so how could the later one be abrogated and the earlier one be the abrogator? Moreover, the story of Dhul-Yadayn has nothing to do with the Prophet's ﷺ prohibition of speaking during prayer, and it is not of that same type, since speaking during prayer deliberately by a worshipper was permitted, and the worshipper knows and is certain that he is in prayer, then that was abrogated, and they were restrained from deliberately speaking during prayer after it had been previously permitted for them. It is not that they had been permitted to speak during prayer forgetfully or absentmindedly, not knowing they were in prayer, and then that was abrogated. Would it be permissible for one in whom reason is established, who understands the slightest bit of knowledge, to say: God rebuked a person when he does not know he is in prayer not to speak, or to say: God forbade a person to speak during prayer while he does not know that God has forbidden speech during prayer? Indeed, a person is only obliged not to speak during prayer after he knows that speech during prayer is prohibited and not permissible. And Mu'awiyah b. al-Hakam al-Sulami spoke while not knowing that speech during prayer was prohibited, and he said during prayer behind the Prophet ﷺ when someone sneezed and he responded to him, and the people threw their glances at him: 'Woe to my mother! Why are you looking at me?' When he spoke these words during prayer while not knowing that this speech was prohibited in prayer, the Prophet ﷺ taught him that the speech of people during prayer is prohibited and not permissible, and he did not command him to repeat that prayer in which he had spoken these words. And in the story of Dhul-Yadayn, the Prophet ﷺ spoke only on the basis that he was not in prayer, and that he had completed the obligatory prayer in full, and Dhul-Yadayn spoke to the Prophet ﷺ while not knowing that some of the obligation remained upon him, since it was possible in his view that the obligation had been reduced to the original obligation of two rak'ahs, as it was at the beginning. Do you not hear him saying to the Prophet ﷺ: 'Has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten?' The Prophet ﷺ answered him that he had not forgotten and it had not been shortened, while at the time of his denial he was not certain that some of that prayer remained upon him, so he sought confirmation from his companions, and said to them: 'Is it as Dhul-Yadayn says?' When he became certain that two rak'ahs of that prayer remained upon him, he made them up. So the Prophet ﷺ did not speak in this story after his knowledge and certainty that some of that prayer remained upon him. As for his companions who answered him and said to the Prophet ﷺ after his question to them: 'Is it as Dhul-Yadayn says?' They said: 'Yes.' This was the obligatory answer they were required to give him, even though they were in prayer, knowing and certain that they were in the very obligatory prayer, since God, Mighty and Majestic, distinguished between His chosen Prophet and others of his community by His honor and favor to him, by making it obligatory upon those praying to answer him, even if they were in prayer, in His saying: 'O you who believe, respond to God and the Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life.' And the chosen Prophet ﷺ said to Ubayy b. Ka'b and to Abu Sa'id b. al-Mu'alla, when he called each one of them individually while he was in prayer and he did not answer him until he finished the prayer: 'Did you not hear what was revealed to me' - or a similar wording - 'O you who believe, respond to God and the Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life.' I have brought out these two reports elsewhere. So between the speech of the Prophet's ﷺ companions on the day of Dhul-Yadayn, and the speech of Dhul-Yadayn according to the manner in which he spoke, and those after them, there is a difference in some rulings. As for the speech of Dhul-Yadayn at the beginning, it is not permissible for anyone after the Prophet ﷺ to speak in the manner of Dhul-Yadayn, since every worshipper after the Prophet ﷺ, when he salutes after two rak'ahs of Zuhr or Asr, knows and is certain that two rak'ahs of his prayer remain, because revelation ceased after the Prophet ﷺ, and it is impossible for any of the obligation to be reduced after the Prophet ﷺ. So every speaker who knows that the obligation of Zuhr and Asr is four rak'ahs, each of them individually, when he speaks after having prayed two rak'ahs and two rak'ahs remain upon him, is knowing and certain that his speech is prohibited for him and forbidden, and that he is speaking before completing the obligatory prayer. Dhul-Yadayn, when the Prophet ﷺ saluted after two rak'ahs, was not knowing or certain that some of the prayer remained upon him, nor was he knowing that speech was prohibited for him, since it was possible in his view at that time that the obligation of that prayer had been reduced to the original obligation of two rak'ahs as it was at the beginning. And his words in addressing the Prophet ﷺ indicate this. Do you not hear him saying to the Prophet ﷺ: 'Has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten?' And I have explained the reason for which the companions of the Prophet ﷺ spoke after the Prophet's ﷺ saying to Dhul-Yadayn: 'I have not forgotten, nor has it been shortened,' and I have made known that it was obligatory and incumbent upon them to answer the Prophet ﷺ even though they were in prayer. This obligation today has lapsed and is not permissible for a Muslim to answer anyone while he is in prayer with speech. So everyone who speaks after the cessation of revelation and says to a worshipper who has saluted after two rak'ahs: 'Has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten?' is obliged to repeat that prayer if he knows that the obligation of that prayer is four and not two rak'ahs. Likewise, everyone who speaks while being certain that he has not performed the obligation of that prayer completely and speaks before saluting from it in two rak'ahs, or after saluting in two rak'ahs, is obliged to repeat it. Likewise, everyone who answers a person while he is in prayer is obliged to repeat that prayer, since God, Mighty and Majestic, has not permitted any human being to answer anyone during prayer except the Prophet ﷺ, whom God singled out for that. And this is a lengthy issue, and I have brought it out at length with mention of the arguments of some who objected to our companions on this issue, and I explain the ugliness of what they argued against our companions on this issue of impossibilities and what resembles delirium, if God grants us success. Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: himself. Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition there is an addition: ﷺ ."],[1045,"Sa'id b. al-Musayyib, Abu Salamah b. Abd al-Rahman, and Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah b. Utbah narrated to me this story, but they did not mention Abu Hurayrah, and his hadith ended at his saying: 'So he completed what remained of his prayer.'"],[1046,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr or Asr, and saluted after two rak'ahs of one of them. Dhul-Shimalayn b. Abd Amr b. Nadlah al-Khuza'i, who was an ally of Banu Zuhrah, said to him: 'Has the prayer been shortened or have you forgotten, O Messenger of Allah?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I have not forgotten, nor has it been shortened.' Dhul-Shimalayn said: 'Some of that has indeed happened.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then turned to the people and said: 'Is Dhul-Yadayn truthful?' They said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up and completed the prayer. And none of them told me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed two prostrations while sitting in that prayer. That, as we see - and God knows best - was because the people made the Messenger of Allah ﷺ certain until he was sure."],[1047,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in Zuhr or Asr."],[1048,"Sa'id b. al-Musayyib, Abu Salamah, and Ubayd Allah b. Abd Allah informed me that Abu Hurayrah said: Then he mentioned something similar to their hadith in the story of Dhul-Yadayn."],[1049,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not prostrate on the day of Dhul-Yadayn."],[1050,"Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah and Abu Bakr b. Sulayman, from Abu Hurayrah."],[1051,"And in what I read to Abd Allah b. Nafi', and Mutarrif narrated to me, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Bakr b. Sulayman b. Abi Hathmah, who said: It reached me."],[1052,"My father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab, that Abu Bakr b. Sulayman b. Abi Hathmah informed him that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said with this report."],[1053,"That the Prophet ﷺ forgot in his prayer."],[1054,"Mutarrif narrated to us, and I read it to Ibn Nafi', from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id b. al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah b. Abd al-Rahman, the like of that."],[1055,"The Prophet ﷺ did not prostrate that day. None of them narrated to him that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated that day, not that they narrated to him from Abu Hurayrah that the Prophet ﷺ did not prostrate that day. The reports from Abu Hurayrah have come through numerous routes that no scholar of reports can reject, that the Prophet ﷺ performed the two prostrations of forgetfulness on the day of Dhul-Yadayn. Abu Bakr said: I have dictated the report of Shu'bah, from Sa'd b. Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah; and the routes of the reports of Yahya b. Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah; and the routes of the reports of Muhammad b. Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah; and the report of Dawud b. al-Husayn, from Abu Sufyan the freed slave of Ibn Abi Ahmad, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated on the day of Dhul-Yadayn the two prostrations of forgetfulness. Abu Bakr said: I have brought out the routes of these reports and their wordings in the large book."],[1056,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one day, then gave the salutation and turned away while one rak'ah of the prayer remained."],[1057,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he forgot and gave the salutation after two rak'ahs, then turned away. A man said to him, 'O Messenger of Allah, you forgot and gave the salutation after two rak'ahs.' He ordered Bilal, and he called the iqamah for the prayer, then he completed that rak'ah. I asked the people about the man who said, 'O Messenger of Allah, you forgot,' and it was said to me, 'Do you know him?' I said, 'No, unless I see him.' Then a man passed by me, and I said, 'It is this one.' They said, 'This is Talhah b. 'Ubaydullah.'"],[1058,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the salutation after three rak'ahs of 'Asr, then stood and entered the room. Al-Khirbaq—a man with long arms—stood and called out to him, 'O Messenger of Allah, has the prayer been shortened?' He came out angry, dragging his lower garment, and asked, and was informed, so he prayed that prayer which he had left, then prostrated two prostrations, then gave the salutation."],[1059,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in five rak'ahs, so we said, 'O Messenger of Allah, has something new occurred in the prayer?' He said, 'No.' We said, 'You led us in such and such.' He said, 'I am only a human being; I forget as you forget. When one of you forgets, let him prostrate two prostrations.' Then he turned ﷺ and prostrated two prostrations."],[1060,"He prayed Zuhr as five rak'ahs, and a man from the people said to him, 'Has there been an addition to the prayer?' He said, 'And what is that?' They said, 'You prayed five.' He said, 'So he prostrated two prostrations after he had given the salutation.'"],[1061,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed five rak'ahs, and it was said to him, 'Has there been an addition to the prayer?' He said, 'No.' Then he prostrated two prostrations."],[1062,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness after the salutation and the speech."],[1063,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness after the speech."],[1064,"The Prophet ﷺ [gave the salutation] in the two prostrations of forgetfulness."],[1065,"'Alqamah led us in Zuhr and prayed five rak'ahs. When he gave the salutation, the people said, 'O Abu Shibl, you have prayed five.' He said, 'Not at all; I did not do that.' They said, 'Yes, you did.' He said, 'I was at the side of the people, and I was a boy, so I said, 'Yes, you have prayed five.' He said to me, 'And you too, O one-eyed, do you say that?' I said, 'Yes.' He turned, prostrated two prostrations, then gave the salutation, then said: 'Abdullah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in five rak'ahs, and when he turned away, the people whispered among themselves. He said, 'What is the matter with you?' They said, 'O Messenger of Allah, has there been an addition to the prayer?' He said, 'No.' They said, 'You have prayed five.' He turned, prostrated two prostrations, then gave the salutation, then said, 'I am only a human being; I forget as you forget.'"],[1066,"The Prophet ﷺ recited the tashahhud in the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then gave the salutation. This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Hatim, which he narrated to us in al-Basrah. He narrated it to us once in Baghdad, and said: The Prophet ﷺ prayed with them, forgot, and prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness after the salutation and the speech. As for Muhammad b. Yahya, he said: The Prophet ﷺ prayed with them, forgot in his prayer, prostrated two prostrations, then recited the tashahhud, then gave the salutation. And Sa'id b. Muhammad said: The Prophet ﷺ prayed with them, prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then recited the tashahhud and gave the salutation."],[1067,"That the Prophet ﷺ named the two prostrations of forgetfulness 'the two humiliating ones.'"],[1068,"We were with al-Mughirah b. Shu'bah, and he was asked: 'Did anyone from this Ummah lead the Prophet ﷺ in prayer other than Abu Bakr?' He said: 'Yes. We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey...' Then he mentioned the hadith at length and said: 'Then we mounted and caught up with the people. 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf had gone ahead and had led them in one rak'ah, and he was in the second. I went to inform him, but he forbade me, so we prayed the rak'ah we caught up with—or made up the one that had preceded us.' Mu'ammal said: 'And we made up the one that had preceded us.'"],[1069,"When the call to prayer is given, do not come to it running. Come to it with tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray; and whatever you miss, complete. For when one of you heads for the prayer, he is in prayer."],[1071,"Witr is not an obligation nor like your prescribed prayers, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Witr and then said: 'O people of the Qur'an, perform Witr, for Allah is Witr (odd) and loves Witr.'"],[1072,"He asked 'Ubadah b. al-Samit about Witr. He said: 'It is a good and beautiful matter which the Prophet ﷺ and the Muslims after him practiced, and it is not obligatory.'"],[1073,"ﷺ in Ramadan eight rak'ahs and the Witr. When the next night came, we gathered in the mosque and hoped that he would come out to us, but we remained in the mosque until morning. Then we entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, we hoped that you would come out to us and lead us in prayer.' He said: 'I disliked that Witr be made obligatory upon you.'"],[1075,"Sufyan narrated to us from 'Amr, from Tawus, who heard it from Ibn 'Umar, and from Ibn Abi Labid, from Abu Salamah, from Ibn 'Umar."],[1076,"What do you think about the two rak'ahs before the dawn prayer? Should I lengthen the recitation in them? He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray at night two by two and perform Witr with one rak'ah."],[1077,"Ibn 'Umar used to perform Witr with one rak'ah. A man came to him and asked him about Witr, and he instructed him to separate it. The man said: I fear that people will say it is the butayra' (the little severed one). Ibn 'Umar said: Do you want the Sunnah of Allah and His Messenger? This is the Sunnah of Allah and His Messenger."],[1078,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ make his riding camel kneel, then he dismounted and prayed ten rak'ahs, and performed Witr with one. He prayed two rak'ahs by two rak'ahs, then performed Witr with one, then prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then led us in the morning prayer."],[1079,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night, performing Witr with five prostrations — meaning rak'ahs — without saying the salam in any of them, then he sat in the last one and said the salam. This is the hadith of Abu Usamah. Bundar said: And he performed Witr from them with five, and did not say the salam except in the last of them."],[1080,"He used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night, performing Witr from them with five, not sitting in any of the five except in the fifth."],[1081,"that he divorced his wife and came to Madinah to sell some property of his there, intending to use the proceeds for weapons and horses and to fight the Romans until he died. He met a group of his people who told him that a group of his people had wanted to do the same during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, but the Prophet ﷺ said, \"Is there not for you in me a good example?\" and forbade them from that. So he called witnesses to his taking back his wife, then he returned to us and informed that he met Ibn 'Abbas and asked him about the Witr. Ibn 'Abbas said: Shall I not tell you of the most knowledgeable person on earth about the Witr of the Messenger of God ﷺ? He said: Yes. He said: 'A'ishah—go to her and ask her, then come back to me and tell me of her reply. He said: So I went to Hakim b. Aflah and asked him to accompany me to her. He said: I will not go near her, for I forbade her from saying anything about these two factions, but she refused to desist. I adjured him, so he came with me and entered upon her. She said: Is it Hakim? She recognized him. He said: Yes—or he said: Indeed. She said: Who is this with you? He said: Sa'd b. Hisham. She said: Who is Hisham? He said: Ibn 'Amir. She then asked for mercy upon him and said: What a good man 'Amir was. I said: O Mother of the Believers, inform me about the Witr of the Messenger of God ﷺ. She said: We used to prepare his toothstick and his water for purification, and God would rouse him at whatever part of the night He willed. He would use the toothstick, perform ablution, then pray eight rak'ahs, not sitting during them except at the eighth, where he would sit, remember God, and supplicate—Harun added in his narration at this point: then he would get up without saying the taslim, then pray the ninth rak'ah, then sit, praise his Lord, and invoke blessings upon his Prophet ﷺ, then say the taslim once so that we could hear him, then pray two rak'ahs while seated. Thus that is eleven rak'ahs, my son. Bundar and Harun both said: When he grew older and gained weight, he would perform Witr with seven and pray two rak'ahs while seated after saying the taslim; thus that is nine rak'ahs, my son."],[1082,"The Prophet ﷺ used to perform Witr with nine rak'ahs. When he grew older and heavier, he would perform Witr with seven, and pray two rak'ahs while seated, reciting in them al-Rahman and al-Waqi'ah."],[1083,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed Witr at every part of the night—at its beginning, its middle, and its end."],[1084,"He asked 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ: How would the Messenger of God ﷺ perform Witr—at the end of the night or at its beginning? She said: He would do all of that; sometimes he would perform Witr at the beginning of the night, and sometimes at its end. I said: Praise be to God who has made the matter flexible."],[1085,"Make the last of your prayer at night a Witr."],[1086,"My beloved ﷺ enjoined upon me three things that I will never abandon, God willing: he enjoined upon me the Duha prayer, Witr before sleeping, and fasting three days of every month."],[1087,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Abu Bakr: \"When do you perform Witr?\" He said: I perform Witr before I sleep. Then he said to 'Umar: \"When do you perform Witr?\" He said: I sleep, then I perform Witr. He said: Then to Abu Bakr he said: \"You have taken the cautious approach,\" or \"the secure approach,\" and to 'Umar he said: \"You have taken the strong approach.\""],[1088,"The Prophet ﷺ said to Abu Bakr: \"When do you perform Witr?\" He said: I perform Witr then sleep. He said: \"You have taken the cautious approach.\" And he asked 'Umar: \"When do you perform Witr?\" He said: I sleep, then I rise during the night and perform Witr. He said: \"You have done as I do.\""],[1089,"Whoever among you fears that he will not wake up at the end of the night, let him perform Witr at its beginning and then sleep. Whoever among you hopes to wake up at the end of the night, let him perform Witr at its end, for the prayer at the end of the night is witnessed, and that is better."],[1090,"Hasten to Witr before dawn breaks."],[1091,"Hasten to Witr before dawn breaks."],[1092,"Perform Witr before morning comes."],[1093,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to glorify Allah on his mount facing whatever direction it took, and he would perform Witr on it, except that he would not pray the obligatory prayer on it."],[1094,"Whoever prays during the night should make the last of his prayer Witr, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that. When dawn comes, all night prayer and Witr have passed, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Witr is before dawn."],[1095,"Whoever is overtaken by morning without having performed Witr, there is no Witr for him."],[1096,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had promised al-Abbas a small herd of camels. He sent me to him after the evening prayer, and he was in the house of Maymunah bt. al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept, and I rested my head on the pillow on which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had rested his head. He slept for a short while, then he ﷺ got up, performed ablution and made it thorough, using a small amount of water. Then he began the prayer. I got up, performed ablution, and stood on his left. He moved me with his hand, took hold of my ear, and made me stand on his right. He would say the taslim after every two rak'ahs. Maymunah was menstruating; she got up, performed ablution, then sat behind him remembering Allah. The Prophet ﷺ said to her: “Did your devil make you get up?” She said: “May my father and mother be ransomed for you, O Messenger of Allah, do I have a devil?” He said: “Yes, by the One who sent me with the truth, I have one too, but Allah helped me against him, so he submitted.” When dawn broke, he got up and performed Witr with one rak'ah, then he prayed the two rak'ahs of dawn, then he lay on his right side until Bilal came and called him to prayer."],[1097,"I went to my maternal aunt... (then he mentioned part of the hadith) and said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to the mosque and stood praying. I stood on his left, and he remained for a short while. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ realized that I wanted to pray following his prayer, he took hold of my forelock and pulled me until he placed me on his right. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed what he had to pray during the night in pairs of rak'ahs, two by two. When the first dawn appeared, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and prayed nine rak'ahs, saying the taslim after every two, and he performed Witr with one, which was the ninth. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ paused until dawn became very bright. Then he got up and prayed the two rak'ahs of dawn. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lay on his side and slept. Then Bilal came."],[1098,"I memorized from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ words that he taught me to say during qunut."],[1099,"I asked al-Hasan b. Ali: What do you remember from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: He used to teach us this supplication: “O Allah, guide me among those You have guided...”"],[1100,"The Prophet ﷺ did not perform qunut except when he was supplicating for a people or against a people. If he wanted to supplicate against a people or for a people, he performed qunut after raising his head from the second rak'ah of the dawn prayer."],[1101,"The Prophet ﷺ performed qunut in Fajr."],[1102,"He used to perform qunut in Maghrib and Subh."],[1103,"Abd al-Rahman b. Abd al-Qari, who was in charge of the treasury with Abdullah b. al-Arqam during the time of Umar b. al-Khattab, reported that Umar went out one night in Ramadan accompanied by Abd al-Rahman b. Abd al-Qari. He toured the mosque, and the people in the mosque were scattered in separate groups: a man praying alone, and a man praying with a small group following his prayer. Umar said: By Allah, I think if we gathered these people behind one reciter, it would be better. Then Umar resolved to do that and ordered Ubayy b. Ka'b to lead them in prayer during Ramadan. Umar came out to them while the people were praying behind their reciter, and he said: This is an excellent innovation, and the one you sleep through is better than the one you stand for—meaning the last part of the night. The people used to stand in the first part of the night, and they used to curse the disbelievers in the middle: O Allah, fight the disbelievers who obstruct Your path, deny Your messengers, and do not believe in Your promise. Divide their unity, cast terror into their hearts, and send upon them Your punishment and torment, O God of Truth. Then he would send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and supplicate for the Muslims with whatever good he could, then he would seek forgiveness for the believers. He said: When he finished cursing the disbelievers, sending blessings upon the Prophet, seeking forgiveness for the believing men and women, and making his requests, he would say: O Allah, You alone we worship, and to You we pray and prostrate, and to You we strive and hasten. We hope for Your mercy, our Lord, and fear Your severe punishment. Indeed, Your punishment will overtake those whom You oppose. Then he would say the takbir and go down into prostration."],[1104,"My father visited us one day during Ramadan and stayed with us, breaking his fast. He led us in prayer that night and prayed Witr with us. Then he went to his own mosque and prayed with his companions until only the Witr remained. Then he brought forward a man from his companions and said: Pray Witr with your companions, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: There are no two Witrs in one night."],[1105,"I asked Aisha about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she said: He used to pray thirteen rak'ahs: he would pray eight rak'ahs, then pray Witr, then pray two rak'ahs while sitting. When he wanted to bow, he would stand and bow. And he would pray two rak'ahs between the call (adhan) and the iqamah. This is the wording of Abu Musa's hadith. Al-Dawraqi said in his hadith: And he would pray Witr with one rak'ah. When he said the salam, he would say the takbir and pray two rak'ahs sitting. And he would pray two rak'ahs between the adhan and the iqamah of Fajr."],[1106,"I visited my maternal aunt Maymunah and coincided with the Prophet's ﷺ night. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up after a long period of the night, performed a thorough ablution, then stood to pray. I got up, performed ablution, then came and stood beside him. When he realized that I wanted to pray with him, he took my hand and moved me to his right. Then he prayed Witr with nine or seven rak'ahs, then prayed two rak'ahs, and lay down on his side until I heard his breathing. Then the prayer was announced, and he went and prayed."],[1107,"He asked Aisha about the Prophet's ﷺ prayer at night. She said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed Isha, he would perform two brief rak'ahs, then sleep with his purification water and tooth-stick near his head. He would get up, use the tooth-stick, perform ablution, and pray, performing two brief rak'ahs. Then he would stand and pray eight rak'ahs, making the recitation equal in them, and pray Witr with the ninth. Then he would pray two rak'ahs while sitting. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew older and put on weight, he made the eight six, and prayed Witr with the seventh, and prayed two rak'ahs while sitting, reciting in them Qul ya ayyuha al-kafirun and Idha zulzilat."],[1108,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray Witr with nine rak'ahs. When he grew older and became heavier, he prayed Witr with seven, and prayed two rak'ahs while sitting, reciting in them al-Rahman, al-Waqi'ah, and the like."],[1109,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and he said: This journey is exhausting and burdensome. So when one of you prays Witr, let him pray two rak'ahs. If he wakes up (for night prayer), then (he may pray more), otherwise those two will be (counted) for him."],[1110,"The two rak'ahs of Fajr are better than the world and all that is in it."],[1111,"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ hasten to any good deed faster than he hastened to the two rak'ahs before Fajr, nor to any booty."],[1112,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ was not more constant in any voluntary prayer than he was in the two rak'ahs before Subh."],[1113,"One night I was between the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and a bedouin. The bedouin said: O Messenger of Allah, how is the night prayer? He ﷺ said: Two by two, and when you fear the Subh, then prostrate one prostration, and prostrate two prostrations before the morning prayer."],[1114,"He used to pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr when the dawn brightened."],[1115,"I said to Ibn 'Umar: Tell me about the two rak'ahs before the morning prayer; should I lengthen the recitation in them? He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray the two rak'ahs before the morning prayer as if the adhan were in his ears."],[1116,"Muhammad b. 'Abd al-Rahman informed me that he heard 'Amrah narrating from 'A'ishah."],[1117,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr and two rak'ahs before 'Asr, not leaving them. She said: And he used to say: \"What excellent two surahs they are, recited in two rak'ahs before Fajr: Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad and Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun.\""],[1118,"The most the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr was: 'Qulu amanna bi-llahi wa ma unzila ilayna wa ma unzila ila Ibrahima' to the end of the verse, and in the other: 'Qul ya ahla al-kitabi ta'alaw ila kalimatin sawa'in baynana wa baynakum' up to His saying: 'Ishhadu bi-anna muslimun.'"],[1119,"He prayed Subh with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and had not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the salam, he stood and prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was looking at him, and he did not disapprove of that."],[1120,"Whoever forgets the two rak'ahs of Fajr, let him pray them when the sun has risen."],[1121,"We stopped for a night's rest with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and did not wake until the sun had risen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Let each person take hold of his mount's head, for this is a stopping place where Satan came to us.\" So we did that. Then he called for water, performed wudu', then prayed two prostrations when the prayer was called, and prayed the morning prayer."],[1122,"Al-Abbas sent me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so I came to him in the evening while he was in the house of my maternal aunt Maymunah bt. al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray at night. When he had prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, he said: O Allah, I ask You for mercy from You with which You guide my heart, gather my affairs, mend my disarray, restore my affection, set right my religion, preserve my absent ones, elevate my present ones, purify my deeds, brighten my face, inspire me with right guidance, and protect me from every evil. O Allah, grant me sincere faith and certainty after which there is no disbelief, and mercy by which I attain the honor of Your generosity in this world and the Hereafter. O Allah, I ask You for success at the time of judgment, the resting place of martyrs, the life of the blessed, the companionship of the prophets, and victory over enemies. O Allah, I bring my need before You, though my judgment is short, my deeds are weak, and I am in need of Your mercy. I ask You, O Decider of affairs, O Healer of hearts, as You separate the seas, to save me from the punishment of the Blaze, from the call of destruction, and from the trial of the graves. O Allah, whatever good my judgment falls short of, my deeds are weak toward, and my intention has not reached—good that You have promised to any of Your servants or good that You will give to any of Your creation—I desire it from You and ask You for it, O Lord of the worlds. O Allah, make us guides who are guided, not astray and not leading astray, at war with Your enemies, at peace with Your allies. We love by Your love the people and we are hostile by Your enmity to those who oppose You. O Allah, this is the supplication, and upon You is the response—or the answer—(Ibn Khalaf was uncertain), and this is the effort, and upon You is the reliance. There is no power and no strength except with Allah. O Allah, Possessor of the strong rope and the right command, I ask You for security on the Day of Warning and Paradise on the Day of Eternity with the near ones who are witnesses, who bow and prostrate, who fulfill the covenants. Indeed, You are Merciful and Loving, and You do what You will. Glory be to the One who has wrapped Himself in might and spoken it. Glory be to the One who has clothed Himself in glory and is generous with it. Glory be to the One to whom glorification is due alone. Glory be to the One who has counted everything and knows it. Glory be to the Possessor of bounty and blessings. Glory be to the Possessor of power and generosity. O Allah, make for me a light in my heart, a light in my grave, a light in my hearing, a light in my sight, a light in my hair, a light in my skin, a light in my flesh, a light in my blood, a light in my bones, a light before me, a light behind me, a light on my right, a light on my left, a light above me, and a light below me. O Allah, increase me in light, grant me light, and make for me a light."],[1123,"When one of you prays the two rak'ahs of Fajr, let him lie down on his right side.\" Marwan b. al-Hakam said to him, \"Is it not enough for one of us to walk to the mosque until he lies down?\" He said, \"No.\" That reached Ibn Umar, and he said, \"Abu Hurayrah has said too much.\" It was said to him, \"Do you reject anything he says?\" He said, \"No, but he was bold while we were timid.\" That reached Abu Hurayrah, and he said, \"What is my sin if I remembered and they forgot?\""],[1124,"I visited my maternal aunt and happened upon the night of the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith and said: Then he prayed two rak'ahs, then lay down until I heard his breathing, then the prayer was called and he went out and prayed."],[1125,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr; if I was awake, he would speak with me."],[1126,"When the iqamah is called, there is no prayer except the obligatory one."],[1127,"The iqamah was called while I had not prayed the two rak'ahs. He saw me praying them and forbade me, pulling me, and said, \"Do you want to pray four for the Subh?\""],[1128,"A man came while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the Subh prayer, and he prayed two rak'ahs. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer he said, \"O so-and-so, which of the two is your prayer: the one you prayed with us or the one you prayed for yourself?\""],[1129,"The Prophet ﷺ came out when the prayer had been commenced (iqamah) and saw people praying two rak'ahs hastily. He said: 'Two prayers together?!' So he forbade praying in the mosque when the iqamah for prayer had been called."],[1130,"O Mother of the Believers, tell me about the character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: 'Do you not recite the Qur'an?'—meaning His saying: 'And indeed, you are of a great moral character.' He said: 'Yes.' She said: 'The character of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the Qur'an.' I said: 'O Mother of the Believers, tell me about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' She said: 'Do you not recite this surah: \"O you wrapped in garments\"?' He said: 'Yes.' She said: 'Allah made night prayer obligatory at the beginning of this surah, so the Prophet of Allah ﷺ and his companions stood in prayer for a year until their feet swelled, and He withheld its concluding part for twelve months in the heaven. Then Allah sent down the relaxation at the end of this surah, and night prayer became voluntary after having been obligatory.' Then they mentioned the hadith, and at the end of the hadith he said: 'I went to Ibn Abbas and told him her hadith, and he said: \"She spoke the truth.\""],[1131,"He came out in the depth of the night and prayed in the mosque, and some men prayed following his prayer. In the morning people talked about that. When it was the third night, the people of the mosque increased in number, and he came out and prayed, and they prayed following his prayer. When it was the fourth night, the mosque could not accommodate its people, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not come out to them. Some men among them began calling out: 'The prayer!' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained concealed until he came out for the Fajr prayer. When he finished the Fajr prayer, he stood up and turned his face toward them, then he recited the tashahhud, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'To proceed: your affair has not been hidden from me, but I feared that the night prayer would be made obligatory upon you, and you would be unable to perform it.'"],[1132,"Do not be like so-and-so; he used to stand in night prayer and then abandoned night prayer."],[1133,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'So-and-so slept last night and missed the prayer (or night prayer).' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'That is a devil who urinated in his ear, or in his ears.'"],[1134,"The devil ties three knots on the back of the head of any of you when he sleeps. With each knot he strikes, saying: 'You have a long night ahead.' If he wakes up and remembers Allah, one knot is loosened. If he performs wudu, two knots are loosened. If he prays, all the knots are loosened, and he wakes up energetic and good in spirit; otherwise he wakes up evil in spirit and lazy."],[1135,"When a servant sleeps, the devil ties three knots on him. If he wakes during the night and remembers Allah, one knot is loosened. If he performs wudu, two knots are loosened. If he prays two rak'ahs, all the knots are loosened. So loosen the knots of the devil, even with two rak'ahs."],[1136,"There is no male or female but has a rope tied on his head when he sleeps. If he wakes up and remembers Allah, one knot is loosened. If he gets up, performs wudu, and prays, the knots are loosened."],[1137,"He was asked: Which prayer is best after the obligatory prayer? And which fasting is best after the month of Ramadan? He said: The best prayer after the obligatory prayer is prayer in the middle of the night, and the best fasting after the month of Ramadan is the month of Allah, al-Muharram."],[1138,"You must observe night prayer, for it is the practice of the righteous before you, it is a means of drawing near to your Lord, an expiation of evil deeds, and a prevention from sin."],[1139,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ felt something one night. When morning came, it was said: O Messenger of Allah, the trace of pain is evident upon you. He said: Indeed, despite what you see, by the praise of Allah, I recited last night the seven long surahs."],[1140,"Do not abandon night prayer, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never used to abandon it. When he was ill or felt lazy, he prayed sitting."],[1141,"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, guided His Prophet to an evidence, so he said to them: Guide me to what Allah guided His Prophet. They said: Allah guided His Prophet to night prayer."],[1142,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me and Fatimah at night and said: Get up and pray. Then he returned to his house. After a portion of the night had passed, he came back and did not hear any movement from us, so he said: Get up and pray. He said: I got up, rubbing my eyes, and said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, we only pray what Allah has decreed for us. Our souls are in Allah's hand; if He wills to raise us, He raises us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned away, striking his hand on his thigh and saying: We only pray what Allah has decreed for us. But man is the most contentious of all things."],[1143,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him and Fatimah bt. the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at night, and said: Will you not pray? I said: O Messenger of Allah, our souls are in Allah's hand; if He wills to raise us, He raises us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left when I said that, and did not reply to me. Then I heard him as he was going away, striking his thigh and saying: But man is the most contentious of all things."],[1144,"Whoever recites the last two verses of Surat al-Baqarah in a night, they will suffice him."],[1145,"Whoever guards these obligatory prayers will not be recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites one hundred verses in a night will not be recorded among the heedless, or he will be recorded among the devout."],[1146,"Whoever prays in a night with one hundred verses will not be recorded among the heedless, and whoever prays in a night with two hundred verses will be recorded among the devout and sincere."],[1147,"Whoever stands in prayer with ten verses will not be recorded among the heedless, whoever stands with one hundred verses will be recorded among the devout, and whoever recites one thousand verses will be recorded among those with abundant rewards."],[1148,"The most beloved prayer to Allah is the prayer of Dawud. He used to sleep half the night, stand in prayer a third of it, and sleep a sixth of it. And the most beloved fasting to Allah is the fasting of Dawud: he used to fast one day and break his fast the next."],[1149,"They testified about the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: \"Indeed Allah grants respite until a third of the night has passed, then He descends and says: 'Is there anyone asking? Is there anyone repenting? Is there anyone seeking forgiveness for a sin?'\" A man said to him: \"Until the break of dawn?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[1150,"I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was encamped at 'Ukaz.\" Then he mentioned the hadith and said: \"So I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, is there any supplication closer than another, or an hour [that is sought or in which it is sought] to be mentioned?' He said: 'Yes. The closest the Lord is to the servant is in the last part of the night. If you can be among those who remember Allah in that hour, then be so.'\""],[1151,"May Allah have mercy on a man who rises at night and prays, and wakes his wife; if she refuses, he sprinkles water on her face. May Allah have mercy on a woman who rises at night and prays, and wakes her husband; if he refuses, she sprinkles water on his face."],[1152,"When the Prophet ﷺ rose at night for tahajjud, he would cleanse his mouth with the tooth-stick."],[1153,"When one of you rises at night, let him begin his prayer with two brief rak'ahs."],[1154,"When the Prophet ﷺ rose at night to perform tahajjud, he would say: \"O Allah, to You belongs praise. You are the Light of the heavens and the earth and all within them. To You belongs praise. You are the Sustainer of the heavens and the earth and all within them. To You belongs praise. You are the King of the heavens and the earth and all within them. To You belongs praise. You are the Truth. Your meeting is true. Your warning is true. The punishment of the grave is true. Paradise is true. Hellfire is true. The Hour is true. The graves are true. Muhammad is true. O Allah, in You I believe, to You I submit, upon You I rely, to You I turn, with You I contend, and to You I appeal for judgment. So forgive me for what I have sent ahead and what I have left behind, what I have concealed and what I have declared. You are the One who brings forward and the One who defers. There is no god but You, and there is no god other than You.\""],[1155,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for tahajjud, he would say after saying the takbir: \"O Allah, to You belongs praise. You are the Light of the heavens and the earth. To You belongs praise. You are the Maintainer of the heavens and the earth. To You belongs praise. You are the Lord of the heavens and the earth and all within them. You are the Truth. Your word is true. Your promise is true. Meeting You is true. Paradise is true. Hellfire is true. The Hour is true. O Allah, to You I submit, in You I believe, upon You I rely, to You I turn, to You I appeal for judgment, and to You I contend; and to You is the final return. O Allah, forgive me for what I have sent ahead and what I have left behind, what I have concealed and what I have declared. You are my God; there is no god but You.\""],[1156,"With what did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ begin his prayer when he rose at night? She said: \"When he rose at night, he would begin his prayer by saying: 'O Allah, Lord of Jibril, Mika'il, and Israfil, Originator of the heavens and the earth, Knower of the unseen and the witnessed, You judge between Your servants concerning that in which they used to differ. Guide me to the truth in that which is disputed, for You guide whom You will to a straight path.'\""],[1157,"I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — in the hadith of al-Thawri: one night — and they said: \"He prolonged it until I thought of doing something bad.\" It was said: \"What did you think of doing?\" He said: \"I thought of sitting down and leaving him.\""],[1158,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: \"Which prayer is most excellent?\" He said: \"Lengthy standing (qunut).\""],[1159,"A man came to 'Umar while he was at 'Arafah and said: \"O Commander of the Faithful, I have come from Kufah and left there a man dictating copies of the Quran from memory.\" 'Umar became angry and swelled up until he almost filled the space between the two sides of the saddle. He said: \"Who is he, woe to you?\" He said: \"'Abd Allah b. Mas'ud.\" 'Umar's anger gradually subsided and abated until he returned to his former state. Then he said: \"Woe to you, I do not know of anyone more deserving of that than him. I will tell you about that. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stay up at night talking with Abu Bakr about some matter of the Muslims' affairs, and one night he stayed up with him while I was with them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out walking, and we went out with him. Suddenly there was a man standing in the mosque praying. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped to listen to his recitation. When we were about to recognize the man, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever would like to recite the Quran fresh as it was revealed, let him recite it according to the recitation of Ibn Umm 'Abd.'\" He said: \"Then the man sat down supplicating, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began saying: 'Ask, and you will be given' twice. 'Umar said: 'I said: By Allah, I will go to him in the morning and give him the good news.' So I went to him in the morning to give him the good news, but I found that Abu Bakr had already preceded me to him and given him the good news. By Allah, I never raced him to any good except that he beat me to it.\" This is the hadith of Abu Musa, except that he did not say \"and swelled up.\" Salm b. Junadah said: \"His anger gradually subsided,\" and he said: \"standing at 'Arafah,\" and he did not say \"used to,\" and he said: \"listening to his recitation,\" and he said: \"'Umar said: By Allah, I will go to him in the morning.\""],[1160,"I asked Ibn Abbas: What was the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ at night like? He said: He would recite in some of his chambers, and those outside would hear him."],[1161,"He asked Umm Salamah about the recitation of the Messenger of God ﷺ and his prayer. She said: What is it to you and his prayer? He would pray, then sleep for as long as he had prayed, then pray for as long as he had slept, then sleep for as long as he had prayed until morning. She described his recitation to him, and she described it as a recitation articulated distinctly, letter by letter."],[1162,"When he would stand at night, he would sometimes raise his voice and sometimes lower it, and he mentioned that the Messenger of God ﷺ would do that."],[1163,"Abd Allah b. Abi Qays narrated to him that he asked Aisha: How was the recitation of the Messenger of God ﷺ at night? Did he recite aloud or quietly? She said: He did all of that. Sometimes he recited aloud and sometimes he recited quietly. Bahr added in his narration: I said: Praise be to God who has made the matter spacious."],[1164,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by Abu Bakr while he was praying, lowering his voice, and he passed by Umar praying, raising his voice. When they gathered with the Prophet ﷺ, he said to Abu Bakr: O Abu Bakr, I passed by you while you were praying, lowering your voice. He said: I made myself heard by the One I was confiding in. And I passed by you, O Umar, while you were raising your voice. He said: O Messenger of God, I sought thereby to awaken the drowsy one. He said to Abu Bakr: Raise your voice a little, and to Umar: Lower your voice."],[1165,"The Prophet ﷺ was in i'tikaf in the mosque, and he heard them reciting aloud. Abd al-Rahman added: while he was in his tent. They both said: He drew back the curtains and said: Indeed, each of you is confiding in his Lord, so let none of you harm one another, and let not some of you raise their voices over others in recitation. Muhammad said: or 'in prayer.'"],[1166,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would fast until we would say: He does not intend to break his fast, and he would break his fast until we would say: He does not intend to fast. And he would recite every night Bani Isra'il and al-Zumar."],[1167,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would pray thirteen rak'ahs at night."],[1168,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed thirteen rak'ahs after the night prayer."],[1169,"He asked Aisha: How was the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ in Ramadan? She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ would not exceed eleven rak'ahs in Ramadan or otherwise. He would pray four, and do not ask about their excellence and length; then he would pray four, and do not ask about their excellence and length; then he would pray three. Aisha said: I said: O Messenger of God, do you sleep before performing witr? He said: O Aisha, my eyes sleep, but my heart does not sleep."],[1170,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would pray nine rak'ahs at night, including the witr."],[1171,"He entered upon 'A'ishah and asked her about the night prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: 'He used to pray thirteen rak'ahs at night. Then he prayed eleven rak'ahs, leaving off two rak'ahs. Then he was taken (in death) while he was praying nine rak'ahs at night, the last of his night prayer being the witr. Then sometimes he would come to this bed of his, and Bilal would come to him and announce the prayer.'"],[1172,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer, he would persist in it. And when he slept at night or fell ill, he prayed twelve rak'ahs during the day."],[1173,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer, he loved to keep it up. And when sleep, illness, or pain kept him from the night vigil, he prayed twelve rak'ahs during the day."],[1174,"Salamah narrated to me, from 'Uqayl. Ibn Shihab said: And al-Sa'ib b. Yazid and 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Abd Allah informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Abd al-Qari said: I heard 'Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the same as it exactly."],[1175,"Whoever comes to his bed intending to get up and pray at night, but his eyes overcome him until morning, what he intended is recorded for him, and his sleep is a charity from his Lord upon him."],[1176,"Whoever tells himself to pray a part of the night but his eyes overcome him and he sleeps, his sleep is a charity upon him, and the like of what he intended to pray is recorded for him."],[1177,"There is no man who has a portion of the night in which he stands (in prayer), and then he sleeps through it, except that Allah records for him the reward of his prayer, and his sleep is a charity given to him."],[1178,"There is no servant who intends to pray, and on one occasion he said: at night, then forgets and sleeps, except that his sleep is a charity upon him from Allah, and what he intended is recorded for him."],[1179,"Do not single out Friday for fasting among the days, and do not single out the night of Friday for night vigil among the nights."],[1180,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer, he loved to keep it up. And I do not know that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ ever recited the whole Quran in a night, nor stood in prayer until morning, nor fasted a complete month other than Ramadan. So I went to Ibn 'Abbas and narrated her hadith to him. He said: She spoke the truth. As for me, if I had entered upon her, I would have gone to her so that she could tell me directly."],[1181,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did an action, he would persist in it. She said: 'And I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stand in prayer for a night until morning, nor fast a month consecutively except Ramadan.'"],[1182,"One day I went out walking for a need, and there was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walking. I thought he intended a need, so I started to keep away from him. I kept doing that until he saw me, then he gestured to me, so I came to him. He took my hand and we set off walking together. Then we came upon a man in front of us praying, performing many bowings and prostrations. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"Do you think he is showing off?\" I said, \"Allah and His Messenger know best.\" He said, \"Then he released his hand and clasped his hands together three times, raising his hands and lowering them, saying: 'Upon you is a moderate way, upon you is a moderate way, upon you is a moderate way; for whoever contends with this religion will be overcome by it.'\""],[1183,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque while a rope was stretched between two pillars. He said, \"What is this?\" They said, \"It is for Zaynab; she prays, and when she becomes tired or weak, she holds onto it.\" He said, \"Untie it.\" Then he said, \"Let one of you pray as long as he is energetic, and when he becomes tired or weak, let him sit.\""],[1184,"They said: \"It belongs to Maymunah bt. al-Harith.\" He said, \"What does she do with it?\" They said, \"She prays standing, and when she becomes exhausted, she leans on it.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ untied it and said, \"Let one of you pray as long as he is energetic, and when he becomes exhausted, let him sit.\""],[1185,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed until his feet became swollen. It was said to him, \"Do you burden yourself with this, O Messenger of Allah, when you have been forgiven?\" He said, \"Should I not be a grateful servant?\""],[1186,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed until his feet swelled. It was said to him, \"Allah has forgiven you your past and future sins.\" He said, \"Should I not be a grateful servant?\""],[1187,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stand until his feet became swollen. It was said to him, \"O Messenger of Allah, do you do this when it has come to you from Allah that He has forgiven you your past and future sins?\" He said, \"Should I not be a grateful servant?\""],[1188,"Whoever prays twelve rak'ahs in a day voluntarily, other than the obligatory prayer, a house will be built for him in Paradise."],[1189,"Whoever prays for Allah every day"],[1190,"Whoever prays twelve prostrations in a day voluntarily, a house will be built for him in Paradise. 'Anbasah said, \"I have not abandoned them since I heard them from Umm Habibah.\" 'Amr b. Aws said, \"I have not abandoned them since I heard them from 'Anbasah.\" Al-Nu'man said, \"I have not abandoned them since I heard them from 'Amr.\" Dawud said, \"As for us, we pray and we abandon.\""],[1191,"Whoever prays twelve rak'ahs in a day, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise: four rak'ahs before Zuhr, two rak'ahs after Zuhr, two rak'ahs before 'Asr, two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and two rak'ahs before Subh."],[1192,"Whoever prays twelve rak'ahs, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise: four before Zuhr, two after it, two rak'ahs before 'Asr, two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and two rak'ahs before Fajr."],[1193,"When death came to him, he suffered severe pain. He said, \"My sister Umm Habibah bt. Abi Sufyan told me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whoever guards four rak'ahs—' and Ibn Ma'mar said: 'Whoever prays four rak'ahs before Zuhr and four after it, Allah will forbid him to the Fire.'\""],[1194,"Whoever maintains four rak'ahs before the noon (al-hajīr) prayer and four after it, Hell is forbidden for him."],[1195,"Al-Nu'mān—meaning Ibn al-Mundhir—reported to us, from Makhūl, from 'Anbasah, from Umm Habībah, that she reported to him that the Messenger of God ﷺ said the exact same thing."],[1196,"May God have mercy on a person who prays four rak'ahs before the 'Asr prayer."],[1197,"He prayed the Maghrib prayer with the Prophet ﷺ, then he prayed until he prayed the 'Ishā' prayer."],[1198,"Whoever prays six rak'ahs after Maghrib, not speaking between them except with the remembrance of God, they will be equivalent for him to twelve years of worship."],[1199,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray two rak'ahs after every obligatory prayer except Fajr and 'Asr."],[1200,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ two rak'ahs before Zuhr, two rak'ahs after it, two rak'ahs after Maghrib in his house, and two rak'ahs after 'Ishā' in his house.\" —This is the end of Ahmad's hadith, and Mu'ammal added— He said: And Hafsah narrated to me, and it was a time when no one would enter upon him. She said: \"He used to pray two rak'ahs until dawn rose, and the caller would call to prayer.\" He said: I think he said: \"Light (rak'ahs), and two rak'ahs after Jumu'ah in his house.\""],[1201,"He used to pray two rak'ahs before Zuhr, two rak'ahs after it, two rak'ahs after Maghrib, and two rak'ahs after 'Ishā'.\" Ibn 'Umar said: And Hafsah mentioned to me—and I did not see it—that he used to pray two rak'ahs when dawn rose."],[1202,"I asked 'Ā'ishah about the Messenger of God's ﷺ supererogatory prayer. She said: He used to pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr in my house, then go out and lead the people in prayer, then return to my house and pray two rak'ahs. He used to lead the people in Maghrib, then return to my house and pray two rak'ahs. Then he would lead them in 'Ishā', then enter my house and pray two rak'ahs. He used to pray nine rak'ahs at night, including the Witr. And when dawn rose, he would pray two rak'ahs, then go out and lead the people in the Fajr prayer."],[1203,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to Banū 'Abd al-Ashhal and prayed Maghrib with them. When he finished, he said: Perform these two rak'ahs in your homes. He said: I saw Mahmūd, who was the imam of his people, lead them in Maghrib, then go out and sit in the courtyard of the mosque until he rose shortly before 'Atamah, then enter the house and pray those two."],[1204,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the Maghrib prayer in the mosque of Banū 'Abd al-Ashhal. When he prayed, some people stood up to offer supererogatory prayers. The Prophet ﷺ said: Perform this prayer in the homes."],[1205,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about prayer in my house and prayer in the mosque. He said: \"You can see how close my house is to the mosque. And for me to pray in my house is dearer to me than to pray in the mosque, except for the obligatory prayer.\""],[1206,"The best prayer of a person is in his house, except for the obligatory prayer."],[1207,"So pray in your homes, O people, for the best prayer of a person is in his house, except for the obligatory prayer."],[1208,"Make some of your prayers in your homes, and do not make them graves."],[1209,"When one of you finishes his prayer in the mosque, let him give his house a share of his prayer, for Allah will place good in his house because of his prayer."],[1210,"Honor your homes with some of your prayers."],[1211,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ said to Bilal at the time of the Fajr prayer: \"O Bilal, tell me of the most hopeful deed you have done that in your view is of benefit in Islam, for I heard last night the rustle of your sandals before me in Paradise.\" He said: \"I have not done any deed in Islam [that I consider more hopeful of] benefit than that I have never performed a complete purification at any time of night or day except that I prayed with that purification to my Lord as much as was decreed for me to pray.\""],[1212,"One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up and called Bilal and said: \"O Bilal, how have you preceded me to Paradise? I entered Paradise last night and heard the rustling of your footsteps before me.\" Bilal said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I have never committed a sin except that I prayed two rak'ahs, and I have never been afflicted by ritual impurity except that I performed ablution at that time.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Because of this.\""],[1213,"The prayer of the night and the day is two rak'ahs at a time."],[1214,"The Prophet ﷺ, when the sun was here like its position at 'Asr, would pray two rak'ahs, and when it was here like its position here at Zuhr, he would pray four. He would pray four before Zuhr and two after it, four before 'Asr, and he would separate between every two rak'ahs by saying the salutation upon the angels brought near and those of the Muslims who followed them."],[1215,"Prayer is two by two, and you perform the tashahhud in every two rak'ahs, and you humble yourself, and you abase yourself, and you raise your hands in supplication, and you say: 'O Allah, O Allah.' Whoever does not do that, it is defective."],[1216,"Prayer is two by two."],[1217,"Waki' narrated to us, from 'Ubaydah b. Mu'attib al-Dabbi, from Ibrahim, from Sahm b. Minjab, from Qaza'ah, from al-Qarth', from Abu Ayyub, from the Prophet."],[1218,"Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Musayyib b. Rafi', from a man from the Ansar, from Abu Ayyub. Abu Bakr said: \"I do not know this 'Ali b. al-Salt, and I do not know from which of Allah's lands he is, and I do not know whether he met Abu Ayyub or not. No one would use such chains of transmission as evidence—to my knowledge—except a stubborn person or an ignorant one.\""],[1219,"Ibrahim b. al-Hakam narrated to us:"],[1220,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came one day from al-'Aliyah, and when he passed by the mosque of Banu Mu'awiyah, he entered and prayed two rak'ahs in it, and we prayed with him. He called upon his Lord for a long time, then turned to us and said: 'I asked my Lord for three things. He gave me two and withheld one. I asked my Lord not to destroy my community by drought, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to destroy my community by drowning, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to make their might be against one another, and He withheld it from me.'"],[1221,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out, and I went out searching for him, asking everyone I passed by, and they said: 'He passed before.' Until I passed and found him praying. I waited for him until he finished, and he had prolonged the prayer. I said: 'I saw you prolong it such that I have not seen you pray it like this.' He said: 'I prayed a prayer of hope and fear. I asked Allah for three things. He gave me two and withheld one. I asked Him not to destroy my community by drowning, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to set an enemy from other than them over them, and He granted it to me. I asked Him not to cast their might among themselves, and He refused me.'"],[1222,"A blind man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'Supplicate Allah to heal me.' He said: 'If you wish, I will delay that, and it is better, and if you wish, I will supplicate.' Abu Musa said: He said: 'Then supplicate.' They said: So he commanded him to perform ablution—Bundar said: 'and to do it well'—and they said: 'and to pray two rak'ahs and to supplicate with this supplication: \"O Allah, I ask You and turn to You by Your Prophet Muhammad, the Prophet of mercy. O Muhammad, I have turned by you to my Lord in this need of mine so that it may be fulfilled. O Allah, accept his intercession for me.\"' Abu Musa added: 'and accept my intercession for him.' He said: Then it was as if he later doubted 'and accept my intercession for him.'"],[1223,"Conceal the proposal. Then perform wudu and make your wudu well. Then pray what Allah has decreed for you. Then praise your Lord and glorify Him. Then say: O Allah, You have power and I have no power. You know and I do not know. You are the Knower of the unseen. If You see that [such-and-such woman]—naming her by name—is good for me in my religion, my worldly life, and my hereafter, then decree her for me. And if someone other than her is better for me than her in my religion, my worldly life, and my hereafter, then decree that for me—or he said: decree her for me."],[1224,"My close friend ﷺ instructed me with three things that I will never abandon, if Allah wills, ever: he instructed me to pray the Duha prayer, to pray Witr before sleeping, and to fast three days of every month."],[1225,"My close friend ﷺ instructed me with three things: to fast three days of every month, not to sleep except after praying Witr, and to pray two rak'ahs of Duha."],[1226,"My close friend ﷺ instructed me with three things that I will not abandon: not to sleep except after praying Witr, not to leave the two rak'ahs of Duha, for it is the prayer of the Awwabin, and to fast three days of every month."],[1227,"No one consistently observes the Duha prayer except an awwab (one who often turns to Allah). He said: \"And it is the prayer of the Awwabin.\""],[1228,"Each of you wakes up in the morning with charity due on every joint of his body. Every tahlil (saying la ilaha illallah), every tahmid (saying alhamdulillah), every takbir (saying Allahu akbar), and every tasbih (saying subhanallah) is charity. Enjoining good and forbidding evil is charity. And two rak'ahs of Duha suffice for all of that."],[1229,"In the human being there are three hundred and sixty joints. It is incumbent upon him to give charity for every joint. He asked: Who can do that, O Prophet of Allah? He said: \"A phlegm in the mosque that you bury, or something you remove from the road. If you are unable, then two rak'ahs of Duha suffice you.\""],[1230,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to some people while they were praying Duha in the mosque of Quba when the sun had risen high. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The prayer of the Awwabin is when the young camels feel the heat of the sun.\""],[1231,"'Amr—meaning Ibn al-Harith—informed me, from Bukayr, from al-Dahhak al-Qurashi, from Anas."],[1232,"The Prophet ﷺ used not to pray Duha except when returning from a journey."],[1233,"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray the Duha prayer at all, except when he returned from a journey, and he would pray two rak'ahs."],[1235,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray the Duha prayer."],[1236,"The Prophet ﷺ entered upon her on the day of the conquest of Makkah, and he performed ghusl and prayed eight rak'ahs. I never saw him pray a lighter prayer than that, except that he completed the bowing and the prostration."],[1237,"On the day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed eight rak'ahs of Duha prayer, he would give the taslim after every two rak'ahs."],[1238,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came after the day had risen on the day of the conquest, and he ordered for a garment to be hung as a screen for him, and he performed ghusl. Then he stood and prayed eight rak'ahs. I do not know whether his standing in them was longer, or his bowing, or his prostration; all of that was close in length.\" She said: \"I did not see him pray that prayer before or after."],[1239,"I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man praying while sitting, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The prayer of the standing person is superior, and the prayer of the sitting person is half of the prayer of the standing person.\""],[1240,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying while sitting. I said: I was told that you say: \"The prayer of the sitting person is half of the prayer of the standing person.\" He said: \"Yes, but I am not like any one of you.\""],[1241,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying cross-legged."],[1242,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not die until most of his prayer was performed sitting. Ibn Rafi' and Ibn Sudran said: until much of his prayer was performed while he was sitting."],[1243,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray while sitting after he had reached old age, and when thirty or forty verses remained of the surah, he would stand and recite them, then bow."],[1244,"I said to ʿĀʾishah: Would the Messenger of God ﷺ pray sitting? She said: “After the people had broken him down.”"],[1245,"I never saw the Messenger of God ﷺ pray his voluntary prayer sitting until one year before his death. Then he used to pray his voluntary prayer sitting, and he would recite the surah at a measured pace, so that it became longer than the longest of them."],[1246,"The Prophet ﷺ used to pray sitting, and when thirty or forty verses of the surah remained for him, he would stand and recite them, then bow."],[1247,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite while sitting, and when he wanted to bow, he would stand for as long as it takes a person to recite forty verses."],[1248,"I asked ʿĀʾishah about the Messenger of God’s ﷺ voluntary prayer. She said: He used to pray long portions of the night standing and long portions of the night sitting. When he recited while standing, he bowed and prostrated while standing; and when he recited while sitting, he bowed and prostrated while sitting."],[1249,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray long portions of the night standing. When he prayed standing, he bowed standing; and when he prayed sitting, he bowed sitting."],[1250,"He asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ while sitting. She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray long portions of the night standing. When he prayed sitting, he bowed sitting; and when he prayed standing, he bowed standing."],[1251,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray standing and sitting. When he began the prayer standing, he bowed standing; and when he began the prayer sitting, he bowed sitting."],[1252,"The prayer of one lying down is half of the prayer of one sitting."],[1253,"I had hemorrhoids, so I asked the Prophet ﷺ about prayer. He said: “Pray standing; if you cannot, then sitting; and if you cannot, then on your side.” In the hadith of Ibn al-Mubārak, he said: I had hemorrhoids."],[1255,"We stopped for a rest with the Messenger of God ﷺ and did not wake up until the sun had risen. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: “Let each person take hold of his mount’s head, for this is a place where Satan came to us and made us heedless.” He called for water, performed ablution, then prayed two prostrations. Then the prayer was announced and he prayed the morning prayer."],[1256,"I traveled with the Prophet ﷺ on eighteen journeys, and I never saw the Messenger of God ﷺ abandon the two rakʿahs when the sun declines from its meridian."],[1257,"I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ while resident and while traveling. I prayed with him while resident: Zuhr as four rak'ahs, and after it two rak'ahs; 'Asr as four rak'ahs, with nothing after it; Maghrib as three, and after it two rak'ahs; 'Isha' as four, and after it two rak'ahs; and Fajr as two, and before it two rak'ahs. And I prayed with him while traveling: Zuhr as two rak'ahs, and after it two rak'ahs; 'Asr as two rak'ahs, with nothing after it; Maghrib as three, and after it two rak'ahs. And he said: \"It is the witr of the day; it is not shortened whether resident or traveling.\" And 'Isha' as two rak'ahs, and after it two rak'ahs; and Fajr as two, and before it two rak'ahs."],[1258,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not praying before it or after it during travel."],[1259,"He saw Hafs b. 'Asim praying voluntary prayers during travel, and 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar was with them on that journey. It was said: \"A brother of yours forbids this.\" So I asked Ibn 'Umar about that, and he said: \"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not doing that; he did not pray before the prayer or after it.\" I said: \"Should I pray at night?\" He said: \"Pray at night as much as you wish.\""],[1260,"I was with Ibn 'Umar on a journey, and he prayed Zuhr and 'Asr as two rak'ahs each. Then he went to a rug of his and saw some people praying voluntary prayers. He said: \"What are these people doing?\" I said: \"They are praying voluntary prayers.\" He said: \"If I were to pray before it or after it, I would have completed it. I accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he passed away, and he did not add more than two rak'ahs, and Abu Bakr, 'Umar, and 'Uthman likewise.\""],[1261,"'Abd Allah b. 'Umar would not pray a single prostration of voluntary prayer during travel before the obligatory prayer or after it until he stood in the middle of the night, and he would not abandon standing in the middle of the night."],[1262,"He asked 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar about his abandoning voluntary prayer during travel, and 'Abd Allah said to him: \"If I were to pray voluntary prayers, I would not mind completing the prayer.\" Al-Zuhri said: \"I said to Salim: 'Did you ask 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar about what Hafs b. 'Asim asked him?' Salim said: 'No, we used to be in awe of him regarding some questions.'\""],[1263,"The Prophet ﷺ would not stop at a place without bidding it farewell with two rak'ahs."],[1264,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ make his mount kneel, then he dismounted and prayed ten rak'ahs and performed witr with one. He prayed two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, then performed witr with one. Then he prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, then he led us in the morning prayer."],[1265,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray voluntary prayers on the mount facing whatever direction it turned, and he would perform witr on it, but he would not pray the obligatory prayer on it."],[1266,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray during travel wherever his mount turned with him. When he wanted to pray the obligatory prayer or witr, he would make it kneel and pray on the ground."],[1267,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray wherever his mount turned with him."],[1268,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying on his mount in whatever direction it turned with him."],[1269,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying on his mount while facing toward Tabuk."],[1270,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to pray on his mount while facing away from Makkah, and then it was revealed: 'So wherever you turn, there is the face of God.'"],[1271,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ praying on a donkey — or on a female donkey — while he was facing toward Khaybar."],[1272,"This verse was only revealed: 'So wherever you turn, there is the face of God' — that you may pray in whatever direction your mount turns with you during travel. When the Messenger of God ﷺ returned from Makkah, he would pray on his mount as a voluntary prayer, gesturing with his head toward Madinah."],[1273,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ while he was on his mount praying the voluntary prayers in every direction, but he would lower the two prostrations from the two rak'ahs and gesture with a nod."],[1274,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade prayer during two times: after the afternoon prayer until the sun sets, and after the dawn prayer until the sun rises."],[1275,"I heard more than one of the companions of the Prophet ﷺ, among them Umar, who was one of the most beloved of them to me, that the Messenger of God ﷺ forbade prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and after the afternoon prayer until the sun sets."],[1276,"Do not deliberately seek your prayer at sunrise or sunset, for it sets between the two horns of a devil.\" And the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"When the edge of the sun appears, then withhold from prayer until it becomes fully visible; and when the edge of the sun disappears, then withhold from prayer until it has completely set.\" This is the hadith of Bundar. Abu Kurayb said: \"For it rises with the two horns of a devil.\""],[1277,"Do not pray when the sun rises and do not pray when it sets, for it rises between the two horns of a devil and sets between the two horns of a devil."],[1278,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of God, is there any hour of the hours of night and day that you would command me not to pray in?' The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Yes, when you have prayed the dawn prayer, then refrain from prayer until the sun rises' — and Ibn Abd al-Hakam said: 'until the sun has risen high, for it rises between the two horns of the devil — then prayer is witnessed, attended, and accepted until midday. When it is midday, then refrain from prayer until the sun declines; for at that time Hell is kindled, and the intensity of the heat is from the blaze of Hell. When the sun declines, then prayer is attended, witnessed, and accepted until you pray the afternoon prayer. When you have prayed the afternoon prayer, then refrain from prayer until the sun sets.'"],[1280,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me after 'Asr and prayed two rak'ahs. I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, what prayer is this? You were not accustomed to pray it!' He said, 'A delegation from Banu Tamim arrived and distracted me from the two rak'ahs that I used to pray after Zuhr.'"],[1281,"He asked 'A'ishah about the two prostrations that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray after 'Asr in her house. She said, 'He used to pray them before 'Asr, but then he was distracted from them or forgot them, so he prayed them after 'Asr, and then he kept to them. Whenever he prayed a prayer, he would keep to it.'"],[1282,"The Prophet ﷺ said to two men after finishing the Fajr prayer: 'When you have prayed in your dwellings, then you come while the imam is praying, then pray with him; it will be a voluntary prayer for you.'"],[1283,"We were informed by 'Abd al-Jabbar b. al-'Ala' and Ahmad b. Mani', who said: We were informed by Sufyan, from Abu al-Zubayr, from 'Abd Allah b. Babah, from Jubayr b. Mut'im, h."],[1284,"I asked the Mother of the Believers 'A'ishah, saying, 'O Mother of the Believers, how was the work of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? Did he single out any particular day?' She said, 'No. His work was continuous, and which of you is able to do what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was able to do?'"],[1285,"There was with me a woman from Banu Asad, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and said, 'Who is this?' I said, 'So-and-so; she mentions her prayer.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Enough. Upon you is what you are able to do, for by Allah, Allah does not tire until you tire.' She said, 'The most beloved religion to him was that upon which its doer perseveres.'"],[1286,"The most beloved deed to the Prophet ﷺ was that which was done persistently, even if little. And whenever the Prophet ﷺ prayed a prayer, he would persist in it."],[1287,"No prayer is to be performed after 'Asr unless the sun is white and raised high."],[1288,"Do not pray after 'Asr unless you pray while the sun is raised high."],[1289,"We were informed by Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim – who is b. Damrah – from 'Ali, from the Prophet ﷺ, with the like of the hadith of Abu Musa exactly. Sufyan said, 'I do not know whether he meant in Makkah or elsewhere.' Abu Bakr said, 'This is a strange hadith. I heard Muhammad b. Yahya say: Wahb b. al-Ajda' has had the label of obscurity lifted from him; al-Sha'bi and Hilal b. Yasaf also narrated from him.'"],[1290,"Between every two calls to prayer (adhan), there is a prayer; between every two calls to prayer, there is a prayer.\" Then he said the third time: \"for whoever wishes.\""],[1291,"When the mu'adhdhin gave the call to prayer (adhan), some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand and hasten to the pillars, praying until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out, and they were still praying the two rak'ahs before Maghrib. There was nothing between the adhan and the iqamah."],[1292,"Pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib.\" Then he said: \"Pray two rak'ahs before Maghrib.\" Then he said the third time: \"for whoever wishes.\""],[1293,"I and my father were sitting on the road, and he would present the Qur'an to me and I would present it to him. He said: He recited the prostration verse and prostrated. I said to him: Do you prostrate on the road? He said: Yes. I heard Abu Dharr saying: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Which mosque was placed on earth first? He said: \"The Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram).\" I said: Then which? He said: \"Then the farthest mosque (al-Masjid al-Aqsa).\" I said: How much was between them? He said: \"Forty years.\" Then he said: \"Wherever the prayer overtakes you, pray, for it is a mosque.\""],[1294,"Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise."],[1295,"Whoever digs a well, no thirsty liver from jinn, human, or bird drinks from it except that Allah will reward him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever builds a mosque like a qata's nest or smaller, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise."],[1296,"The most beloved lands to Allah are their mosques, and the most hated lands to Allah are their markets."],[1297,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded the construction of mosques in dwellings."],[1298,"The Prophet ﷺ scraped it with his hand - meaning the phlegm or spit - then smeared it with saffron; he called for it.\" He said: Therefore saffron was made in mosques."],[1299,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque, and his face reddened. A woman from the Ansar came and scraped it off, then put perfume (khaluq) in its place. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"How excellent this is!\""],[1300,"The rewards of my nation were shown to me, even the speck that a man removes from the mosque. And the sins of my nation were shown to me, and I did not see a sin greater than a surah of the Qur'an or a verse that a man was given and then forgot."],[1301,"I said to Ibn Umar: What is the origin of this pebble in the mosque? He said: We were rained upon at night, so we came to the mosque for prayer. He said: A man would carry pebbles in his garment and throw them, then pray on them. When morning came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"What is this?\" They told him, and he said: \"What an excellent carpet this is.\" He said: So the people adopted it.\" He said: I said: What was the origin of this saffron? He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came for the dawn prayer and found phlegm in the qiblah of the mosque. He scraped it and said: \"How ugly this is.\" He said: The man who had spat came and scraped it, then smeared saffron over it... He said: \"This is better than that.\" He said: I said: What about one of us when he relieves himself and looks at it when he gets up from it? He said: \"The angel says to him: Look at what you were stingy with and what it has become.\""],[1302,"A black woman used to sweep the mosque, and she died. The Messenger of God ﷺ missed her, and after some days he asked about her. It was said to him, \"She has died.\" He said, \"Why did you not inform me?\" Then he went to her grave and prayed over her."],[1303,"A woman used to pick up rags and sticks from the mosque."],[1304,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah—who is Ibn Marthad—from Sulayman b. Buraydah, from his father, [that he said:]"],[1305,"Whoever hears a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, let him say to him, \"May God not return it to you,\" for mosques were not built for this."],[1306,"Ibn Mas'ud heard a man announcing a lost animal in the mosque, so he became angry and insulted him. A man said to him, \"You were not one to use obscene language, O Ibn Mas'ud.\" He said, \"We were commanded to do this.\""],[1307,"He prohibited buying and selling in the mosque, reciting poetry in it, announcing lost items in it, and sitting in circles on Friday before the prayer."],[1308,"If you see someone selling or buying in the mosque, say, \"May God not make your trade profitable,\" and if you see someone announcing a lost item in it, say, \"May God not return it to you.\""],[1309,"The Prophet ﷺ prohibited selling and buying, announcing lost items, reciting poetry, and sitting in circles for conversation on Friday before the prayer."],[1310,"Umar passed by Hassan while he was reciting poetry in the mosque and glanced at him. Hassan said, \"I used to recite poetry in it when there was someone better than you.\" Then he turned to Abu Hurayrah and said, \"I ask you by God, did you hear the Messenger of God ﷺ say, 'Respond on my behalf. O God, support him with the Holy Spirit'?\" He said, \"Yes.\""],[1311,"The deeds of my community were presented to me, their good and their bad. I found among their good deeds the removal of harm from the path, and I found among their bad deeds sputum in the mosque that is not buried."],[1312,"Spittle in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is burying it."],[1313,"Whoever enters this mosque and spits or expectorates in it, let him dig deep into it and put it far away; let him bury it. If he does not do so, then let him spit into his garment and then leave with it."],[1314,"When one of you expectorates in the mosque, let him conceal his sputum so that it does not strike the skin or garment of a believer and harm him."],[1315,"Whoever expectorates toward the qiblah of the mosque will be resurrected with it on his face."],[1316,"The one who expectorates toward the qiblah will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection with it on his face."],[1317,"Whoever spits toward the qiblah will come on the Day of Resurrection with his spit between his eyes."],[1318,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ scraped off spittle that was in the qiblah of the mosque."],[1319,"The Prophet ﷺ said to a man who passed by with arrows in the mosque: \"Hold them by their heads.\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[1320,"He commanded a man who was distributing arrows in charity in the mosque not to pass with them unless he was holding them by their heads."],[1321,"\"When one of you passes through our mosque or our market and has arrows with him, let him hold their heads with his palm so that nothing of them strikes any Muslim,\" or he said: \"Let him grasp their heads.\""],[1322,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade pecking like a crow, spreading out like a beast of prey, and a man making a place or station habitual for himself as a camel makes it habitual — meaning in the mosque."],[1323,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to a group of the Ansar while they were building a mosque, and he said to them: \"Expand it and you will fill it.\""],[1324,"We set out with Anas intending to go to al-Zawiya. He said: We passed by a mosque and the time for the morning prayer came. Anas said: If only we prayed in this mosque, for some of the people go to the other mosque! They said: Which mosque? We mentioned a mosque. He said: Indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A time will come upon people when they will vie with one another in [building] mosques, but they will not attend them except rarely — or he said — they will attend them rarely."],[1325,"Indeed among the signs of the Hour is that people will vie with one another in [building] mosques."],[1326,"The Hour will not come until people vie with one another in [building] mosques."],[1327,"The mosque in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was built of mud bricks, its roof was of palm branches, and its pillars were of palm trunks. Abu Bakr did not add anything to it. Umar added to it and built it upon its original construction from the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with mud bricks and palm branches, and he replaced its pillars with wood. Then Uthman changed it, adding greatly to it, building its walls with carved stones and plaster, making its pillars of carved stones, and its roof of teak. Muhammad b. Yahya said: \"And its pillars were of palm trunks.\""],[1328,"When one of you enters the mosque, he should not sit down until he prays two rak'ahs."],[1329,"A man met 'Abdullah and said: Peace be upon you, O Ibn Mas'ud. 'Abdullah said: Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Among the signs of the Hour is that a man will pass through the mosque without praying two rak'ahs in it, that a man will not greet except the one he knows, and that a child will be made to grow old by an old person."],[1330,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came while the entrances of his companions' houses opened into the mosque, and he said: \"Turn these houses away from the mosque.\" Then the Prophet ﷺ entered, but the people did nothing, hoping that a concession would be revealed for them regarding that. He later came out to them and said: Turn these houses away from the mosque, for I do not permit the mosque for a menstruating woman or a person in a state of major ritual impurity."],[1331,"The delegation of Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he lodged them in the mosque so that their hearts might be softened."],[1332,"I heard Jabir b. 'Abdullah saying regarding His [Exalted] saying: 'The polytheists are impure, so let them not approach the Sacred Mosque after this year of theirs.' He said: \"Unless he is a slave or one of the people of the covenant.\""],[1333,"I used to spend the night in the mosque during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while I was a bachelor."],[1334,"One of us would pass through the mosque while he was in a state of major ritual impurity, merely crossing through."],[1335,"A black slave girl belonged to a clan of the Arabs. They manumitted her, and she remained with them. One day a young girl of theirs went out wearing a red leather ornamented sash. It fell from her, and a kite passed by, thought it was meat, and snatched it. They searched for it but did not find it, so they accused her of taking it. They searched her, even examining her private part. He said: While they were in that state, the kite passed by and dropped the sash, and it fell among them. She said to them: 'This is what you accused me of, and I am innocent of it. Here it is as you see.' Then she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and embraced Islam. She had a tent or a small room in the mosque. She said: She used to come to me and sit with me. She would hardly sit with me without saying: 'The day of the sash is among the wonders of our Lord. Indeed, He delivered me from the land of disbelief.' I said to her: 'Why is it that you never sit with me without saying this?' She said: ... Then she told me the story."],[1336,"Sa'd was struck in his median cubital vein. The Prophet ﷺ pitched a tent for him in the mosque so that he could visit him from nearby. He said: His wound began to close and heal. He said: 'O Allah, You know that the people I most loved to fight were those who denied Your Prophet, expelled him, and did such and such. I think You have ended the war between us and them. So cause this wound to burst open, so that my death occurs from it.' He said: While they were one night, his wound burst open and blood flowed from his injury until it reached the people's tent. They called out: 'O people of the tent, what is this blood coming to us from your direction?' They looked and found that the base of his neck had burst open from his median cubital vein, and the blood was gushing from him."],[1337,"When Sulayman b. Dawud finished building the mosque of Bayt al-Maqdis, he asked Allah for three things: a judgment that would coincide with His judgment, a kingdom that no one after him would have, and that no one should come to this mosque intending only to pray in it except that he would leave free of his sins as on the day his mother gave birth to him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'As for two of them, they have been granted to him, and I hope that the third has also been granted.'"],[1338,"On the day of the Confederates, he said: 'What is wrong with them? May Allah fill their graves and their houses with fire, just as they kept us busy from the middle prayer until the sun set.'"],[1339,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the day of the Trench: 'May Allah fill their hearts and their graves with fire, just as they kept us busy from the middle prayer.'"],[1340,"They kept us busy from the middle prayer, the 'Asr prayer. May Allah fill their graves — or he said: their houses — with fire.' Al-Ashajj said: 'Their houses and their graves with fire. Then he prayed between the two evening prayers.' Salm added: 'Between Maghrib and 'Isha.'"],[1341,"The middle prayer is the 'Asr prayer."],[1342,"He used to dislike sleeping before 'Isha and did not like conversing after it."],[1344,"A man came to 'Umar while he was standing at 'Arafah and said: 'O Amir al-Mu'minin, I have come from Kufah and left there a man dictating the masahif from memory.' 'Umar became angry and said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stay up conversing with Abu Bakr through the night in matters concerning the Muslims.'"],[1345,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to narrate to us about the Children of Israel until dawn, and he would not get up except for the most important part of prayer."],[1346,"We were with Sa'id b. al-'As in Tabaristan, and he said: \"Which of you prayed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" Hudhayfah said: \"I did.\" He said: So Hudhayfah stood and arranged the people in two rows behind him, one row behind him and one row facing the enemy. He prayed one rak'ah with those behind him, then these withdrew to the place of those, and those came forward, and he prayed one rak'ah with them, and they did not make up anything."],[1347,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed at Dhu Qarad. Abu Musa said: \"Like the prayer of Hudhayfah.\" And Bundar mentioned the hadith like the hadith of Hudhayfah, and he said at the end: \"And they did not make up anything.\""],[1348,"Rukayn b. al-Rabi' narrated to me, from al-Qasim b. Hassan, who said: I asked Zayd b. Thabit about that, and he narrated to me something similar."],[1349,"Allah made the prayer obligatory on the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ: four in residence, two in travel, and one in fear."],[1350,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear with them. One row stood in front of him, and one row behind him. He prayed one rak'ah and two prostrations with those behind him, then these advanced until they stood in the place of their companions, and those came until they stood in the place of these. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed one rak'ah and two prostrations with them, then said the salam. Thus the Prophet ﷺ had two rak'ahs and they had one rak'ah."],[1351,"Al-Hakam narrated to us, and Mis'ar b. Kidam, from Yazid al-Faqir, from Jabir b. 'Abd Allah, from the Prophet ﷺ with the like of it, but he did not say: \"Then he said the salam.\""],[1352,"Shu'bah narrated to us, from Simak al-Hanafi, from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet ﷺ [the like of that]."],[1353,"That the Prophet ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear with his companions. He bowed with all of them, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, and the row that was next to him prostrated, while the others remained standing. When he rose, those prostrated by themselves twice. Then the front row moved back until they stood in the place of those, and those came forward until they stood in the place of the front row. The Prophet ﷺ bowed with all of them, then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, and the row that was next to him prostrated. When they raised their heads, those prostrated twice by themselves. All of them bowed with the Prophet ﷺ, and prostrated twice by themselves. The enemy was in the direction of the qiblah."],[1354,"From the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding the prayer of fear. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood, and one group behind him, and another group behind that group, sitting, their faces all toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the takbir, and both groups said the takbir. He bowed, and the group behind him bowed, while the others remained sitting. Then he prostrated, and they prostrated also, while the others remained sitting. Then he stood and they stood, and they walked backward until they were in the place of their companions, sitting. The other group came, and he prayed one rak'ah and two prostrations with them, while the others remained sitting. Then he said the salam. Then both groups stood and prayed for themselves one rak'ah and two prostrations, one rak'ah and two prostrations."],[1355,"That he prayed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two rak'ahs with one of the two groups, then prayed two rak'ahs with the other group. Thus the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed four rak'ahs, and each group prayed two rak'ahs."],[1356,"The Prophet of God ﷺ prayed two rak'ahs with a group of the people while another group was standing guard. Then he said the taslim, and those who had prayed departed, and the others came, and he prayed two rak'ahs with them, then he said the taslim."],[1357,"The Prophet of God ﷺ led them in the prayer of fear. He prayed one rak'ah with a group behind him while another group faced the enemy. Then the group that had prayed stood up and faced the enemy, and the others came, and the Prophet ﷺ prayed one rak'ah with them, then he said the taslim. Then these prayed one rak'ah, and those prayed one rak'ah."],[1358,"Ma'mar narrated to us something similar."],[1359,"From Sahl b. Abi Hathmah regarding the prayer of fear, he said: The imam stands facing the qiblah, and one group of them stands with him while another group faces the enemy, their faces toward the enemy. He bows one rak'ah with them. Abu Musa said: \"Then they stand and bow.\" Bundar said: \"Then they bow for themselves and prostrate for themselves twice in their place, then they go to the position of those [others], and those [others] come, and he bows one rak'ah with them and prostrates twice with them. So it is two [rak'ahs] for him and one for them, then they bow.\" Abu Musa said: \"For themselves one rak'ah and they prostrate twice.\""],[1360,"Yahya b. Sa'id narrated to me, from Shu'bah, from 'Abd al-Rahman b. al-Qasim, from his father, from Salih b. Khawwat, from Sahl b. Abi Hathmah, from the Prophet ﷺ. Bundar said something similar to the hadith of Yahya b. Sa'id, and Yahya said to me: \"Write it next to it, and I do not remember the hadith, but it is like the hadith of Yahya b. Sa'id.\" Abu Musa said: Yahya said to me: \"Did you hear from me the hadith of Yahya b. Sa'id on the prayer of fear?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then write it next to it similarly.\""],[1361,"He said concerning the prayer of fear: \"One group stands behind the imam and another group behind them. He prays one rak'ah and two prostrations with those behind him, then he sits in his place until they complete one rak'ah and two prostrations. Then they move to the position of their companions, and their companions move to the position of these. Then he prays one rak'ah and two prostrations with them, then he sits in his place until they pray one rak'ah and two prostrations, then he says the taslim.\""],[1362,"Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman b. al-Qasim, from al-Qasim, from Salih b. Khawwat, from Sahl b. Abi Hathmah, from the Prophet ﷺ, similar to this. In the Dar al-Mayman edition, ["],[1363,"Yahya b. Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar, from al-Qasim, from Salih b. Khawwat, from his father, similarly. Thus"],[1364,"He asked Abu Hurayrah: \"Did you pray the prayer of fear with the Prophet ﷺ?\" Abu Hurayrah said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"When?\" He said: \"It was the year of the expedition of Najd. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood for the 'Asr prayer, and a group stood with him and another group facing the enemy, their backs to the qiblah. The Messenger of God ﷺ said the takbir, and they all said the takbir with him—those who were with him and those who were facing the enemy. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ bowed one rak'ah, and the group that was next to him bowed with him. Then he prostrated, and the group that was next to him prostrated, while the others remained standing facing the enemy. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up, and the group that was next to him stood up and went to the enemy and faced them. The group that had been facing the enemy came forward, and they bowed and prostrated while the Messenger of God ﷺ remained standing as he was. Then they stood up, and the Messenger of God ﷺ bowed another rak'ah, and they bowed with him [and he prostrated] and they prostrated with him. Then the group that had been facing the enemy came forward, and they bowed and prostrated while the Messenger of God ﷺ and those with him were sitting. Then came the taslim, and the Messenger of God ﷺ said the taslim, and they all said the taslim. So the Messenger of God ﷺ had two rak'ahs, and each man of the two groups had two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs.\""],[1365,"I heard Abu Hurayrah while Marwan b. al-Hakam asked him about the prayer of fear. Abu Hurayrah said: \"I was with the Messenger of God ﷺ in that expedition.\" He said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ split the people into two groups.\" Then he mentioned the hadith with similar meaning, and regarding the second rak'ah he said: \"The group that had prayed behind him took their weapons, then they walked backward on their heels until they stood facing the enemy.\" And he added at the end of the hadith: \"The people stood up having shared with him in the prayer.\""],[1366,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed the prayer of fear at Dhat al-Riqa'. She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ split the people into two groups. One group lined up behind him, and another group stood facing the enemy. She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said the takbir, and the group that lined up behind him said the takbir. Then he bowed and they bowed, then he prostrated and they prostrated, then he raised his head and they raised theirs. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ remained sitting, and they prostrated for themselves the second prostration. Then they stood up and retreated on their heels, walking backward, until they stood behind them. The other group came forward—Ahmad said: \"the other [group]\"—and they both said: They lined up behind the Messenger of God ﷺ and said the takbir, then they bowed for themselves. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ prostrated his second prostration, and they prostrated. Ahmad b. al-Azhar added: They prostrated with him. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up in his rak'ah, and they prostrated for themselves the second prostration. Then both groups stood up together. And they both said: They lined up behind the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he bowed one rak'ah with them, and they all bowed. Then he prostrated, and they all prostrated. Abu al-Azhar said: Then he raised his head and they raised with him. Muhammad b. 'Ali said: And they raised in his place, and he did not say: \"Then he raised his head.\" They both said: That was from the Messenger of God ﷺ very quick, not failing to lighten as much as he could. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said the taslim, and they said the taslim. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up, and the people had shared with him in his entire prayer."],[1367,"He heard Jabir b. 'Abd Allah being asked about the prayer during travel: \"Is it shortened to two?\" He said: \"No. Indeed, two rak'ahs during travel are not a shortening. Shortening is only one rak'ah at the time of fighting.\" Then he said: \"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and the iqamah was called. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood up, and a group stood behind him while another group faced the enemy. He prayed one rak'ah with those behind him and prostrated twice with them. Then they left and stood in the position of those who had been facing the enemy, and that other group came. The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed one rak'ah with them and prostrated twice with them. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said the taslim, and those behind him said the taslim, and those others said the taslim.\""],[1368,"We were with Sa'id b. al-'As at Tabaristan, and with him were a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. He said to them: 'Which of you witnessed the prayer of fear with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' Hudhayfah said: 'I did. Command your companions to stand as two groups: one group of them facing the enemy and one group of them behind you. You say the takbir and they all say the takbir. Then you bow and they bow. Then you rise and they all rise. Then you prostrate and the group that is near you prostrates, while the other group stands facing the enemy. When you raise your head, those near you stand and the others prostrate. Then you bow and they all bow. Then you prostrate and the group that is near you prostrates, while the other group stands facing the enemy. When you raise your head from prostration, those facing the enemy prostrate. Then you say the taslim upon them. And command your companions that if the enemy attacks them with an assault, fighting and speaking have become permissible for them.'\""],[1369,"When he was asked about the prayer of fear, he mentioned the hadith in full and said: 'If the fear is more intense than that, they pray on foot standing, or mounted, facing the qiblah or not facing it.'\""],[1370,"Al-Shafi'i Muhammad b. Idris narrated to us, from Malik; (h) and al-Rabi' narrated to us, from al-Shafi'i, from Malik: \"\"\""],[1371,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Maghrib with the people three rak'ahs, then he departed. The others came and he prayed three rak'ahs with them. So the Prophet ﷺ had six rak'ahs, and the people had three [each].\""],[1372,"If you are harmed by rain or if you are ill—'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf said: 'he was wounded'\""],[1373,"The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah. So when you see them, pray.\""],[1374,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He stood up in alarm, fearing that it might be the Hour. He stood until he reached the mosque and began praying with the longest standing, bowing, and prostration I ever saw him do in any prayer. Then he said: 'These signs that Allah sends are not for the death or life of anyone, but Allah sends them to frighten His servants thereby. So when you see any of that, hasten to His remembrance, supplication, and seeking forgiveness.'\""],[1375,"The sun eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The people said, 'It has only eclipsed for the death of Ibrahim.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and addressed the people, saying: 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. When you see that, then praise Allah, magnify Him, glorify Him, and pray until the eclipse clears, whichever of them has eclipsed.'\" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended and prayed two rak'ahs.\""],[1376,"While I was shooting with some arrows of mine during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the sun eclipsed. I threw them down and went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I arrived while he was standing, raising his hands, glorifying, magnifying, praising, and supplicating until it cleared. He recited two surahs and bowed in two rak'ahs.\""],[1377,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ when the sun eclipsed. He went to the mosque dragging his cloak in haste, and the people gathered around him. He prayed two rak'ahs as you pray. When it cleared, he addressed us and said: 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah by which Allah frightens His servants. They do not eclipse for the death of anyone among people. So when you see anything of them, pray and supplicate until what has befallen you clears.'\""],[1378,"When the sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a call was made: 'The prayer is congregational.'\""],[1379,"Humayd b. al-Aswad narrated to us, from Hajjaj al-Sawwaf. Abu Tahir narrated to us. He said:"],[1380,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ while the people were with him. He stood for a long time, about the length of Surat al-Baqarah, then bowed for a long time, then raised [his head] and stood for a long time, but it was less than the first standing, then bowed for a long time, but it was less than the first bowing, then prostrated, then stood for a long time, but it was less than that first standing, then bowed for a long time, but it was less than that first bowing, then raised [his head] and stood for a long time, but it was less than that first standing, then bowed for a long time, but it was less than that bowing, then prostrated, then finished, and the sun had become clear. He said, 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of God; they do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone. When you see that, remember God.' They said, 'O Messenger of God, we saw you reach for something in this place of yours—al-Rabiʿ said: something—then we saw you as if you recoiled.' And the other two said, 'you recoiled.' He said, 'I saw Paradise,' and they said, 'and I reached for a cluster from it; had I taken it, you would have eaten from it as long as the world remains.' Al-Rabiʿ said, 'and I saw, or I was shown, the Fire,' and the other two said, 'and I saw the Fire,' and they said, 'I never saw a sight like today, and I saw that most of its people were women.' Al-Rabiʿ said: they said, 'Why?' And the other two said, 'Because of what, O Messenger of God?' He said, 'Because of their ingratitude.' It was said, 'Do they disbelieve in God?' He said, 'They are ungrateful to their husbands and deny kindness. If you were good to one of them for a lifetime, then she sees something from you, she says, \"I have never seen any good from you.\"'"],[1381,"The Messenger of God ﷺ rode a mount of his to a nearby place, and he did not return until the sun eclipsed. So I went out among some women, and we were in front of the chambers. The Prophet ﷺ came from his mount quickly and stood at his usual place of prayer, and the people stood behind him. He said the takbir, [and stood for a long time, then bowed for a long time, then raised his head], then stood and prolonged the standing, but it was less than the first standing, then bowed and prolonged the bowing, but it was less than the first bowing, then raised his head, then prostrated and prolonged the prostration, then raised his head, then prostrated with a prostration less than the first prostration, then stood and prolonged the standing, but it was less than the first standing, then bowed and prolonged the bowing, but it was less than the first bowing, then raised his head and stood and prolonged the standing, but it was less than the first standing, then bowed and prolonged the bowing, but it was less than the first bowing, then prostrated, and finished. His prayer was four bowings in four prostrations. Then he sat, and the sun had become clear."],[1382,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. So the Messenger of God ﷺ stood in prayer, then recited a recitation aloud, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head, then recited about the same as his first recitation, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head and prostrated, then stood for the second rakʿah and did the same as he did in the first. Then he said, 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of God; they do not eclipse for the death of any human being. When that happens, hasten to prayer.' He said, 'That was because Ibrahim had died that day, and the people said, \"This was only because of the death of Ibrahim.\"'"],[1383,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ on a very hot day. He prayed with his companions, prolonging the standing until they began to fall down. Then he bowed and prolonged it, then raised his head and prolonged it, then prostrated twice, then stood and did something similar. It was four bowings and four prostrations. Then he said, 'Everything you are promised was shown to me.' And he mentioned the hadith at length, and said, 'They used to say that the sun and the moon do not eclipse except for the death of a great person, but they are two signs among the signs of God that He shows you. So when they eclipse, pray until it clears.'"],[1384,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ on a very hot day. The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed with his companions, prolonging the standing until they began to fall down. Then he bowed and prolonged it, then stood and did the same. Then he began to move forward and step back. It was four bowings and four prostrations. Then he said, 'Everything you are promised was shown to me. Paradise was shown to me until I reached for a cluster from it, and if I had wished, I could have taken it. Then I reached for a cluster and my hand fell short of it. Then the Fire was shown to me, and I began to step back for fear that it would overwhelm you. I saw in it a tall black Himyarite woman being punished for a cat of hers that she had tied up; she neither fed it nor left it to eat from the vermin of the earth. And I saw Abu Thumamah ʿAmr b. Malik dragging his intestines in the Fire. And they used to say that the sun and the moon do not eclipse except for the death of a great person, but they are two signs among the signs of God that God shows you. So when they eclipse, pray until it clears.'"],[1385,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed six bowings and four prostrations in an eclipse prayer."],[1386,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He led the people in a very long standing. He would stand, then bow, then stand, then bow, and he bowed two genuflections in each rakʿah with three bowings. He bowed the third time, then prostrated. On that day, men fainted, and buckets of water were poured on them because of the length of his standing. When he said the takbir, he said, 'God is greatest,' and when he raised his head, he said, 'God hears those who praise Him.' He did not finish until the sun became clear. Then he stood, praised God and extolled Him, and said, 'The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone, but they are two signs among the signs of God by which He frightens you. So when they eclipse, hasten to the remembrance of God until they clear.'"],[1387,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and that was the day on which his son Ibrahim, the son of the Messenger of God ﷺ, died. The people said, 'The sun eclipsed only because of the death of Ibrahim.' So the Messenger of God ﷺ stood and prayed with the people six bowings in four prostrations. He said the takbir, then recited and prolonged the recitation, then bowed for about the length of his standing, then raised his head and recited a recitation shorter than the first, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head and recited a recitation shorter than the second, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head, then went down and prostrated twice, then stood and performed three bowings before prostrating, none of which was except that the one before it was longer than the one after it, except that his bowing was about the length of his standing. Then he stepped back in his prayer, and the rows stepped back with him, then he stepped forward, and the rows stepped forward with him. He completed the prayer, and the sun had become bright. Then he said, 'O people, the sun and the moon are only two signs among the signs of God, and they do not eclipse for the death of any human being. So when you see anything of that, pray until it clears.'"],[1388,"He prayed in an eclipse: he recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then recited, then bowed, then prostrated, and the other [rakʿah] was the same."],[1389,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and that was the day on which his son Ibrahim, the son of the Messenger of God ﷺ, died. The people said, 'The sun eclipsed only because of the death of Ibrahim.' So the Messenger of God ﷺ stood and prayed with the people six bowings in four prostrations. He said the takbir, then recited and prolonged the recitation, then bowed for about the length of his standing, then raised his head and recited a recitation shorter than the first, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head and recited a recitation shorter than the second, then bowed for about the length of his recitation, then raised his head, then went down and prostrated twice, then stood and performed three bowings before prostrating, none of which was except that the one before it was longer than the one after it, except that his bowing was about the length of his standing. Then he stepped back in his prayer, and the rows stepped back with him, then he stepped forward, and the rows stepped forward with him. He completed the prayer, and the sun had become bright. Then he said, 'O people, the sun and the moon are only two signs among the signs of God, and they do not eclipse for the death of any human being. So when you see anything of that, pray until it clears.'"],[1390,"The sun eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He went out to the mosque, stood and said the takbir, and the people lined up behind him. The Messenger of God ﷺ recited a long recitation, then said the takbir and bowed for a long time, then raised his head and said, 'God hears those who praise Him; our Lord, to You belongs praise.' Then he stood and recited a long recitation, which was shorter than the first recitation, then said the takbir and bowed for a long time, which was shorter than the first bowing, then said, 'God hears those who praise Him; our Lord, to You belongs praise.' Then he did the same in the last rakʿah, completing four bowings and four prostrations, and the sun cleared before he finished. Then he stood and addressed the people, praising God as He deserves, and said, 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of God; they do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone. So when you see them, hasten to prayer.'"],[1391,"The sun eclipsed, and 'Ali led the people in prayer... He mentioned the hadith, and they said: 'He stood in the second rak'ah and did as he had done in the first rak'ah. Then he sat supplicating and beseeching until the sun became clear. Then he told them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that.' Yusuf said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that.'"],[1392,"The sun eclipsed one day during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray, and he stood so long that he almost did not bow. Then he bowed so long that he almost did not raise his head. Then he raised his head, and he almost did not prostrate. Then he prostrated and almost did not raise his head. Then he raised his head and almost did not prostrate. Then he prostrated and almost did not raise his head."],[1393,"Then he prostrated and prolonged the prostration. Then he raised his head, then prostrated with a prostration shorter than the first prostration."],[1395,"The sun eclipsed one day during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray, and he stood so long that he almost did not bow. Then he bowed so long that he almost did not raise his head. Then he raised his head and almost did not prostrate. Then he prostrated and almost did not raise his head. He began to blow and weep, saying: 'My Lord, did You not promise me that You would not punish them while I am among them? My Lord, did You not promise me that You would not punish them while we seek Your forgiveness?' When he had prayed two rak'ahs, the sun became clear. He stood, praised Allah, and extolled Him, and said: 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. When they eclipse, hasten to the remembrance of Allah.' Then he said: 'Paradise was shown to me until if I wished I could have taken a bunch of its fruits. And Hell was shown to me, and I began to blow on it, fearing that it would overwhelm you. So I began to say: My Lord, did You not promise me that You would not punish them while I am among them? My Lord, did You not promise me that You would not punish them while they seek forgiveness?' He said: 'I saw in it the tall black Himyari woman, the owner of the cat. She used to confine it, neither feeding it nor giving it water, nor letting it eat from the insects of the earth. I saw her every time it turned away, it bit her, and every time it turned toward her, it bit her, in the Fire. And I saw the owner of the two sandals, the brother of Banu Da'da', being pushed into the Fire with a staff having two prongs. And I saw the owner of the hooked staff in the Fire, who used to steal from the pilgrims with his hooked staff and say: I am not stealing; only the hooked staff steals. I saw him in the Fire leaning on his hooked staff.'"],[1396,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and prolonged the standing until it was said: 'He will not bow.' Then he bowed and prolonged the bowing until it was said: 'He will not raise his head.' Then he raised his head and prolonged the standing until it was said: 'He will not prostrate.' Then he prostrated and prolonged the prostration until it was said: 'He will not raise his head.' Then he raised his head and sat until it was said: 'He will not prostrate.' Then he prostrated, then he stood and did the same in the second rak'ah. Then the sun became clear."],[1397,"The sun eclipsed, and 'Ali led the people in prayer... He mentioned the hadith, and they said: 'He stood in the second rak'ah and did as he had done in the first rak'ah. Then he sat supplicating and beseeching until the sun became clear. Then he told them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that.' Yusuf said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that.'"],[1398,"When it became clear, he stood — meaning the Prophet ﷺ — and addressed the people, praised Allah, extolled Him, and said: 'The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. They do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone. O community of Muhammad! By Allah, there is no one more jealous than Allah that His servant or His female servant should commit adultery. O community of Muhammad! By Allah — or by the One in whose hand is my soul — if you knew what I know, you would laugh little and weep much. Indeed, have I conveyed?'"],[1401,"The sun eclipsed during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, and he led the people in prayer. He mentioned the hadith and said at its end: 'Then he finished and said: The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah. When you see that, hasten to prayer.' This is the statement of al-Zuhri. He said: 'And Hisham added in it: When you see that, give charity and pray.'"],[1402,"The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith in full and said: 'When you see that, hasten to prayer, to the remembrance of Allah, and to charity.'"],[1403,"The sun eclipsed on the day Ibrahim, the son of the Messenger of God ﷺ, died, so the people thought it eclipsed because of his death. The Prophet ﷺ stood and said: O people, the sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of God; they do not eclipse for the death or life of anyone. When you see that, then hasten to prayer, to the remembrance of God, supplicate, and give charity."],[1404,"He commanded manumission when the sun eclipsed."],[1405,"The sun was eclipsed, and the Prophet of God ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs until it cleared. Then he said: The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone, but they are two creations among His creations. God brings about whatever He wills in His creation. Then indeed, when God, Blessed and Exalted, manifests Himself to something of His creation, it humbles itself to Him. So whichever of them is eclipsed, pray until it clears or until God brings about a matter."],[1406,"The sun was eclipsed during the time of the Messenger of God."],[1407,"ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, from Khālid, from Abū Qilābah, from al-Nuʿmān b. Bashīr, similar to the ḥadīth of Ayyūb."],[1408,"An amir among the commanders sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the rain prayer. Ibn ʿAbbās said: What prevents him from asking me himself? The Messenger of God ﷺ went out humble, shabby, submissive, imploring, and prayed two rakʿahs as he prays in the Eid, and he did not deliver a sermon like this sermon of yours."],[1409,"The Prophet ﷺ went out to the prayer ground and prayed for rain. He turned his cloak inside out and prayed two rakʿahs."],[1410,"We went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ for the rain prayer. He delivered a sermon, faced the qiblah, supplicated, prayed for rain, turned his cloak inside out, and led them in prayer."],[1411,"So-and-so sent me to Ibn ʿAbbās to ask him about the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ in the rain prayer. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ went out shabby, imploring, humble, and he did not deliver a sermon like this sermon of yours; he prayed two rakʿahs."],[1413,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out one day to pray for rain and led us in two rakʿahs, without adhan or iqamah."],[1414,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out with the people to pray for rain, led them in two rakʿahs, recited aloud, turned his cloak inside out, raised his hands, prayed for rain, and faced the qiblah."],[1415,"The Prophet ﷺ did not raise his hands in any of his supplications except in the rain prayer."],[1416,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought rain like this, extending his hands and making their palms face the ground until I saw the whiteness of his armpits."],[1417,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extending his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits. Sulayman said: I thought he was supplicating in the rain prayer."],[1418,"The Prophet ﷺ went out to the prayer place and sought rain, then he turned his cloak and prayed two rak'ahs. Al-Mas'udi said: On the authority of Abu Bakr, on the authority of 'Abbad b. Tamim, I said to him."],[1419,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought rain while wearing a black cloak. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wanted to take hold of its lower part and make it the upper part, but when it was too heavy for him, he turned it over his shoulders. Ibrahim b. Hamza said: over his shoulder."],[1420,"The weepers came to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: O Allah, give us rain, a beneficial, abundant, pleasant, fertilizing, swift and not delayed, useful and not harmful rain. Then the sky covered them."],[1421,"O Allah, give us rain."],[1422,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to seek rain, faced the qiblah, turned his back to the people, turned his cloak, and prayed two rak'ahs, reciting in them and reciting aloud in them."],[1423,"Al-Walid b. 'Utba, the governor of Madinah, sent him to Ibn 'Abbas, saying: O my nephew, ask him how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did in the rain prayer the day he prayed for rain with the people. Ishaq said: I entered upon Ibn 'Abbas and said: O Abu al-'Abbas, how did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do in the rain prayer the day he sought rain? He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out humble and unpretentious, and he did in it as he did in the prayer of Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha."],[1424,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to seek rain, faced the qiblah, turned his back to the people, turned his cloak, and prayed two rak'ahs, reciting in them and reciting aloud in them."],[1425,"Whenever drought afflicted them, 'Umar b. al-Khattab would go out to seek rain through al-'Abbas, and say: O Allah, we used to seek rain through Your Prophet and You would give us rain, and today we seek rain through the uncle of Your Prophet—or our Prophet—so give us rain. Then they would be given rain."],[1426,"The Prophet of Allah ﷺ went out one day to seek rain and prayed two rak'ahs with us without adhan or iqamah. Then he delivered a sermon to us, supplicated Allah, turned his face towards the qiblah raising his hands, then turned his cloak, putting the right side on the left and the left side on the right."],[1427,"The Prophet ﷺ was delivering the Friday sermon when people stood up and shouted, saying: O Prophet of Allah, the rain has been withheld, the trees have turned red, and the animals have perished. So pray to Allah to give us rain. He said: O Allah, give us rain; O Allah, give us rain. By Allah, we saw no trace of cloud in the sky, then a cloud arose and spread, and then it rained. The Prophet of Allah ﷺ descended, prayed, and left, and it did not stop raining until the following Friday. When the Prophet ﷺ stood to deliver the sermon, they shouted: O Prophet of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the roads have been cut off. Pray to Allah to hold it back from us. He smiled and said: O Allah, around us and not upon us. Then it cleared away from Madinah and rained around it, while not a drop fell on Madinah. I looked at Madinah and it was like a crown."],[1428,"That the Prophet ﷺ went out with the people to the prayer ground to seek rain for them. He stood and supplicated while standing, then turned toward the qiblah and reversed his cloak, and they were given rain."],[1429,"The prayer of Al-Adha is two rak'ahs, the Friday prayer is two rak'ahs, the prayer of Al-Fitr is two rak'ahs, and the traveler's prayer is two rak'ahs, complete, not shortened, on the tongue of your Prophet [may Allah bless him], and whoever fabricates has failed."],[1430,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of Fitr until he had eaten, and he would not eat on the day of sacrifice until he had slaughtered."],[1431,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us on the day of Al-Adha after the prayer. Abu Burdah b. Niyar said: 'I slaughtered my sheep and had my morning meal before I came to the prayer.' He said: 'Your sheep is a sheep for meat.'"],[1432,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would break his fast on the day of Fitr with some dates, then he would go out."],[1433,"He would not go out on the day of Fitr until he had eaten some dates, and he would eat them in an odd number."],[1434,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out on the day of Adha or Fitr to the prayer ground and led them in prayer, then he left."],[1435,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go out on the two Eid days with Al-Fadl b. Abbas, Abdullah b. Abbas, Al-Abbas, Ali, Ja'far, Al-Hasan, Al-Husayn, Usamah b. Zayd, Zayd b. Harithah, and Ayman the son of Umm Ayman, raising his voice with the tahlil and takbir. He would take the road of the blacksmiths until he came to the prayer ground, and when he finished he returned by the shoemakers until he reached his home."],[1436,"I attended the Eid with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he did not give the adhan or the iqamah."],[1437,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would plant the spear on the day of Fitr and the day of sacrifice and pray toward it, and he would deliver the sermon after the prayer."],[1438,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to go out on the day of Fitr and the day of Adha with the spear, planting it in front of him when he stood to pray."],[1439,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he went out to the prayer ground on the days of Adha and Fitr, would go out with the spear before him until it was planted in the prayer ground, and he would pray toward it. That was because the prayer ground was an open space with nothing built in it to screen himself with."],[1440,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out on the day of Fitr or Adha—and my stronger knowledge is that it was the day of Fitr—and prayed two rak'ahs, not praying before them or after them. Then he came to the women, and with him was Bilal, and he commanded them to give charity. So a woman began to throw her earring and her necklace."],[1441,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed before the sermon on the day of Eid."],[1442,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ say the takbir seven and five times on Adha, and on Fitr the like of that."],[1443,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to say the takbir in the two Eids: in the first rak'ah seven takbirs, and in the second rak'ah five takbirs before the recitation."],[1444,"Umar b. al-Khattab asked me what the Messenger of God ﷺ recited in the prayer of going out on the two Eids? I said: He recited “The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split” and “Qaf. By the Glorious Qur'an.”"],[1446,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to go out on the day of al-Adha and al-Fitr and begin with the prayer. When he had finished his prayer and said the salam, he would stand, then face the people while they were seated in their prayer place. If he had a need to send an expedition or anything else, he would mention it to the people; and if he had a need, he would command them concerning it. He used to say: \"Give charity, give charity, give charity.\" The majority of those who gave charity were women. Then he would leave. This continued until Marwan b. al-Hakam came. I went out holding Marwan by the arm until we reached the prayer place, and there was Kathir b. al-Salt, who had built a pulpit of clay and bricks. Marwan was pulling my hand as if dragging me toward the pulpit, while I was pulling him toward the prayer place. When I saw that from him, I said: \"Where is beginning with the prayer?\" Marwan said: \"O Abu Sa'id, what you know has been abandoned.\" I raised my voice: \"No, by the One in whose hand is my soul, you will not bring anything better than what I know,\" three times. Then I left."],[1447,"The Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the sermon after the prayer."],[1448,"The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of al-Fitr and began with the prayer before the sermon, then delivered the sermon to the people. When the Prophet of God ﷺ finished, he descended and came to the women, and he reminded them while leaning on Bilal's hand. Bilal was spreading out his garment, and the women were throwing charity into it."],[1449,"The Prophet ﷺ delivered a sermon on the day of Eid on his riding camel."],[1451,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ delivering the sermon on Friday standing, then he sat a sitting in which he did not speak, then he stood and delivered another sermon. So whoever tells you that he saw the Messenger of God ﷺ delivering the sermon sitting has lied."],[1452,"The Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the sermon standing, and he would sit between the two sermons and recite verses from the Qur'an. His sermon was moderate, and his prayer was moderate—except that al-Hasan said: And he would recite on the minbar in his sermon verses from the Qur'an."],[1453,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to go out on the day of al-Adha and al-Fitr and begin with the prayer. When he had finished his prayer and said the salam, he would stand, then face the people while they were seated in their prayer place. If he had a need to send an expedition or anything else, he would mention it to the people; and if he had a need, he would command them concerning it. He used to say: \"Give charity, give charity, give charity.\" The majority of those who gave charity were women. Then he would leave. This continued until Marwan b. al-Hakam came. I went out holding Marwan by the arm until we reached the prayer place, and there was Kathir b. al-Salt, who had built a pulpit of clay and bricks. Marwan was pulling my hand as if dragging me toward the pulpit, while I was pulling him toward the prayer place. When I saw that from him, I said: \"Where is beginning with the prayer?\" Marwan said: \"O Abu Sa'id, what you know has been abandoned.\" I raised my voice: \"No, by the One in whose hand is my soul, you will not bring anything better than what I know,\" three times. Then I left."],[1454,"I never saw the Messenger of God ﷺ raising his hands while supplicating on his pulpit or anywhere else, but I saw him saying like this: and he pointed with his index finger, moving it."],[1455,"He saw Bishr b. Marwan on the pulpit raising his hands, and said: \"May God disgrace these two hands. I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ doing no more than pointing with his finger.\""],[1456,"I came as a delegate to the Messenger of God ﷺ, the seventh of seven or the ninth of nine. We attended the Friday prayer. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood leaning on a bow or a staff, praised God and extolled Him with good, light, blessed words."],[1457,"The Prophet ﷺ saw me while he was delivering the sermon, and he commanded me, so I moved to the shade."],[1458,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded me to recite to him while he was on the pulpit. I recited to him from Surat al-Nisa' until I reached: \"So how will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and We bring you as a witness against these?\" I looked at him and his eyes were flowing with tears."],[1459,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon to us one day and recited Ṣād. When he came to the prostration verse, he descended and prostrated, and we prostrated. He recited it another time, and when he reached the prostration verse, we prepared ourselves to prostrate. When he saw us, he said, \"It is only the repentance of a prophet, but I see that you have readied yourselves for prostration.\" So he descended and prostrated, and we prostrated."],[1460,"While the Messenger of God ﷺ was on the pulpit delivering a sermon, al-Hasan and al-Husayn came walking and stumbling, wearing two red shirts. He said: The Messenger of God ﷺ descended and carried them both, then said: \"God has spoken the truth: 'Your wealth and your children are only a trial.' I saw these two boys walking and stumbling, and I could not bear it until I descended and carried them.\""],[1461,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was delivering a sermon and said: \"A man has come asking about his religion; he does not know what his religion is.\" The Prophet ﷺ turned to me and left his sermon. Then a chair was brought, whose legs I thought were of iron. The Messenger of God ﷺ sat on it and began teaching me from what God had taught him. Then he returned to his sermon and completed it."],[1462,"I attended the Eid al-Fitr prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman. All of them prayed it before the sermon. Then the Prophet of God ﷺ descended, and it is as if I am looking at him seating the men with his hand. Then he came, making his way through them, until he reached the women, and with him was Bilal. He recited: \"O Prophet, when believing women come to you to give you allegiance...\" until he finished the verse. Then when he finished, he said: \"Are you upon that?\" One woman—no one else answered him, al-Hasan does not know who she was—said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Then give charity.\" He said: Bilal spread out his garment and said: \"Come, may my father and mother be your ransom.\" They began throwing large rings and rings into Bilal's garment."],[1463,"The Prophet ﷺ stood on the day of al-Fitr and began with the prayer before the sermon, then delivered the sermon to the people. When the Prophet of God ﷺ finished, he descended and came to the women, and he reminded them while leaning on Bilal's hand. Bilal was spreading out his garment, and the women were throwing charity into it."],[1464,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded them to fear Allah, admonished them, reminded them, praised Allah and extolled Him, urged them to obey Him, then said: \"Give charity, for most of you are the firewood of Hell.\" A woman with dark cheeks from the middle of the women said: \"Why, O Messenger of Allah?\" He said: \"Because you complain a lot and are ungrateful to your husbands.\" So they began [removing their necklaces] and their ornaments, earrings and rings, throwing them into Bilal's garment, giving them as charity."],[1466,"I attended the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of Eid; he prayed and said: \"We have finished the prayer, so whoever wishes may sit for the sermon, and whoever wishes to leave may leave.\""],[1467,"The Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the two Eids, and once he said: \"In the Eid, [he recited] 'Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High' and 'Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming?' If that coincided with Friday, he would recite them.\""],[1468,"Did you witness with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two Eids that came together on one day? He said: \"Yes. He prayed the Eid at the beginning of the day, then gave a concession regarding Jumu'ah, saying: 'Whoever wishes to perform Jumu'ah, let him do so.'\""],[1469,"\"I witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr in Makkah when he was governor, and the day of Fitr—or Adha—coincided with Friday. He delayed going out until the day had risen, then he went out and ascended the pulpit, delivered a long sermon, then prayed two rak'ahs and did not pray Jumu'ah. Some people from Banu Umayyah b. 'Abd Shams criticized him for that. This reached Ibn 'Abbas, and he said: 'Ibn al-Zubayr followed the Sunnah.' It reached Ibn al-Zubayr, and he said: 'I saw 'Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ when two Eids came together do the same.'\""],[1470,"We used to prevent our adolescent girls from going out. Then a woman arrived and stayed at the palace of Banu Khalaf, and she narrated that her sister was married to a man from the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who had participated in twelve battles with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. My sister accompanied him in six battles. She said: \"We used to treat the wounded and tend to the sick.\" My sister asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"Is there any blame on one of us if she does not have a jilbab and therefore does not go out?\" He said: \"Let her companion clothe her from her jilbab, and let her witness the good and the supplication of the believers.\" When Umm 'Atiyyah arrived, I asked her—or we asked her—: \"Did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say such-and-such?\" She would not mention the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without saying: \"May my father be sacrificed for him.\" She said: \"Yes, may my father be sacrificed for him. He said: 'Let the adolescent girls of seclusion, or the adolescent girls and those of seclusion, and the menstruating women go out and witness the good and the supplication of the believers, and let the menstruating woman keep away from the prayer place.'\" I said to Umm 'Atiyyah: \"The menstruating woman?\" She said: \"Does she not witness 'Arafah, and witness such-and-such, and witness such-and-such?\""],[1471,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to bring out the virgins, adolescent girls, those of seclusion, and menstruating women on the day of Eid. As for the menstruating women, they would keep away from the prayer place and witness the good and the supplication of the Muslims. One of them said: \"What if one of us does not have a jilbab?\" He said: \"Let her sister lend her one of her jilbabs.\""],[1472,"When the Prophet ﷺ went out for the two Eids, he would return by a different path from the one he took going out."],[1473,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of Eid until he had eaten. Then he would go out and pray two rak'ahs with the people. When he returned, he would pray two rak'ahs in his house, and he would not pray anything before the prayer."],[1474,"Yahya b. Sa'id narrated to us from Shu'bah something similar.\" Abu Bakr said: \"This wording is of the type I indicated in the 'Book of Faith,' that the Arabs may mention a number for something with parts and branches without intending to negate what exceeds that number. The Prophet ﷺ did not mean by his saying 'twenty-five' that it is not superior by more than this number. The proof of the correctness of what I interpreted is: , :"],[1475,"A man's prayer in congregation is superior to his prayer alone by twenty-seven degrees."],[1476,"The prayer of a man in congregation is better than his prayer alone by twenty-odd prayers."],[1477,"Whoever prays ‘Isha in congregation is like standing half the night in prayer, and whoever prays Fajr in congregation is like standing the whole night in prayer."],[1478,"From the Prophet ﷺ, regarding His saying: “Indeed, the recitation of dawn is ever witnessed,” he said: “The angels of the night and the angels of the day witness it.”"],[1479,"If they knew what is in ‘Atamah and Subh, they would come to them even if crawling."],[1480,"I came to Madinah and met Ubayy b. Ka‘b. I said: O Abu al-Mundhir! Tell me the most amazing hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the Fajr prayer with us – or led us – then he turned and said: “Is so-and-so present?” We said: No, and he did not attend the prayer. He said: “Is so-and-so present?” We said: No, and he did not attend the prayer. Then he said: “The heaviest prayers upon the hypocrites are the ‘Isha prayer and the Fajr prayer. If they knew what is in them, they would come to them even if crawling. The first row is like the row of the angels, and if you knew its virtue you would race to it. Your prayer with one man is purer than your prayer alone, and your prayer with two men is purer than your prayer with one man, and the more [you increase], the more beloved it is to Allah.”"],[1481,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the Subh prayer, then he said: “Is so-and-so present?”"],[1482,"O Messenger of Allah! Madinah has many vermin and beasts. He said: “Do you hear ‘Hayy ‘ala al-salah, hayy ‘ala al-falah’?” I said: Yes. He said: “Then come to it, even if crawling.”"],[1483,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ faced the people after the ‘Isha prayer and said: “I was on the verge of ordering those who stay behind from this prayer to have their houses burned upon them.” Ibn Umm Maktum stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, you know my condition, and I have no guide. He said: “Do you hear the call (iqamah)?” He said: Yes. He said: “Then attend it,” and he did not give him a concession."],[1484,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am an old blind man with a distant home, and I have a guide who does not stay with me, so is there any concession for me? He said: “Do you hear the call?” He said: Yes. He said: “I find no concession for you.”"],[1485,"I was on the verge of ordering my young men to establish the prayer, and order young men to go to men who stay behind from prayer and burn their houses upon them. If any of them knew that he would be called to a bone with meat, or to a tharid, he would surely answer."],[1486,"Safwan and Abu 'Asim narrated to me, they said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, and he mentioned the hadith:"],[1487,"I have seen us, and none would stay behind from it except a hypocrite whose hypocrisy was clear. And I have seen us, and a man would be supported between two men until he was stood in the row."],[1488,"The heaviest prayer upon the hypocrites is the later 'Isha' prayer and Fajr. If they knew what is in them, they would come to them even if crawling. I was about to order that the prayer be established, then order a man to lead the prayer, then take bundles of fire and burn the houses of people who stay behind from the prayer. This is the hadith of Ibn Numayr. In the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah he said: 'I was about to,' and he said: 'then order a man to lead the people in prayer, then go with men carrying bundles of firewood to a people who do not attend the prayer, and burn their houses with fire.'"],[1489,"When we missed a man at the later 'Isha' prayer and the morning prayer, we thought ill of him."],[1490,"\"Where is your dwelling?\" I said, \"A village near Hims.\" Abu al-Darda' said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"There are no three people in a village or desert where the prayer is not established among them but that Satan has gained mastery over them. So you must adhere to the congregation, for the wolf eats the stray sheep.\" And al-Masruqi said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said, and he said: \"The wolf takes the stray sheep.\""],[1491,"The foot of the Messenger of God ﷺ was injured, so we entered upon him and found him sitting in a room of his with a small upper room in front of him. He said: He prayed sitting, and we stood behind him and prayed. When he finished the prayer, he said: \"When I pray sitting, then pray sitting. And when I pray standing, then pray standing. And do not stand as Persia stands for its tyrants and kings.\""],[1492,"The Messenger of God ﷺ did not come out to us for three days. The prayer was called, so Abu Bakr went to lead the people. The Prophet ﷺ lifted the curtain, and we saw no sight more pleasing to us than when the face of the Messenger of God ﷺ became clear to us. The Messenger of God ﷺ gestured to Abu Bakr to step forward, and the Prophet of God ﷺ let the curtain down. And he was not reached until he ﷺ died."],[1493,"Whoever performs ablution and completes it, then walks to an obligatory prayer and prays it with the imam, his sin is forgiven."],[1494,"A man's prayer in congregation exceeds his prayer alone in his house and in his market by more than twenty degrees. That is because when one of you performs ablution and does it well, then goes out to the prayer intending nothing else, he does not take a step but that God raises him a degree by it and removes from him a sin by it. This is the hadith of Bundar. Abu Musa said: \"or removes from him.\" Bishr b. Khalid, Salm b. Junadah, and al-Dawraqi said: \"and removes from him.\" Al-Dawraqi said: \"until he enters the mosque.\""],[1495,"None of you performs ablution, makes it good and complete, then comes to the mosque intending only the prayer in it, but that God rejoices at him as the family of an absent person rejoices at his appearance."],[1496,"When a man purifies himself, then passes to the mosque observing the prayer, his scribe—or his two scribes—writes for him ten good deeds for every step he takes to the mosque. And the one sitting observing the prayer is like the one standing in devotion, and he is written among the praying people, from when he leaves his house until he returns."],[1497,"Every soul has charity enjoined upon it every day the sun rises. Among that is: judging justly between two people is charity; helping a man with his mount and lifting him onto it is charity; removing harm from the road is charity; and among that is helping a man with his mount and lifting him onto it, and lifting his goods onto it is charity; a good word is charity; and every step you take to the prayer is charity."],[1498,"A good word is charity, and every step you take to prayer is charity."],[1499,"Abdullah b. Amr passed by Muadh b. Jabal while he was standing at his door gesturing with his hand, as if he were talking to himself. Abdullah said to him, \"What is the matter with you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, that you are talking to yourself?\" He said, \"What is wrong with me? Does the enemy of Allah want to distract me from words I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said, 'You strive your whole life now in your house, not going out to the gathering to speak? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever strives in the path of Allah is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever visits a sick person is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever goes to the mosque in the morning or returns in the evening is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever enters upon an imam to visit him is guaranteed by Allah, and whoever sits in his house and does not backbite anyone with evil is guaranteed by Allah.' So the enemy of Allah wants to get me out of my house to the gathering.\""],[1500,"Whoever goes to the mosque in the morning or returns in the evening, Allah has prepared for him hospitality in Paradise each time he goes or returns."],[1501,"Upon every joint of a person there is a charity due each day. A man among the people said, \"This is one of the hardest things you have brought us.\" He said, \"Your enjoining good and forbidding evil is charity, your carrying for the weak is charity, your removing filth from the road is charity, and every step you take to prayer is charity.\""],[1502,"Let those who walk to the mosques in darkness rejoice in complete light on the Day of Resurrection."],[1503,"Give glad tidings to those who walk in darkness of complete light."],[1504,"There was a man from the Ansar whose house was the farthest house in Madinah, and he never missed prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I felt sorry for him and said, \"O so-and-so, if only you bought a donkey to protect you from the heat, lift you from the low ground, and protect you from the vermin of the earth.\" He said, \"By Allah, I would not love that my house be adjacent to the house of Muhammad ﷺ.\" He said, \"I carried a burden for him until I came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He called him and asked him, and he mentioned the same to him, and mentioned that he hoped for reward in his affair. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, 'You shall have what you hoped for.' In the hadith of al-San'ani: 'I informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he asked him about that. He said, \"O Prophet of Allah, so that my footsteps may be recorded, and my returning to my family and my coming to it,\" or as he said. He said, \"Allah has given you all of that, and has given you all that you hoped for.\"'"],[1505,"The greatest of people in reward for prayer is the farthest of them in walking to it, then the next farthest. And the one who waits for prayer until he prays it with the imam in congregation has greater reward than the one who prays and then sleeps."],[1506,"When you see a man frequenting the mosque, bear witness to his faith. Allah [Most High] says: 'Only those who believe in Allah and the Last Day maintain the mosques of Allah.'"],[1507,"A man does not make the mosques his habitual place for prayer except that Allah rejoices over him from the moment he leaves his house, just as the family of an absent person rejoices over their absent one when he arrives."],[1508,"When one of you performs ablution and then comes to the mosque, nothing moves him except prayer, he intends nothing but prayer, then when he enters the mosque he is in prayer as long as prayer is what holds him there. And the angels invoke blessings upon one of you as long as he remains in the place where he prayed, saying: 'O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him; O Allah, accept his repentance,' as long as he does not harm anyone and does not break his ablution."],[1509,"When the prayer is established, do not come to it running; come to it walking. Upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, make up."],[1510,"We were with Abu Hurayrah in the mosque, and a mu'adhdhin called the adhan. A man stood up and left, so he said: \"As for this one, he has disobeyed Abu al-Qasim ﷺ.\" Bundar said: \"He has indeed contradicted Abu al-Qasim ﷺ.\""],[1511,"\"The people should be led by the one most versed in the Book of Allah among them. If they are equal in recitation, then the most knowledgeable of them in the Sunnah. If they are equal in the Sunnah, then the earliest of them in migration. If they are equal in migration, then the oldest of them.\" This is the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah. In the hadith of Shu'bah: \"The most versed in the Book of Allah among them, and the earliest in recitation.\""],[1512,"When they are three, one of them should lead them, and the most entitled of them to lead is the one most versed in the Quran among them."],[1513,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent out a military expedition, and they were a small group. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called them and said: \"How much of the Quran do you have with you?\" So he assessed them until he came to a man among them who was one of the youngest of them in age. He said: \"How much do you have with you, O so-and-so?\" He said: \"I have such-and-such, and Surah al-Baqarah.\" He said: \"You have Surah al-Baqarah?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Go, for you are their leader.\" A man who was one of their nobles said: \"By the One who is such-and-such, O Messenger of Allah, nothing prevented me from learning the Quran except fear that I would not be able to fulfill it.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Learn the Quran, recite it, and sleep. For the likeness of the Quran for one who learns it, recites it, and acts upon it is like a leather bag filled with musk, whose fragrance spreads everywhere. And one who learns it and sleeps while it is in his heart is like a leather bag tied over musk.\""],[1514,"My companion and I came to the Prophet ﷺ. When we wanted to return, he said to us: \"When the prayer becomes due, give the adhan, then the iqamah, then let the older of you two lead you.\" Al-Dawraqi added in his narration: He said: I said to Abu Qilabah: \"What about recitation?\" He said: \"They were close to each other.\""],[1515,"When the Muhajirun came to Madinah, they settled beside Quba. When the prayer became due, Salim, the freed slave of Abu Hudhayfah, led them, and he had the most Quran among them. Among them were 'Umar b. al-Khattab and Abu Salamah b. 'Abd al-Asad."],[1516,"We were at a settlement, and riders would pass by us returning from the Prophet ﷺ, so I would come close to them and listen until I memorized Quran. He said: The people were waiting for the conquest of Makkah for their Islam. When it was conquered, a man would come to him and say: 'O Messenger of Allah, I am the delegate of Banu So-and-so, and I have come to you with their Islam.' My father went with the Islam of his people. When he returned, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Put forward the one with the most Quran among you.' He said: They looked, and I was near a large tent. Al-Dawraqi said: a large tent. Abu Hashim said: a tent. They both said: They did not find anyone among them with more Quran than me, so they put me forward while I was a boy. I led them and had a cloak of mine; when I bowed or prostrated, my private parts would show. When we finished praying, an old woman of the age of ignorance would say to us: 'Cover from us the buttocks of your reciter.' He said: They cut a shirt for me, I think he said: from the cloth of the two seas, and he mentioned that he rejoiced greatly over it. Al-Dawraqi said: 'Let the one with the most Quran among you lead you.'"],[1517,"Whoever leads the people in prayer and hits the right time and completes the prayer, then it is for him and for them. And whoever falls short of anything of that, it is on him and not on them."],[1518,"The Prophet ﷺ stayed behind, and al-Mughirah b. Shu'bah stayed behind with him. Then he mentioned the hadith in full, and said: He said: We reached the people, and 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf had already led one rak'ah. When he sensed the coming of the Prophet ﷺ, he went to step back, but the Prophet ﷺ signaled to him to pray. When 'Abd al-Rahman finished the prayer and gave the salutation, the Prophet ﷺ and al-Mughirah stood up and completed what had preceded them."],[1519,"He went on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the expedition of Tabuk. Al-Mughirah said: I came with him until we found that the people had put 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf forward, and he led them in prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ caught up with one of the two rak'ahs, so he prayed the final rak'ah with the people. When 'Abd al-Rahman gave the salutation, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to complete his prayer. That alarmed the Muslims, and they greatly glorified Allah. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, he turned to them and said: \"You have done well\" or \"You have been correct,\" commending them for praying the prayer at its time."],[1520,"\"Do not lead a man in prayer in his place of authority or among his household, and do not sit on his honored seat except with his permission,\" or he said: \"unless he permits you.\""],[1521,"There was fighting among the Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he prayed Zuhr, then went to them to make peace. Then he said to Bilal: \"O Bilal, if 'Asr arrives and I have not come, tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer...\" - and he mentioned the hadith in full. He mentioned in the report that the Prophet ﷺ came and stood behind Abu Bakr, and gestured to him: \"Continue your prayer.\""],[1522,"Three people whose prayer is not accepted and does not ascend to heaven, nor does it go beyond their heads: a man who leads a people while they dislike him, a man who prays over a funeral without being appointed, and a woman whose husband calls her at night and she refuses him."],[1523,"Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from 'Amr b. al-Harith, from Yazid b. Abi Habib, from 'Amr b. al-Walid, from Anas b. Malik, raising it, meaning: the like of this. Abu Bakr said: \"I dictated the first part and it is mursal, because the hadith of Anas that comes after it was narrated to us by 'Isa immediately after it, meaning with its like. Were it not for that, I would not have included the mursal report in this book.\""],[1524,"Malik b. al-Huwayrith came to us. When the prayer became due, it was said to him: \"Go forward (lead).\" He said: \"Let a man from among you lead you.\" When they had prayed, he said: \"I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'When a man visits a people, he should not lead them; rather a man from among them should lead them.'\" In the hadith of Waki', he said: \"Let one of you go forward, so that I may tell you why I do not go forward.\""],[1525,"A group of people came to him disputing about the minbar: from which wood it was and who made it. Sahl said: \"By Allah, I know from which wood it is and who made it. I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the first day he stood on it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to a certain woman - he named her that day, but I have forgotten her name - saying: 'Tell your carpenter slave to make me some wooden planks on which I can address the people.' So he made these three steps from tamarisk wood of al-Ghabah. I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stand on it and say the takbir, and the people behind him said the takbir. Then he bowed and the people bowed. Then he raised his head and descended backward, then prostrated at the base of the minbar. Then he returned until he completed the rest of his prayer. Then he turned to them and said: 'I did this only so that you may follow me and learn my prayer.'\""],[1526,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hazim. He mentioned the hadith but did not say: \"I did this only so that you may follow me and learn my prayer.\""],[1527,"Hudhayfah led us in prayer on an elevated bench, and he prostrated on it. Abu Mas'ud pulled him, and Hudhayfah complied with him. When he finished the prayer, Abu Mas'ud said: \"Has this not been forbidden?\" Hudhayfah said to him: \"Did you not see that I complied with you?\""],[1528,"I spent the night at my maternal aunt Maymunah's. When it was part of the night, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up to pray. He went to a hanging water-skin, performed a light ablution, then stood and prayed. I got up and performed ablution, doing the same as he had done. Then I stood on his left, and he moved me to his right. He prayed as long as Allah willed, then lay down and slept until he snored. Then the mu'adhdhin came to him to announce the prayer, so he went out and prayed.\" This is the hadith of 'Abd al-Jabbar. Al-Makhzumi said: from Kurayb, and he said: \"So he went out and prayed without performing ablution.\" And he said: He described his ablution, making it light."],[1529,"The mu'adhdhin of the Prophet ﷺ would give the adhan, then wait. When he saw the Prophet ﷺ approaching, he would begin the iqamah."],[1530,"\"When the prayer is called, do not stand until you see me.\" Ahmad b. Sinan said: \"When the mu'adhdhin begins the adhan, do not stand until you see me.\""],[1531,"The prayer was called while a man was confiding with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until his companions fell asleep. Then he stood and prayed. Al-Dawraqi said: \"The prayer was called while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in private conversation with a man in a corner of the mosque, and he did not stand for prayer until some of the people had fallen asleep.\""],[1532,"The imam is a guarantor, and the mu'adhdhin is entrusted. O Allah, guide the imams, and forgive the mu'adhdhins."],[1533,"Abu Hurayrah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said ... And Zuhayr narrated it from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet ﷺ said the like of it."],[1535,"The mu'adhdhins are trustees, and the imams are guarantors. O Allah, forgive the mu'adhdhins and guide the imams aright.\" (Three times). This is the wording of the hadith of 'Ali b. Hujr. And al-Husayn b. al-Hasan said: \"May Allah guide the imams aright, and may He forgive the mu'adhdhins.\""],[1536,"and He pardoned the mu'adhdhin."],[1537,"I spent the night at my maternal aunt Maymunah's. When it was part of the night, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ got up to pray. He went to a hanging water-skin, performed a light ablution, then stood and prayed. I got up and performed ablution, doing the same as he had done. Then I stood on his left, and he moved me to his right. He prayed as long as Allah willed, then lay down and slept until he snored. Then the mu'adhdhin came to him to announce the prayer, so he went out and prayed.\" This is the hadith of 'Abd al-Jabbar. Al-Makhzumi said: from Kurayb, and he said: \"So he went out and prayed without performing ablution.\" And he said: He described his ablution, making it light."],[1538,"I spent the night in the house of my maternal aunt Maymunah, and I watched how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed. Then he stood to pray, so I came and stood beside him—I stood on his left. He said: He took me and made me stand on his right."],[1539,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to pray the Maghrib prayer. I came to him and stood beside him on his left, and he positioned me and made me on his right. Then a companion of mine came, so we formed a row behind him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer in a single garment, with its ends drawn across."],[1540,"I entered upon Jabir b. 'Abd Allah, I and Abu Salamah b. 'Abd al-Rahman, and we found him standing praying in a waist-wrapper. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: \"We came with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he went out for some need of his, so I poured water for his ablution. He performed ablution and wrapped himself in his waist-wrapper. I stood on his left, and he made me on his right. Then another came and stood on his left, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped forward to pray, and we prayed with him. He prayed thirteen rak'ahs including the witr.\""],[1541,"I prayed beside the Prophet ﷺ while 'A'ishah prayed behind us with us, and I was beside the Prophet ﷺ praying with him."],[1542,"He, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, his mother, and his maternal aunt were present. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in prayer, placing Anas on his right and his mother and his maternal aunt behind them."],[1543,"I and an orphan prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and my mother prayed behind us."],[1544,"Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ishaq b. 'Abd Allah b. Abi Talhah narrated to us that he heard Anas b. Malik saying something similar."],[1545,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell ill and lost consciousness. Then he regained consciousness and said: \"Has the prayer come?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Tell Bilal to call the adhan, and tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\""],[1546,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to touch our shoulders during prayer and say: \"Stand straight and do not differ, lest your hearts differ.\" Abu Mas'ud said: \"So today you are more differing.\""],[1547,"Straighten your rows, for straightening the rows is part of the completeness of the prayer. This is the hadith of Bundar. Salm b. Junadah said: From Qatadah, and he said: \"Indeed, part of the excellence of the prayer is establishing the row.\""],[1548,"\"Will you not form rows as the angels form rows before their Lord?\" We said: \"O Messenger of Allah, how do the angels form rows before their Lord?\" He said: \"They complete the first rows and stand close together in the row.\""],[1549,"Make your rows compact, bring them close together, and align the necks, for by the One in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, I see the devil entering through the gaps in the row as if they were small black sheep."],[1550,"Complete the front row, and if there is any deficiency, let it be in the last row."],[1551,"Complete the first and second rows, and if there is any gap, let it be in the third."],[1552,"When you stand, straighten your rows and close the gaps, for I see you from behind my back."],[1553,"Whoever joins a row, Allah joins him, and whoever cuts off a row, Allah cuts him off."],[1554,"Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows."],[1555,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to us when we stood for prayer and touch our shoulders and chests, saying: \"Do not let your chests differ, lest your hearts differ. Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row.\" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Beautify the Quran with your voices.\""],[1556,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to us and wipe our shoulders and chests, saying: \"Do not let your rows become disordered, lest your hearts become disordered. Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row\" — or \"the first rows.\""],[1557,"Zuhayr narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd Allah b. Abi Basir, from his father, who said: I came to Madinah and met Ubayy b. Ka'b. [Hidden text linked to 11017361: I came to Madinah and met Ubayy b. Ka'b and said: O Abu al-Mundhir...]"],[1558,"If the people knew what is in the call to prayer and the first row, they would draw lots for it."],[1559,"If they knew — or if you knew — what is in the first row, it would only be by drawing lots."],[1560,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to the row from one end to the other, wiping our shoulders or chests, saying: \"Do not become disordered, lest your hearts become disordered.\" He said: And he used to say: \"Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the first rows.\" And I think he said: \"Adorn the Quran with your voices.\""],[1561,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to come to the side of the row and straighten the chests and shoulders of the people, saying: \"Do not become disordered, lest your hearts become disordered. Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first rows.\""],[1562,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek forgiveness for the foremost row three times, and for the second row once."],[1563,"People will continue to lag behind from the first row until Allah, the Exalted, will place them in the Fire."],[1564,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and saw some people at the back of the mosque, so he said: \"What is keeping you back? People will continue to lag behind until Allah — Mighty and Majestic — puts them behind. Advance and follow me, and let those behind you follow you.\""],[1565,"The best of the rows of men is the first one, and the worst of them is the last one; and the best of the rows of women is the last one, and the worst of them is the first one."],[1566,"The best rows of men are the foremost, and the worst are the last; and the best rows of women are the last, and the worst are the foremost. O assembly of women, when the men prostrate, guard your gaze.\" I said to 'Abd Allah: \"Why is that?\" He said: \"Because of the tightness of their lower garments.\""],[1567,"When we prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we loved to be on his right, and I heard him say when he finished: \"My Lord, protect me from Your punishment on the Day You resurrect Your servants.\""],[1568,"It pleased us to pray on the side adjacent to the right of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, because he would begin the salam from his right."],[1569,"When we prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we loved to be on his right. And I heard him say when he finished: \"My Lord, protect me from Your punishment on the Day You resurrect Your servants.\""],[1570,"The best of you are those with the softest shoulders in prayer."],[1571,"We were forbidden to pray between the pillars, and we were driven away from it sternly."],[1572,"I prayed beside Anas b. Malik, and he pushed us toward the pillars, then said: \"We used to avoid this in the time of the Messenger of Allah.\""],[1573,"We prayed behind him, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ finished the prayer. Then he saw a man praying alone behind the row, so the Prophet of Allah ﷺ stood over him until he finished his prayer, then said to him: \"Repeat your prayer, for there is no prayer for a lone person behind the row.\""],[1575,"When one of you enters the mosque while the people are bowing, let him bow when he enters, then let him crawl while bowing until he joins the row, for that is the Sunnah.\" 'Ata' said: \"And I saw him doing that.\""],[1576,"Let those of understanding and intellect stand closest to me, then those who follow them, then those who follow them. And do not differ, lest your hearts differ. And beware of the clamor of the marketplaces."],[1577,"While I was in Madinah in the mosque in the front row standing in prayer, a man pulled me from behind with a strong pull, moved me aside, and stood in my place. He said: By Allah, I could not focus on my prayer. When he finished, it was Ubayy b. Ka'b, and he said: \"O young man, may Allah not displease you. This is a covenant from the Prophet ﷺ to us that we should stand closest to him.\" Then he faced the qiblah and said: \"Perish the people of the knot, by the Lord of the Ka'bah\" - three times - then said: \"By Allah, I do not grieve for them, but I grieve for those whom they have led astray.\" He said: I said: \"Whom do you mean by this?\" He said: \"The rulers.\""],[1578,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to reconcile between Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf. The prayer time came, so the mu'adhdhin came to Abu Bakr and ordered him to lead the people in prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and split the rows until he stood in the front row."],[1579,"The imam is only appointed to be followed. So when he prays and says the takbir, you say the takbir; when he bows, you bow; and do not differ from him. When he says: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' then you say: 'Our Lord, and to You belongs praise.' When he prostrates, you prostrate, and do not rush before him."],[1580,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us, saying: 'Do not race the imam. When the imam says the takbir, then you say the takbir; when he bows, then you bow; and when he says: \"not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,\" then you say: Ameen. When he says: \"Allah hears the one who praises Him,\" then you say: \"O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise.\" And do not race the imam in bowing and prostration.'"],[1581,"So when the imam says: 'Allahu Akbar,' then you say: 'Allahu Akbar.' And when he says: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' then you say: 'Our Lord, to You belongs praise.'"],[1582,"Samurah b. Jundub and Imran b. Husayn were conversing, and Samurah narrated that he had memorized from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ two silences: a silence when he said the takbir and a silence when he finished his recitation at the time of bowing."],[1583,"When the Prophet ﷺ said the takbir in prayer, he would pause between the takbir and the recitation. So I said to him: 'May my father and mother be your ransom, I saw your pause between the takbir and the recitation.'"],[1584,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to lengthen the first rak'ah of Fajr and Zuhr, and we used to think that he did that so that the people could catch up."],[1585,"The Prophet ﷺ led us in the morning prayer, and the recitation became heavy for him. When he finished, he said: 'I see that you recite behind your imam?' We said: 'Yes, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, quickly.' He said: 'Do not do that except with the Opening of the Book, for there is no prayer for one who does not recite it.'"],[1586,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us, saying: 'When the imam says the takbir, then you say the takbir. And when he recites: \"not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,\" then you say: Ameen.'"],[1587,"When the imam says Ameen, then you say Ameen. For whoever's saying of Ameen coincides with the saying of Ameen of the angels, his previous sins will be forgiven."],[1588,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari led us in prayer, and when he finished he said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon to us, explaining our sunnah and teaching us our prayer. He said: \"When the imam says the takbir, then you say the takbir. And when he says: 'not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray,' then you say: Ameen. Allah will love you.\"'"],[1589,"A Jew entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'As-Sam upon you (death be upon you).' The Prophet ﷺ responded: 'And upon you.' Aisha said: 'I wanted to speak, but I recognized the Prophet's dislike of that, so I remained silent. Then another entered and said: 'As-Sam upon you.' He responded: 'And upon you.' I wanted to speak, but I recognized the Prophet's dislike of that. Then the third entered and said: 'As-Sam upon you.' I could not bear it, so I said: 'And upon you be death, and the anger of Allah, and His curse, O brothers of apes and pigs! Do you greet the Messenger of Allah with what Allah has not greeted him?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Allah does not love obscenity and obscene speech. They said a statement, so we returned it to them. The Jews are an envious people, and they do not envy us for anything as they envy us for the greeting of salam and for Ameen.'"],[1590,"We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'Allah has given me three special qualities.' A man from among his companions asked: 'What are these qualities, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'He has given me prayer in rows, He has given me the greeting (at-tahiyyah), which is the greeting of the people of Paradise, and He has given me saying Ameen, which He did not give to any of the prophets before me, except that Allah gave it to Harun: Musa would supplicate and Harun would say Ameen.'"],[1591,"In His saying, Mighty and Majestic: “And do not recite [too] loudly in your prayer or [too] quietly” (Q 17:110), he said: “It was revealed while the Messenger of God ﷺ was hiding in Makkah. When he prayed with his companions, he would recite the Qur'an aloud.” Al-Dawraqi said: “He raised his voice with the Qur'an.” They both said: “The polytheists, when they heard it, would revile the Qur'an, the One who sent it down, and the one who brought it. So God said to His Prophet ﷺ: ‘And do not recite [too] loudly in your prayer,’ meaning in your recitation, ‘lest the polytheists hear and revile the Qur'an; and do not recite [too] quietly,’ away from your companions, ‘lest they not hear; but seek a way between that’ (Q 17:110).” Al-Dawraqi said: “Away from your companions, lest you make them hear.”"],[1592,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to recite in Zuhr, and sometimes he would let us hear a verse occasionally, and he would lengthen the first rak'ah."],[1593,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting in Maghrib with Surah al-Tur."],[1594,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting “By the fig and the olive” in the last Isha prayer, and I have never heard a recitation better than his ﷺ."],[1595,"He heard the Prophet ﷺ reciting Surah Qaf in the morning prayer, and I heard him recite: “and tall palm trees with clustered fruit” (Q 50:10). And he said once: “tall, with clustered fruit.”"],[1596,"While I was between the Corner and the Maqam, I heard him say: one whom he spoke to. Then he mentioned the hadith of the Mi'raj in its entirety, and he said: “Then it was announced: ‘Verily, for you, with every prayer, there are ten [rewards].’” He said: “So I descended. When the sun had passed the meridian, Jibril descended in a row of angels and prayed with him, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded his companions, so they formed rows behind him. He followed Jibril, and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ followed the Prophet ﷺ. He prayed four [rak'ahs] with them, reciting quietly. Then he left them until the sun declined and was white and pure. Jibril descended and prayed four [rak'ahs] with them, reciting quietly therein. The Prophet ﷺ followed Jibril, and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ followed the Prophet ﷺ. Then he left them until the sun had set. Jibril descended and prayed three [rak'ahs] with them, reciting aloud in two rak'ahs and quietly in one. The Prophet ﷺ followed Jibril, and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ followed the Prophet ﷺ. Then he left them until the twilight had disappeared. Jibril descended and prayed four rak'ahs with them: reciting aloud in two rak'ahs and quietly in two. The Prophet ﷺ followed Jibril, and the companions of the Prophet ﷺ followed the Prophet ﷺ . They spent the night until morning. Jibril descended and prayed two rak'ahs with them, lengthening the recitation therein.”"],[1597,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari led us in prayer. When he sat in the last part of his prayer, a man among them said: “Prayer has been joined with righteousness and zakah.” When Abu Musa al-Ash'ari finished, he said: “Which of you said the words such-and-such? Do you not know what you say in your prayer? The Messenger of God ﷺ addressed us and explained our Sunnah to us and taught us our prayer. He said: ‘When you pray, straighten your rows, and let one of you lead you. When the imam says the takbir, say the takbir. When he says: “not of those who earned wrath nor of those who went astray,” say: “Amin,” and God will love you. When he says the takbir and bows, say the takbir and bow. The imam bows before you and rises before you.’” The Prophet of God ﷺ said: “That is for that. When he says the takbir and prostrates, prostrate. The imam prostrates before you and rises before you.” Bundar added: The Prophet of God said: “That is for that.”"],[1598,"Indeed I have become heavy, so do not race me in bowing and prostration. For whatever I precede you in when I bow, you will catch up with me when I rise; and whatever I precede you in when I prostrate, you will catch up with me when I rise."],[1599,"Whoever catches a rak'ah of the prayer has caught it before the imam straightens his back."],[1600,"Abu Musa al-Ash'ari led us in prayer. When he sat in the last part of his prayer, a man among them said: “Prayer has been joined with righteousness and zakah.” When Abu Musa al-Ash'ari finished, he said: “Which of you said the words such-and-such? Do you not know what you say in your prayer? The Messenger of God ﷺ addressed us and explained our Sunnah to us and taught us our prayer. He said: ‘When you pray, straighten your rows, and let one of you lead you. When the imam says the takbir, say the takbir. When he says: “not of those who earned wrath nor of those who went astray,” say: “Amin,” and God will love you. When he says the takbir and bows, say the takbir and bow. The imam bows before you and rises before you.’” The Prophet of God ﷺ said: “That is for that. When he says the takbir and prostrates, prostrate. The imam prostrates before you and rises before you.” Bundar added: The Prophet of God said: “That is for that.”"],[1601,"Whoever obeys me has obeyed Allah, and whoever disobeys me has disobeyed Allah. Whoever obeys the amir has obeyed me, and whoever disobeys the amir has disobeyed me. The imam is only a shield. So if he prays sitting, then pray sitting. And when he says, 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' then say, 'O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise.' If the saying of the people of the earth coincides with the saying of the people of heaven, his past sins are forgiven. Kisra will perish and there will be no Kisra after him, and Caesar will perish and there will be no Caesar after him."],[1602,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head from bowing, we would remain standing until we saw that he had prostrated."],[1603,"I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he raised his head from bowing, none of us would bend his back until we saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had settled in prostration."],[1604,"Does not the one who raises his head before the imam fear that Allah will turn his head into the head of a donkey?"],[1606,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said one day, after finishing the prayer, turning his face toward us: 'O people, I am your imam, so do not precede me in bowing, prostration, standing, sitting, or departing. For I see you from behind me. By the One in whose hand is my soul, if you saw what I saw, you would laugh little and weep much.' We said, 'O Messenger of Allah, what did you see?' He said, 'I saw Paradise and the Fire.'"],[1607,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose for the second rak'ah, he would begin with 'Alhamdu lillahi rabb il-'alamin' and would not pause."],[1608,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the lightest of people in prayer while completing it fully."],[1609,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'I keep away from the dawn prayer because of so-and-so, who lengthens it for us.' I never saw the Prophet ﷺ more angry in an exhortation than he was that day. He said, 'O people, there are among you those who drive people away. Whoever of you leads the people in prayer, let him be brief, for among them are the weak, the elderly, and those with needs.'"],[1610,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to be brief, and he would lead us in prayer with al-Saffat."],[1611,"My father had stopped praying with us. I said, 'What is the matter with you that you do not pray with us?' He said, 'You make the prayer too brief!' I said, 'Where is the saying of the Prophet ﷺ: \"Among you are the weak, the elderly, and those with needs\"?' He said, 'I heard 'Abdullah b. Mas'ud say that.' Then he led us in prayer three times longer than what you pray."],[1612,"The last instruction the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave me when he sent me to al-Ta'if was: 'O 'Uthman, be brief in prayer, and measure the people by the weakest among them, for among them are the elderly, the weak, the sick, and those with needs.'"],[1613,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to hear the crying of a child with its mother, so he would recite a short or light surah."],[1614,"Indeed I enter the prayer intending to lengthen it, then I hear the crying of a child, so I shorten my prayer because of what I know of his mother's distress from his crying."],[1615,"Mu'adh used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return to his people and lead them in prayer. One night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the 'Isha' prayer, then Mu'adh returned and led his people. He began with Surah al-Baqarah, so a man stepped aside and prayed alone, then left. They said, 'What is the matter with you, O so-and-so? Have you become a hypocrite?' He said, 'I have not become a hypocrite, and I will certainly go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and inform him!' So he went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, Mu'adh prays with you then returns and leads us. Last night you delayed the 'Isha' prayer, then he came and led us, and he began with Surah al-Baqarah. We are people who work with our hands at watering camels!' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'Are you a troublemaker, O Mu'adh? Recite Surah such-and-such, and Surah such-and-such.' We said to 'Amr: 'Abu al-Zubayr says: \"Glorify the name of your Lord [the Most High]\" and \"By the sky and the night comer\"?' He said, 'It is something like that.'"],[1617,"The imam is a guarantor, or a leader. If he prays sitting, then pray sitting; and if he prays standing, then pray standing."],[1618,"People entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was ill, and he prayed with them sitting, so they prayed standing. He gestured to them to sit, and said: 'The imam is only to be followed. When he prays sitting, pray sitting; when he prays standing, pray standing; when he bows, bow; when he prostrates, prostrate; and when he rises, rise.'"],[1619,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rode a horse in Madinah, and it threw him onto a palm stump, dislocating his foot. We came to visit him and found him in an upper room of 'A'ishah's, glorifying Allah while sitting. We stood behind him, and he gestured to us, so we sat down. When he finished the prayer, he said: 'When the imam prays sitting, pray sitting; and when the imam prays standing, pray standing. Do not do as the Persians do with their great ones.'"],[1620,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ became ill with the illness in which he died, Bilal came to inform him of the prayer. He said: 'Tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.' We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, Abu Bakr is a tender-hearted man, and whenever he stands in your place he weeps and cannot manage it. If only you would order 'Umar to lead the people in prayer!' He said: 'Tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer' - three times - 'for you are like the women of Yusuf.' She said: So we sent for Abu Bakr, and he led the people in prayer. The Prophet ﷺ then felt some relief and came out, supported between two men, his feet dragging on the ground. When Abu Bakr sensed him, he moved to step back, but the Prophet ﷺ gestured to him: 'Stay where you are.' He said: The Prophet ﷺ came and sat beside Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr followed the Prophet ﷺ, and the people followed Abu Bakr, ؓ . This is the hadith of Waki'. In the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah, he said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting, and Abu Bakr was standing.'"],[1622,"Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah. 1619 - It was also narrated from 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Abd Allah b. 'Utbah and Masruq b. al-Ajda', from 'A'ishah, that Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the row."],[1623,"Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah. 1619 - It was also narrated from 'Ubayd Allah b. 'Abd Allah b. 'Utbah and Masruq b. al-Ajda', from 'A'ishah, that Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the row."],[1624,"Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the row behind him."],[1625,"Abu Bakr led the people in prayer while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the row behind him."],[1626,"When you come and we are prostrating, then prostrate, and do not count it as anything. Whoever catches the rak'ah has caught the prayer."],[1627,"The Messenger of God ﷺ went out to Banu Amr b. Awf to reconcile between them. The time for prayer came, and the mu'adhdhin came to Abu Bakr and said, \"Will you lead the people in prayer so I may give the iqamah?\" He said, \"Yes.\" So Abu Bakr prayed. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ came while the people were in prayer, and he made his way through until he stood in the row. The people clapped, and Abu Bakr would not turn around during his prayer. When the people increased their clapping, he turned around and saw the Messenger of God ﷺ. The Messenger of God ﷺ gestured to him to remain in his place. Abu Bakr raised his hands and praised God for what the Messenger of God ﷺ had commanded him of that. Then Abu Bakr stepped back until he stood in the row, and the Messenger of God ﷺ came forward and prayed. When he finished, he said, \"O Abu Bakr, what prevented you from remaining when I commanded you?\" Abu Bakr said, \"It was not for the son of Abu Quhafah to pray in front of the Messenger of God ﷺ.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Why did I see you clapping so much? Whoever has something happen to him in his prayer, let him say subhan Allah; for when he says subhan Allah, attention is turned to him. Clapping is only for women.\""],[1628,"The Messenger of God ﷺ became ill and lost consciousness. Then he regained consciousness and said, \"Has the prayer come?\" We said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Tell Bilal to give the adhan, and tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\" Then he lost consciousness again. When he regained consciousness, he said, \"Has the prayer come?\" We said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Tell Bilal to give the adhan, and tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\" Then he lost consciousness again and then regained consciousness. 'A'ishah said, \"My father is a tender-hearted man, so if you would appoint someone else.\" Then he lost consciousness and regained it, saying, \"Has the prayer come?\" We said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Tell Bilal to give the adhan, and tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\" 'A'ishah said, \"My father is a tender-hearted man, so if you would appoint someone else.\" He said, \"You are like the female companions of Yusuf. Tell Bilal to give the adhan, and tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer.\" Then he lost consciousness, so they commanded Bilal and he gave the adhan and the iqamah, and they commanded Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. Then he regained consciousness and said, \"Has the prayer been established?\" I said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Bring me someone to lean on.\" They brought Burayrah and another man, and he leaned on them. Then he went out to the prayer and was seated next to Abu Bakr. Abu Bakr began to withdraw, but he held him until he finished the prayer."],[1629,"There was fighting among the Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, so he prayed Zuhr, then went to them to make peace. Then he said to Bilal: \"O Bilal, if 'Asr arrives and I have not come, tell Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer...\" - and he mentioned the hadith in full. He mentioned in the report that the Prophet ﷺ came and stood behind Abu Bakr, and gestured to him: \"Continue your prayer.\""],[1630,"We had a mat that we spread in the daytime, and the Messenger of God ﷺ would enclose it at night and pray in it. Some Muslims followed him, praying with his prayer. He became aware of them and said, \"Take on only what you can bear, for God does not tire until you tire.\" The deeds he loved most were those done consistently, even if small. And when he prayed a prayer, he would keep it consistent."],[1631,"Anas said. (H)"],[1632,"The prayer was established and the rows were straightened. The Messenger of God ﷺ came out to us, and when he stood at his place of prayer, he remembered that he was in a state of janabah. He gestured to us and said, \"Stay in your places.\" Then he entered, performed ghusl, came out, and led us in prayer."],[1633,"The Messenger of God ﷺ began the prayer, then gestured to them to stay in their places, then he went in, then came out with his head dripping, and prayed with them."],[1634,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ said the takbir in prayer, he would remain silent for a short while. I said, \"O Messenger of God, may my father and mother be ransomed for you, what do you say during your silence between the takbir and recitation?\" He said, \"I say: O God, distance me from my sins as You have distanced the East from the West. O God, purify me from my sins as a white garment is purified from dirt. O God, wash me from my sins with snow, water, and hail.\""],[1636,"A man came after the Messenger of God ﷺ had prayed. The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Which of you will trade with this man?\" A man from the people stood up and prayed with him."],[1637,"Mu'adh b. Jabal used to pray with the Messenger of God ﷺ, then return and lead his people in that same prayer."],[1638,"Mu'adh used to pray 'isha' with the Messenger of God ﷺ, then return and lead his companions. One day he returned and led them, and a young man from his people prayed behind him. When it became long for the young man, he prayed and left, taking the reins of his camel and departing. When Mu'adh finished, he was told about it. He said, \"This is hypocrisy. I will tell the Messenger of God ﷺ.\" Mu'adh told him what the young man had done. The young man said, \"O Messenger of God, he stays long with you, then returns and prolongs for us.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Are you a troublemaker, O Mu'adh?\" And he said to the young man, \"What do you do, O son of my brother, when you pray?\" He said, \"I recite the Opening of the Book, ask God for Paradise, and seek refuge from the Fire. I do not know your murmuring or Mu'adh's murmuring.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Mu'adh and I are about these two, or similar to that.\" The young man said, \"But Mu'adh will know when the people arrive and are informed that I have gone far, though he has come near.\" So they arrived, and the young man was martyred. The Prophet ﷺ later said to Mu'adh, \"What happened to my adversary and your adversary?\" Mu'adh said, \"O Messenger of God, he was true to God and I lied. He was martyred.\""],[1641,"I and 'Alqamah entered upon Ibn Mas'ud, and he said: Did these people pray behind you? We said: No. He said: Then get up and pray. We went to stand behind him, but he took us by the hands and placed one of us on his right and the other on his left, and he prayed without an adhan or an iqamah. When he bowed, he interlaced his fingers and placed them between his legs. When he finished praying, he said: This is how I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do. Then he said: There will be rulers who kill the prayer, strangling it until the time of the death rattle of the dead. Whoever of you lives to see that should pray the prayer at its time, and let him make his prayer with them a supererogatory prayer."],[1642,"Ibn Ziyad delayed the prayer, so 'Abd Allah b. al-Samit came to me, and I placed a chair for him, and he sat on it. I mentioned to him what Ibn Ziyad had done, and he bit his lips, then struck his hand on my thigh and said: I asked Abu Dharr, and he struck my thigh as I struck your thigh, and said: I asked the Messenger of God ﷺ as you asked me, and he struck my thigh as I struck your thigh, and said: Pray the prayer at its time. Then if you catch it with them, pray, and do not say: I have already prayed, so I will not pray. This is the hadith of Bundar, and Yahya b. Hakim said: He bit his lips."],[1644,"How will you be when you remain among a people who delay the prayer beyond its time? He said to him: Pray the prayer at its time. Then if you catch them and they have not prayed, pray with them, and do not say: I have already prayed, so I will not pray."],[1645,"Perhaps you will live to see people who pray the prayer at other than its time. If you catch them, pray in your homes at the time that you know, then pray with them, and make it a supererogatory prayer."],[1646,"I came upon Ibn 'Umar while he was sitting on the paved area and the people were praying. I said: Will you not pray? He said: I have already prayed. I said: Will you not pray with them? He said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Do not pray a prayer twice in one day."],[1647,"The Messenger of God ﷺ turned aside, and I was with him, during the expedition of Tabuk before dawn. I turned aside with him, and the Messenger of God ﷺ made his camel kneel to relieve himself. I poured water from the waterskin over his hands, and he washed his palms, then he washed his face, then he uncovered his forearms, and the sleeves of his jubbah were tight, so he inserted his hand and brought them out from under the jubbah, and he washed them up to the elbows, then he wiped his head, then he performed ablution over his leather socks, then he mounted. We proceeded until we found the people in prayer, having put 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf forward, and he had led them in one rak'ah of the dawn prayer. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood and joined the row with the Muslims, and prayed behind 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf the second rak'ah. Then 'Abd al-Rahman b. 'Awf said the salam, and the Messenger of God ﷺ stood to complete his prayer. The Muslims were alarmed and said subhan Allah repeatedly because they had preceded the Messenger of God ﷺ in prayer. When the Messenger of God ﷺ said the salam, he said to them: You have done well, or you have done correctly."],[1648,"A young man stood up to 'Imran b. Husayn, and he said: He took hold of the bridle of his mount and asked him about the prayer during travel. He turned to us and said: This young man is asking me about a matter, and I would like to tell all of you about it. I went on military expeditions with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he did not pray except two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he returned to Madinah. Ziyad b. Ayyub added: And I performed the pilgrimage with him, and he did not pray except two rak'ahs until he returned to Madinah. And both of them said: He stayed in Makkah during the conquest for eighteen nights, praying two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, then he said to the people of Makkah: Pray four, for we are a traveling people. And I went on military expeditions with Abu Bakr, and I performed the pilgrimage with him, and he did not pray except two rak'ahs until he returned. And I performed the pilgrimage with 'Umar several times, and he did not pray except two rak'ahs until he returned. And 'Uthman prayed it for seven years of his rule as two rak'ahs during the pilgrimage until he returned to Madinah, then after that he prayed it as four. Ahmad added: Then he said: Have I made it clear to you? We said: Yes."],[1649,"While we were with the Messenger of God ﷺ, he heard a noise. He said: What is the matter with you? They said: O Messenger of God, we hurried to the prayer. He said: Do not do that. When the prayer has been established, do not stand until you see me. You must be calm. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, complete."],[1650,"There are two matters about which I will not ask anyone after what I witnessed from the Messenger of God ﷺ. We were with him on a journey, and he went out to relieve himself, then he came and performed ablution, wiping his forelock and the sides of his turban, and wiped over his leather socks. He said: And the prayer of the imam behind a man with his congregation. And I witnessed from the Messenger of God ﷺ that he was on a journey, and the prayer time came, but the Prophet ﷺ was delayed from them, so they established the prayer and put Ibn 'Awf forward, and he led them in part of the prayer. Then the Prophet ﷺ came and prayed behind Ibn 'Awf the remainder of the prayer. When Ibn 'Awf said the salam, the Prophet ﷺ stood and made up what he had missed."],[1651,"When the prayer has been established, come to it with calmness and dignity. Pray what you catch, and complete what you miss."],[1652,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed and omitted a verse, and Ubayy b. Ka'b was among the people. He said: O Messenger of God, did you forget such-and-such verse, or has it been abrogated? He said: I forgot it."],[1653,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite in prayer, and he omitted something he did not recite. A man said to him, 'O Messenger of Allah, did you omit such-and-such a verse?' He said, 'Why did you not prompt me?' Muhammad b. Yahya added: 'I thought it had been abrogated.'"],[1654,"I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of the conquest, and he prayed the morning prayer, then he took off his sandals and placed them to his left."],[1655,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not come out to us for three days."],[1656,"When dinner is served and the prayer is called, begin with dinner."],[1657,"ʿAbd Allāh b. al-Arqam used to travel and people who followed his example would accompany him. He would call the adhan for his companions and lead them in prayer. One day the call to prayer was made, then he said: 'Let one of you lead you in prayer, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: If one of you needs to relieve himself and the prayer is called, let him begin with relieving himself.'"],[1658,"ʿItbān b. Mālik, who was one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and one of the Anṣār who witnessed Badr, came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have noticed my eyesight is failing, and I lead my people in prayer. When it rains, the valley between me and them floods and I cannot reach their mosque to lead them. I would like you, O Messenger of Allah, to come and pray in a place I will take as a prayer place.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'I will do so, God willing.' ʿItbān b. Mālik said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Abū Bakr came in the morning after the sun had risen, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked permission, and I gave him permission. He did not sit until he entered the house, then he said: 'Where would you like me to pray in your house?' I pointed to a corner of the house. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and said the takbīr, and we stood and formed rows, and he prayed two rakʿahs then said the salām. We seated him on a dish of khazīrah that we had prepared for him. Some men from the neighborhood gathered around us until a number of men had collected in the house. He said: 'Where is Mālik b. al-Dukhayshin?' Some of them said: 'He is a hypocrite who does not love Allah and His Messenger.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: 'Do not say that. Do you not see that he says lā ilāha illā Allāh, seeking thereby the face of Allah?' He said: 'Allah and His Messenger know best. We see his face and his advice are towards the hypocrites.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Allah has forbidden the Fire for whoever says lā ilāha illā Allāh, seeking thereby the face of Allah.'"],[1659,"I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'I have noticed my eyesight is failing, and the floods come between me and the mosque of my people. I would like you to come and pray in a place in my house that I will take as a mosque.' The Prophet ﷺ said: 'I will do so, God willing.'"],[1660,"He called the adhan at Ḍajnān one night for the evening prayer, then said: 'Pray in your dwellings.' Then he narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that on a rainy and cold night during travel."],[1662,"I went out on a dark night to the mosque for the evening prayer. When I returned and asked for the door to be opened, my father said: 'Who is this?' They said: 'Abū Mulayḥ.' He said: 'I remember us with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyah when a light rain fell on us that did not even wet the soles of our sandals, and the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced: Pray in your dwellings.'"],[1663,"Rain fell on us with the Prophet ﷺ on the day of Hunayn, so the Prophet ﷺ said: Prayer is in the dwellings."],[1664,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and we were rained upon, so he said: Let whoever wishes among you pray in his dwelling."],[1665,"When Abu Hurayrah died, I said: By Allah, if I were to go to Abu Sa'id al-Khudri... So I went to him, and he mentioned a long hadith about the story of the palm stalks. He said: Then the sky stirred that night, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out for the Isha prayer, a lightning flash occurred, and he saw Qatadah b. al-Nu'man, so he said: \"What is the night travel, O Qatadah?\" He said: I knew, O Messenger of Allah, that those witnessing the prayer tonight would be few, so I wished to attend it. He said: \"When you pray, stay put until I pass by you.\" When he finished, he gave him the palm stalk and said: \"Take this, it will light for you ten in front of you and ten behind you. When you enter your house and see a blackness in a corner of the house, strike it before you speak, for it is Satan.\" He said: He did so, and that is why we love these palm stalks."],[1666,"Whoever eats from this plant - meaning garlic - must not come to the mosques."],[1667,"Whoever eats from this vegetable must not harm us with it in this mosque of ours."],[1668,"Whoever spits toward the qiblah will come on the Day of Resurrection with his spit between his eyes, and whoever eats from this foul vegetable must not come near our mosque."],[1669,"Whoever eats garlic or onions must keep away from us, or keep away from our mosque, and sit in his house."],[1670,"Whoever eats from this plant - garlic - then he said after: \"and onions and leeks, must not come near our mosque, for the angels are harmed by what harms humans.\""],[1671,"Umar b. al-Khattab ؓ addressed the people on Friday and then said: O people, you eat two plants that I consider nothing but foul: this garlic and this onion. I used to see a man whose breath was detected, so he would be taken by the hand and brought out to al-Baqi'. Whoever eats them must kill them by cooking."],[1672,"No sooner had Khaybar been conquered than we fell upon that vegetable - garlic - and ate it greedily. He said: Some people were hungry. Then we went to the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ detected the smell, so he said: Whoever eats from this foul plant must not come near us in our mosque. The people said: It has been forbidden, it has been forbidden. That reached the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"O people, it is not for me to forbid what Allah has made lawful, but it is a plant whose smell I dislike.\""],[1673,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade eating onions and leeks. He said: There was no garlic in our land at that time. He said: Whoever eats from this plant must not come near our mosque, for the angels are harmed by what harms humans."],[1674,"Garlic, onions, and leeks were mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and it was said: O Messenger of Allah, the strongest of all that is garlic; will you forbid it? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Eat it, but whoever among you eats it must not come near this mosque until its smell leaves him.\""],[1675,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sent him some food with vegetables in which there was onion or leek, and he did not see the trace of the Messenger of God ﷺ in it, so he refused to eat it. The Messenger of God ﷺ said to him: \"What prevented you from eating?\" He said: I did not see your trace in it, O Messenger of God. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: I am shy before the angels of God, and it is not prohibited."],[1676,"The Prophet ﷺ stayed with us, and we prepared for him some food in which there were some vegetables. When it was placed before him, he said to his companions: Eat, for I am not like any one of you; I fear that I might offend my companion. Abu Qudamah said: From Umm Ayyub: I stayed with her, and she narrated to me, saying: He stayed with us."],[1677,"I ate garlic, then I came to the Prophet ﷺ and found that he had preceded me by one rak'a. When he finished the prayer, I stood to make up what I had missed, and he smelled the odor of garlic and said: Whoever eats this vegetable should not come near our mosque until its smell has gone. When I finished the prayer, I went to him and said: O Messenger of God ﷺ I have an excuse; give me your hand. I found him easygoing, so he gave me his hand, and I inserted it from my sleeve to my chest, and he found it bandaged. He said: \"You have an excuse.\""],[1678,"The Messenger of God ﷺ and Abu Bakr went early in the day when the sun had risen. [The Messenger of God] ﷺ asked permission, and I gave him permission, but he did not sit until he entered the house. Then he said: Where would you like me to pray in your house? He said: So I pointed to a corner of the house. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood up and said the takbir, and we stood and formed rows, and he prayed two rak'as, then said the taslim."],[1679,"We came with the Messenger of God ﷺ until when we were at al-Suqya or at al-Qaha, he said: Is there a man who will go to the pool of al-Uthaya, repair it with mud, draw water into it, and draw for us into our water-skins until we come to it? I said: I am a man. Jabir b. Sakhr said: I am a man. So we set out on foot until we reached it in the evening. We repaired the pool with mud and drew water into it. Then we laid our heads down until the night grew dark. A man came and stood at the pool, his she-camel pulling him toward the pool while he pulled her reins, then he said: \"Do you two permit me to water my camel?\" And behold, it was the Messenger of God ﷺ, so we said: Yes, may our fathers and mothers be ransomed for you. He let her loose, and she drank until she was satisfied. Then Jabir b. 'Abd Allah said to us: He came near and made his camel kneel at the stony ground at al-'Arj. He went out to relieve himself, and I poured water for his ablution, and he performed ablution. He wrapped himself in his waist-wrapper, and I stood on his left. He placed me on his right. Then another man came and stood on his left. The Messenger of God ﷺ stepped forward and prayed, and we prayed with him thirteen rak'as including witr."],[1680,"He spent the night at the house of Maymuna, the Mother of the Believers, who was his maternal aunt. I lay down across the width of the cushion, and the Messenger of God ﷺ and his wife lay along its length. He slept until the middle of the night, or a little before or after it. The Messenger of God ﷺ woke up and sat up, wiping his face with his hand. Then he recited the ten closing verses of Surah Al 'Imran. Then he went to a hanging water-skin, performed ablution from it, and did his ablution well. Then he stood to pray. Ibn 'Abbas said: I got up and stood beside him. The Messenger of God ﷺ placed his right hand on my head and took hold of my right ear and twisted it. He prayed two rak'as, then two light rak'as. Then he went out and prayed the dawn prayer."],[1681,"Let us go and visit the martyr. He gave her permission that the call to prayer be made for her, and that she lead the people of her household in the obligatory prayer. She had memorized the Qur'an."],[1682,"If one of you's wife asks permission to go to the mosque, he should not prevent her."],[1683,"Do not prevent your women from going to the mosques at night."],[1684,"Do not prevent the female servants of God from the mosques of God, and let them go out when they go out without perfume."],[1685,"When one of you women attends the mosque, she should not touch perfume."],[1686,"Any woman who perfumes herself and passes by a group so that they may smell her scent is an adulteress, and every eye is adulterous."],[1687,"A woman passed by Abu Hurayrah while her fragrance was blowing strongly. He said to her, \"Where do you intend to go, O female slave of the Compeller?\" She said, \"To the mosque.\" He said, \"Have you applied perfume?\" She said, \"Yes.\" He said, \"Then go back and perform ghusl, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Allah does not accept from a woman a prayer when she goes out to the mosque while her fragrance is blowing strongly until she returns and performs ghusl.'\""],[1688,"The best mosques for women are the innermost parts of their houses."],[1689,"Do not prevent your women from the mosques, but their houses are better for them. A son of 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar said, \"No, by Allah, we shall prevent them.\" Ibn 'Umar said, \"You hear me narrating from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and you say what you say?\""],[1690,"The woman is 'awrah. When she goes out, the Shaytan seeks to tempt her, and the closest she is to the face of her Lord is when she is in the innermost part of her house."],[1691,"The woman is 'awrah. When she goes out, the Shaytan seeks to tempt her, and she is not closer to the face of Allah than when she is in the innermost part of her house."],[1692,"Sa'id b. Bashir narrated to us from Qatadah from Muwarriq from Abu al-Ahwas from 'Abd Allah that the Prophet ﷺ said something similar. Abu Bakr said: \"I only said, 'Did Qatadah hear this report from Abu al-Ahwas?' because of Sulayman al-Taymi's narration of this report from Qatadah from Abu al-Ahwas; for he dropped Muwarriq from the chain, while Hammam and Sa'id b. Bashir included Muwarriq in the chain. And I also doubted its authenticity because I do not find evidence that Qatadah heard this report from Muwarriq.\""],[1693,"A woman's prayer in her house is greater than her prayer in her chamber."],[1694,"She came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, \"O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I love to pray with you.\" He said, \"I know that you love to pray with me. Your prayer in your house is better than your prayer in your chamber; your prayer in your chamber is better than your prayer in your dwelling; your prayer in your dwelling is better than your prayer in your people's mosque; and your prayer in your people's mosque is better than your prayer in my mosque.\" She gave instructions, and a mosque was built for her in the farthest and darkest part of her house, and she used to pray there until she met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."],[1695,"A woman's prayer in her inner room is better than her prayer in her house, and her prayer in her house is better than her prayer in her chamber."],[1696,"The prayer most beloved to Allah that a woman performs is in the darkest, most secluded place of her house."],[1697,"The prayer most beloved to Allah that a woman performs is to pray in the darkest, most secluded place of her house."],[1698,"The best of the rows of men is the first one, and the worst of them is the last one; and the best of the rows of women is the last one, and the worst of them is the first one."],[1699,"\"O company of women, when the men prostrate, guard your gazes.\" I said to 'Abdullah, \"From what is that?\" He said, \"From the tightness of the waist-wrappers.\""],[1700,"\"[Guard your gazes] from the private areas of the men.\" Then he mentioned the hadith:"],[1701,"The women used to be commanded during prayer in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ not to raise their heads until the men had taken their sitting places, due to the ugliness of the garments."],[1703,"A beautiful woman, one of the most beautiful of people, used to pray behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Some of the people would advance to the first row so as not to see her, and some would remain behind until they were in the rear row, and when they bowed they would look from under their armpits. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning her: 'And We know those of you who press forward, and We know those who hang back.'"],[1704,"Nuh narrated to us, from 'Amr b. Malik, something similar to it:"],[1705,"\"If the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had seen what women have brought about after him, he would have prevented them from the mosques, just as the women of the Children of Israel were prevented.\" I said, \"What is this? Were the women of the Children of Israel prevented?\" She said, \"Yes.\""],[1706,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the world and said: \"Indeed the world is green and sweet, so beware of it, and beware of women.\" Then he mentioned three women of the Children of Israel: two tall women who were known, and a short woman who was not known. She took two wooden legs and fashioned a ring, and stuffed it with the most fragrant of perfume, musk, and made for it a clasp. When she passed by the mosque or by the assembly, she would gesture with it and open it, and its fragrance would waft. The narrator said, \"He indicated with his left little finger, raising it slightly apart from his other three fingers, while holding the three closed.\""],[1707,"When he saw women, he would say: \"Put them behind where Allah has placed them.\" And he said: \"Among the Children of Israel, they used to stand in rows with the men. A woman would wear a wooden sandal and make herself tall for her lover. Then menstruation was imposed upon them, and the mosques were forbidden to them.\" And when 'Abdullah saw them, he would say: \"Put them behind where Allah has placed them.\""],[1708,"When three come together, one of them should lead them, and the most entitled of them to lead is the one who recites the Qur'an best."],[1709,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with us in Mina, when we were at our greatest number and most secure, two rak'ahs."],[1710,"We were detained on the Day of the Trench from prayer until after sunset, with a portion of the night having passed, until we were relieved. That is His saying: 'And Allah sufficed the believers in battle; and Allah is Strong, Mighty.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called Bilal, and he gave the iqamah for prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr as beautifully as he used to pray it. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed 'Asr likewise. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed Maghrib likewise. Then he gave the iqamah and prayed 'Isha' likewise, before the prayer of fear was revealed: either on foot or mounted."],[1711,"They went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ in the year of Tabuk. The Messenger of God ﷺ used to combine Zuhr and ʿAsr, and Maghrib and ʿIsha. He said: One day he delayed the prayer, then he came out and prayed Zuhr and ʿAsr together, then he entered, then he came out and prayed Maghrib and ʿIsha together."],[1712,"Nafiʿ b. Jubayr sent me to al-Saʾib b. Yazid to ask him. I asked him, and he said: Yes, I prayed Jumuʿah in the enclosure with Muʿawiyah. When he said the salutation, I stood up to pray. He sent for me, so I came to him, and he said: When you pray Jumuʿah, do not connect it with another prayer unless you leave or speak, for the Messenger of God ﷺ commanded that."],[1713,"I used to recognize the end of the Messenger of God's ﷺ prayer by the takbir."],[1714,"Raising the voice with remembrance when departing from the obligatory prayer was practiced in the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Ibn ʿAbbas said: I would know when they had departed by that when I heard it."],[1715,"When we prayed behind the Messenger of God ﷺ, one of us would gesture to his brother with his hand, to his right and to his left. When the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed, he said: What is the matter with one of you doing this, as if they were the tails of restive horses? It is enough for one of you - or - is it not enough for one of you to say like this? And he placed his hand on his right thigh and pointed with his finger, then he gave salutation to his brother on his right and on his left."],[1716,"He was aware of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he was aware of a mouthful of water that the Messenger of God ﷺ spat from a bucket from a well in their house into his face. Mahmud claimed that he heard ʿItban b. Malik al-Ansari, who was among those who witnessed Badr with the Messenger of God ﷺ, say: I used to lead my people Banu Salim in prayer, and a valley separated me from them when the rains came. He said: It was hard for me to cross it to their mosque, so I came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said to him: I have noticed my eyesight, and the valley that separates me from my people overflows when the rains come, and it is hard for me to cross it. I wish you would come to me and pray in my house in a place that I would take as a prayer place. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: I will do so. The Messenger of God ﷺ came to me with Abu Bakr after the day had advanced, and the Messenger of God ﷺ asked my permission, and I gave him permission. He did not sit until he said: Where would you like me to pray for you in your house? I pointed to the place where I would like him to pray. The Messenger of God ﷺ stood and said the takbir, and we lined up behind him. He bowed two rakʿahs, then he said the salutation, and we said the salutation when he said it."],[1717,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to give salutation to our right sides, and that some of us should return it to others."],[1718,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to return the salutation to our imams, to love one another, and that some of us should greet others."],[1719,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ give salutation to his right and to his left until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen. Al-Zuhri said: This has not been heard from the hadith of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Ismaʿil said: Have you heard all the hadith of the Prophet ﷺ? He said: No. He said: Then two-thirds? He said: No. He said: Then half? He said: No. He said: Then this is in the half that you have not heard."],[1720,"I witnessed the Hajj of the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said: I prayed Fajr with him in the mosque of al-Khayf. When he finished his prayer and turned aside, there were two men at the end of the people."],[1721,"Let not one of you make for Satan a portion of himself, seeing that it is only obligatory upon him not to depart except from his right. Most of what I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ depart was from his left."],[1722,"The Messenger of God ﷺ led us in prayer one day, and when he said the salutation, he turned his face toward us."],[1723,"One day, he (the Prophet) ﷺ finished the prayer and turned his face toward us and said: “O people, I am your imam, so do not precede me in bowing, nor in prostrating, nor in standing, nor in sitting, nor in leaving. I see you from behind me, and by the One in whose hand is my soul, if you saw what I saw, you would laugh little and weep much.” We said: “O Messenger of God, what did you see?” He said: “I saw Paradise and the Fire.” This is Harun's hadith. ʿAli did not say “nor in sitting,” and he said: “I see you from in front of me and from behind me.”"],[1724,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was the lightest of people in prayer while completing it fully. He said: I prayed with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and the moment he gave the salutation he would stand up, then I prayed with Abu Bakr, and when he gave the salutation he sprang up from his place as if he were rising from hot stones."],[1725,"The women in the time of the Prophet ﷺ, when they gave the salutation after the obligatory prayer, would get up, while the Messenger of God ﷺ and the men who prayed behind him would remain seated. Then when the Messenger of God ﷺ stood up, the men would stand up."],[1726,"When he (the Prophet) ﷺ gave the salutation after the prayer, he would not remain except a little while, until he stood up."],[1727,"Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Aʿraj, from Abu Hurayrah; and from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said…"],[1728,"Upon every adult male is the going to Friday prayer, and upon whoever goes to Friday prayer is the ritual bath."],[1729,"The Prophet ﷺ gathered the women of the Ansar in a house, and ʿUmar came to us, stood at the door, gave the greeting, and we returned the greeting to him. He said: “I am the messenger of the Messenger of God ﷺ to you.” We said: “Welcome to the Messenger of God and his messenger.” He said: “Do you pledge allegiance that you will not associate anything with God, nor steal, nor commit adultery?” She said: We said: “Yes,” and we stretched out our hands from inside the house, and he stretched out his hand from outside. She said: And he ordered us to bring out the menstruating women and the adolescent girls on the two Eid days, and we were forbidden from following funeral processions, and there is no Friday prayer upon us. He said: I said to her: “What is the good deed that you were forbidden from?” She said: “Wailing.”"],[1730,"Abu ʿAsim narrated to us, from Ishaq b. ʿUthman, with the like of it. And he did not say: “You will not associate anything with God,” …"],[1731,"I was the guide of my father Kaʿb b. Malik when he lost his sight. When I went out with him to Friday prayer and he heard the call to prayer, he would pray for Abu Umamah Asʿad b. Zurarah. He continued doing that for a while, never hearing the call to Friday prayer without praying for him and asking forgiveness for him. I said to myself: By God, this is a shortcoming on my part that I do not ask him why, when he hears the call to Friday prayer, he prays for Abu Umamah Asʿad b. Zurarah. So I went out with him one Friday as I used to, and when he heard the call to Friday prayer, he prayed for Abu Umamah and asked forgiveness for him. I said to him: “O my father, why is it that when you hear the call to Friday prayer, you pray for Abu Umamah?” He said: “O my son, he was the first to lead the Friday prayer in Madinah, at Hazm Bani Bayadah, in a place called Naqiʿ al-Khadimat.” I said: “How many were you that day?” He said: “Forty men.”"],[1732,"The first Friday prayer held after the Friday prayer in the mosque of the Messenger of God ﷺ was in the mosque of ʿAbd al-Qays at Juwatha in al-Bahrayn."],[1733,"The sun has not risen nor set on a day better than Friday. Allah guided us to it, and the people went astray from it, and the people are followers for us in it. So it is for us, and for the Jews Saturday, and for the Christians Sunday. Indeed, in it there is an hour which no believer coincides with while praying and asking Allah for something except that He gives it to him."],[1734,"The sun does not rise on a day nor set on one more excellent or greater than Friday. And there is no creature that is not frightened on account of Friday except these two heavy ones: the jinn and mankind."],[1735,"The master of days is Friday. In it Adam was created, in it he was admitted to Paradise, and in it he was expelled from it. And the Hour will not be established except on Friday."],[1736,"The best day on which the sun has risen is Friday. In it Adam was created, in it he was admitted to Paradise, and in it he was expelled from it, and in it the Hour will be established."],[1737,"Indeed, Allah will resurrect the days on the Day of Judgment in their forms, and He will resurrect Friday as radiant and shining. Its people will surround it like a bride being escorted to her honored one. It will shine for them, and they will walk in its light. Their colors will be as white as snow, and their fragrance will spread like musk. They will wade through mountains of camphor. The two heavy ones will look at them, not lowering their gaze in amazement, until they enter Paradise. No one will mix with them except the mu'adhdhins who seek reward."],[1738,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took me by the hand and said: Indeed, Allah created the soil on Saturday, created the mountains in it on Sunday, created the trees on Monday, created the disliked on Tuesday, created the light on Wednesday, spread the beasts in it on Thursday, and created Adam after the afternoon on Friday as the last creation in the last hour of the hours of Friday, between the afternoon and the night."],[1739,"\"O Salman, what is Friday?\" I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: \"O Salman, what is Friday?\" He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: \"O Salman, what is Friday?\" I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: \"O Salman, Friday? By it your father or your forefather was gathered. I will tell you about Friday. There is no man who purifies himself on Friday as you have been commanded, leaves his house until he comes to the Friday prayer and sits, then remains silent until he finishes his prayer, except that it will be an expiation for what came before it from the previous Friday.\""],[1740,"Indeed, among the best of your days is Friday. In it Adam was created, in it he was taken, in it is the blast, and in it is the thunderbolt. So increase in sending blessings upon me in it, for your blessings are presented to me.\" They said: How are our blessings presented to you when you have decayed? He said: \"Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden the earth to consume the bodies of the prophets.\""],[1741,"They mean: You have decayed."],[1742,"Indeed, in Friday there is an hour which no Muslim servant coincides with while asking Allah for something good except that He gives it to him."],[1743,"I came to al-Tur and met Ka'b al-Ahbar there. I narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he narrated from the Torah, and we did not disagree until I passed by the day of Friday. I said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"In every Friday there is an hour which no believer coincides with while he is praying, asking Allah for something, except that He gives it to him.\"' Ka'b said: 'Rather, in every year.' I said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not say that.' He went back and recited, then said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke the truth: in every Friday.' Then he mentioned the hadith in full with the story of Abdullah b. Salam."],[1744,"Indeed, in Friday there is an hour which no Muslim standing and praying coincides with while asking Allah for good in it except that He gives it to him. And he said with his hand: 'He belittles it and makes it insignificant.'"],[1745,"I came to al-Tur and met Ka'b al-Ahbar there. I narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he narrated from the Torah, and we did not disagree until I passed by the day of Friday. I said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"In every Friday there is an hour which no believer coincides with while he is praying, asking Allah for something, except that He gives it to him.\"' Ka'b said: 'Rather, in every year.' I said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not say that.' He went back and recited, then said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke the truth: in every Friday.' Then he mentioned the hadith in full with the story of Abdullah b. Salam."],[1746,"Abdullah b. Umar said to me: 'Did you hear your father narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the matter of the hour on Friday?' He said: 'I said: Yes, I heard him say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: It is between when the imam sits on the minbar until the prayer is completed.'"],[1747,"Indeed, in Friday there is an hour which no Muslim standing and praying coincides with while asking Allah for good in it except that He gives it to him. Ibn Awn said: And he said with his hand on his head. We said: 'He belittles it.'"],[1748,"By Allah, if I were to go to Abu Sa'id and ask him about this hour, perhaps he has knowledge of it. So I went to him and he mentioned a long hadith, and said: 'I said: O Abu Sa'id, indeed Abu Hurayrah...'"],[1749,"Taking a bath on Friday is obligatory on every adult male."],[1750,"Indeed, the ghusl on Friday is upon every adult male, and the siwak, and to touch some perfume if he is able to."],[1751,"Taking a bath on Friday is obligatory on every adult male, and he should touch perfume if he has any."],[1752,"Taking a bath on Friday is obligatory on every adult male, and to use the siwak, and to touch perfume if he finds it."],[1753,"Bathing on Friday is obligatory upon every adult."],[1755,"While ʿUmar was addressing the people on Friday, ʿUthmān b. ʿAffān entered, and he alluded to him, saying: What is the matter with men who delay after the call? ʿUthmān said: O Commander of the Faithful, I did no more than perform ablution when I heard the call and then came. He said: Ablution too? Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: When one of you comes to Friday, let him bathe?"],[1756,"Whoever of you comes to Friday, let him bathe."],[1757,"When one of you comes to Friday, let him bathe."],[1758,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said while addressing the people: When one of you comes to the mosque, let him bathe."],[1759,"Whoever attends Friday from among men and women, let him bathe; and whoever does not attend it, no bath is required of him from among men and women."],[1760,"The people were workers for themselves, and they used to go to Friday in their working clothes, so it was said to them: If only you would bathe."],[1761,"The people used to come to Friday from their dwellings in the outskirts, arriving in cloaks, and dust and sweat would afflict them, and odor would emanate from them. A man from among them came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was with me, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: If only you would purify yourselves for this day of yours."],[1762,"Two men from the people of Iraq came to him and asked him about bathing on Friday: Is it obligatory? Ibn ʿAbbās said to them: Whoever bathes, it is better and purer. I will tell you why bathing began: In the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the people were needy, wearing wool, and watering palm trees on their backs. The mosque was narrow with a low roof. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out on a Friday on a scorching hot summer day, and his pulpit was short—only three steps. He addressed the people, and the people sweated in the wool, and their odors arose—the smell of sweat and wool—until they were harming one another, until their odors reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was on the pulpit. He said: O people, when this day comes, bathe, and let each of you apply the best perfume or oil he finds."],[1763,"Whoever performs wudu on Friday and perfects the wudu, then comes to the Friday prayer, draws near, remains silent, and listens, will be forgiven for what occurred between that Friday and the next Friday, plus three additional days. And whoever touches the pebbles has engaged in idle talk."],[1764,"Whoever performs wudu has done well, and whoever performs ghusl, that is better."],[1765,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said, mentioning Friday: Whoever washes and performs ghusl, goes early, arrives early, draws near, remains silent, and does not engage in idle talk, for every step he takes will have the reward of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."],[1766,"Perform ghusl on Friday and wash your heads, even if you are not in a state of major ritual impurity, and apply perfume. Ibn Abbas said: As for perfume, I do not know; as for ghusl, yes."],[1767,"Abu Qatada entered upon me on Friday while I was performing ghusl. He said: Is this ghusl of yours for major ritual impurity? I said: Yes. He said: Then perform another ghusl, for I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Whoever performs ghusl on Friday will remain in a state of purity until the next Friday."],[1768,"It is a duty upon every Muslim to bathe every seven days, and to apply perfume if he finds it."],[1769,"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, uses the tooth-stick, applies perfume if he has it, wears the best of his clothes, then comes to the mosque, does not step over people's necks, then prays as much as God wills, then remains silent when his imam comes out until he prays, it will be an expiation for what occurred between that Friday and the Friday before it."],[1770,"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday and perfects the ghusl, then wears the best of his clothes, then applies some of the oil of his house that God has decreed for him, or some of his perfume, then does not separate between two people, God will expiate for him what occurred between that Friday and the Friday before it. Saeed said: I mentioned it to Umarah b. Amr b. Hazm, and he said: He spoke the truth, plus three additional days."],[1771,"Yahya b. Saeed narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Saeed al-Maqburi, from his father, this hadith. Abu Bakr said: Bundar said to us: I memorized it from his mouth, and from his father. But this, in my opinion, is an error. The correct chain is: from Saeed, from his father."],[1772,"The Prophet ﷺ gave a sermon on Friday and saw them wearing garments of Nimari cloth. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: What is wrong with one of you, if he finds the means, to acquire two garments for his Friday other than the two garments of his daily work?"],[1773,"The Prophet ﷺ had a fine suit that he would wear on the two Eids and on Friday."],[1774,"Whoever washes and performs ghusl, then goes early, arrives early, sits close to the imam, listens, and remains silent, will have the reward of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."],[1775,"The one who hastens to the Friday prayer is like one who offers a camel, the next like one who offers a cow, the next like one who offers a sheep, and the next like one who offers a bird."],[1776,"When Friday comes, at every gate of the mosque there are angels who record the people according to their ranks, the first then the next. When the imam comes out, the scrolls are folded up.\" 'Abd al-Jabbar said: \"When the imam sits down, they fold up the scrolls.\" They both said: \"And they listen to the sermon. The one who goes early to the prayer is like one who offers a camel, then the next like one who offers a cow, then the next like one who offers a ram,\" until he mentioned the chicken and the egg."],[1777,"At every gate of the mosque on Friday there are two angels who record the first then the next, like a man who presents a camel, like a man who presents a cow, like a man who presents a sheep, like a man who presents a bird, and like a man who presents an egg. When the imam sits down, the scrolls are folded up."],[1778,"The angels are sent to the gates of the mosque on Friday to record the arrival of the people. When the imam comes out, the scrolls are folded up and the pens are lifted. The angels say to one another: 'What kept so-and-so?' The angels say: 'O Allah, if he is astray, guide him; if he is sick, heal him; if he is poor, enrich him.'\" This is the hadith of al-Muqri'. Al-Quta'i said: \"The angels sit at the gates of the mosque.\" He also said: \"They say to one another: 'O Allah, if he is astray, guide him; if he is...\""],[1779,"When the prayer is established, do not come to it running, but come to it walking. Upon you is tranquility. Whatever you catch, pray, and whatever you miss, make up."],[1780,"The call that Allah mentioned in the Qur'an on Friday was when the imam came out, and when the prayer was established, during the time of the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar, until it was Uthman. The people became numerous, so he commanded the third call on al-Zawra'. It remained established until now."],[1781,"The adhan during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar was two adhans on Friday, until the time of Uthman. The people became numerous, so he commanded the first adhan at al-Zawra'."],[1782,"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, touches some perfume if he has it, wears his best clothes, then goes out to the mosque and prays if he wishes, and does not harm anyone, then listens attentively when his imam comes out until he prays, it will be an expiation for what is between it and the next Friday."],[1783,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stand on Friday leaning his back against a wooden pillar, a trunk, or a palm tree — al-Mubarak was uncertain — and when the people became numerous, he said: 'Build a pulpit for me.' So they built him a pulpit. When he turned to it, the piece of wood moaned like a grieving mother, and it did not cease until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended from the pulpit, came to it, and embraced it, and it became still."],[1784,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to stand on Friday and lean his back against a trunk erected in the mosque and deliver the sermon. A Byzantine came and said: 'Shall we make something for you to sit on, as if you were standing?' So he made him a pulpit with two steps, and he would sit on the third. When the Prophet of Allah ﷺ sat on the pulpit, the trunk bellowed like a bull, and the mosque shook with its bellowing out of grief for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended from the pulpit to it and embraced it while it was bellowing. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ embraced it, it became silent. Then he said: 'By the One in whose hand is my soul, if I had not embraced it, it would have remained like this until the Hour, out of grief for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that it be buried — meaning the trunk."],[1785,"He saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing on a bow or a staff when he came to them. He said: I heard him saying: 'By the sky and the night comer.' I knew it in the ignorant era while I was a polytheist, then I recited it in Islam. Thaqif called me and said: 'What did you hear from this man?' So I recited it to them. Those of Quraysh who were with them said: 'We know our companion better. If we knew that what he says is true, we would have followed him.'"],[1786,"They differed about the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, what it was made of. They sent to Sahl b. Sa'd, and he said: 'No one among the people remains who knows it better than I. It is from the tamarisk of al-Ghabah.'"],[1787,"When the Prophet ﷺ sat firmly on the minbar, he said to the people: \"Sit down.\" Ibn Mas'ud heard him while he was at the gate of the mosque, so he sat down. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"Come here, O Ibn Mas'ud.\""],[1788,"The Prophet ﷺ used to deliver two sermons on Friday, sitting between them."],[1789,"'Ammar b. Yasir delivered a sermon to us; he was thorough and concise. When he descended, we said to him: \"O Abu al-Yaqzan, you were thorough and concise. If only you had extended it.\" He said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Indeed, the length of a man's prayer and the brevity of his sermon are a sign of his understanding. So prolong the prayer and shorten the sermon, for some eloquence is magic.'\""],[1792,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say in his sermon, praising Allah and extolling Him with what He deserves, then he would say: \"Whomever Allah guides, none can mislead; and whomever He sends astray, none can guide. The truest speech is the Book of Allah, the best guidance is the guidance of Muhammad, the worst of matters are their innovations, and every innovation is a heresy, every heresy is an error, and every error leads to the Fire.\" Then he would say: \"I and the Hour have been sent like these two,\" and when he mentioned the Hour, his cheeks would redden, his voice would rise, and his anger would intensify, as if he were warning of an army: \"The Hour has come upon you morning and evening.\" Then he would say: \"Whoever leaves wealth, it is for his family; and whoever leaves debt or dependents, they are to me and upon me, and I am the guardian of the believers.\""],[1793,"I did not memorize Surah Qaf except from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he used to recite it every Friday. And our oven and the oven of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were one."],[1794,"I recited 'Qaf. By the glorious Qur'an' from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He used to recite it every Friday on the minbar when he addressed the people."],[1795,"A man entered the mosque on Friday from a gate that was near the court building while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing on the minbar delivering a sermon. He faced the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while standing, then said: \"O Messenger of Allah, wealth has been destroyed and the roads have been cut off. Supplicate to Allah to send us rain.\" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands, then said: \"O Allah, send us rain. O Allah, send us rain. O Allah, send us rain.\" Anas said: \"By Allah, we saw no cloud in the sky, nor any wisp of cloud, and there was no house or dwelling between us and Sal'.\" Then a cloud like a shield appeared from behind it, and when it reached the middle of the sky, it spread, and then it rained. Anas said: \"By Allah, we did not see the sun for seven days.\" He said: Then a man entered from that gate the following Friday while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was standing delivering a sermon. He faced him while standing and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, wealth has been destroyed and the roads have been cut off. Supplicate to Allah to withhold it from us.\" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his hands, then said: \"O Allah, around us and not upon us. O Allah, on the hills and the small mountains, and the bottoms of the valleys and the places where trees grow.\" He said: It cleared, and we went out walking in the sun."],[1796,"Anas was asked: \"Did the Prophet of Allah ﷺ raise his hands?\" He said: \"It was said on Friday: 'O Messenger of Allah, the rain has failed, the land has become barren, and the livestock have perished.'\" He said: \"So he raised his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits, and he prayed for rain while we saw no cloud in the sky.\" He said: \"We had not finished the prayer before the young man whose house was near was concerned about returning to his family due to the heavy rain. It lasted for a week. Then they said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the travelers have been detained.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and said with his hand: 'O Allah, around us and not upon us.' Then it cleared from Madinah.\""],[1797,"Anas was asked: \"Did the Prophet of Allah ﷺ raise his hands?\" He said: \"It was said on Friday: 'O Messenger of Allah, the rain has failed, the land has become barren, and the livestock have perished.'\" He said: \"So he raised his hands until I saw the whiteness of his armpits, and he prayed for rain while we saw no cloud in the sky.\" He said: \"We had not finished the prayer before the young man whose house was near was concerned about returning to his family due to the heavy rain. It lasted for a week. Then they said: 'O Messenger of Allah, the houses have collapsed and the travelers have been detained.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled and said with his hand: 'O Allah, around us and not upon us.' Then it cleared from Madinah.\""],[1798,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not raise his hands in any of his supplications, or during any of his supplications, except in seeking rain; for he would raise his hands until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."],[1800,"Bishr b. Marwan delivered a sermon while raising his hands supplicating. 'Umarah said: \"May Allah disfigure these two hands. I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the minbar, and he did not say anything except like this,\" indicating with his finger."],[1801,"Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, all of them from Husayn."],[1802,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon to us one day and recited Ṣād. When he came to the prostration verse, he descended and prostrated, and we prostrated. He recited it another time, and when he reached the prostration verse, we prepared ourselves to prostrate. When he saw us, he said, \"It is only the repentance of a prophet, but I see that you have readied yourselves for prostration.\" So he descended and prostrated, and we prostrated."],[1804,"When I approached the city of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I made my camel kneel, untied my saddlebag, put on my fine suit, and entered while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ greeted me, and the people stared at me. I said to the one sitting next to me, \"O servant of Allah, has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned anything about me?\" He said, \"Yes, he mentioned you in the best way. While he was preaching, it occurred in his sermon that he said, 'There will enter upon you from this door or from this mountain pass one of the best of the people of Yemen, and on his face there is the touch of an angel.'\" He said, \"So I praised Allah for what He had bestowed upon me.\""],[1805,"When I approached the city of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, I made my camel kneel, untied my saddlebag, put on my fine suit, and entered while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ greeted me, and the people stared at me. I said to the one sitting next to me, \"O servant of Allah, has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned anything about me?\" He said, \"Yes, he mentioned you in the best way. While he was preaching, it occurred in his sermon that he said, 'There will enter upon you from this door or from this mountain pass one of the best of the people of Yemen, and on his face there is the touch of an angel.'\" He said, \"So I praised Allah for what He had bestowed upon me.\""],[1806,"Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī entered on Friday while Marwān b. al-Ḥakam was delivering the sermon, and he stood to pray. The guards came to make him sit down, but he refused until he had prayed. When Marwān finished, we went to him and said, \"May Allah have mercy on you, they almost did something to you.\" He said, \"I would not leave them after something I saw from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" Then he mentioned that a man came on Friday while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching in a shabby condition, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the people to give charity, and they threw clothes. He ordered two garments for him and ordered him to pray two rakʿahs while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching. Then the next Friday came, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching, so he commanded the people to give charity. The man threw one of his two garments, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shouted at him or rebuked him and said, \"Take your garment.\" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, \"This man entered in a shabby condition, so I commanded the people to give charity, and they threw clothes. I ordered two garments for him. Then he entered today, and I commanded the people to give charity, so this man threw one of his two garments.\" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to pray two rakʿahs."],[1807,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was preaching and said, \"O Messenger of Allah, a stranger has come asking about his religion, he does not know what his religion is.\" He turned to me and left his sermon. A chair was brought to him, and I thought its legs were iron—Ḥumayd said: I think he saw black wood and thought it was iron—and he began teaching me from what Allah had taught him. Then he went back to his sermon and completed its end."],[1808,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching when al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn came, wearing two red shirts, stumbling and getting up. He descended, took them, and placed them in front of him, then said, \"Allah and His Messenger have spoken the truth: 'Your wealth and your children are only a trial' (Q 64:15). I saw these two and could not be patient.\" Then he resumed his sermon."],[1809,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching on the minbar—and he said: \"I could not be patient until I descended and carried them\"—but he did not say: \"Then he resumed his sermon.\""],[1810,"When it is Friday, and a man performs ghusl, washes his head, applies the best of his perfume, wears his good clothes, then goes out to the prayer and does not separate between two people, then listens to the imam, he will be forgiven from one Friday to the next, plus three more days."],[1811,"If you speak on Friday, you have engaged in idle talk and caused others to engage in idle talk."],[1812,"Muḥammad b. Muslim narrated to me, from Saʿīd b. al-Musayyib, that Abū Hurayrah said:"],[1813,"When a man says to another man while the imam is preaching, 'Listen,' he has engaged in idle talk. This is merely Abu Hurayrah's wording. Al-Makhzumi said: \"When you say to your companion, 'Listen,' on Friday while the imam is preaching, you have engaged in idle talk.\""],[1814,"I entered the mosque on Friday while the Prophet ﷺ was preaching, and I sat near Ubayy b. Ka'b. The Prophet ﷺ recited Surat Bara'ah, so I said to Ubayy, 'When was this surah revealed?' He frowned at me and did not speak to me. Then I waited for a while, then asked him again, and he frowned at me and did not speak to me. Then I waited for a while, then asked him again, and he frowned at me and did not speak to me. When the Prophet ﷺ finished praying, I said to Ubayy, 'I asked you and you frowned at me and did not speak to me.' Ubayy said, 'You have nothing from your prayer except what you engaged in idle talk.' So I went to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'O Prophet of Allah, I was next to Ubayy while you were reciting Bara'ah, and I asked him, \"When was this surah revealed?\" He frowned at me and did not speak to me, then said, \"You have nothing from your prayer except what you engaged in idle talk.\"' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Ubayy spoke the truth.'"],[1815,"Ibn Abi Maryam informed me with something similar to it: \":, :, :, : as in the Dar al-Miman edition, and in the Islamic Office edition.\""],[1816,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching on Friday when he recited a verse. A man who was next to Abdullah b. Mas'ud said, 'When was this verse revealed? For I have not heard it except just now.' Abdullah said, 'Glory be to Allah.' The man fell silent. Then he recited another verse, and the man said something similar to Abdullah, and Abdullah said, 'Glory be to Allah.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished the prayer, Ibn Mas'ud said to the man, 'You did not pray Friday with us.' He said, 'Glory be to Allah!' So he went to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'Ibn Umm Abd spoke the truth, Ibn Umm Abd spoke the truth.'"],[1818,"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, then touches some perfume of his wife if she has any, and wears his best clothes, then does not step over people's necks and does not engage in idle talk during the admonition, it will be an expiation for what is between them. And whoever engages in idle talk or steps over people, it will be merely a Zuhr prayer for him."],[1819,"I was sitting with Abdullah b. Busr on Friday, and he continued narrating to us until the imam came out. Then a man came stepping over people's necks, and he said to me, 'A man came stepping over people's necks while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was preaching, and he said to him, \"Sit down, for you have harmed and delayed.\"'"],[1820,"Whoever performs ghusl on Friday and does it well, or purifies himself and does it well, then wears his best clothes, and touches whatever perfume Allah has decreed for him or his family's oil, and does not separate between two people, Allah will forgive him until the next Friday."],[1821,"Three types of people attend Friday: a man who attends it and engages in idle talk, and that is his share from it; a man who attends it with supplication, and he is a man who called upon Allah, so if Allah wills, He will grant him, and if He wills, He will withhold from him; and a man who attends it with dignity, silence, and stillness, and does not step over a Muslim's neck and does not harm anyone, and it is an expiation for him until the following Friday, plus three days, because Allah says: 'Whoever brings a good deed shall have ten times its like.'"],[1822,"The five prayers, and Friday to Friday, are expiations for what is between them as long as major sins are not committed."],[1823,"That the Prophet ﷺ forbade sitting with the knees drawn up to the chest on Friday while the imam is preaching."],[1824,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade buying and selling in the mosques, that poems be recited in them, that a lost animal be announced in them, and (he forbade) circles on Friday before the prayer."],[1825,"When a man purifies himself and performs ablution well, then comes to Friday and does not engage in idle talk or act ignorantly until the imam finishes, it is an expiation for what is between it and the (next) Friday."],[1826,"Whoever performs ablution on Friday and performs it well, then comes to Friday, draws near, listens attentively, and remains silent, he will be forgiven what is between it and the (next) Friday plus three more days. And whoever touches the pebbles has engaged in idle talk."],[1827,"When one of you becomes drowsy on Friday in his sitting place, he should move from that sitting place of his."],[1828,"None of you should make his brother stand up from his seat and then take his place in it. I said to him: On Friday? He said: On Friday and other days. He said: Nafi' said: A man would stand up for Ibn 'Umar from his seat but he would not sit in it."],[1829,"When one of you stands up from his seat and then returns, he has more right to it."],[1830,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade a man making his brother stand up from his seat and then taking his place, but (he said) make room and spread out."],[1831,"That the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon standing, and a caravan from Syria arrived, so the people turned away to it until only twelve men remained. Then this verse that is in (surat) al-Jumu'ah was revealed: 'And when they see merchandise or diversion, they scatter to it and leave you standing.'"],[1832,"\"Give the mosques their due.\" It was said: \"What is their due?\" He said: \"Two rak'ahs before you sit.\""],[1833,"When one of you enters the mosque, let him pray two rak'ahs."],[1834,"Abd al-Rahman—meaning Ibn Mahdi—narrated to us from Malik, from 'Amir b. 'Abd Allah b. al-Zubayr with this chain of narration its like. He added: \"before he sits\" :, :, :"],[1835,"When one of you enters the mosque, he should not sit until he prays two rak'ahs."],[1836,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ one day, when he said: \"Did you enter the mosque?\" I said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Did you pray in it?\" I said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then go and pray two rak'ahs.\""],[1837,"I entered the mosque while the Messenger of God ﷺ was sitting among the people, so I sat down. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"What prevented you from praying two rak'ahs before sitting down?\" I said: \"O Messenger of God! I saw you sitting and the people sitting.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"When one of you enters the mosque, he should not sit down until he has prayed two rak'ahs.\""],[1838,"Marwan was delivering a sermon, and Abu Sa'id prayed. The guards came to him to make him sit down, but he refused until he had prayed. When he finished the prayer, we came to him and said: \"They almost did something to you; may God forgive you.\" He said: \"I will never abandon them after I heard it from the Messenger of God.\""],[1839,"When one of you comes to the mosque while the imam is delivering the sermon, let him pray two rak'ahs before sitting down."],[1840,"Sulayk al-Ghatafani entered the mosque on Friday while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon. He said to him: \"Have you prayed?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then pray two rak'ahs.\""],[1841,"A man entered while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon. He said: \"Have you prayed?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Stand and pray two rak'ahs.\""],[1842,"A man came while the Prophet ﷺ was on the pulpit on Friday delivering the sermon. He said to him: \"Have you prayed two rak'ahs?\" He said: \"No.\" He said: \"Then pray.\""],[1843,"Sulayk al-Ghatafani came on Friday while the Messenger of God ﷺ was delivering the sermon, and he sat down. He said to him: \"O Sulayk, stand and pray two rak'ahs, and make them brief.\" Then he said: \"When one of you comes on Friday while the imam is delivering the sermon, let him pray two rak'ahs and make them brief.\""],[1844,"Was Ibn Umar praying before the Friday prayer? He said: \"He used to lengthen the prayer before it, and he would pray two rak'ahs after it in his house, and he reported that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do that.\""],[1845,"The Messenger of God ﷺ had only one muezzin. When he came out, he gave the adhan; when he descended, he gave the iqamah. Abu Bakr and Umar were likewise. When it was Uthman and the people increased, he ordered a third call on a house in the market called al-Zawra'. When he came out, he gave the adhan; when he descended, he gave the iqamah."],[1846,"The Prophet ﷺ used to descend from the pulpit on Friday and speak with a man, and the man would speak with him, then he would go to his place of prayer and pray."],[1847,"We used to pray the Friday prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ when the sun had declined, then we would return and seek the shade."],[1848,"We used to pray Jumu'ah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, hurrying to the shade, and there would be only about a foot or two feet of shade."],[1849,"We used to go early—meaning for Jumu'ah—then take a midday nap."],[1850,"I heard Anas b. Malik—Yazid al-Dabbi called out to him on a Friday during the time of al-Hajjaj, saying: O Abu Hamzah, you have attended prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and you have attended prayer with us, so how did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ use to pray? He said: When the cold became intense, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray early, and when the heat became intense, he would delay the prayer until it was cooler."],[1851,"Marwan used to appoint Abu Hurayrah as his deputy over Madinah. He led them in prayer on Friday and recited Surah al-Jumu'ah and 'When the hypocrites come to you' (al-Munafiqun). I said: O Abu Hurayrah, you have recited to us a recitation that Ali recited to us in Kufah. Abu Hurayrah said: I heard my beloved Abu al-Qasim ﷺ reciting these two."],[1852,"Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Ja'far, regarding the second rak'ah: 'When the hypocrites come to you' (al-Munafiqun)."],[1853,"Al-Dahhak b. Qays wrote to al-Nu'man b. Bashir asking him: What did the Prophet ﷺ used to recite on Friday along with Surah al-Jumu'ah? He wrote back to him: He used to recite with it 'Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming Event?' (al-Ghashiyah)."],[1854,"We asked him: What did the Prophet ﷺ use to recite on Friday along with the surah in which Jumu'ah is mentioned? He said: He used to recite with it 'Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming Event?' (al-Ghashiyah)."],[1855,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Jumu'ah prayer: 'Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High' (al-A'la) and 'Has there come to you the report of the Overwhelming Event?' (al-Ghashiyah)."],[1856,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of a prayer has caught it. Al-Makhzumi said: Whoever catches one rak'ah of the prayer has caught it."],[1857,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of the prayer has caught the prayer."],[1858,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of the Jumu'ah prayer has caught the prayer."],[1859,"Whoever catches one rak'ah of Jumu'ah should add another one to it."],[1860,"While the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the Friday khutbah standing, a caravan from Madinah arrived. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rushed to it, and only twelve men remained with him, among them Abu Bakr and Umar. The verse was revealed: 'When they see merchandise or diversion, they scatter off to it and leave you standing.'"],[1861,"The Prophet ﷺ said to a people who stayed behind from Jumu'ah: 'I was on the verge of ordering a man to lead the people in prayer, then setting fire to the houses of men who stay behind from Jumu'ah.'"],[1862,"'I was on the verge.'"],[1863,"Let people desist from abandoning the Jumu'ahs, or Allah will seal their hearts, and then they will be among the heedless."],[1864,"Whoever abandons Jumu'ah three times without necessity, Allah seals his heart."],[1865,"Whoever abandons Jumu'ah three times without an excuse."],[1866,"Whoever abandons Jumu'ah three times out of negligence toward it, Allah seals his heart."],[1867,"Beware, perhaps one of you will take a small flock of sheep at the distance of a mile or two, and then the pasture will become difficult for him at the distance of a mile or two, so he will keep going up until Jumu'ah comes and he does not attend it, then Jumu'ah comes and he does not attend it, then Jumu'ah comes and he does not attend it, until a seal is placed on his heart."],[1868,"The people of Quba' used to perform the Friday prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Abdullah b. Umar said: 'The Ansar used to attend Jumu'ah with Umar b. al-Khattab, then they would leave and take a midday nap at his place due to the heat and in preparation for the prayer (i.e., they would rest until the time of prayer). The people used to do that.'"],[1869,"Hammam b. Yahya informed us, from Qatadah, with the like of it: :, :, :, :"],[1870,"I passed by 'Abd al-Rahman b. Samurah on Friday while he was at the canal of Umm 'Abd Allah, and it was flowing with water over his servants and freedmen. I said to him, \"O Abu Sa'id, Jumu'ah!\" He said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'When the rain is heavy (wabil), then pray in your dwellings.'\""],[1871,"He witnessed the Prophet ﷺ at the time of al-Hudaybiyah, and rain fell on them on a Friday, but the soles of their sandals did not get wet. The Prophet ﷺ ordered them to pray in their dwellings."],[1872,"Ibn 'Abbas ordered the muezzin to give the call to prayer on Friday, and that was a rainy day. He said: 'Allahu akbar, Allahu akbar, ashhadu an la ilaha illa Allah, ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah.' Then he said to him: 'Call out to the people: Let them pray in their homes.' The people said to him: 'What is this that you have done?' He said: 'One better than me did this. Do you order me to bring the people out, or to have them come treading through mud up to their knees?'"],[1873,"Ibn 'Abbas said to his muezzin on a rainy day: 'When you say \"Ashhadu anna Muhammadan rasul Allah,\" do not say \"Hayya 'ala al-salah.\" Say: \"Pray in your homes.\" The people seemed to disapprove of that, so he said: 'Are you surprised at this? One better than me did it. Indeed, Jumu'ah is an obligation, but I disliked to make you go out and walk in mud and slippery ground.'"],[1874,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on a rainy Friday: 'Pray in your dwellings.'"],[1875,"Nafi' b. Jubayr sent me to al-Sa'ib b. Yazid to ask him something. I asked him, and he said: 'Yes. I prayed Jumu'ah in the enclosure with Mu'awiyah. When I said the salam, I stood up to pray. He sent for me, so I came to him, and he said to me: \"When you have prayed Jumu'ah, do not connect it with another prayer unless you leave or speak, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that.\"'"],[1876,"Nafi' b. Jubayr sent him to al-Sa'ib b. Yazid to ask him about something that Mu'awiyah had seen from him. He said: 'I prayed with him in the enclosure, then I stood up to pray in my place. He said to me: \"Do not connect it with another prayer until you move forward from that spot or speak, because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that.\"'"],[1877,"When he prayed Jumu'ah, he would enter his house and pray two rak'ahs."],[1878,"He saw the Prophet ﷺ praying two rak'ahs in his house after Jumu'ah and two rak'ahs after Maghrib."],[1879,"He used to pray two rak'ahs after Jumu'ah."],[1880,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Banu 'Amr b. 'Awf on a Wednesday, and he saw things on the palm trees that he had not seen before, such as fortification. He said: 'If only, when you come to this 'Eid of yours, you would stay until you hear from my speech.' They said: 'Yes, by our fathers and mothers, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: When they attended on Friday, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in Jumu'ah prayer, then he prayed two rak'ahs after Jumu'ah in the mosque. He was not seen praying two rak'ahs in the mosque after Jumu'ah on that day; he would go to his house before that day."],[1881,"Pray four rak'ahs after Jumu'ah. 'Abd al-Jabbar said: 'The Prophet ﷺ commanded them to pray four rak'ahs after Jumu'ah.'"],[1882,"Whoever among you wants to pray after Jumu'ah, let him pray four rak'ahs after it."],[1883,"We used to perform Jumu'ah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return and have lunch and take a midday nap."],[1884,"We would not have lunch or take a midday nap except after Jumu'ah."],[1885,"We used to perform Jumu'ah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return and take a midday nap."],[1886,"I saw 'Abd Allah b. Busr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: when he prayed Jumu'ah, he would leave the mosque for a considerable distance, then return to the mosque and pray as much as Allah willed him to pray. I said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, why do you do this? He said: Because I saw the master of the Muslims ﷺ do this, meaning the Prophet ﷺ, and he recited this verse: 'And when the prayer has been concluded, disperse within the land and seek from the bounty of Allah' to the end of the verse."],[1887,"I said to Ibn 'Abbas: I have a jar in which I soak dates for myself, and I drink from it, but if I sit with people for a long time, I fear that I may be disgraced because of its sweetness. He said: The delegation of 'Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'Welcome to the delegation, not disgraced nor regretful.' They said: O Messenger of Allah, between us and you are the polytheists of Mudar, and we can only reach you during the sacred months, so tell us of a matter of the religion by which, if we act upon it, we will enter Paradise by it, and we call to it those behind us. He said: 'I command you with four things and I forbid you from four things: faith in Allah, and do you know what faith in Allah is?' They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: 'The testimony that there is no god but Allah, the establishment of prayer, the giving of zakat, the fast of Ramadan, and that you give the fifth of the spoils. And I forbid you from soaking in gourds, hollowed-out stumps, green jars, and pitch-covered vessels.'"],[1888,"Islam is built upon five: the testimony that there is no god but Allah, the establishment of prayer, the giving of zakat, the pilgrimage to the House, and the fast of the month of Ramadan."],[1889,"'Asim, meaning Ibn Muhammad b. Zayd b. 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar b. al-Khattab, narrated to us: I heard my father narrate, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the like of it."],[1890,"When the month of Ramadan comes, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of the Fire are closed, and the devils are shackled."],[1891,"When the first night of Ramadan begins, the rebellious jinn among the devils are shackled, the gates of the Fire are closed, and no gate of it is opened, the gates of the Gardens are opened, and no gate of them is closed, and a caller calls out: O seeker of good, come forward; O seeker of evil, desist. And Allah has those freed from the Fire."],[1892,"Your month has come upon you, by the oath of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: no month has passed for the Muslims that is better for them than it, and no month has passed for the hypocrites that is worse for them than it. By the oath of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: its reward and its voluntary good deeds are written before it enters, and its burden and its misery are written before it enters. That is because the believer prepares in it strength from spending for worship, and the hypocrite prepares in it to follow the heedlessness of the believers and to follow their faults. So it is a gain that the believer gains."],[1893,"He faces you and you face him - three times. 'Umar b. al-Khattab said: O Messenger of Allah, has revelation descended? He said: No. He said: Has an enemy appeared? He said: No. He said: Then what? He said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, forgives on the first night of the month of Ramadan all the people of this qibla, and he pointed with his hand towards it. A man began shaking his head and saying: Excellent, excellent. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: O so-and-so, has your chest become constrained by it? He said: No, but I remembered the hypocrite. He said: The hypocrites are the disbelievers, and the disbeliever has no share of that."],[1895,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say one day, when Ramadan had begun: \"If the servants knew what Ramadan is, my community would wish that the whole year were Ramadan.\" A man from Khuza'ah said: \"O Prophet of Allah, tell us.\" He said: \"Paradise is adorned for Ramadan from the beginning of the year to the next year. When the first day of Ramadan comes, a wind blows from beneath the Throne and rustles the leaves of Paradise. The houris with wide eyes look at that and say: 'O Lord, appoint for us spouses from Your servants in this month, by whom our eyes will be delighted and their eyes will be delighted by us.' He said: 'There is no servant who fasts a day of Ramadan except that he is wedded to a wife from the houris with wide eyes in a tent of pearl, as Allah [Mighty and Majestic] described: 'Houris, restrained in pavilions.' Upon each woman are seventy garments, no garment of which is the same color as another. She is given seventy kinds of perfume, no kind of which has the same scent as another. For each woman among them are seventy thousand maidservants for her needs and seventy thousand servant boys, and with each servant boy is a platter of gold containing a kind of food, the last morsel of which has a pleasure not found in the first. For each woman among them are seventy couches of red ruby, on each couch seventy mattresses, their linings of rich brocade, and above each mattress seventy canopied beds. Her husband is given the like of that on a couch of red ruby, adorned with pearls, with two bracelets of gold upon him. This is for every day he fasted in Ramadan, apart from what he did of good deeds.\""],[1896,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us on the last day of Sha'ban and said: \"O people, a great month has overshadowed you, a blessed month, a month in which there is a night better than a thousand months. Allah has made its fasting obligatory and its night prayer voluntary. Whoever draws near in it with a good quality is like one who performs an obligatory act in other than it, and whoever performs an obligatory act in it is like one who performs seventy obligatory acts in other than it. It is the month of patience, and the reward of patience is Paradise. It is the month of consolation, and a month in which the provision of the believer increases. Whoever provides iftar to a fasting person in it, it will be forgiveness for his sins and release of his neck from the Fire, and he will have the like of his reward without any diminution from his reward.\" They said: \"Not all of us find what to provide iftar to a fasting person.\" He said: \"Allah gives this reward to one who provides iftar to a fasting person with a date, or a drink of water, or a sip of milk. It is a month whose beginning is mercy, its middle forgiveness, and its end release from the Fire. Whoever lightens the burden of his slave, Allah will forgive him and release him from the Fire. Increase in it four qualities: two by which you please your Lord, and two which you cannot do without. As for the two by which you please your Lord: bearing witness that there is no god but Allah, and seeking His forgiveness. As for the two which you cannot do without: asking Allah for Paradise and seeking refuge in Him from the Fire. Whoever satisfies a fasting person in it, Allah will give him a drink from my pond, a drink after which he will not thirst until he enters Paradise.\""],[1897,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended the pulpit and said: \"Amin, Amin, Amin.\" It was said to him: \"O Messenger of Allah, what were you doing this for?\" He said: \"Gabriel said to me: 'May Allah rub the nose in the dust of a servant - or may he be distant - who enters Ramadan and is not forgiven.' I said: 'Amin.' Then he said: 'May the nose be rubbed in the dust of a servant - or may he be distant - who reaches his parents, or one of them, and does not enter Paradise.' I said: 'Amin.' Then he said: 'May the nose be rubbed in the dust of a servant - or may he be distant - before whom you are mentioned and he does not invoke blessings upon you.' I said: 'Amin.'\""],[1898,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the most generous of people in good, and he was most generous in the month of Ramadan until it ended. Gabriel would come to him and review the Qur'an with him. When Gabriel met him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more generous in good than the sent wind."],[1899,"Fasting is a shield."],[1900,"I entered upon 'Uthman b. Abi al-'As, and he called for milk of a milch camel. I said: \"I am fasting.\" He said: \"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Fasting is a shield from the Fire, like the shield of one of you in battle.' He said: 'And a good fast is the fast of three days of every month.'\""],[1901,"Fasting is a shield as long as it is not rent."],[1902,"I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, direct me to a deed.\" He said: \"You must observe fasting, for there is nothing equal to it.\""],[1903,"Whoever fasts Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven, and whoever stands in prayer on the Night of Qadr out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven."],[1904,"Allah revealed to Yahya b. Zakariyya five words to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon them. He seemed to delay them, so 'Isa came to him and said: \"Allah commanded you with five words to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon them. Either you inform them, or I inform them.\" He said: \"O my brother, do not do that, for I fear that if you precede me with them, the earth will swallow me or I will be punished.\" He said: He gathered the Children of Israel in the House of the Sanctuary, until the mosque was full and they sat on the balconies. Then he addressed them and said: \"Allah revealed to me five words to act upon and to command the Children of Israel to act upon them: The first of them: that you do not associate anything with Allah, for the likeness of one who associates with Allah is like a man who bought a slave from his pure wealth with gold or silver, then lodged him in a house and said: 'Work and bring it to me.' He began to work and bring it to someone other than his master. Which of you would be pleased for his slave to be like that? Allah created you and provided for you, so do not associate anything with Him. When you stand for prayer, do not turn away, for Allah turns His face toward the face of His servant as long as he does not turn away. I command you with fasting. Its likeness is like a man in a group with a pouch of musk; all of them love to find its scent. Indeed, fasting is sweeter to Allah than the scent of musk. I command you with charity. Its likeness is like a man whom the enemy captured, and they bound his hand to his neck and brought him forward to strike his neck. He began to say: 'Would you let me ransom myself from you?' And he began to give little and much until he ransomed himself. I command you with much remembrance of Allah. The likeness of the remembrance of Allah is like a man whom the enemy pursued swiftly in his tracks until he came to a strong fortress and secured himself in it. Likewise, the servant does not escape from Satan except by the remembrance of Allah.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"And I command you with five things which Allah commanded me: the congregation, listening and obedience, emigration, and jihad in the way of Allah. Whoever separates from the congregation by a handspan has cast off the bond of faith and Islam from his head, unless he returns. Whoever claims the claim of the Jahiliyyah, he is among the heaps of Jahannam.\" It was said: \"O Messenger of Allah, even if he fasts and prays?\" He said: \"Even if he fasts and prays. Call one another by the call of Allah, by which He named you: the believers, the Muslims, the servants of Allah.\""],[1905,"Every deed of the son of Adam is for him except fasting, for it is for Me, and I reward it. Fasting is a shield. By Him in whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, the breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah on the Day of Resurrection than the scent of musk. The fasting person has two joys: when he breaks his fast, he rejoices in his breaking, and when he meets his Lord, he rejoices in his fast."],[1906,"Every deed of the son of Adam is for him: a good deed is multiplied ten times its like, up to seven hundredfold. Allah said: Except fasting, for it is Mine, and I give reward for it. He leaves his food for My sake, leaves his drink for My sake, leaves his pleasure for My sake, and leaves his wife for My sake. And the breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the fragrance of musk. The fasting person has two joys: a joy when he breaks his fast, and a joy when he meets his Lord."],[1907,"I was with Hanzalah b. Ali at al-Baqi' with Abu Hurayrah, and Abu Hurayrah narrated to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: 'The one who eats and is grateful is like the one who fasts and is patient.'"],[1908,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the like of it. Abu Bakr said: 'Both chains are sound from Sa'id al-Maqburi and from Hanzalah b. Ali, all from Abu Hurayrah. Do you not hear al-Maqburi saying: I was with Hanzalah b. Ali at al-Baqi' with Abu Hurayrah?'"],[1909,"Fasting is for Me, and I give reward for it. The fasting person has two joys: when he breaks his fast he rejoices, and when he meets Allah and He rewards him he rejoices. By the One in Whose hand is the soul of Muhammad, the breath of the fasting person is sweeter to Allah than the fragrance of musk."],[1910,"Three people's supplications are not rejected: the fasting person until he breaks his fast, a just ruler, and the supplication of the oppressed. Allah raises it above the clouds and opens for it the gates of the heavens, and the Lord, Mighty and Exalted, says: 'By My might, I will surely help you, even if after a while.'"],[1911,"The fasting people have a gate in Paradise called al-Rayyan; no one enters through it except them. When the last of them enters, it is closed. Whoever enters drinks, and whoever drinks never thirsts again."],[1912,"During Ramadan in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whoever wished fasted, and whoever wished broke his fast and redeemed it by feeding a poor person, until the verse was revealed: 'So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it' [the verse]."],[1913,"The companions of Muhammad ﷺ, when one of them was fasting and the time of breaking the fast came, if he slept before breaking his fast, he would not eat that night or the next day until evening. Qays b. Sirmah was fasting, and when the time of breaking the fast came, he went to his wife and said: 'Do you have any food?' She said: 'No, but I will look for some.' She went to look for him, and he had been working that day, so sleep overcame him. His wife came and said: 'Woe to you!' He woke up, and when midday came, he fainted. That was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, and this verse was revealed: 'It has been made lawful for you, on the night of fasting, to go to your wives.' They rejoiced greatly, and he said: 'And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread of dawn.'"],[1914,"When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it, break your fast. If it is obscured from you, then estimate for it."],[1915,"Allah has made the new moons as appointed times, so when you see it, fast, and when you see it, break your fast. If it is obscured from you, then estimate for it, and know that the month does not exceed thirty."],[1916,"The month is twenty-nine nights, so do not fast until you see it, and do not break your fast until you see it, unless it is obscured from you. If it is obscured from you, then estimate for it."],[1917,"If it is obscured from you, then count thirty."],[1918,"The month is like this, and like this, and like this: thirty. And the month is like this, and like this, and like this — and he folded his thumb on the third, meaning twenty-nine. If it is overcast for you, then complete thirty."],[1919,"The Messenger of God ﷺ would watch for the crescent of Sha'ban more than he watched for others, then he would fast upon the sighting of Ramadan. If it was overcast for him, he would count thirty days, then fast."],[1920,"Do not precede this month until you see the crescent or complete the count."],[1921,"I entered upon Ikrimah on the day about which there is doubt whether it is from Ramadan, while he was eating. He said, \"Come near and eat.\" I said, \"I am fasting.\" He said, \"By God, you shall come near.\" I said, \"Then narrate to me.\" He said:"],[1922,"The month is twenty-nine — and he folded his thumb — so do not fast until you see it, and do not break fast until you see it. If it is overcast for you, then estimate for it."],[1923,"We were with 'Ammar when a roasted sheep was brought. He said, \"Eat.\" Some of the people withdrew, saying, \"I am fasting.\" 'Ammar said, \"Whoever fasts the day about which there is doubt has disobeyed Abu al-Qasim.\""],[1924,"We sighted the crescent of Ramadan while we were at Dhat 'Irq. He said: We sent a man to Ibn 'Abbas to ask him. Ibn 'Abbas said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"God has extended it for you for its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count.\""],[1925,"Umm al-Fadl bt. al-Harith sent him to Mu'awiyah in Syria. He said: I arrived in Syria, fulfilled her errand, and the crescent of Ramadan was sighted while I was in Syria. We saw the crescent on Friday night, and the people saw it and fasted, and Mu'awiyah fasted. I arrived in Madinah at the end of the month, and 'Abdullah b. 'Abbas asked me — then he mentioned the crescent — saying, \"When did you see the crescent?\" I said, \"We saw it on Friday night.\" He said, \"You yourself saw it on Friday night?\" I said, \"Yes, I saw it on Friday night, and the people saw it and fasted, and Mu'awiyah fasted.\" He said, \"But we saw it on Saturday night, so we will continue to fast until we complete thirty or we see it.\" I said, \"Is the sighting of Mu'awiyah and his fasting not sufficient for you?\" He said, \"No. Thus did the Messenger of God command us.\""],[1926,"The month is twenty-nine."],[1927,"The month is only twenty-nine."],[1928,"We went out for 'umrah, and when we stopped at Batn Nakhla we saw the crescent. Some of the people said, \"It is three nights old.\" Others said, \"It is two nights old.\" He said: Then we met Ibn 'Abbas and said, \"We saw the crescent, and some of the people said it was three nights old, and others said it was two nights old.\" He asked, \"On which night did you see it?\" We said, \"On such-and-such night.\" He said, \"The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'God extends it for its sighting, so it is for the night on which you see it.'\""],[1929,"The month is thus and thus and thus."],[1930,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ secluded himself from his wives, I said: O Messenger of Allah, you have been in the upper room for twenty-nine. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The month can be twenty-nine."],[1931,"I fasted with the Prophet ﷺ twenty-nine [days] more than I fasted with him thirty."],[1932,"A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I saw the crescent tonight. He said: \"Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger?\" He said: Yes. He said: Stand, O so-and-so, and announce to the people that they should fast tomorrow."],[1933,"He ordered Bilal, who announced it to the people."],[1935,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the white thread from the black thread? Are they two threads? He said: \"You have a wide back of the neck! Have you ever seen the two threads?\" Then he said: No, rather it is the blackness of night and the whiteness of day."],[1936,"The dawn is two: as for the first, it does not forbid food, nor does it permit the prayer; as for the second, it forbids food and permits the prayer."],[1937,"Let not the call of Bilal prevent any of you from his pre-dawn meal, for he calls or makes the call to awaken your sleeper and bring back your standing one. He said: \"It is not that it should appear thus or he said thus, but until it appears thus and thus - meaning in length, but like this - meaning in breadth.\""],[1938,"Let not the call of Bilal deceive you, nor this whiteness of the pillar of dawn until it spreads."],[1939,"Eat and drink, and let not the rising vertical light deceive you; eat and drink until the red appears to you across [the horizon]. And he gestured with his hand."],[1940,"Bilal makes the call at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum makes the call."],[1941,"Indeed, Bilal gives the call to prayer at night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer. He said: And there was not between them except the amount that this one descends and this one ascends. Al-Dawraqi said from Qasim, and he also said: \"When Bilal gives the call to prayer, then eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktum gives the call to prayer.\" He said: And there was not between them except that this one descends and this one ascends."],[1942,"Whoever does not intend the fast before dawn, there is no fast for him."],[1945,"Abu Bakr b. 'Ayyash narrated to us with this chain of transmission its exact like, attributed to the Prophet: \"...\""],[1946,"Take the pre-dawn meal (suhur), for indeed in the suhur there is blessing."],[1947,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ inviting a man to the suhur, and he said: Come to the blessed midday meal (al-ghada' al-mubarak)."],[1948,"Seek help through the pre-dawn meal for fasting the day, and through the midday nap for standing in prayer at night."],[1949,"The distinction between our fasting and the fasting of the People of the Book is the eating of the pre-dawn meal (suhur)."],[1950,"We took the pre-dawn meal (suhur) with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then we stood up for the prayer. I said: How much was between them? He said: \"The amount of reciting fifty verses.\""],[1951,"I used to take the pre-dawn meal (suhur) with my family, then I would be quick enough to catch the dawn prayer with the Messenger of Allah."],[1952,"Al-Shafi'i said: Malik informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd b. 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah (h)."],[1953,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I am destroyed. He said: \"And what has destroyed you?\" He said: I had intercourse with my wife during the month of Ramadan. He said: \"Can you free a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you fast two consecutive months?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you feed sixty poor persons?\" He said: No. He said: \"Sit down.\" So he sat down. Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a large basket (araq) containing dates—he said: the araq is the huge basket—and he said: \"Take this and give it in charity.\" He said: O Messenger of God, to a household poorer than us? For between its two lava tracts there is no household poorer than us. The Prophet ﷺ laughed until his canine teeth appeared, and said: \"Go and feed your family.\""],[1954,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said to him: The other one has had intercourse with his wife during Ramadan. He said: He said to him: \"Do you find something to free a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you fast two consecutive months?\" He said: No. He said: \"Do you find something to feed sixty poor persons?\" He said: No. He said: Then the Messenger of God ﷺ was brought a large basket (araq) containing dates, which is the zanbil, and he said: \"Feed this on your behalf.\" He said: Between its two lava tracts there is no household more needy than us. He said: \"Then feed your family.\""],[1955,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ in the mosque during Ramadan and said: O Messenger of God, I am burned. The Prophet ﷺ asked him what was the matter with him, and he said: I had intercourse with my wife. He said: \"Give charity.\" He said: By God, I have nothing and I am not able to. He said: \"Sit down.\" So he sat down. While he was in that state, a man came driving a donkey loaded with food. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Where is the burned one?\" The man stood up, and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Give this in charity.\" He said: To others than us? By God, we are hungry and we have nothing. He said: \"Then eat it.\""],[1956,"The Prophet ﷺ was in a shade, and a man from Banu Bayadah came to him and said: O Prophet of God, I am burned. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: \"What is the matter with you?\" He said: I had intercourse with my wife while I was fasting, and that was in Ramadan. The Messenger of God ﷺ said to him: \"Free a slave.\" He said: I cannot find one. He said: \"Feed sixty poor persons.\" He said: I do not have it. He said: \"Sit down.\" So he sat down. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ was brought a large basket (araq) containing twenty sa's, and he said: \"Where is the one who asked just now?\" He said: Here I am, O Messenger of God. He said: \"Take this and give it in charity.\" He said: O Messenger of God, to those more needy than me and my family? For by the One who sent you with the truth, we have no dinner for a night. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Then take it back for yourself and your family.\""],[1958,"While I was sitting with the Messenger of God ﷺ, a man came to him and said: O Messenger of God, I am destroyed. He said: \"Woe to you, what is the matter with you?\" He said: I had intercourse with my wife in Ramadan. He said: \"Free a slave.\" He said: I cannot find one. He said: \"Fast two consecutive months.\" He said: I am not able. He said: \"Feed sixty poor persons.\" He said: I cannot find it. He said: Then the Messenger of God ﷺ was brought a large basket (araq) containing dates, and he said: \"Take it and give it in charity.\" He said: I find no one more entitled to it than my family, O Messenger of God. Between the two ropes of Madinah, there is no one more in need of it than me. The Messenger of God ﷺ laughed until his canine teeth appeared, and said: \"Take it and seek forgiveness from God.\""],[1959,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was brought a basket containing fifteen or twenty sa's of dates. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Take it and feed it on your behalf.\""],[1960,"A man came to the Messenger of God ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith. And he said: He was brought a basket containing fifteen sa's, or twenty sa's."],[1961,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I am destroyed. He said: \"And what has destroyed you?\" He said: I had intercourse with my wife during the month of Ramadan. He said: \"Can you free a slave?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you fast two consecutive months?\" He said: No. He said: \"Can you feed sixty poor persons?\" He said: No. He said: \"Sit down.\" So he sat down. Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought a large basket (araq) containing dates—he said: the araq is the huge basket—and he said: \"Take this and give it in charity.\" He said: O Messenger of God, to a household poorer than us? For between its two lava tracts there is no household poorer than us. The Prophet ﷺ laughed until his canine teeth appeared, and said: \"Go and feed your family.\""],[1962,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: My sister died and she owed fasting two consecutive months. He said: \"If your sister had a debt, would you pay it?\" She said: Yes. He said: \"Then the right of God is more deserving.\""],[1963,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and he had had intercourse with his wife in Ramadan. He mentioned the hadith. And at the end of it he said: \"Then fast a day and seek forgiveness from God.\""],[1965,"The Prophet ﷺ vomited and broke his fast. I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus and mentioned that to him, and he said: \"He has spoken the truth; I poured out his ablution water for him.\""],[1966,"The Prophet ﷺ vomited and broke his fast. I met Thawban in the mosque of Damascus and mentioned that to him, and he said: \"He has spoken the truth; I poured out his ablution water for him.\""],[1967,"Harb b. Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya b. Abi Kathir, from Abd al-Rahman b. Amr, from Ya'ish, from Ma'dan b. Abi Talhah, from Abu al-Darda' something similar to the hadith of Abu Musa."],[1968,"From Ya'ish b. al-Walid b. Hisham: As for Bundar, he attributed him to his grandfather, and they both said: M'dan reported. So by the narration of Hisham and Harb b. Shaddad it is known that what is correct is what Abu Musa narrated, and that Ya'ish b. al-Walid heard from M'dan, and his father was not between them:"],[1969,"If the fasting person deliberately vomits, he breaks his fast; and if he is overcome by vomiting, he does not break his fast."],[1970,"Whoever is overcome by vomiting does not have to make it up, and whoever deliberately vomits must make it up."],[1971,"Abu Qilabah al-Jarmi narrated to me that Abu Asma' al-Rahabi narrated to him, from Thawban, the freed slave of the Messenger of God,"],[1972,"that he went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ on the eighteenth of Ramadan to al-Baqi'. The Messenger of God ﷺ looked at a man cupping, and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.'"],[1973,"The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast."],[1974,"'The cupper and the cupped have broken their fast.' Some of those who disagree with us on this issue said: 'Cupping does not invalidate the fasting person's fast,' and they cited as evidence that the Prophet ﷺ was cupped while fasting and in ihram. This report does not indicate that cupping does not invalidate the fast of the fasting person, because the Prophet ﷺ was only cupped while fasting during travel, not while resident; for he was never in ihram while residing in his own city. He was in ihram only while traveling. A traveler, even if he intended to fast, may have part of the day pass while he is fasting from food and drink, and eating and drinking break his fast. It is not as some scholars imagined, that a traveler, once he begins a fast, cannot break it until he completes the fast of that day. Since he may eat and drink, though he had intended to fast and part of the day has passed while he is fasting, and eating and drinking break his fast, it was permissible for him to be cupped while traveling during part of the fasting day, even though cupping is something that breaks the fast. The evidence that the fasting person may break his fast by eating and drinking during travel in a day part of which has passed while he is fasting is:"],[1975,"I am not like you; I am riding while you are walking, and I am the least burdened of you, so drink. They kept looking at him, wondering what he would do. When they refused, he turned his saddle, dismounted, drank, and the people drank. The reports of Ibn Abbas and Anas b. Malik I have included in the Book of Fasting in the large book. Is it permissible for an ignorant person to say: Drinking is allowed for the fasting person and does not break his fast? Since the Prophet ﷺ commanded his companions, while fasting, to drink, and when they refused, he drank while fasting and they drank. Whoever understands knowledge and comprehends fiqh knows that the Prophet ﷺ and his companions had become compelled to drink water; they had intended to fast and part of the day had passed, and they were allowed to break their fast because they were traveling, not resident. Likewise, the Prophet ﷺ was allowed to be cupped while fasting during travel, even though cupping breaks the fast, because whoever is allowed to drink—even though drinking breaks the fast—is allowed to be cupped—even though cupping breaks the fast. As for the argument of some Iraqis on this issue, that breaking the fast is caused by what enters, not what exits, this is ignorance and negligence on the part of its speaker, and a deception upon those who do not possess knowledge or understand fiqh. This statement, if taken literally, contradicts the clear evidence of the Book of God, the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ, and the consensus of all the people of prayer from the people of God. God has indicated in His clear revelation that 'mubasharah' (touching) means sexual intercourse during the day of fasting, and the chosen Prophet ﷺ obligated the one who has intercourse in Ramadan to free a slave if he can, or to fast two consecutive months if he cannot find a slave, or to feed sixty poor people if he cannot fast. The one who has intercourse does not have anything enter his inside; rather, semen exits from him if he ejaculates. He may have intercourse without ejaculating in the vagina, so no semen exits from his inside either. The meeting of the two circumcised parts without ejaculation breaks the fast and necessitates expiation, and nothing enters the inside of the one having intercourse nor exits from it when his state is like this. The chosen Prophet ﷺ informed that deliberate vomiting breaks the fast of the one who deliberately vomits, and the people of prayer and people of knowledge have agreed that deliberate vomiting invalidates the fast. If the fasting person's fast were only invalidated by what enters his inside, then intercourse and deliberate vomiting would not invalidate the fast. Some ignorant people have come up with an amazing thing on this issue, claiming that the Prophet ﷺ only said 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast' because they were backbiting. When it is said to him: Does backbiting invalidate the fasting person's fast? He claims that it does not. Then it is said to him: If, according to you, the Prophet ﷺ only said 'the cupper and the cupped have broken their fast' because they were backbiting, and backbiting, according to you, does not invalidate the fast, then would anyone who believes in God say such a thing? He claims that the Prophet ﷺ informed his nation that the two who backbite are people who have broken their fast, while he himself says: Rather, they are fasting and have not broken their fast. Thus he contradicts the Prophet ﷺ, whom God obligated His servants to obey and follow, promised guidance for following him, warned against opposing him, and negated faith for anyone who finds in himself any discomfort with his ruling. He said: {But no, by your Lord, they will not believe until they make you judge in what arises between them.} The verse. And God, exalted and high, has not given anyone any choice in what God and His Messenger have decreed. He said, blessed and exalted: {It is not for a believing man or a believing woman, when God and His Messenger have decreed a matter, to have any choice in their affair.} The one who uses this report as evidence has clearly contradicted the Prophet ﷺ in his own mind, without any doubt or interpretation that the report could bear, when he claims that the Prophet ﷺ only said to the cupper and the cupped 'you have broken your fast' due to their backbiting, and then he himself claims that backbiting does not break the fast. Thus he has completely contradicted the Prophet ﷺ without any doubt or interpretation. So it is in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'they have broken their fast' (dual form). So it is in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'with drinking'. So it is in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'contradiction'."],[1976,"The Prophet ﷺ permitted kissing for the fasting person and cupping for the fasting person."],[1977,"The Messenger of God ﷺ permitted kissing for the fasting person."],[1978,"Cupping and kissing were permitted for the fasting person."],[1979,"He said about cupping: 'They only used to dislike' — or he said: 'fear' — weakness."],[1980,"Cupping was only disliked for the fasting person out of fear of weakness."],[1981,"Abd al-Rahman narrated to us. Abu Bakr said: “This chain is erroneous; it does not contain Ata b. Yasar or Abu Said. And Abd al-Rahman b. Zayd is not among those whose hadith the people of verification use as proof, due to his poor retention of chains. He is a man whose craft is worship, asceticism, admonition, and renunciation, not one of the hadith specialists who preserve chains.”"],[1982,"Whoever vomits, has a wet dream, or is cupped does not break the fast."],[1983,"Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Zayd b. Aslam, from a man, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said."],[1984,"Whoever vomits, has a wet dream, or is cupped does not break the fast."],[1985,"Ibn Abi Sabrah narrated to us, from Zayd b. Aslam, from Ata b. Yasar, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like of it."],[1986,"Zayd b. Aslam narrated to us, from Ata b. Yasar, who said: The Messenger of God said."],[1987,"Three things do not break the fast of the fasting person: a wet dream, vomiting, and cupping."],[1988,"There is no harm in cupping for the fasting person."],[1989,"He did not consider cupping for the fasting person to be harmful."],[1990,"There is no harm in cupping for the fasting person."],[1991,"Abdah narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Naji, from Abu al-Mutawakkil: that Abu Sa'id did not [narrate it] from the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I do not think Ma'mar uttered it."],[1992,"I went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ when eighteen [days] of Ramadan had passed. He passed by a man being cupped and said, \"The cupper and the cupped have broken the fast.\""],[1993,"The cupper and the cupped have broken the fast."],[1995,"While I was sleeping, two men came to me, took hold of my upper arms, and brought me to a rugged mountain. They said, \"Climb.\" I said, \"I cannot manage it.\" They said, \"We will make it easy for you.\" I climbed until, when I was on level ground on the mountain, there were loud voices. I said, \"What are these voices?\" They said, \"This is the howling of the people of the Fire.\" Then I was taken on, and I came upon a people suspended by their Achilles tendons, their cheeks split, blood flowing from their cheeks. He said: I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"These are those who break the fast before the proper time of their fast.\" Then he said, \"The Jews and Christians have failed.\" Sulaym said: I do not know whether Abu Umamah heard it from the Messenger of God ﷺ or whether it is something of his own opinion. Then he went on, and I came upon a people with the most severe bloating, the foulest smell, and the worst appearance. I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"These are those killed from the disbelievers.\" Then he went on, and I came upon a people with the most severe bloating and the foulest smell, as if their smell were that of latrines. I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"These are the male and female adulterers.\" Then he went on, and I came upon women whose breasts were being bitten by snakes. I said, \"What is the matter with these?\" He said, \"These are those who deny their children their milk.\" Then he went on, and I came upon boys playing between two rivers. I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"These are the offspring of the believers.\" Then he ascended a height, and I came upon three people drinking from wine they had. I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"These are Ja'far, Zayd, and Ibn Rawahah.\" Then he took me up to another height, and I came upon three people. I said, \"Who are these?\" He said, \"This is Ibrahim, Musa, and 'Isa, and they are looking at me.\""],[1998,"Bundar narrated to us, from Abu Dawud, from Shu'bah, with this chain, a similar report, and he added: Shu'bah said: Habib said: I met Abu al-Mutawwis and he narrated it to me."],[1999,"If one of you forgets while fasting and eats and drinks, let him complete his fast, for it is only God who has fed him and given him drink."],[2000,"Whoever breaks the fast in the month of Ramadan out of forgetfulness, there is no making up and no expiation upon him.\" This is the hadith of Muhammad. Ibrahim said in his hadith: \"Whoever eats or drinks in Ramadan out of forgetfulness, there is no making up and no expiation upon him.\""],[2001,"We broke the fast in Ramadan on a cloudy day during the time of the Messenger of God ﷺ, then the sun came out. He said: I said to Hisham - and Abu 'Ammar said: It was said to Hisham - \"Were they commanded to make it up?\" He said: \"That is inevitable.\""],[2002,"When one of you is fasting, let him not utter obscene speech or act ignorantly. If someone insults him, let him say, 'I am fasting.'"],[2003,"When one of you is fasting on a day, he should not engage in obscene speech. If someone insults him or abuses him or fights him, he should say: 'I am fasting.'"],[2004,"Do not engage in mutual abuse while you are fasting. If someone abuses you, then say: 'I am fasting,' and if you are standing, then sit down."],[2005,"Whoever does not abandon false speech and acting upon it, Allah has no need for him to abandon his food and drink."],[2006,"Fasting is not merely from eating and drinking; rather, fasting is from idle talk and obscene speech. If someone abuses you or acts ignorantly toward you, then say: 'I am fasting, I am fasting.'"],[2007,"Many a fasting person obtains nothing from his fast except hunger and thirst, and many a one standing in prayer obtains nothing from his standing except sleeplessness."],[2008,"We said: 'Did the Prophet ﷺ have intimate contact while fasting?' She said: 'He used to do so, but he had more control over his desires than you.' Abu Bakr said: 'Allah, majestic be His praise, addressed His Prophet ﷺ and his nation in the language of the Arabs, the most capacious of all languages, whose complete knowledge none but a prophet encompasses. The Arabs in their languages apply the name of one thing to two things, and to things of number. A single thing may be named by several names. Allah may prohibit one thing and permit another thing other than the prohibited thing, and the one name falls upon both things together: upon the permissible and the prohibited. Likewise, He may permit the prohibited thing, and the one name falls upon both of them together. Thus the one name falls upon the two different things, one of which is permissible and the other prohibited, while their name is one. Whoever is ignorant of the Arabic tongue does not understand this, and carries the meaning in that to one thing, imagining that the two matters are contradictory when an act called by a name is permitted and an act called by the same name is prohibited. Whoever has only this much knowledge is not allowed to engage in fiqh or give fatwas; he must learn or remain silent until he attains the knowledge with which fatwa and engaging in knowledge are permissible. Whoever understands this craft knows that what is permitted is other than what is prohibited, even if the one name may fall upon both the permissible and the prohibited. Of this kind that I mentioned is that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, indicated in His Book that intimate contact with women during the daytime of fasting is not permissible by His saying, Blessed and Exalted: \"So now have relations with them and seek that which Allah has decreed for you. And eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread. Then complete the fast until the night.\" Thus Allah, Mighty and Majestic, permitted intimate contact with women, and eating and drinking at night, then commanded us to complete the fast until night, indicating that the intimate contact permitted at night, coupled with eating and drinking, is the sexual intercourse that breaks the fast of the fasting person. And Allah permitted, by the action of the chosen Prophet ﷺ, the intimate contact that is less than sexual intercourse during fasting, since he used to have intimate contact while fasting. The intimate contact that Allah mentioned in His Book as breaking the fast of the fasting person is other than the intimate contact that the Prophet ﷺ used to engage in during his fast. The word \"intimate contact\" is one name falling upon two acts: one of them permissible during the daytime of fasting, and the other prohibited during the daytime of fasting, breaking the fast of the fasting person. Of this kind is His saying, Mighty and Majestic: \"O you who have believed, when the call is made for prayer on the day of Jumu'ah, then hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave off trade. [That is better for you, if you only knew].\" Our Lord, Majestic and Exalted, commanded hastening to Jumu'ah, and the chosen Prophet ﷺ said: \"When you come to prayer, do not come while you are hastening; come walking, and upon you should be tranquility.\" The name \"hastening\" falls upon trotting, and intensity of walking, and going to the place. So the hastening that Allah commanded to Jumu'ah is going to it, and the hastening that the Prophet ﷺ prohibited when coming to prayer is trotting and quick walking. Thus the name \"hastening\" falls upon two acts: one commanded and the other prohibited. I will, if Allah, Exalted, wills, clarify this kind in the Book of Meanings of the Qur'an if Allah grants success to that.' Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'commanded.' Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'one of them.' What is in brackets is not present in the Maktab al-Islami edition. Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktab al-Islami edition: 'come to it.'"],[2009,"One day I felt desire, so I kissed while fasting. I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'Today I did a great thing; I kissed while fasting.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'What do you think if you rinsed your mouth with water while fasting?' He said: I said: 'There is no harm in that.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said. Al-Rabi' said: I think he said: 'Then what about it?'"],[2010,"I asked 'Abd al-Rahman b. al-Qasim: 'Did you hear your father narrate from 'A'ishah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to kiss her while fasting?' He was silent for a while, then said: 'Yes.'"],[2011,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would remain fasting, not caring what he kissed of my face until he broke his fast. Yusuf said: 'And he kissed whatever he wished of my face.' Al-Za'farani said: 'And he kissed any place he wished of my face.'"],[2013,"He used to kiss her while fasting and suck her tongue."],[2014,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ leaned toward me to kiss me. I said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'And I am fasting,' and he kissed me."],[2015,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted a concession regarding the kiss for the fasting person."],[2017,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ—too many times to count—using the tooth-stick while fasting."],[2018,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stopped at Khaybar, and I stopped with him. He called me for kohl of antimony and applied it during Ramadan while fasting—antimony that was not perfumed."],[2019,"Mu'awiyah sent 'Abd al-Rahman b. al-Harith to 'A'ishah. Abu Bakr said: I went with my father and heard 'A'ishah saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be overtaken by dawn while junub, and he would fast."],[2020,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would be overtaken by morning while junub from his wives, without a wet dream, then he would continue fasting."],[2022,"He informed Zayd b. Thabit about Abu Hurayrah's statement that he said: Whoever the dawn overtakes in the month of Ramadan while junub and has not bathed has broken his fast and must make it up. Zayd b. Thabit said: Allah prescribed fasting upon us as He prescribed prayer upon us. If a man was overtaken by sunrise while sleeping, would he abandon the prayer? He said: I said to Zayd: Should he fast, and fast another day? Zayd said: Two days for one?"],[2023,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would reach morning junub from women, without a wet dream, then remain fasting."],[2024,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking his legal opinion while she was listening from behind the door. He said: O Messenger of Allah, prayer overtakes me while I am junub; should I fast? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Prayer also overtakes me while I am junub, and I fast. He said: You are not like us, O Messenger of Allah; Allah has forgiven your past and future sins. He said: By Allah—meaning—I hope to be the most fearful of Allah among you, and the most knowledgeable of what I avoid."],[2025,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a concession in some matter, but some men abstained from it. He said: What is the matter with men whom I command regarding a matter and they abstain from it? By Allah, I am the most knowledgeable of them about Allah, and the most intense in fear of Him."],[2026,"Fasting during travel is not part of righteousness."],[2028,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man around whom people had gathered, and he had been shaded. They said: This is a fasting man. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: It is not righteousness that you fast during travel."],[2029,"Then he fainted, and water began to be sprinkled on him."],[2030,"The Messenger of God ﷺ set out in the year of the conquest toward Mecca and fasted until he reached Kura' al-Ghamim, and the people fasted. Then he called for a vessel of water and raised it until the people looked at it, then he drank it. It was said to him after that: Some of the people have fasted. He said: Those are the disobedient ones, those are the disobedient ones."],[2031,"The Prophet ﷺ traveled during Ramadan, and fasting became severe upon a man among his companions, so his riding camel began to wander with him beneath the trees. The Prophet ﷺ was informed of this, so he ordered him to break the fast. Then the Prophet ﷺ called for a vessel and placed it upon his hand, then he drank while the people were watching."],[2032,"The Messenger of God ﷺ granted a concession in some matter, but some men abstained from it. He said: What is the matter with men whom I command in a matter, yet they abstain from it? By God, I know God better than they do, and I fear Him more than they do."],[2034,"I came to Abu Sa'id while he was surrounded by people. When the people dispersed from him, I said: I will not ask you about what these people ask you. I asked him about fasting during travel. He said: We traveled with the Messenger of God ﷺ to Mecca while we were fasting. We stopped at a place, and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: You have drawn near your enemy, and breaking the fast is stronger for you. So it was a concession. Among us were those who fasted and those who broke the fast. Then we stopped at another place, and he said: You will encounter your enemy in the morning, and breaking the fast is stronger for you, so break the fast. It was a firm command, so we broke the fast. Then he said: I saw us fasting with the Messenger of God ﷺ after that during travel."],[2035,"Whoever turns away from my Sunnah is not of me."],[2037,"O Messenger of God, I find in myself strength to fast during travel, so is there any sin upon me? The Messenger of God ﷺ said: It is a concession from God. Whoever takes it, it is good, and whoever prefers to fast, there is no sin upon him."],[2038,"God loves that His concessions be taken, just as He loves that His disobedience be abandoned."],[2039,"Hamzah b. 'Amr al-Aslami asked the Prophet ﷺ about fasting during travel, and he was a man who fasted continuously. The Prophet ﷺ said: You have the choice: if you wish, fast; and if you wish, break the fast."],[2040,"They two traveled with the Prophet ﷺ, and the one who fasted would fast, and the one who broke the fast would break the fast, and the one who broke the fast would not criticize the one who fasted, nor would the one who fasted criticize the one who broke the fast."],[2041,"We traveled with the Messenger of God ﷺ in Ramadan, and among us were those who fasted and those who broke the fast. The one who broke the fast did not criticize the one who fasted, nor did the one who fasted criticize the one who broke the fast. They considered that whoever found strength and fasted, that was good and excellent, and whoever found weakness and broke the fast, that was good and excellent."],[2042,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ at Marr al-Zahran. Food was brought to him, and he said to Abu Bakr and 'Umar: \"Come close and eat.\" They said, \"We are fasting.\" He said: \"Work for your two companions; saddle up for your two companions; come close and eat.\""],[2043,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was on a journey. Some fasted and some broke their fast. Those who broke their fast girded themselves and worked, while those who fasted became too weak for some of the work. He said about that: \"Those who broke their fast have taken the reward today.\""],[2044,"We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey. Among us were those who fasted and those who broke their fast. We stopped at a place on a very hot day. Some of us were shielding ourselves from the sun with their hands. Most of us in the shade were the owners of cloaks; the fasting people were shading themselves with them. Those who broke their fast got up, pitched tents, and watered the mounts. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Those who broke their fast have taken the reward today.\""],[2045,"The Messenger of God ﷺ fasted in the year of the conquest until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast."],[2046,"The Messenger of God ﷺ fasted in the year of the conquest until he reached al-Kadid, then he broke his fast."],[2047,"The Messenger of God ﷺ left Madinah heading for Makkah and fasted until he reached 'Usfan. He called for a vessel and placed it on his hand until the people saw it, then he broke his fast. Ibn 'Abbas used to say: \"Whoever wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast.\" This is the hadith of al-Hasan b. Muhammad. Yusuf said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ traveled in Ramadan and fasted until he reached 'Usfan, then he called for a vessel and drank during the day so that the people could see him, then he broke his fast until he arrived in Makkah.\" He said: \"Ibn 'Abbas used to say: The Messenger of God ﷺ fasted while traveling and broke his fast, so whoever wishes may fast, and whoever wishes may break his fast.\""],[2049,"We set out with the Messenger of God ﷺ when two nights of Ramadan had passed. We set out fasting until we reached al-Kadid, and he commanded us to break our fast. So we awoke as two groups: among us were those who fasted and those who broke their fast. When we reached Marr al-Zahran and he informed us of the meeting with the enemy, he commanded us to break our fast, and we broke it."],[2050,"The Messenger of God ﷺ was on a journey with his companions, and fasting became difficult for them. The Messenger of God ﷺ called for a vessel containing water and drank while he was on his mount, with the people watching him."],[2052,"Dihyah b. Khalifah went out from his village to the village of 'Uqbah b. 'Amir from al-Fustat in Ramadan, and he broke his fast, and the people broke their fast with him. Others disliked breaking their fast. When he returned to his village, he said: \"By God, I saw something today that I never thought I would see: a people who turned away from the guidance of the Messenger of God ﷺ and his companions.\" He said that about those who fasted. At that he said: \"O God, take me to You.\""],[2054,"Abu Qilabah narrated this hadith to me, then said to me: \"Would you like to meet the one who narrated it to me?\" He guided me to him, and I met him. He said: \"A relative of mine named Anas b. Malik narrated to me: I came to the Messenger of God ﷺ about some camels of mine that had been taken, and I found him eating. He invited me to his food, but I said, 'I am fasting.' He said: 'Come close,' or he said: 'Come, let me tell you about that. God has relieved the traveler of fasting and half the prayer, and the pregnant and the breastfeeding woman.'\" After that he would say: \"Would that I had eaten from the food of the Messenger of God ﷺ when he invited me to it.\""],[2055,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was having lunch, and he said: \"Come close.\" The man said, \"I am fasting.\" He said: \"Come close and I will tell you about fasting. God has relieved the traveler of fasting and half the prayer, and the pregnant or breastfeeding woman.\""],[2056,"and the breastfeeding woman."],[2058,"I have not seen among those deficient in intellect and religion any who more remove the mind of a resolute man than one of you. O company of women. They said to him: What is the deficiency of our religion and intellect, O Messenger of God? He said: \"Is not the testimony of a woman like half the testimony of a man?\" They said: Yes. He said: \"That is due to the deficiency of her intellect. Is it not that when a woman menstruates, she does not pray and does not fast?\" He said: \"That is from the deficiency of her religion.\""],[2059,"There would be fasting from Ramadan owed by me, and I would not make it up until Sha'ban came."],[2060,"Yahya b. Sa'id narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from 'A'ishah, similar to it."],[2061,"There would be something from Ramadan owed by me, then I would not be able to fast it until Sha'ban came. And I thought that that was because of her position with the Prophet ﷺ. Yahya says it. He said: He used to delay it as long as another Ramadan did not catch up with him."],[2062,"I would not make up what remained owed by me from Ramadan during the time of the Prophet ﷺ except in Sha'ban."],[2063,"Throughout the entire lifetime of the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[2064,"I did not make up anything of what was owed by me from Ramadan except in Sha'ban until the Messenger of God was taken."],[2065,"Whoever dies while fasting is owed by him, his guardian fasts on his behalf."],[2066,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of God, my mother died while she owed the fast of fifteen days. He said: \"Do you think that if your mother died while she owed a debt, would you pay it?\" She said: Yes. He said: \"Pay the debt of your mother.\""],[2067,"A woman rode the sea and vowed to fast a month, then she died. Her brother asked the Prophet ﷺ, and the Prophet ﷺ ordered him to fast on her behalf."],[2068,"A woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: My sister died while she owed the fast of two consecutive months. He said: \"Do you think that if your sister owed a debt, would you pay it?\" She said: Yes. He said: \"The right of God is more deserving.\""],[2069,"Whoever dies while owing a month of fasting, let someone feed a poor person on his behalf for each day."],[2070,"Whoever dies while owing Ramadan without having made it up, let someone feed on his behalf half a sa' of wheat for each day."],[2071,"When the night approaches, the day retreats, and the sun sets, the fasting person breaks his fast."],[2072,"People will remain upon good as long as they hasten the breaking of the fast."],[2073,"The religion will remain manifest as long as people hasten the breaking of the fast, for the Jews and Christians delay it."],[2074,"My ummah will not cease to follow my Sunnah as long as they do not wait for the stars to appear before breaking their fast. He said: When the Prophet ﷺ was fasting, he would order a man to go up on something, and when he said, 'The sun has set,' he would break his fast."],[2075,"The most beloved of My servants to Me are those who are quickest to break their fast."],[2076,"He would not pray Maghrib until he had broken his fast, even if it was with a drink of water."],[2077,"Whoever equips a warrior, or equips a pilgrim, or takes care of his family in his absence, or provides iftar to a fasting person, will have the like of their rewards without their rewards being diminished in any way."],[2078,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was fasting, he would not pray until we brought him fresh dates and water, and he would eat and drink when fresh dates were available. In winter, he would not pray until we brought him dried dates and water."],[2079,"Whoever finds dried dates, let him break his fast with them, and whoever does not, let him break his fast with water, for it is purifying."],[2080,"Charity to the poor is one charity, and to a relative it is two charities: charity and maintaining ties. And he ﷺ said: When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dried dates, for that is a blessing; if he does not find any, then with water, for it is purifying. And he ﷺ said: Slaughter the 'aqiqah on behalf of the boy, remove the harm from him, and shed blood for him. This is the hadith of 'Abd al-Jabbar. The other two said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you breaks his fast, let him break it with dried dates; if he does not find any, then let him break it with water, for it is purifying."],[2081,"Beware of continuous fasting. They said: O Messenger of Allah, but you fast continuously? He said: I am not like any one of you; I spend the night while my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."],[2082,"Beware of continuous fasting. They said: O Messenger of Allah, but you fast continuously? He said: I spend the night being fed and given drink."],[2083,"The Prophet ﷺ fasted continuously during the month of Ramadan, and some of the Muslims fasted continuously. This reached him, so he said: If the month were extended for us, I would fast continuously in such a way that those who go to extremes would abandon their extremism. You are not like me; I remain in the shade, and my Lord feeds me and gives me drink."],[2084,"Beware of continuous fasting — he said it three times. They said: But you fast continuously, O Messenger of Allah? He said: You are not like me in that; I spend the night while He feeds me and gives me drink. So undertake only such deeds as you are able to bear."],[2085,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast continuously until the pre-dawn meal, and some of his companions did so, so he forbade him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, but you do that? He said: You are not like me; I remain in the presence of my Lord, who feeds me and gives me drink."],[2086,"So which of you fasted continuously from one pre-dawn to the next?"],[2088,"None of you should say: I fasted the whole of Ramadan, or I stood in prayer the whole of Ramadan."],[2089,"He was asked: Which prayer is best after the prescribed prayer, and which fasting is best after the month of Ramadan? He said: The best prayer after the prescribed prayer is prayer in the middle of the night, and the best fasting after Ramadan is the month of Allah, al-Muharram."],[2090,"The month most beloved to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to fast was Sha'ban, and he would then join it to Ramadan."],[2091,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not fast any month of the year more than he fasted Sha'ban; he used to fast all of it."],[2092,"And he used to say: 'Take from deeds what you are able to bear, for Allah does not tire until you tire.' And the prayer most beloved to him was that which one was consistent upon, even if it was little. And when he performed a prayer, he would persist in it."],[2093,"The day of Ashura was a day that Quraysh used to fast in the pre-Islamic era, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast it. When he arrived – meaning in Madinah – he fasted it and commanded its fasting. Then when Ramadan was revealed, Ramadan became the obligatory fast, and he left Ashura. So whoever wished fasted it, and whoever wished did not fast it."],[2094,"Al-Ash'ath b. Qays entered upon 'Abdullah on the day of Ashura while he was eating lunch. 'Abdullah said to him: 'Come close, O Abu Muhammad, and eat.' He said: 'I am fasting.' 'Abdullah said: 'Do you know what Ashura used to be?' He said: 'And what was it?' He said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast it before Ramadan was revealed, then he abandoned it.' 'Ali b. Khashram and Yusuf said: 'When Ramadan was revealed, he abandoned it.'"],[2095,"Ashura was a day that the people of the pre-Islamic era used to fast. When Ramadan was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about it, and he said: 'It is a day from among the days of Allah, so whoever wishes may fast it, and whoever wishes may leave it.'"],[2096,"We used to fast Ashura before Ramadan was made obligatory. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to urge us upon it and check on us regarding it. When Ramadan was made obligatory, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not urge us upon it nor check on us regarding it, and we still used to do it."],[2097,"When the Prophet ﷺ arrived in Madinah, he found the Jews fasting Ashura. They were asked about that, and they said: 'This is the day on which Allah made Musa and the Children of Israel prevail over Pharaoh, and we fast it in veneration of it.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'We have a greater right to Musa than you,' and he commanded its fasting."],[2098,"He delivered a sermon in Madinah on an arrival he made on the day of Ashura, and said: 'Where are your scholars, O people of Madinah? I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: \"This is the day of Ashura, and fasting it has not been prescribed for you. I am fasting, so whoever likes to fast may fast.\"'"],[2100,"Ibn 'Abbas was asked about fasting the day of Ashura, and he said: 'I do not know the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to have fasted any day seeking its virtue except [the day of] Ashura, and this month, the month of Ramadan.'"],[2101,"Fasting the day of Ashura – I consider that Allah will expiate the sins of the year before it. And fasting the day of 'Arafah – I consider that Allah will expiate the sins of the year before it and the year after it."],[2103,"I said to my mother: 'Did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ [say anything] about Ashura?' She said: 'He used to venerate it, and he would call for his suckling infants and the suckling infants of Fatimah, and he would put saliva in their mouths, and he would command their mothers not to breastfeed them until nightfall.'"],[2105,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us on the day of Ashura and said: 'Have you fasted this day of yours?' Some of them said: 'Yes,' and some said: 'No.' He said: 'Then complete the rest of your day,' and he commanded them to inform the people of the outskirts to complete the rest of that day of theirs."],[2106,"He said to a man from Aslam: Announce among your people, or among the people, on the day of Ashura: whoever has eaten, let him fast the remainder of his day; and whoever has not eaten, let him fast."],[2108,"Today is Ashura; whoever wishes, let him fast it; and whoever wishes, let him break the fast."],[2109,"Fast the day of Ashura, and differ from the Jews; fast a day before it or a day after it."],[2110,"I asked Ibn Abbas while he was in the Sacred Mosque reclining on his cloak, and I asked him about fasting Ashura. He said: Count, and when you wake on the morning of the ninth of Muharram, wake fasting. He said: I said: Is that how Muhammad ﷺ used to fast? He said: That is how he used to fast."],[2111,"He was reclining on his cloak at Zamzam."],[2112,"Regarding the day of Ashura, he said: It is the ninth day. I said: Thus did Muhammad fast."],[2113,"The day of Arafah, the day of sacrifice, and the days of Tashriq are our festival, O people of Islam; they are days of eating and drinking."],[2114,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade fasting on the day of Arafah at Arafat."],[2115,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ broke the fast at Arafah; milk was brought to him and he drank."],[2116,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not fast the ten days. Abu Bakr said in his hadith: She said: I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasting during the ten days at all."],[2117,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to leave a deed while he loved to do it, fearing that it would be taken as a sunnah and then made obligatory upon them; and he loved what was light upon people among the obligations."],[2118,"I was a hard-working man, so my father married me off. Then he visited me and said to the woman, 'How do you find your husband?' She said, 'A fine man, but he does not sleep and does not break his fast.' He said, 'My father then took hold of me and said, \"I married you to a woman of the Muslims and you have confined her with your worship.\"' I did not care what he said to me, because of the strength and diligence I found in myself. Then that reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said, 'But I sleep and I pray, I fast and I break my fast. So sleep, pray, break your fast, and fast three days of each month.' I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am stronger than that.' He said, 'Then fast the fast of Dawud: fast one day and break your fast one day, and recite the whole Qur'an every month.' I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am stronger than that.' He said, 'Recite it every fifteen days.' I said, 'O Messenger of Allah, I am stronger than that.' Husayn said: Mansur mentioned to me from Mujahid that it reached seven. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'Indeed every deed has a burst of energy, and every burst of energy has a slackening. Whoever's slackening is toward my sunnah has been guided, and whoever's slackening is toward other than that has perished.' Abdullah said, 'If I had accepted the concession of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that would be dearer to me than having the like of my family and my wealth. Today I am an old man, grown old and weak, and I dislike to abandon what the Messenger of Allah commanded me.'"],[2119,"I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him about fasting. He said, 'Fast one day from each month, and you will have the reward of the remainder.' I said, 'I am able to do more than that.' He said, 'Fast two days from each month, and you will have the reward of the remainder.' I said, 'I am able to do more than that.' He said, 'Fast three days, and you will have the reward of the remainder.' I said, 'I am able to do more than that.' He said, 'Fast four days, and you will have the reward of the remainder.' He said, 'I am able to do more than that.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said, 'Indeed the most beloved of fasting is the fasting of Dawud; he would fast one day and break his fast one day.'"],[2121,"It reached the Prophet ﷺ that I fast continuously and pray at night. He either sent for him or met him, and said, 'Have I not been told that you fast and do not break your fast, and pray at night? Do not do that, for your eyes have a right over you, your self has a right over you, and your family has a right over you. So fast and break your fast, pray and sleep, and fast one day out of every ten, and you will have the reward of nine.' He said, 'I find myself stronger for that, O Messenger of Allah.' He said, 'Then fast the fast of Dawud.' He said, 'How did Dawud fast, O Messenger of Allah?' He said, 'He would fast one day and break his fast one day, and he would not flee when he met the enemy.' He said, 'Who can guarantee me that, O Prophet of Allah?' Ata' said, 'I do not know how he mentioned the perpetual fast.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'He has not fasted who fasts perpetually.'"],[2122,"Ikrimah narrated to us: [What] I heard from Abdullah b. Amr b. al-As, on the authority of the Prophet."],[2123,"Umar said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, 'What about one who fasts two days and breaks his fast one day?' He said, 'Can anyone bear that?' Umar said, 'What about one who fasts one day and breaks his fast one day?' He said, 'That is the fast of Dawud.' He said, 'What about one who fasts one day and breaks his fast two days?' He said, 'I wish I were given the strength to do that.'"],[2124,"No servant fasts a day in the cause of Allah except that Allah, because of that day, distances his face from the Fire by seventy autumns."],[2125,"No servant fasts a day in the cause of Allah, seeking the face of Allah, except that Allah distances his face from the Fire by seventy autumns."],[2126,"Whoever fasts Ramadan and then follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is as if he has fasted the entire year."],[2127,"The fast of Ramadan is like ten months, and the fast of six days is like two months, so that is the fast of the year."],[2128,"The Prophet ﷺ used to fast on Monday and Thursday."],[2129,"While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Umar came to him and said, 'O Prophet of Allah, fasting on Monday?' He said, 'A day on which I was born and a day on which I shall die.' This is the hadith of Qatadah. In the hadith of Waki': a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not mention Umar, and he said, 'On it I was born, and on it revelation came to me.' Hadith of Shu'bah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about his fasting, and he became angry; and he was asked about fasting on Monday and Thursday, and he said, 'That is a day – meaning Monday – on which I was born and on which I was sent' – or he said, 'on which it was sent down to me.'"],[2130,"While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Umar came to him and said, 'O Prophet of Allah, fasting on Monday?' He said, 'A day on which I was born and a day on which I shall die.' This is the hadith of Qatadah. In the hadith of Waki': a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he did not mention Umar, and he said, 'On it I was born, and on it revelation came to me.' Hadith of Shu'bah: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about his fasting, and he became angry; and he was asked about fasting on Monday and Thursday, and he said, 'That is a day – meaning Monday – on which I was born and on which I was sent' – or he said, 'on which it was sent down to me.'"],[2131,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to fast on Mondays and Thursdays, and he would say: \"Deeds are presented on these two days.\""],[2132,"The deeds of people are presented twice every week: on Monday and on Thursday. Every believer is forgiven except a servant between whom and his brother there is enmity. It is said: \"Leave these two or delay them until they reconcile.\""],[2133,"I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked him about fasting. He said: \"Fast one day from the month, and you will have the reward of the rest.\""],[2134,"My beloved instructed me with three things that I will never abandon, if Allah wills: he instructed me to pray the Duha prayer, to pray Witr before sleeping, and to fast three days of every month."],[2135,"My friend Abu al-Qasim ﷺ instructed me with three things: fasting three days of every month, praying Witr before sleeping, and praying two rak‘ahs of Duha."],[2137,"Uthman b. Abi al-‘As al-Thaqafi called for milk to give him to drink. Mutarrif said: \"I am fasting.\" He said: \"I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: 'Fasting is a shield from the Fire, like the shield of one of you in battle.' And I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'An excellent fast is the fast of three days of the month.'\""],[2138,"Fasting three days of every month is fasting a lifetime."],[2139,"Amr b. Uthman b. Mawhab narrated to me, from Musa b. Talhah, from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, from Abu Dharr, similarly. Abu Bakr said: \"I have presented this chapter in full in the 'Large Book' and explained that Musa b. Talhah heard from Abu Dharr the story of fasting, without the story of the rabbit, and he narrated both stories from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah.\""],[2140,"When you fast from the month, fast the thirteenth, fourteenth, and fifteenth."],[2141,"He used to fast three days from the beginning of every month, and Friday would fall during his fast."],[2142,"I asked ʿĀʾishah: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fast from the month, or from every month, three days? She said: \"Yes.\" I said: From which of it? She said: \"He would not care from which of it he fasted.\""],[2143,"\"Which of you arose this morning fasting?\" So Abū Bakr said: \"I [did].\" Then he said: \"Which of you fed a poor person today?\" Abū Bakr said: \"I [did].\" Then he said: \"Which of you followed a funeral procession today?\" Abū Bakr said: \"I [did].\" He said: \"Which of you visited a sick person today?\" Abū Bakr said: \"I [did].\" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"These traits have never combined in a man except that he enters Paradise.\""],[2144,"I asked ʿĀʾishah: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray al-Ḍuḥā? She said: \"No, unless he came from his absence. And I asked her: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fast a complete month? She said: \"No, by Allah, he did not fast a complete month other than Ramadan, until he passed on his way. And a month would not pass until he would fast some of it, and he would not break his fast until he would fast some of it.\" And I asked her: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray with the pre-dawn? She said: \"No, nor the praying ones.\""],[2145,"That he asked ʿĀʾishah about the fasting of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; so she said: He would fast until we would say: He will not break his fast, and he would break his fast until we would say: He will not fast, and he would fast Shaʿbān or most of Shaʿbān."],[2146,"Anas b. Mālik was asked about the fasting of the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: He would fast from the month until we would think that he did not intend to break his fast at all from it, and he would break his fast from the month until we would think that he did not intend to fast at all from it. And you would not wish to see him during the night praying except that you would see him, nor sleeping except that you would see him."],[2147,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast until I would think that he would not break his fast, and he would break his fast until I would say: He is not fasting. And most of his fasting was in Shaʿbān."],[2148,"\"Indeed in Paradise there are chambers whose exteriors are seen from their interiors, and their interiors from their exteriors.\" So a Bedouin stood up and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, for whom are they?\" He said: \"They are for the one who speaks good words, feeds food, persists in fasting, and stands for Allah at night while the people are asleep.\""],[2149,"Indeed in Paradise there is a chamber whose exterior is seen from its interior, and its interior from its exterior. Allah has prepared it for the one who feeds food, is gentle in speech, persists in fasting, and prays at night while the people are asleep."],[2150,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her while she was fasting, so she brought food near to him. He said: \"Come and eat.\" She said: \"I am fasting.\" So the Prophet ﷺ said: \"The fasting person, when food is eaten in his presence, the angels send blessings upon him.\""],[2151,"I heard a female client of ours called Laylā, from her grandmother Umm ʿUmārah bt. Kaʿb, the like of it exactly. And he added: \"until they finish, or they complete his eating.\" Shuʿbah was uncertain. ʿAlī said: Wakīʿ said: Ḥabīb."],[2152,"The fasting person, when those not fasting eat in his presence, the angels send blessings upon him until evening."],[2153,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to like our food, so he came one day and said: \"Do you have any of that food?\" I said: \"No.\" So he said: \"Then I am fasting.\""],[2155,"‘Ā’ishah bt. Ṭalḥah narrated to me, from ‘Ā’ishah, the Mother of the Believers."],[2156,"The Messenger of God ﷺ established brotherhood between Salmān and Abū al-Dardā’. Salmān came to visit Abū al-Dardā’ and found Umm al-Dardā’ in worn-out clothes. He said to her: “What is the matter with you?” She said: “Your brother has no need for this world.” Yūsuf added: “He fasts during the day and stands in prayer at night.” They said: When Abū al-Dardā’ came, he welcomed him and brought food near to him. He said to him: “Eat.” He said: “Am I not fasting?” He said: “I will not eat until you eat.” So he ate with him. He spent the night at his place. When it was the last part of the night, Abū al-Dardā’ went to stand in prayer, but Salmān held him back. When it was near dawn, he said: “Get up now.” So they got up and prayed. Then Salmān said to him: “Your Lord has a right over you, your self has a right over you, and your family and your guest have a right over you. So give each one with a right its due.” As for the Prophet ﷺ, when this was mentioned to him, he said: “Salmān al-Fārisī has spoken the truth.”"],[2157,"The cold spoil is fasting in winter."],[2158,"Men I am pleased with testified before me, among them ‘Umar, and the one I am most pleased with among them is ‘Umar, that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: “No prayer after the dawn prayer until the sun rises, and no prayer after the afternoon prayer until the sun sets.” And he forbade fasting on two days: the day of Fiṭr and the day of Naḥr."],[2159,"It is as if I am looking at ‘Alī on the white mule of the Messenger of God ﷺ in the mountain pass of the Anṣār, while he is saying: “O people, the Messenger of God ﷺ said: These are not days of fasting; these are days of eating and drinking.”"],[2160,"He invited a bedouin to his food after the day of Naḥr. The bedouin said: “I am fasting.” He said: “I heard ‘Abd Allāh b. ‘Umar say: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ forbidding fasting on these days.”"],[2161,"He and ‘Abd Allāh entered upon ‘Amr b. al-‘Āṣ on the day after or the day after the day after the day of sacrifice. ‘Amr brought food near to them, and ‘Abd Allāh said: “I am fasting.” ‘Amr said to him: “Break your fast, for these are the days that the Messenger of God ﷺ commanded to break fast and forbade fasting on them.” So ‘Abd Allāh broke his fast, ate, and I ate with him."],[2162,"Whoever fasts perpetually has neither fasted nor broken his fast, or: he did not fast and he did not break his fast."],[2163,"It was said to the Messenger of God ﷺ: “So-and-so never breaks his fast during the daytime perpetually.” He said: “He neither fasted nor broke his fast.”"],[2164,"“Was I not informed that you stand in prayer at night and fast during the day?” I said: “Indeed I [do not do that].” He said: “Then do not do it. For if you do that, your eye will become sunken and your self will become fatigued. Your self has a right over you, your family has a right over you, and your eye has a right over you. So sleep and stand, and fast and break your fast.”"],[2165,"I used to fast consecutively during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: O Messenger of Allah, I fast and do not break the fast. Should I fast while traveling? He said: If you wish, then fast, and if you wish, then break the fast."],[2167,"Whoever fasts perpetually, Hellfire will be constricted for him like this — and he clenched his hand into a fist (indicating ninety)."],[2168,"The one who fasts perpetually, Hellfire will be constricted for him as tightly as this — and he clenched his hand (indicating ninety)."],[2169,"I asked Abdullah b. Umar about fasting perpetually. He said: \"We used to count those among us as being from the foremost.\" He said: And I asked him about fasting one day and breaking the fast one day. He said: \"That does not leave for a fasting person any place of fasting.\" And I asked him about fasting three days of every month. He said: \"That is fasting perpetually and breaking it.\""],[2170,"I heard Abu Hurayrah say, while he was circumambulating the House: \"By the Lord of the Kabah, I did not prohibit fasting on Friday; Muhammad ﷺ, by the Lord of the Kabah, prohibited it.\""],[2171,"Do not fast on Friday, unless you fast a day before it or a day after it."],[2174,"Friday is a day of Eid, so do not make your day of Eid a day of fasting, unless you fast before it or after it."],[2175,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Juwayriyah bt. al-Harith while she was fasting on Friday. He said: \"Did you fast yesterday?\" She said: No. He said: \"Will you fast tomorrow?\" She said: No. He said: \"Then break your fast.\" Harun said: He said: \"Do you intend to fast tomorrow?\""],[2176,"Do not fast on Saturday, except in what is made obligatory upon you. And if one of you finds nothing but a grapevine twig or the bark of a tree, let him chew it."],[2177,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited fasting on Saturday, and he would say: \"If one of you finds nothing but a green twig, let him break his fast with it.\""],[2179,"Friday is an Eid, so do not make Friday a day of fasting, unless it is fasted along with a day before it or after it."],[2180,"Kurayb, the freed slave of Ibn Abbas, informed him that Ibn Abbas and some people from the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Umm Salamah to ask her on which days the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted most. She said: Saturday and Sunday. I returned to them and informed them, and it was as if they rejected that. So they all went to her and said: We sent this one to you about such and such, and he mentioned that you said such and such. She said: He spoke the truth. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted most often on Saturday and Sunday. He used to say: \"They are two days of Eid for the polytheists, and I want to differ from them.\""],[2181,"A woman may not fast any day outside the month of Ramadan while her husband is present, except with his permission."],[2182,"We met Abu Dharr while he was at the middle jamrah, and I asked him about Laylat al-Qadr. He said: No one was more eager to ask the Messenger of Allah about it than I. I said: O Messenger of Allah, was Laylat al-Qadr sent down upon the prophets with revelation to them therein, then it returns? He said: \"Rather, it is until the Day of Resurrection.\" So I said: O Messenger of Allah, which of them is it? He said: \"Had I been permitted, I would have told you, but seek it in the two sevenths, and do not ask me about it after that.\" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to the people and began speaking, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, in which of the two sevenths is it? He became angry with me with an anger the like of which he had never been angry with me before or after. Then he said: \"Did I not forbid you to ask me about it? Had I been permitted, I would have told you about it — or I would have told you it — but I am not sure that it is in the last seven.\""],[2183,"I asked Abu Dharr. He said: I said: Did you ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about Laylat al-Qadr? He said: I was the most inquisitive of people about it. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, inform me about Laylat al-Qadr — is it in Ramadan or in other than it? He said: \"Rather, it is in Ramadan.\" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will it remain with the prophets as long as they are present, and when the prophets are taken, will it be lifted? Or is it until the Day of Resurrection? He said: \"No, rather it is until the Day of Resurrection.\" He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, in which Ramadan is it? He said: \"Seek it in the first ten and the last ten.\" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke and spoke, so I seized upon a moment of his inattention, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, I adjure you by Allah to inform me, or why did you not inform me in which of the two tens it is? He said: He became angry with me like he had never been angry with me before or after. Then he said: \"If Allah willed, He would have made it known to you. Seek it in the last seven.\""],[2184,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed i'tikaf during the first ten days of Ramadan, then he observed i'tikaf during the middle ten in a Turkish tent, over the opening of which was a piece of matting. He said: He took the matting with his hand and moved it aside in a corner of the tent, then he looked out with his head and spoke to the people, so they drew near to him. He said: \"I observed i'tikaf during the first ten days seeking this night, then I observed i'tikaf during the middle ten, then I came and it was said to me: It is in the last ten. So whoever of you wishes to observe i'tikaf, let him do so.\" So the people observed i'tikaf with him. He said: \"And I was shown it on an odd night, and I saw myself prostrating on the morning of it in mud and water.\" Then he woke on the night of the twenty-first, and he had stood for the dawn prayer when the sky rained, and the mosque leaked, and I saw the mud and water. He came out when he finished the dawn prayer, and his forehead and nose were in water and mud, and it was the night of the twenty-first in the last ten."],[2185,"Umar used to invite me with the companions of Muhammad ﷺ and say to me: Do not speak until they speak. He said: He invited them and asked them about Laylat al-Qadr. He said: What do you think of the saying of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"Seek it in the last ten\"? On which night do you think it is? He said: Some of them said: The night of the first, some said: The night of the third, and another said: The fifth, while I was silent. He said: He said: What is the matter with you that you do not speak? He said: I said: If you permit me, O Commander of the Faithful, I will speak. He said: He said: I did not send for you except that you should speak. He said: I said: Shall I tell you my opinion? He said: [He said:] About that we ask you. He said: I said: The seventh, for I saw that Allah — Mighty and Majestic — mentioned seven heavens and of the earth the like thereof, and He created man from seven, and the growth of the earth is seven. He said: He said: This you have told me of what I know. What do you think of what I do not know? What is your saying: The growth of the earth is seven? He said: I said: Allah says: \"Then We split the earth in clefts, and We caused to grow therein\" up to His saying \"and fruits and herbage\", and herbage is what the earth produces of that which the animals eat and people do not eat. He said: Umar said: Were you unable to say as this young man said, whose skull bones have not yet joined? By Allah, I do not see the correct view except as you have said. And he said: I had commanded you not to speak until they speak, but now I command you to speak with them."],[2186,"Umar used to ask me along with the elders of the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would say: Do not speak until they speak. So he asked them about Laylat al-Qadr and said: You know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Seek it in the last ten, on odd nights.\""],[2187,"Al-abb: that which the earth produces of what people do not eat, and which livestock eat."],[2188,"Laylat al-Qadr was mentioned in the presence of Abu Bakrah, and he said: I will not seek it except in the last ten, after a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I heard him say: \"Seek it in the last ten — in nine remaining, or in seven remaining, or in five remaining, or in three remaining, or in the last night.\" So he would not pray in the twenty [nights] except like his prayer in the rest of the year, but when the ten entered, he exerted himself."],[2189,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed i'tikaf in the middle ten of Ramadan seeking Laylat al-Qadr before it became clear to him, then he ordered the building to be dismantled, and it was revealed to him that it was in the last ten, so he ordered it to be rebuilt. Then he came out to us and said: \"Indeed, Laylat al-Qadr was revealed to me, and I came out to make it clear to you, but two men disputed and it was taken away from me, so seek it in the ninth, the seventh, and the fifth.\" He said: I said: O Abu Sa'id, you know better about counting than us, so which night is the ninth, the seventh, and the fifth? He said: Yes, and we have more right to that. When it is the twenty-first night, the one that follows it is the ninth, then skip a night, then the one that follows is the seventh, then skip a night, then the one that follows is the fifth. O Abu Sa'id, the one you call the twenty-fourth, the twenty-sixth, and the twenty-second."],[2190,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said [meaning the like of it], and he added: \"and the third.\""],[2191,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ secluded himself from his wives, I said: O Messenger of Allah, [but] you were in a room for twenty-nine days. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"Indeed, the month may be twenty-nine.\""],[2192,"We mentioned Laylat al-Qadr in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: \"How much of the month has passed?\" We said: Twenty-two have passed and eight remain. He said: \"No, rather seven remain.\" They said: No, rather eight remain. He said: \"No, rather seven remain.\" They said: No, rather eight remain. He said: \"No, rather seven remain. The month is twenty-nine.\" Then he made a gesture with his hand until he counted twenty-nine. Then he said: \"Seek it this night.\""],[2193,"He was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: \"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'Seek it tonight,' and that was the night of the twenty-third. Then a man said: 'O Messenger of God, that is then the first of the eight [remaining nights].' He said: 'Rather, the first of the seven, for the month does not complete [thirty days].'\""],[2195,"The people used to see dreams, and they would relate them to the Messenger of God ﷺ. So the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"I see that your dreams have concurred upon the last seven [nights]. So whoever is seeking it, let him seek it in the last seven.\""],[2196,"Seek it in the last ten [nights]—meaning Laylat al-Qadr—and if one of you is weak or incapable, then let him not be overcome regarding the seven remaining [nights]."],[2198,"We sat with 'Abdullah b. Unays in the gathering of Juhaynah in this month, and we said: \"O Abu Yahya, did you hear the Messenger of God ﷺ [say anything] about this blessed night?\" He said: \"Yes. We sat with the Messenger of God ﷺ at the end of this month, and a man said to him: 'When should we seek this blessed night?' He said: 'Seek it this night, the twenty-third.' Then a man from the people said: 'That, then, is the first of the eight [remaining nights].'\""],[2199,"He was asked about Laylat al-Qadr, and he said: \"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'Seek it tonight,' and that was the night of the twenty-third. Then a man said: 'O Messenger of God, that is then the first of the eight [remaining nights].' He said: 'Rather, the first of the seven, for the month does not complete [thirty days].'\""],[2200,"\"Were it not for your foolish ones, I would place my hands over my ears and proclaim: Laylat al-Qadr is the twenty-seventh [night]. The report of one who did not belie me, from the report of one who did not belie him\"—meaning Ubayy b. Ka'b, from the Prophet."],[2201,"Laylat al-Qadr—I indeed know it. It is the night which the Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us [to seek]. It is the night of the twenty-seventh."],[2202,"Seek Laylat al-Qadr on the last night [of Ramadan]."],[2203,"I was shown Laylat al-Qadr, then I was made to forget it. It is in the last ten [nights] of its night, and it is a pleasant, bright night, neither hot nor cold."],[2204,"I asked Ubayy b. Ka'b, and said: \"Your brother Ibn Mas'ud says: 'Whoever stands in prayer the whole year will attain Laylat al-Qadr.'\" He said: \"May God have mercy on him. He intended that they not rely solely on it. He certainly knew that it is in the month of Ramadan, that it is in the last ten [nights], and that it is the night of the twenty-seventh.\" He said: We said: \"O Abu al-Mundhir, by what sign is that known?\" He said: \"By the mark, or by the sign that\""],[2205,"On Laylat al-Qadr: \"A mild night, neither hot nor cold; its sun rises on its day red and weak.\""],[2206,"I said to Ubayy b. Ka'b: Tell me about Laylat al-Qadr, for our companion—meaning Ibn Mas'ud—was asked about it and said: Whoever stands in prayer the whole year will attain it. He said: \"May God have mercy on Abu 'Abd al-Rahman. He knew that it is in Ramadan, but he disliked that they should rely on that, or he loved that they should not rely on it. By God, it is in Ramadan on the night of the twenty-seventh, without exception.\" He said: I said: Abu al-Mundhir, how did you know that? He said: By the sign that the Messenger of God ﷺ informed us. He said: I said to Zirr: What is the sign? He said: The sun rises on the morning of that night with no rays, like a large dish, until it rises high."],[2207,"Laylat al-Qadr is the night of the twenty-seventh or the twenty-ninth, and on that night the angels on earth are more numerous than the number of pebbles."],[2208,"Whoever prays the last Isha prayer in congregation in Ramadan has attained Laylat al-Qadr."],[2209,"I was shown Laylat al-Qadr, then my family woke me and I was made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten nights."],[2210,"I was shown Laylat al-Qadr, then my family woke me and I was made to forget it, so seek it in the last ten nights."],[2211,"The Prophet ﷺ came out to inform us about Laylat al-Qadr, but two Muslim men were disputing, so he said: \"I came out to inform you about Laylat al-Qadr, but so-and-so and so-and-so were disputing, so it was lifted, and perhaps that is better for you. So seek it in the ninth, the seventh, and the fifth.\""],[2212,"Whoever fasts Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his past sins will be forgiven."],[2213,"I said: O Messenger of God, I am in the desert, and by the praise of God I pray there, so instruct me to a night on which I should come down to this mosque and pray in it. He said: \"Come down on the night of the twenty-third.\" He said: I said to Ibn 'Abd Allah: How did your father do? He said: He would enter for the Asr prayer, then not leave until he prayed the Fajr prayer, then he would leave while his mount—meaning at the gate of the mosque—and he would ride it and go to his family."],[2214,"Will you not narrate to us a hadith you heard from your father, which your father heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ? He said: Yes. When Ramadan approached, the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Ramadan is a month in which God has made fasting obligatory, and I have established for the Muslims its night prayer. Whoever fasts it and prays in it out of faith and seeking reward will come out of his sins as on the day his mother gave birth to him.\""],[2215,"He used to command the night prayer of Ramadan without commanding it as an obligation, saying: \"Whoever prays in Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his past sins will be forgiven.\""],[2216,"Whoever prays in Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward will be forgiven."],[2217,"I heard al-Nu'man b. Bashir on the pulpit of Hims saying: We stood in prayer with the Messenger of God ﷺ in the month of Ramadan on the night of the twenty-third until a third of the night, then we stood with him on the night of the twenty-fifth until half the night, then we stood with him on the night of the twenty-seventh until we thought we would not attain the suhur, and we used to call it the suhur, but you say: the seventh night of the twenty-third, and we say: the seventh of the twenty-seventh, so are we more correct or you?"],[2218,"The Messenger of God ﷺ led us in prayer during the month of Ramadan on the night of the twenty-third until the first third of the night. Then he said, \"I do not think what you seek is anything but behind you.\" Then he led us on the night of the twenty-fifth until half the night. Then he said, \"I do not think what you seek is anything but behind you.\" Then we stood with him on the night of the twenty-seventh until dawn."],[2219,"We fasted with the Prophet ﷺ during Ramadan, and he did not lead us in night prayer until seven nights remained of the month. He led us until a third of the night had passed. Then he did not lead us on the sixth, and he led us on the fifth until half the night had passed. I said, \"O Messenger of God ﷺ, would that you had let us pray the remainder of this night as supererogatory prayer?\" He said, \"Indeed, whoever stands with the imam until he finishes, it is recorded for him as standing a whole night.\" Then he did not pray with us until three nights remained of the month. He led us on the third, and he gathered his family and his women, and he led us until we feared we would miss the falah. I said, \"What is the falah?\" He said, \"The suhur.\""],[2220,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came out in the middle of the night and prayed in the mosque. Some men prayed following his prayer. In the morning, people began to talk about it. On the third night, the people of the mosque increased in number, so he came out and prayed, and they prayed following his prayer. On the fourth night, the mosque could not contain its people, but the Messenger of God ﷺ did not come out to them. Some of the men began calling out, \"Prayer!\" but he did not come out. The Messenger of God ﷺ stayed inside until he came out for the dawn prayer. When he finished the dawn prayer, he stood up, faced them, pronounced the testimony of faith, praised and extolled God, then said, \"Now then, your situation has not been hidden from me, but I feared that the night prayer would be made obligatory upon you and you would be unable to perform it.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ used to encourage them to stand in prayer during Ramadan without commanding them decisively, saying, \"Whoever fasts Ramadan out of faith and seeking reward, his previous sins will be forgiven.\" Then the Messenger of God ﷺ passed away, and the matter remained thus during the caliphate of Abu Bakr and the early part of the caliphate of Umar, until Umar gathered them behind Ubayy b. Ka'b, who led them in prayer. That was the first time people gathered for the night prayer of Ramadan."],[2221,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came out and found people in Ramadan praying in a corner of the mosque. He said, \"Who are these?\" It was said, \"These are people who do not have the Quran with them, and Ubayy b. Ka'b is leading them in prayer, and they are praying following his prayer.\" The Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"They have done right – or excellent is what they have done.\""],[2222,"We fasted with the Prophet ﷺ during Ramadan, and he did not lead us in night prayer until seven nights remained of the month. He led us until a third of the night had passed. Then he did not lead us on the sixth, and he led us on the fifth until half the night had passed. I said, \"O Messenger of God ﷺ, would that you had let us pray the remainder of this night as supererogatory prayer?\" He said, \"Indeed, whoever stands with the imam until he finishes, it is recorded for him as standing a whole night.\" Then he did not pray with us until three nights remained of the month. He led us on the third, and he gathered his family and his women, and he led us until we feared we would miss the falah. I said, \"What is the falah?\" He said, \"The suhur.\""],[2224,"A man from Quda'ah came to the Messenger of God ﷺ and said to him, \"O Messenger of God, what do you think if I testify that there is no god but God and that you are the Messenger of God, and I perform the five prayers, fast the month, stand in prayer during Ramadan, and pay the zakat?\" The Prophet ﷺ said, \"Whoever dies upon this will be among the truthful and the martyrs.\""],[2225,"I asked Aisha, \"O mother, tell me about the night prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ.\" She said, \"His night prayer in the month of Ramadan and at other times was thirteen rak'ahs.\" This is the hadith of Abd al-Jabbar. Abu Hashim said: I came to Aisha and asked her about the prayer of the Messenger of God ﷺ in the month of Ramadan. She said, \"His prayer was thirteen rak'ahs, including the two rak'ahs of dawn.\""],[2226,"When the last ten days of Ramadan entered, the Messenger of God ﷺ would tighten his waist-wrapper, keep the night alive, and wake his family."],[2227,"The Prophet ﷺ would strive in the last ten days as he did not strive at other times."],[2228,"When Ramadan entered, the Messenger of God ﷺ would tighten his waist-wrapper, then he would not come to his bed until the month had ended."],[2229,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ wanted to perform spiritual retreat, he would pray the dawn prayer, then enter the place where he intended to retreat. When he wanted to retreat during the last ten of Ramadan, a tent was pitched for him. Aisha ordered that a tent be pitched for her, and Hafsa ordered that a tent be pitched for her. When Zaynab saw their two tents, she ordered that a tent be pitched for her. When the Messenger of God ﷺ saw that, he did not perform retreat in Ramadan, but performed retreat in Shawwal."],[2231,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed spiritual retreat during the first ten of Ramadan, then he performed retreat during the middle ten in a Turkish tent with a piece of matting over its entrance."],[2232,"We performed spiritual retreat with the Prophet ﷺ during the middle ten of Ramadan. When the morning of the twentieth came, we returned and he slept. He was shown the Night of Decree, then he was made to forget it. In the evening, he sat on the pulpit and addressed the people. He mentioned the hadith and said, \"Whoever was performing retreat with the Messenger of God ﷺ, let him return to his place of retreat.\""],[2233,"The Messenger of God ﷺ used to perform spiritual retreat (i'tikaf) in every Ramadan during the last ten days, but in the year in which he was taken, he performed retreat for twenty days."],[2234,"The companions of the Prophet ﷺ passed the middle seven of Ramadan, so the Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever among you is seeking it, let him seek it in the last seven.\""],[2235,"He used to perform spiritual retreat during the last ten days of Ramadan until God took him."],[2236,"The Prophet ﷺ intended to perform spiritual retreat, so 'A'ishah asked his permission to retreat with him, and he permitted her. She pitched her tent, and Hafsah asked her to seek permission for her to retreat with him. When Zaynab saw it, she pitched a tent with them, and she was a jealous woman. The Messenger of God ﷺ saw their tents and said: \"What is this? Do they intend piety by this?\" So he abandoned the retreat until he broke his fast from Ramadan, then he retreated for ten days in Shawwal."],[2237,"He used to perform spiritual retreat during the last ten days of Ramadan, but he did not retreat one year, so he retreated the following year for twenty nights."],[2238,"The Prophet ﷺ used to perform spiritual retreat during the last ten days of Ramadan, but he traveled one year and did not retreat, so he retreated the following year for twenty nights."],[2239,"The Prophet ﷺ used to perform spiritual retreat during the last ten days of Ramadan, but he did not retreat one year. When the following year came, he retreated for twenty.\""],[2240,"The 'umrah of the Messenger of God ﷺ from al-Ji'ranah was mentioned in the presence of Ibn 'Umar, and he said: \"He did not perform 'umrah from it.\" He said: \"'Umar had a vow in the pre-Islamic era to perform spiritual retreat for one night, so he asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered him to fulfill it, so he entered the mosque that night.\""],[2241,"'Umar had a vow in the pre-Islamic era to perform spiritual retreat for one night. He asked the Prophet ﷺ, and he ordered him to retreat. The Prophet ﷺ had given him a slave girl from the captives of Hunayn. While he was in retreat in the mosque, the people entered shouting \"Allahu Akbar,\" and he said: \"What is this?\" They said: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ has sent the captives of Hunayn.\" He said: \"So they sent that slave girl.\""],[2242,"'A'ishah, when she performed spiritual retreat in the mosque and entered her house for a need, she would not ask about a sick person except while passing by. 'A'ishah said: \"Indeed, the Messenger of God ﷺ would not enter the house except for human need, and he would put his head through to me while he was in the mosque, and I would comb it.\""],[2243,"his head to me:"],[2244,"He was performing spiritual retreat in the mosque, and 'A'ishah would come and he would put out his head, and she would comb it while she was menstruating."],[2245,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was observing i'tikaf. I came to visit him at night, and I talked to him. Then I got up to leave, and he got up to escort me back. Her dwelling was in the house of Usama. Two men of the Ansar passed by, and when they saw the Prophet ﷺ, they hastened. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Take your time. She is Safiyya bint Huyayy.\" They said: \"Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah!\" He said: \"Satan runs through the human being like blood flows, and I feared that he might cast evil into your hearts,\" or he said, \"something.\""],[2246,"She came to the Prophet ﷺ to visit him during his i'tikaf in the mosque in the last ten days of Ramadan. She spoke with him for a while, then she got up to leave. The Prophet ﷺ got up with her to escort her back, until when she reached the door of the mosque that was near the door of Umm Salama, two men of the Ansar passed by her."],[2247,"I used to converse at night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in i'tikaf. Or he said: She said: \"I used to stay up.\""],[2248,"When he observed i'tikaf, his bedding would be spread for him, or his bed would be placed for him behind the pillar of repentance."],[2249,"A house of palm fronds was built for the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, and he observed i'tikaf in Ramadan. Then when it was one night, he put out his head and heard them reciting, so he said: \"When a person prays, he converses privately with his Lord, so let each of you know what he is saying to Him; some of you should not raise their voices over others.\""],[2250,"We observed i'tikaf with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the middle ten days of Ramadan. When it was the morning of the twentieth, we went to move our belongings. He said to us: \"Whoever among you has observed i'tikaf should return to his place of i'tikaf, for I was shown this night and then made to forget it, and I was shown myself prostrating in water and mud.\""],[2251,"Umar b. al-Khattab asked the Prophet ﷺ , saying: \"I vowed to observe i'tikaf for one night in the pre-Islamic period.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Fulfill your vow.\""],[2253,"Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him."],[2255,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to observe i'tikaf in the middle ten days of Ramadan. He observed i'tikaf one year, until when it was the night of the twenty-first, which is the night from whose morning he would exit his i'tikaf, he said: \"Whoever observed i'tikaf with us, let him observe i'tikaf in the last ten days.\""],[2256,"While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was one day sitting out in the open before the people, a man came to him walking and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is faith?\" He said: \"To believe in Allah, His angels, His Book, the meeting with Him, and His messengers, and to believe in the final resurrection.\" He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is Islam?\" He said: \"To worship Allah and not associate anything with Him, to establish the prayer, to give the obligatory zakat, and to fast Ramadan.\" He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, what is ihsan?\" He said: \"To worship Allah as if you see Him; for if you do not see Him, He sees you.\" He said: \"O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour?\" He said: \"The one asked about it is not more knowledgeable than the questioner, but I will tell you about its signs: when the slave woman gives birth to her mistress\" – meaning the concubines – \"for that is one of its signs; and when the shepherds of black camels compete in constructing tall buildings, for that is one of its signs; and when the barefoot naked ones become heads of the people, for that is one of its signs – among five things that no one knows except Allah.\" Then he recited: \"Indeed, Allah [alone] has knowledge of the Hour...\" to the end of the surah. Then the man turned away, and the Prophet ﷺ said: \"This is Jibril, teaching the people their religion.\""],[2257,"The delegation of Abd al-Qays came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, this tribe of Rabi'a, and the disbelievers of Mudar have come between us and you, and we can only reach you during the sacred month. So command us with something we can take and call those behind us to.\" He said: \"I command you with four things and forbid you from four. I command you to believe in Allah, to testify that there is no god but Allah, to establish the prayer, to give the zakat, and to give one-fifth of the spoils of war. And I forbid you from al-dubba', al-hantam, al-naqir, and al-muzaffat.\""],[2258,"\"Faith in Allah.\" Then he explained it to them: \"The testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah.\" Then he mentioned the hadith in full."],[2259,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ passed away, the Arabs apostatized. Umar b. al-Khattab said, 'O Abu Bakr, do you intend to fight the Arabs?' He said: Abu Bakr said, 'The Messenger of God ﷺ only said: I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but God and that I am the Messenger of God, and establish prayer, and give zakat. By God, if they withhold from me a young she-goat that they used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ, I will surely fight them for it.' He said: Umar said, 'When I saw that Abu Bakr's opinion had been opened up for him, I knew that it was the truth.'"],[2260,"I have been commanded to fight the people until they testify that there is no god but God, and establish prayer, and give zakat. Then their blood and their wealth become inviolable to me, and their reckoning is with God."],[2261,"The first group to enter Paradise and the first group to enter the Fire were shown to me. As for the first group to enter Paradise: the martyr, a slave who perfects the worship of his Lord and gives sincere counsel to his master, and a self-restrained, chaste person with dependents. As for the first group to enter the Fire: a tyrannical ruler, a wealthy person who does not fulfill God's right in his wealth, and a boastful poor person."],[2262,"The consumer of riba, its agent, its two witnesses when they know it, the woman who tattoos, the woman who gets tattooed, the one who withholds charity, and the one who reverts to being a Bedouin after emigration are cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad ﷺ on the Day of Resurrection."],[2263,"I reached the Prophet ﷺ while he was sitting in the shade of the Ka'bah. When he saw me, he said, 'They are the greatest losers, by the Lord of the Ka'bah.' He said: I sat down, but I could not settle before I stood up and said, 'Who are they, may my father and mother be your ransom?' He said, 'They are those who have the most wealth, except for the one who gives his wealth like this and like this' — four times — 'and how few they are. And no owner of camels, cattle, or sheep who does not pay its zakat except that it comes on the Day of Resurrection as large and fat as it ever was, goring him with its horns and trampling him with its hooves. Every time the last of them passes by, the first of them returns to him, until judgment is passed among the people.' This is the hadith of Ishaq. And Ja'far said: On the authority of Abu Dharr, he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'No owner of camels...'"],[2264,"No servant fails to pay the zakat of his wealth except that he is brought with his wealth, and plates are heated for him in the Fire of Hell, and his sides, forehead, and back are branded with them, until God judges between His servants on a day whose measure is a thousand years of what you count. Then he sees his path, either to Paradise or to the Fire. And no servant fails to pay the charity of his camels except that he is brought with his camels as abundant as they ever were, and a wide, level plain is spread out for them, and they walk over him. Every time the last of them passes, the first of them returns, until God judges between His servants on a day whose measure is a thousand years of what you count. Then he sees his path, either to Paradise or to the Fire. And no servant fails to pay the charity of his sheep except that he is brought with his sheep as abundant as they ever were, and a wide, level plain is spread out for them, and they walk over him. Every time the last of them passes, the first of them returns, trampling him with their cloven hooves and goring him with their horns, none of them having twisted horns or no horns, until God judges between His servants on a day whose measure is a thousand years of what you count. Then he sees his path, either to Paradise or to the Fire.' They said, 'O Messenger of God, what about horses?' He said, 'Horses have good tied to their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection. Horses are of three kinds: for one man they are a reward, for another they are a covering, and upon another they are a burden.' Then he mentioned the hadith in its entirety. They said, 'What about donkeys, O Messenger of God?' He said, 'God has not revealed to me anything concerning them except this unique, comprehensive verse: So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.'"],[2265,"Suhayl b. Abi Salih narrated to us with this chain, and he mentioned the hadith in its entirety, and he said in all of them: 'On a day whose measure was fifty thousand years of what you count,' and he also said: 'Then they run freely over him.'"],[2266,"The treasure of one of you will appear on the Day of Resurrection as a bald-headed snake with two fangs, pursuing its owner while he seeks refuge from it. It will keep pursuing him while he flees from it, until it makes him swallow his finger."],[2267,"Whoever leaves behind a treasure, it will be represented for him on the Day of Resurrection as a bald-headed snake with two fangs, pursuing him. He will say, 'Woe to you, what are you?' It will say, 'I am your treasure that you left behind.' It will keep pursuing him until it makes him swallow his hand, and then it will crunch it, and then it will pursue the rest of his body."],[2268,"No man fails to pay its zakat except that a snake is made for him on the Day of Resurrection, coiled around his neck on the Day of Resurrection.' Then the Prophet ﷺ recited to us its confirmation from the Book of God: 'They will be collared with what they withheld on the Day of Resurrection.'"],[2269,"Indeed, the one who does not pay the zakat of his wealth will have his wealth represented on the Day of Resurrection as a bald-headed snake with two fangs. It will cling to him or be coiled around his neck, saying, 'I am your treasure.'"],[2270,"When you pay the zakat of your wealth, you have removed its evil from yourself."],[2271,"I gave allegiance to the Messenger of God ﷺ to establish prayer, to give zakat, and to give sincere counsel to every Muslim.\""],[2272,"When we came to the land of Abyssinia, we had the best of neighbors there, the Negus. She then mentioned the hadith in full, and said in the hadith: She said: The one who spoke to him was Ja'far b. Abi Talib. He said to him: O king, we were a people of ignorance, worshipping idols, eating carrion, committing indecencies, severing ties of kinship, and mistreating neighbors. The strong among us would devour the weak. We remained upon that until God sent to us a Messenger from among us, whose lineage, truthfulness, trustworthiness, and chastity we recognized. He called us to God to declare His oneness, to worship Him, and to cast off what we and our fathers worshipped besides Him of stones and idols. He commanded us to speak the truth, fulfill trusts, maintain ties of kinship, be good to neighbors, and refrain from what is forbidden and from bloodshed. He forbade us from indecencies, false speech, consuming the orphan's property, and slandering chaste women, and commanded that we worship God alone, associating nothing with Him. He commanded us to pray, give zakat, and fast.\" She said: He enumerated to him the matters of Islam. So we believed him and had faith in him, and followed him in what he brought from God. We worshipped God alone, without associating anything with Him, forbade what He forbade us, and deemed lawful what He made lawful for us."],[2273,"That when Abu Bakr was appointed caliph, he wrote for him when he sent him to Bahrain. He wrote this letter for him: \"In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the obligation of charity that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger. And he mentioned the hadith, and said: 'No decrepit animal, nor one with a defect, nor a billy goat may be given in charity, unless the charity collector so wishes.'\""],[2274,"Bring one quarter of one tenth from every forty dirhams, and there is nothing on less than two hundred. If it is two hundred dirhams, then five dirhams are due, and whatever exceeds that is calculated accordingly."],[2275,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs of silver."],[2277,"For every freely grazing camel, for every forty a bint labun is due. Camels are not to be separated from their count. Whoever gives it seeking reward will have its reward, and whoever withholds it, I will take it and half of his camels, as a firm decree from the decrees of our Lord. Nothing of it is lawful for the family of Muhammad."],[2278,"The Prophet ﷺ wrote down the charity but did not send it out to his agents until the Prophet ﷺ passed away. Then he mentioned the hadith in full. He said regarding sheep: for every forty grazing alone, one sheep up to one hundred and twenty."],[2279,"The Prophet ﷺ sent him to Yemen and told him to take from cattle: for every thirty cattle a tabi' (one-year-old male or female), and for every forty cattle a musinnah (two-year-old female), and from every adult a dinar or its equivalent in Ma'afir garments."],[2280,"The Prophet ﷺ wrote for him a letter, in which: regarding cattle: for thirty cattle a tabi', and for forty a musinnah."],[2282,"No zakat is due on a plow animal."],[2283,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him as a zakat collector. His father said: \"Do not leave until you have made a covenant with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.\" When he wanted to leave, he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: \"O Qays, do not come on the Day of Resurrection with a camel on your neck that bellows, or a cow that lows, or a sheep that bleats, and do not be like Abu Righal.\" Sa'd said: \"And who is Abu Righal?\" He said: \"A zakat collector whom Salih sent. He found a man in Al-Ta'if with a small flock of sheep, nearly a hundred except for one, with their young; and the man had a small son whose mother had died, and the milk of that one sheep was his livelihood.\" The owner of the sheep said: \"Who are you?\" He said: \"I am the messenger of the Messenger of God.\" He welcomed him, and said: \"These are my sheep, so take whichever you like.\" He looked at the milch ewe and said: \"This one.\" The man said: \"This boy, as you see, has no food or drink other than it.\" He said: \"If you love milk, I love it too.\" The man said: \"Take two sheep in its place,\" but he refused. He kept increasing and offering until he offered five sheep in its place, and he refused. When the man saw that, he took his bow and shot him, killing him. Then he said: \"It is not fitting that anyone should bring this news to the Messenger of God before me.\" The owner of the sheep came to Salih the Prophet ﷺ and told him, and Salih said: \"O God, curse Abu Righal; O God, curse Abu Righal.\" Sa'd b. 'Ubadah said: \"O Messenger of Allah, exempt Qays from collecting zakat.\""],[2284,"That when Abu Bakr was appointed caliph, he wrote for him when he sent him to Bahrain. He wrote this letter for him: \"In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the obligation of charity that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger. And he mentioned the hadith, and said: 'No decrepit animal, nor one with a defect, nor a billy goat may be given in charity, unless the charity collector so wishes.'\""],[2285,"That he sent to a man, and the man sent him a weaned camel colt. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"The zakat collector of Allah and the zakat collector of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came, and he sent a weaned camel colt. O Allah, do not bless him in it, nor in his camels.\" That reached the man, so he sent him a she-camel of the finest and most beautiful of them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: \"What the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said reached So-and-so, so he sent a she-camel of the finest of them. O Allah, bless him and his camels.\""],[2286,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Mu'adh b. Jabal to Yemen and said: \"You will come to a people of the People of the Book. When you come to them, call them to testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. If they obey that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them five prayers every day and night. If they obey that, then inform them that Allah has made obligatory upon them a charity from their wealth, to be taken from their rich and returned to their poor. If they obey you in that, then beware of taking the best of their wealth, and fear the supplication of the oppressed, for there is no barrier between it and Allah.\""],[2288,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me as a zakat collector over Bali and 'Udhrah and all of Banu Sa'd b. Hudhaym of Quda'ah. He said: I collected zakat from them until I passed by a man from them whose dwelling and land was the nearest of their dwellings to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Madinah. He said: When he gathered his wealth for me, I found nothing due on it except a bint makhad [a female camel in its second year]. He said: I said to him: \"Give the bint makhad, for it is your zakat.\" He said: \"That is one that has no milk and no riding ability. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never stood over my wealth, nor any messenger of his before you, and I would not lend Allah from my wealth what has no milk and no riding ability. But take this young, great, fat she-camel and take it.\" I said: \"I will not take what I have not been commanded to take. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is near you. If you wish, go to him and present to him what you presented to me, and do so; if he accepts it from you, it is accepted, and if he rejects it, it is rejected.\" He said: \"I will do that.\" So he went out with me and brought the she-camel he had offered me until we came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said to him: \"O Prophet of Allah, your messenger came to me to take the zakat of my wealth. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah never stood over my wealth, nor any messenger of his ever before him. I gathered my wealth for him, and he claimed that what I owe on it is a bint makhad, and that is what has no milk and no riding ability. I offered him a young, great, fat she-camel to take, but he refused, and here it is. I have brought it to you, O Messenger of Allah, so take it.\" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: \"That is what is due on you, and if you voluntarily give good, Allah will reward you for it, and we will accept it from you.\" He said: \"Here it is, O Messenger of Allah. I have brought it to you, so take it.\" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that it be taken, and he supplicated for blessing in his wealth."],[2289,"And time passed from that until the governorship of Mu'awiyah b. Abi Sufyan, who appointed Marwan b. al-Hakam over Madinah. He sent me as a zakat collector over Bali and 'Udhrah and all of Banu Sa'd b. Hudhaym of Quda'ah. He said: I passed by that man, now an old man among his wealth, and I collected from him thirty hiqqahs [three-year-old she-camels], including their stud, from one thousand five hundred camels. Ibn Ishaq said: We see that 'Umarah did not take the stud along with them except in a year when a man's zakat reached thirty hiqqahs, in which case the stud would be added to them."],[2290,"That Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, when he became caliph, wrote to him: \"In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the obligation of charity that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger. They mentioned the hadith, and said: 'Separate herds should not be combined, nor combined herds separated, for fear of charity. And whatever belongs to two partners, they shall settle between themselves equally.'\""],[2291,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ in the year of the conquest saying: \"O people, whatever alliance existed in Jahiliyyah, Islam only increased it in strength, and there is no alliance in Islam. The Muslims are one hand against those besides them. Their humblest may grant protection, and their farthest returns their spoils. Their raiding parties return to their main body. A believer is not to be killed for a disbeliever. The blood money for a disbeliever is half that of a believer. No jalab and no janab, and their zakat is not to be taken except in their own lands.\""],[2292,"That Abu Bakr wrote to him: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. This is the obligatory charity that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger. Then they mentioned the hadith, and they said in the hadith: Whoever owes a jadha‘ah as charity but does not have a jadha‘ah and has a hiqqah, it will be accepted from him, and he will add with it two sheep if they are easy for him, or twenty dirhams. Bundar said: 'And he gives in its place two sheep. And whoever owes a hiqqah as charity but does not have a hiqqah and has a jadha‘ah, the jadha‘ah will be accepted from him, and the collector will give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. And whoever owes a hiqqah but has only a bint labun, a bint labun will be accepted from him, and he will give with it two sheep or twenty dirhams. And whoever owes a bint labun but does not have one and has a hiqqah, the hiqqah will be accepted from him, and the collector will give him with it twenty dirhams or two sheep. And whoever owes a bint labun but does not have one and has a bint makhad, a bint makhad will be accepted from him, and he will give with it twenty dirhams or two sheep. And whoever owes a bint makhad but does not have one and has a bint labun, a bint labun will be accepted from him, and the collector will give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. And whoever does not have a bint makhad as required and has a male ibn labun, it will be accepted from him and nothing with it.'"],[2293,"The Banu Murrah b. 'Ubayd sent me with the charity of their wealth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I arrived in Madinah. I found him sitting among the Muhajirun and the Ansar. I brought camels that were like the roots of the arta tree. He said: 'Who is the man?' I said: 'Ikrash b. Dhu'ayb.' He said: 'Go higher in lineage.' I said: 'Son of Hurqus b. Ja'dah b. 'Amr b. al-Nazzal b. Murrah b. 'Ubayd, and these are the charities of the Banu Murrah b. 'Ubayd.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled, then said: 'These are the camels of my people, these are the charities of my people.' Then he ordered that they be branded with the branding iron of charity camels and be added to them. Then he took my hand and went with me to the house of Umm Salamah. And he mentioned the hadith."],[2294,"When my mother gave birth, I took the child to the Prophet ﷺ to perform tahnik. There was the Prophet ﷺ in a sheep pen of ours, branding sheep."],[2295,"I have waived for you charity on horses and slaves, so pay the zakat of wealth from every forty dirhams."],[2296,"A Muslim is not liable for charity on his slave or his horse."],[2297,"Then 'Abdullah b. Dinar narrated to us, from Sulayman b. Yasar, from 'Irak b. Malik, saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah trace it back: A Muslim is not liable for charity on his horse or his slave."],[2298,"A Muslim is not liable for charity on his horse or his slave."],[2299,"A Muslim is not liable for charity on his slave or his horse except the sadaqat al-fitr."],[2300,"There is no charity on a slave except the sadaqat al-fitr."],[2301,"Some people from the people of Syria came to 'Umar and said: 'We have acquired wealth: horses and slaves, and we would like to have zakat and purification for them.' He said: 'What my two companions before me did not do, I will not do.' He consulted the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, and among them was 'Ali. 'Ali said: 'It is good, as long as it is not a jizyah levied on them regularly.'"],[2302,"There is no slave who has wealth and does not pay its zakat but it will be gathered on the Day of Resurrection, and plates will be heated in Hell and his sides and back will be branded with them, until Allah judges between His servants on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years of what you count. Then he will see his way, either to Paradise or to the Fire.' And he mentioned the hadith in full regarding the story of camels and sheep. He said: It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what about horses?' He said: 'Horses have good tied to their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection. Horses are of three kinds: for one man they are a reward, for another a covering, and for another a burden. As for the one for whom they are a reward, he is the one who keeps them for the sake of Allah and prepares them for Him. Whatever goes into their bellies is recorded for him as a reward, even if he passes by a meadow or two meadows and lets them graze there, it is recorded for him as a reward for what they put into their bellies. Even if they run up a hill or two hills, a reward is recorded for him for every step they take. Even if he comes to a river and waters them from it, he is rewarded for every drop that goes into their bellies, until he mentioned the reward in their dung and urine. As for the one for whom they are a covering, he is the one who keeps them for dignity, beauty, and protection, and does not neglect their due on their backs and bellies in ease or hardship. As for the one upon whom they are a burden, he is the one who keeps them out of pride, vanity, and ostentation.' They said: 'What about donkeys, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Allah has not revealed anything to me about them except this comprehensive, unique verse: So whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.'"],[2303,"A flock of charity sheep gathered with the Messenger of God ﷺ. He said, \"Go out with it, O Abu Dharr.\" He said: So I went out with it to al-Rabadhah."],[2304,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs of silver."],[2305,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs, nor on what is less than five camels, nor on what is less than five wasqs."],[2306,"There is no charity on what is less than five wasqs, nor on what is less than five uqiyyahs, nor on what is less than five camels."],[2307,"When Abu Bakr al-Siddiq ؓ was appointed caliph, he wrote for him: \"In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligation of charity that the Messenger of God ﷺ imposed upon the Muslims, which God commanded His Messenger.\" They mentioned the hadith, and they said in the hadith: \"And on silver, one quarter of one tenth; if it is only one hundred and ninety, there is no charity on it unless its owner wills.\" And Abu Musa said: \"If there is no wealth except one hundred and ninety.\""],[2308,"Bring one quarter of one tenth from every forty dirhams, and there is nothing on less than two hundred. If it is two hundred dirhams, then five dirhams are due, and whatever exceeds that is calculated accordingly."],[2309,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs of silver."],[2310,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs of silver, nor on what is less than five camels, nor on what is less than five wasqs of dates."],[2311,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs, nor on what is less than five camels, nor on what is less than five wasqs of dates."],[2312,"Zakat is not due on wheat and dates until they reach five wasqs, nor on silver until it reaches five uqiyyahs, nor on camels until they reach five."],[2313,"There is no charity on what is less than five wasqs, nor on what is less than five uqiyyahs, nor on what is less than five camels."],[2314,"There is no charity on what is less than five uqiyyahs, nor on what is less than five camels, nor on what is less than five wasqs of dates."],[2315,"There is no zakah upon a Muslim man in his vineyard or his crops if it is less than five wasqs."],[2316,"From Jabir and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, they both said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said— Abu Bakr said: 'Amr b. Dinar did not hear this report from Jabir—: \"\""],[2318,"There is no sadaqah on grains less than five wasqs, and no sadaqah on dates less than five wasqs."],[2319,"For that which the sky waters, one-tenth; and for that which is watered by a camel-drawn bucket, half of one-tenth."],[2320,"That for what the sky and springs water, or is athari (naturally irrigated)—one-tenth; and for what is watered by animal-drawn irrigation, half of one-tenth."],[2321,"For what rivers and rain clouds water, one-tenth; and for what is watered by camel-drawn bucket, half of one-tenth."],[2322,"There is no zakah on less than five wasqs. A wasq is sixty makhtūm (sā's)."],[2323,"Some people were blaming one another over their bad fruits, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: \"And do not aim toward the defective from it, spending, while you would not take it except with veiled eyes.\" He said: \"Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade two kinds: the ju'rūr and a kind of ḥubayq dates.\""],[2324,"Regarding this verse which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: \"And do not aim toward the defective from it, spending,\" he said: \"It is the ju'rūr and the kind of ḥubayq; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that they be accepted in sadaqah.\""],[2325,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded sadaqah, so a man came with bunches from this sahl — Sufyan said: meaning shīṣ (bad dates) — and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Who brought this?' And no one would bring anything except that it was attributed to the one who brought it. And it was revealed: 'And do not aim toward the defective from it, spending.' He said: 'And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade ju'rūr and the kind of ḥubayq from being accepted in sadaqah.'"],[2326,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of Tabuk until we came to Wadi al-Qura, and there was a woman in a garden of hers. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his companions: 'Estimate (the yield).' So the people estimated, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ estimated ten wasqs. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the woman: 'Count what comes out of it until I return to you, if Allah wills.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to Tabuk, then returned and we returned with him until we came to Wadi al-Qura. He said to the woman: 'How much did your garden produce?' She said: 'Ten wasqs, the estimate of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"],[2327,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to send Ibn Rawahah to estimate the palm trees when the first fruit ripened, then he would give the Jews the choice to take them according to that estimate or to hand them over to the Jews according to that. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the estimation so that the zakah could be calculated before the fruit was eaten and dispersed."],[2329,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said regarding the zakat of the vine: \"It is assessed as date palms are assessed, then its zakat is paid as raisins as the zakat of date palms is paid as dates.\""],[2330,"The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded ʿAttāb b. Asīd to assess grapes as he assesses date palms, then its zakat is paid as raisins as it is paid as dates.\" He said: So that is the Sunnah of the Messenger of God ﷺ regarding date palms and grapes."],[2332,"So that is the Sunnah of the Messenger of God ﷺ regarding date palms and grapes."],[2333,"He came to us while we were in the market, and said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"When you assess, take and leave a third; if you do not take or leave a third—Shuʿbah was uncertain about the third—then leave a fourth.\""],[2334,"When you assess, take and leave a third; if you do not leave a third, then leave a fourth."],[2335,"There is no owner of camels who does not pay their due from their strength and their ease except that he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection as abundant as they ever were, and he will be thrown down on a wide, level plain for them. They will trample him with their feet and their ferocious ones will crush him. Every time their last passes, their first will be returned until judgment is passed among the creatures. Then he will see his way. And there is no owner of cows who does not pay their due from their strength and their ease except that they will be brought on the Day of Resurrection as abundant as they ever were and as numerous as they ever were, and he will be thrown down on a wide, level plain for them. They will gore him with their horns and trample him with their hooves. Every time their last passes, their first will return upon him until judgment is passed among the creatures. Then he will see his way. And there is no owner of sheep who does not pay their due from their strength and their ease except that they will be brought on the Day of Resurrection as abundant as they ever were and as numerous as they ever were, and he will be thrown down on a wide, level plain for them. They will gore him with their horns and trample him with their hooves. Every time their last passes, their first will return upon him until judgment is passed among the creatures. Then he will see his way, or his way.\""],[2337,"A man from Banū ʿĀmir passed by him and it was said: This is among the wealthiest of people. Abū Hurayrah called him and asked him about that. He said: Yes, I have a hundred red camels, a hundred dark ones, and such and such sheep. Abū Hurayrah said: Beware of the camels' pads, beware of the sheep's hooves. I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"There is no man who has camels and does not pay their due in their hardship and their ease—and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: Their hardship and their ease, their difficulty and their ease—except that he will come out for them on a wide, level plain, and they will come to him as swift and strong as they can be, as fat or as large as they can be—Shuʿbah was uncertain—and they will trample him with their pads. Every time their last passes over him, their first will be brought back upon him on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the people, and then he will see his way. And there is no servant who has sheep and does not pay their due in their hardship and their ease—the Messenger of God ﷺ said: Their hardship and their ease, their difficulty and their ease—[except that he will come out for them] on a wide, level plain, as swift and strong as they can be and as fat or as large as they can be—Shuʿbah was uncertain—and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns. Every time their last passes over him, their first will be brought back upon him on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the people, and then he will see his way. And there is no man who has cows and does not pay their due [in their hardship] and their ease—and the Messenger of God ﷺ said: Their hardship and their ease, their difficulty and their ease—except that he will come out for them on a wide, level plain, as swift and strong as they can be and as fat or as large as they can be—Shuʿbah was uncertain—and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns. Every time their last passes over him, their first will be brought back upon him on a day whose measure is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the people, and then he will see his way.\" So the ʿĀmirī said to him: What is the due of camels, O Abū Hurayrah? He said: \"You give the generous one, grant the abundant milk, lend the mount, allow the stud, and provide milk.\""],[2338,"The Messenger of God ﷺ took alms from the mines of al-Qabaliyyah, and he granted Bilāl b. al-Ḥārith the entire ʿAqīq. When ʿUmar came, he said to Bilāl: \"The Messenger of God ﷺ did not grant it to you to withhold it from the people; he granted it to you only to work it.\" He said: So ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb distributed the ʿAqīq to the people."],[2339,"Banū Shabābah, a clan from Fahm, used to give the Messenger of God ﷺ a tenth of their honey, one skin from every ten skins, and he protected two valleys for them. When ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb came, he appointed Sufyān b. ʿAbd Allāh al-Thaqafī over them, but they refused to give him anything, saying: That is something we used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ. Sufyān wrote to ʿUmar about that, and ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb ؓ wrote to him: \"Bees are only rain-flies that God drives as provision to whom He wills. If they give you what they used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ, then protect their two valleys for them; otherwise leave the people free between them.\" So they gave him what they used to give to the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he protected their two valleys for them."],[2340,"Banū Shabābah, a clan from Fahm"],[2341,"The jubar (camel that causes injury or death) is that for which there is no blood money."],[2342,"A man from Muzaynah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'What do you think about what is found in a frequented road or in an inhabited village?' He said: 'Announce it for a year. If its seeker comes, give it to him. Otherwise, it is your business. If its seeker ever comes, then give it to him. As for what is in a road other than a frequented one, or a village other than an inhabited one, then on it and on buried treasure (rikaz) the khums is due.'"],[2343,"Jarir narrated to us, from Muhammad b. Ishaq. Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition, and in the Maktabah al-Islami edition."],[2344,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the collection of charity. Someone said, criticizing: 'Ibn Jamil, Khalid b. al-Walid, and al-Abbas b. Abd al-Muttalib have refused to give charity.'"],[2345,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent Umar b. al-Khattab as a collector for zakat."],[2347,"The Prophet ﷺ borrowed a young male camel from a man and said: 'When the zakat comes, we will repay you.' When the zakat came, he said to Abu Rafi': 'Give the man his young camel.' I looked and found only a four-year-old camel or older. I told the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: 'Give it to him, for the best of people are those who are best in repaying.'"],[2348,"The collector of unlawful taxes (maks) will not enter Paradise."],[2349,"The zakat collector who fulfills his duty is like the warrior in the path of Allah until he returns to his home."],[2350,"There is no faith for the one who has no trustworthiness, and the one who transgresses in charity is like the one who withholds it."],[2351,"That the Messenger of God ﷺ was one day in her house, and with him were men from his companions conversing, when a man came and said: O Messenger of God, the zakat of such-and-such dates is so-and-so? The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"So-and-so.\" The man said: But so-and-so transgressed against me and took from me so-and-so, adding a sa'. The Messenger of God ﷺ said to him: \"Then how will it be when one who transgresses against you more severely than this transgression is set over you as collector?\" The people entered into discussion and the talk overwhelmed them until one of them said: O Messenger of God, if a man is away at his camels, his livestock, and his crops, and he pays the zakat of his wealth, but the right is transgressed against him, what should he do while he is away? The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Whoever pays the zakat of his wealth with a good soul, seeking the face of God and the Hereafter, has not hidden anything of his wealth, and establishes prayer, then pays the zakat but the right is transgressed against him, so he takes up his weapon and fights and is killed, he is a martyr.\""],[2352,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, when he prayed 'Asr, would go to Banu 'Abd al-Ashhal and converse with them until it was time for Maghrib. Abu Rafi' said: While the Prophet ﷺ was hurrying to Maghrib, we passed by al-Baqi', and he said: \"Uff to you, uff to you.\" That seemed grave to me, so I fell back, thinking he meant me. He said: \"What is with you? Walk.\" I said: You have done something. He said: \"What is with you?\" I said: You said uff to me. He said: \"No, but this so-and-so I sent as a collector over Banu so-and-so, and he embezzled a striped cloak, so he will be clothed in something like it of fire.\""],[2353,"\"Whoever among you works for us in any capacity and conceals from us a needle or anything greater, it is embezzlement he will bring on the Day of Resurrection.\" A black man from the Ansar stood up—it is as if I see him—and said: O Messenger of God, accept my resignation. He said: \"Why?\" He said: I heard you say such-and-such. He said: \"And I say that: whoever we appoint to any task, let him bring its little and its much. What is given to him he takes, and what he is forbidden he desists.\""],[2354,"The Messenger of God ﷺ appointed a man from al-Azd called Ibn al-Utbiyya over zakat. When he came, he said: This is for you, and this was gifted to me. The Messenger of God ﷺ addressed the people, praised and extolled God, then said: \"What is the matter with an agent we send, and he comes and says: This is for you, and this was gifted to me? Would that he sat in his father's house and his mother's house, and see whether a gift comes to him or not. By the One in whose hand is Muhammad's soul, none of you comes with anything but he will be paraded with it on the Day of Resurrection, carrying it on his neck—if it is a camel, it has a grumble; or a cow, it has a moo; or a bull, it bellows—and perhaps he said: it bleats.\" He said: Then he raised his hands until we saw the whiteness of his armpits, then said: \"O God, have I conveyed?\" three times."],[2355,"The Messenger of God ﷺ appointed a man from al-Azd over the zakat of Banu Sulaym, called Ibn al-Lutbiyya. When he came, he audited him, and he said: This is your wealth, and this is a gift. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Then would that you sat in your father's house and your mother's house until your gift came to you, if you are truthful.\" Then he addressed us, praised and extolled God, then said: \"Now then, I appoint a man among you to a task from what God has entrusted to me, and he comes and says: This is your wealth, and this is a gift to me. Would that he sat in his father's house and his mother's house until his gift came to him, if he is truthful. By God, none of you takes anything without his right except that he meets God carrying it on the Day of Resurrection. I shall recognize any of you who meets God carrying a camel that grumbles, or a cow that moos, or a sheep that bleats.\" Then he raised his hands until the whiteness of his armpits was seen, then said: \"O God, have I conveyed? My eyes have seen and my ears have heard.\""],[2356,"\"When the zakat collector comes to you, let him leave you while he is pleased with you.\""],[2357,"That his father Rabi'a b. al-Harith and al-'Abbas b. 'Abd al-Muttalib said to 'Abd al-Muttalib b. Rabi'a and al-Fadl b. 'Abbas: Go to the Messenger of God ﷺ and say to him: O Messenger of God, we have reached the age you see, and we would like to marry. You, O Messenger of God, are the most dutiful and most connected of people, and our fathers do not have what they can spend on us. Appoint us, O Messenger of God, over the zakat collections, and we will deliver to you as the agents deliver, and we will earn from it whatever benefit there is. He said: Then 'Ali b. Abi Talib came while we were in that state and said to us: The Messenger of God ﷺ, no by God, will not appoint any of you over zakat. Rabi'a b. al-Harith said to him: This is from your envy; you have attained the good of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and we did not envy you for it. He threw off his cloak, then lay down on it, then said: I am Abu Hasan the chief. By God, I will not leave my place here until your two sons return to you with the answer of what you sent to the Messenger of God ﷺ. 'Abd al-Muttalib said: I and al-Fadl went until the noon prayer had been established, and we prayed with the people. Then I and al-Fadl hurried to the door of the room of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he was that day with Zaynab bt. Jahsh. We stood at the door until the Messenger of God ﷺ came, took hold of my ear and al-Fadl's ear, then said: \"Bring out what you are hiding.\" Then he entered and gave permission to me and al-Fadl. We entered and deferred speech a little, then I spoke to him, or al-Fadl spoke to him—'Abd Allah b. al-Harith was uncertain about that. He said: When we spoke to him with what our fathers had commanded us, the Messenger of God ﷺ was silent for a while and raised his eyes toward the ceiling of the house until it seemed long to us that he would not return anything, until we saw Zaynab motioning from behind the screen with her hands not to be hasty and that the Messenger of God ﷺ was considering our matter. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ lowered his head and said to us: \"This zakat is only the filth of people, and it is not lawful for Muhammad or the family of Muhammad. Call Nawfal b. al-Harith for me.\" So he called Nawfal b. al-Harith, and he said: \"O Nawfal, marry 'Abd al-Muttalib.\" So he married me. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Call Mahmiya b. Jaz'.\" He was a man from Banu Zubayd whom the Messenger of God ﷺ had appointed over the fifth shares. The Messenger of God ﷺ said to Mahmiya: \"Marry al-Fadl.\" So Mahmiya b. Jaz' married him. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: \"Arise and pay on their behalf from the fifth shares so-and-so.\""],[2358,"Al-hawr is the answer."],[2359,"The Messenger of God ﷺ sent a man from Banu Makhzum over zakat. He said to me: Accompany me. I said: No, not until I go to the Messenger of God ﷺ and ask him. He said: So he went to him and asked him, and he said: \"Zakat is not lawful for us, and the freed slaves of a people are from themselves.\""],[2360,"The Messenger of God ﷺ, when a household gave zakat to him, would pray for them. My father gave zakat to him, and he said: \"O God, bless the family of Abu Awfa.\""],[2362,"I asked al-Hasan b. Ali: What do you remember of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: I remember that I took a date from the dates of charity and put it in my mouth, and he pulled it out of my mouth and said: 'We, the family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us.'"],[2363,"What do you remember of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: I remember of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that I took a date from the dates of charity and put it in my mouth, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extracted it with its saliva and threw it among the dates. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what harm would this date have done to this boy? He said: 'We, the family of Muhammad, charity is not lawful for us.' And he used to say: 'Leave what causes you doubt for what does not cause you doubt; for goodness is tranquility, and lying is doubt.'"],[2364,"What do you remember of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: I remember that he brought me with him into the room of charity, and I took a date and threw it into my mouth. He said: 'Spit it out, for it is not lawful for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nor for anyone of his household.'"],[2370,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Sa'd to visit him in Makkah. He said: Sa'd wept, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'What makes you weep?' He said: I feared that I might die in the land from which I emigrated, as Sa'd b. Khawlah died. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'O Allah, cure Sa'd; O Allah, cure Sa'd.' He said: O Messenger of Allah, I have abundant wealth, and only a daughter will inherit from me. Should I bequeath all my wealth? He said: 'No.' He said: Then two-thirds? He said: 'No.' He said: Then half? He said: 'No.' He said: Then a third. He said: 'A third, and a third is much. Your charity from your wealth is charity; and your spending on your dependents is charity for you; and what your wife eats from your food is charity for you; and if you leave your family in good condition — or he said: with a livelihood — it is better for you than leaving them destitute, begging from people.'"],[2372,"I set out with Husayn b. Samurah and Amr b. Muslim to Zayd b. Arqam, and we sat with him. Husayn said to him: O Zayd, you saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, prayed behind him, heard his hadith, and went on campaigns with him. You have attained great good, O Zayd. Narrate to us, O Zayd, a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and what you witnessed with him. He said: Yes, O son of my brother, my time has grown distant, my age has advanced, and I have forgotten some of what I used to retain from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whatever I narrate to you, accept it; and whatever I do not narrate, do not burden me with it. He said: He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood one day as a preacher among us at a watering place called Khumm, praised Allah and extolled Him, admonished and reminded, then said: Now then, O people, I am only a human being; the messenger of my Lord is about to come to me and I will answer him. I am leaving among you the two weighty things: the first is the Book of Allah, in which there is guidance and light. Whoever holds fast to it and takes it will be upon guidance, and whoever abandons it and misses it will be upon misguidance. And my household. I remind you of Allah regarding my household.' Three times. Husayn said: So who are the people of his household, O Zayd? Are not his wives from his household? He said: Yes, his wives are from his household, but his household are those who are forbidden from charity. He said: Who are they? He said: 'The family of Ali, the family of Aqil, the family of Ja'far, and the family of al-Abbas.' Husayn said: And all of these are forbidden from charity? He said: 'Yes.'"],[2373,"And al-Layth b. Sa'd narrated to me that Sa'id b. Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi narrated to him from Sharik b. Abi Namir that he heard Anas b. Malik."],[2374,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking his help in a blood-money burden. He said: 'Stay with us, and either we will bear it on your behalf or we will help you in it. And know that begging is not lawful for anyone except for one of three: a man who bears a burden on behalf of a people and asks for it until he pays it, then withholds. A man who is struck by a calamity that destroys his wealth, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, or a support of livelihood, then withholds. And a man struck by poverty, so that three people of understanding from his people, or of righteousness, testify that begging has become lawful for him; then he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, or a support of livelihood, then withholds. Whatever asking is other than that, O Qabisah, is unlawful gain which its owner consumes unlawfully.'"],[2375,"I was sitting with Qabisah when a group of his people came to him asking him regarding the marriage of their companion, and he refused to give them anything. When they left, I said: A group of your people came to you asking you regarding the marriage of a companion of theirs, and you are the chief of your people, yet you did not give them anything? He said: They asked me for something that was not right. If their companion had taken his penis and tied it with a leather strip until it withered, it would have been better for him than the asking they asked of me. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Begging is not lawful except for three: a man whose wealth is struck by a destructive calamity, so he asks until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood, then withholds from begging. A man who bears a burden among his people, so he asks until he fulfills his burden, then withholds from begging. And a man for whom three people of understanding from his people swear by Allah that begging has become lawful for so-and-so. Anything other than that is unlawful gain; he consumes nothing but unlawfully.'"],[2376,"I bore a burden, and I came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him about it. He said: 'Stay, O Qabisah, until charity comes to me, and I will order it for you.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Charity is not lawful except for one of three: a man who bears a burden, so begging becomes lawful for him until he obtains a support of livelihood — or he said: a sufficiency of livelihood —; a man struck by a calamity that destroys his wealth, so begging becomes lawful for him until he obtains a support of livelihood — or a sufficiency of livelihood —; and a man struck by poverty, so charity becomes lawful for him until he obtains a support of livelihood. Whatever is other than that, O Qabisah, is unlawful gain which its owner consumes unlawfully.'"],[2377,"The Prophet's ﷺ charity collector came to us, took the charity from our rich, and distributed it among our poor. I was an orphan boy, and he gave me a young she-camel from it."],[2378,"The miskin is not the one who goes around, nor the one who is turned away by a morsel or two morsels, nor by a date or two dates. Rather, the miskin is the one who restrains himself, who does not ask people for anything, and who is not recognized so that he may be given charity."],[2379,"Umar b. al-Khattab appointed me over the sadaqah. When I finished with it and delivered it to him, he ordered a payment for me. I said, \"I have only worked for Allah, and my reward is with Allah.\" He said, \"Take what I give you, for I worked during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he gave me a payment. I said something like what you said, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: 'If I give you something without you asking, then take it and give it in charity.'\""],[2380,"He came to Umar b. al-Khattab during his caliphate, and Umar said to him, \"Have I not been told that you take charge of one of the people's tasks, and when you are given the payment you dislike it?\" I said, \"Yes.\" Umar said, \"What brought you to that?\" I said, \"I have horses and slaves, and I am well-off. I want my work to be charity for the Muslims.\" Umar said to him, \"Do not do that, for I had wanted what you wanted, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give me a gift, and I would say, 'Give it to someone poorer than me.' Once he gave me some wealth, and I said, 'Give it to someone poorer than me.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Take it, and either make use of it or give it in charity. And whatever comes to you of this wealth while you are not covetous or asking, take it. But what does not, do not let your soul pursue it.'\""],[2381,"He used to give to Ibn al-Khattab, and Umar would say, \"Give it to someone poorer than me.\" He said, \"Take it, and either keep it as wealth or give it in charity.\""],[2382,"In the year of the ashes, when the land was barren in the regions, Umar b. al-Khattab wrote to Amr b. al-As: \"From Abdullah Umar, Commander of the Believers, to al-As b. al-As. By my life, you do not care if you and those with you are fat while I and those with me are lean. O help!\" Amr wrote back: \"Peace. After that: at your service, at your service. A caravan is coming to you, its first part is with you and its last part is with me, though I hope to find a way to send some by sea.\" When the first of the caravan arrived, he summoned al-Zubayr and said, \"Go out with the first of this caravan, head toward Najd with it, and bring me every household you can carry. For those you cannot carry, order each household to have a camel with what is on it, and tell them to wear the two cloaks that contain wheat, to slaughter the camel, to render its fat, to preserve its meat, to use its hide for shoes, then to take a quantity of dried meat, a quantity of fat, and a handful of flour, cook and eat until Allah brings them provision.\" Al-Zubayr refused to go out. He said, \"By Allah, you will not find its like until you depart from this world.\" Then he summoned another—I think it was Talha—who refused. Then he summoned Abu Ubaydah b. al-Jarrah, and he went out for that. When he returned, he sent him a thousand dinars. Abu Ubaydah said, \"I did not work for you, O Ibn al-Khattab. I worked only for Allah, and I will not take anything for that.\" Umar said, \"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave us in matters he sent us for, and we disliked it, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ insisted on that for us. So accept it, O man, and use it to help yourself in your worldly affairs and your religion.\" So Abu Ubaydah b. al-Jarrah accepted it."],[2383,"Charity is not lawful for a wealthy person except for five: the worker on it, the debtor, the one who buys it, the worker in the cause of Allah, or a poor neighbor who is given charity and then gives it as a gift to the wealthy person."],[2384,"Whoever we appoint to a task and give him provision, then whatever he takes after that is embezzlement."],[2385,"Whoever is a worker for us, let him acquire a wife. If he has no servant, let him acquire a servant. And whoever has no dwelling, let him acquire a dwelling."],[2386,"He was never asked for anything for the sake of Islam but that he gave it. He said: A man came to him and asked him, and he ordered for him a large number of sheep between two mountains from the sadaqah sheep. He said: He returned to his people and said, \"O people, embrace Islam, for Muhammad gives a gift like one who does not fear poverty.\""],[2387,"A man came to the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, and he ordered for him sheep between two mountains. He returned to his people and said, \"Embrace Islam, for Muhammad gives a gift like a man who does not fear poverty.\""],[2388,"Ali sent from Yemen to the Prophet ﷺ a piece of gold that had not been purified from its dust. He divided it among four: al-Aqra b. Habis al-Hanzali, Uyaynah b. Hisn al-Muradi, Alqamah b. Ulathah al-Jafari—or Amir b. al-Tufayl, he was uncertain—and Zayd al-Ta'i. Some of his companions from the Ansar and others felt resentment about that. It reached him, and he said, \"Will you not trust me, when I am the trustee of the One in the heaven? The news of the One in the heaven comes to me morning and evening.\""],[2389,"Charity is not lawful for a wealthy person except for five: the worker on it, a man who bought it with his own wealth, a debtor, a warrior in the cause of Allah, or a poor person who was given charity and then gave a gift from it to a wealthy person."],[2390,"I took on a heavy debt, so I came to the Prophet ﷺ to ask him about it. He said: “We will pay it on your behalf and give it out from the camels of the charity.” Then he said: “O Qabīṣah, asking is forbidden except in three cases: a man who has taken on a debt, for whom asking is permissible until he pays it off, then he refrains. And a man struck by a calamity that destroyed his wealth, for whom asking is permissible until he obtains a means of livelihood or a sufficiency of livelihood, then he refrains. And a man struck by calamity and poverty until he speaks, or until three people of sound judgment from his people testify that asking has become permissible for him, until he obtains a sufficiency of livelihood or a means of livelihood, then he refrains. Anything beyond that is unlawful gain.”"],[2391,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prepared for Hajj and ordered the people to prepare with him. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out and the people set out with him. When he arrived, I came to him. He said: “What prevented you from going out with us on this journey of ours, O Umm Ma‘qil?” I said: O Messenger of Allah, I had prepared, but this sore afflicted us, and Abū Ma‘qil died, and I was struck with illness from it. We had a camel that we wanted to travel on, but Abū Ma‘qil bequeathed it in the way of Allah. He said: “Why did you not go out on it? For Hajj is in the way of Allah.”"],[2392,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted us on weak camels from the camels of the charity for Hajj. We said: O Messenger of Allah, we do not think these will carry us. He said: “There is no camel but on its hump there is a devil. So mention the name of Allah over them when you mount them, as He commanded you, then use them for your own needs, for Allah is the one who carries.”"],[2393,"I was a man given such ability in intercourse with women as no one else was given. When Ramadan entered, I made ẓihār from my wife, fearing that I might have something with her in some part of the night and continue in that without being able to stop until morning overtook me. While she was one night serving me, something of her became visible, so I jumped on her. When morning came, I went to my people and told them my news. I said: Come with me to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so I may inform him. They said: No, by Allah, we will not go with you. We fear that Qur’an may be revealed concerning us, or the Messenger of Allah ﷺ may say something about us whose shame will remain upon us. Go yourself and do what seems best to you. So I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him of my news. He said: “You did that?” I said: I did that, and here I am, so carry out Allah’s judgment upon me, for I am patient and expecting reward. He said: “Free a slave.” I struck the side of my neck with my hand and said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I have not become morning owning anything other than it. He said: “Fast two consecutive months.” I said: O Messenger of Allah, nothing befell me except in fasting. He said: “Feed sixty poor people.” I said: O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, we spent this night of ours hungry, with no supper. He said: “Go to the collector of the charity, the charity of Banū Zurayq, and order him to give it to you. Feed from it a wasq to sixty poor people, and use the rest of it for your dependents.” So I came to my people and said: I found with you hardship."],[2394,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man as a collector over the charity and ordered him to take from the rich and divide it among the poor. He ordered for me a young she-camel."],[2395,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to collect the charities of Juhaynah. The last person I came to was a man from among them, from those closest to Madinah, and he gathered his wealth for me."],[2397,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent a man from the people of Yemen over its zakat, and he came with many black goods. When I sent someone to receive it from him, he said: This is for me and this is for you. If he is asked: Where did you get this? He says: It was gifted to me. Why, if he was truthful, was it gifted to him while he was in the house of his father or his mother? Then he said: “I do not send a man on a task and he embezzles from it anything, except he will come with it on the Day of Resurrection on his neck—a camel bellowing, or a cow mooing, or a sheep bleating.” Then he said: “O Allah, have I conveyed?”"],[2398,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Peace be upon you, O young man of Banū ‘Abd al-Muṭṭalib. He said: “And upon you.” He said: I am a man from your maternal uncles, from Banū Sa‘d b. Bakr, and I am the messenger of my people to you and their delegate. I will ask you and press my asking, and I will adjure you and press my adjuration. He said: “Take it easy, O brother of Banū Sa‘d.” He said: Who created you? And who created those before you? And who is the creator of those after you? He said: “Allah.” He said: I adjure you by that—did He send you? He said: “Yes.” He said: We have found in your Book, and your messengers commanded us, that you take from the excess of our wealth and return it to our poor. I adjure you by that—did He command you to do that? He said: “Yes.”"],[2399,"A man from his people called Ibn Abī Ḥathmah informed him that a group of them went to Khaybar and dispersed there. They found one of them killed. They said to those with whom they found him: You killed our companion. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we went to Khaybar... and he mentioned the ḥadīth. At its end he said: The Prophet of Allah ﷺ disliked that his blood go unavenged, so he paid blood money for him with one hundred camels from the charity."],[2400,"Charity given to a poor person is charity, and to a relative it is two things: charity and maintaining family ties."],[2401,"The best charity is that given to a hostile relative."],[2402,"Charity is not lawful for a rich person, nor for one who is strong and able-bodied."],[2405,"I said to al-ʿAbbās: 'Ask the Prophet ﷺ to appoint you over the sadaqah.' He replied: 'I would not appoint you over the washing away of people's sins.'"],[2406,"Whoever asks for something while he has sufficiency from it only increases himself in the Fire. It was said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what is the sufficiency with which begging is not appropriate?' He replied: 'That he has a day and a night's fill, or a night and a day's.'"],[2407,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say when he ordained the sadaqah of breaking the fast: 'A sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley.' And he did not give anything but dates."],[2408,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordained for the sadaqah of breaking the fast a sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley."],[2409,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us with the sadaqah of breaking the fast before zakah was revealed. When zakah was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we kept doing it."],[2410,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordained the sadaqah of Ramadan upon the male and the female, the free and the slave: a sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley."],[2411,"There is no sadaqah due from a Muslim for his horse, nor for his slave, nor for his slave-girl, except the sadaqah of breaking the fast."],[2412,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordained the zakah of Ramadan on behalf of the free and the slave, the male and the female: a sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley. He said: 'Then the people made it equivalent to half a sāʿ of wheat.' He said: 'And Ibn ʿUmar, when he gave, gave dates, except one year when dates were scarce, so he gave barley.' He said: I said: 'When did Ibn ʿUmar give the sāʿ?' He said: 'When the collector sat down.' I said: 'When did the collector sit down?' He said: 'A day or two before the breaking of the fast.'"],[2413,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordained the zakah of breaking the fast in Ramadan upon every soul of the Muslims, free or slave, male or female, young or old: a sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley."],[2414,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordained the zakah of breaking the fast in Ramadan upon the people: a sāʿ of dates or a sāʿ of barley, upon every free person and slave, male and female of the Muslims."],[2415,"male or female: as in the Dār al-Mayman edition, and in the al-Maktab al-Islāmī edition"],[2416,"They used to give the zakah of breaking the fast during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with the mudd that the people of the house consumed, or the sāʿ that they consumed. All the people of Madinah did that."],[2418,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made obligatory the charity of the breaking of the fast — and Nasr said: the charity of Ramadan — upon the young and the old, the free and the slave: a Sa' of dates or a Sa' of barley."],[2419,"He used to give Zakat al-Fitr as a Sa' of dates or a Sa' of barley, and that 'Abd Allah said: \"The people made the equivalent of barley and dates as two Mudds of wheat.\""],[2420,"He used to give Zakat al-Fitr as a Sa' of dates and a Sa' of barley. He said: \"And 'Abd Allah b. 'Umar used to say: 'The people made the equivalent [of that] as two Mudds of wheat.'\""],[2421,"The charity at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was only dates, raisins, and barley; there was no wheat."],[2422,"We continued to give during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a Sa' of dates, a Sa' of barley, and a Sa' of dried yoghurt. This continued until Mu'awiyah came, and he said: \"I think that a Sa' of Syrian wheat is equivalent to two Sa's of dates.\" So the people adopted that."],[2423,"We used to give Zakat al-Fitr during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a Sa' of food, or a Sa' of dried yoghurt, or a Sa' of dates, or a Sa' of raisins, or a Sa' of barley. We continued to give it until Mu'awiyah came to us from Syria as a pilgrim or as a performer of 'Umrah — and he was then the caliph — and he addressed the people from the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: Then he mentioned Zakat al-Fitr and said: \"Indeed, I think that two Mudds of Syrian wheat are equivalent to a Sa' of dates.\" So he was the first to remind the people of the two Mudds at that time."],[2424,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded the charity of the breaking of the fast on behalf of every young and old, free or slave: a Sa' of barley or a Sa' of dates. Then afterwards the people made it equivalent to two Mudds of wheat."],[2425,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood as a preacher and commanded the charity of the breaking of the fast: a Sa' of dates or a Sa' of barley for every one, or for every head, for the young and the old, the free and the slave."],[2426,"The Prophet ﷺ made obligatory the charity of the breaking of the fast upon the free, the slave, the male, the female, the young, and the old among the Muslims: a Sa' of barley, or a Sa' of dates, or a Sa' of raisins, or a Sa' of dried yoghurt."],[2427,"Zakat is upon the Muslims: a Sa' of dates, or a Sa' of raisins, or a Sa' of dried yoghurt, or a Sa' of barley."],[2428,"Mu'awiyah b. Abi Sufyan used to command them to give the charity of Ramadan as half a Sa' of wheat or a Sa' of dates. Abu Sa'id said: \"We will not give except what we used to give during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: a Sa' of dates, or a Sa' of dried yoghurt, or a Sa' of raisins, or a Sa' of barley.\""],[2429,"We used to give in the charity of Fitr a saa' of dates, or a saa' of barley, or a saa' of raisins, or a saa' of aqit, or a saa' of sult."],[2430,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to pay the Zakat of Ramadan as a saa' of food on behalf of the young and the old, the free and the slave. Whoever gave sult, it was accepted from him, and I think he said: and whoever gave fine flour, it was accepted from him, and whoever gave sawiq, it was accepted from him."],[2431,"The charity of Fitr is a saa' of barley, or a saa' of dates, or a saa' of sult."],[2432,"The charity of Ramadan is a saa' of food. Whoever brought wheat, it was accepted from him; whoever brought barley, it was accepted from him; whoever brought dates, it was accepted from him; whoever brought sult, it was accepted from him; whoever brought raisins, it was accepted from him; and I think he said: and whoever brought sawiq or fine flour, it was accepted from him."],[2433,"We used to give the charity of Fitr, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among us, as a saa' of food, or a saa' of dates, or a saa' of barley, or a saa' of raisins, or a saa' of aqit. And we continued to do so until Mu'awiyah came to us from Syria to Madinah on a visit, and among what he said to the people was: I do not think that two mudds of Syrian wheat are equivalent except to a saa' of this. So the people adopted that. Abu Sa'id said: I will continue to give it as I used to give it at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forever, or as long as I live."],[2434,"And they mentioned the charity of Ramadan in his presence, so he said: I will not give except what I used to give at the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: a saa' of dates, or a saa' of wheat, or a saa' of barley, or a saa' of aqit. A man from the group said to him: Or two mudds of wheat? He said: No, that is the value of Mu'awiyah; I do not accept it, nor do I act upon it."],[2435,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about this verse: 'He has succeeded who purifies himself, and mentions the name of his Lord and prays' (Qur'an 87:14-15). He said: It was revealed concerning Zakat al-Fitr."],[2436,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded the giving of Zakat al-Fitr before the people go out to the prayer."],[2437,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out to the prayer on the day of Fitr."],[2438,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the charity of Fitr be paid before the people go out to the musalla."],[2439,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entrusted me to guard the Zakat of Ramadan. A visitor came to me in the middle of the night and began scooping up food. I seized him and said: I will take you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: Let me go, for I am in need. So I let him go. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said after praying the morning prayer: O Abu Hurayrah, what did your prisoner do last night? Or he said: the previous night? I said: O Messenger of Allah, he complained of need, so I let him go, and he claimed that he would not return. He said: Indeed, he has lied to you, and he will return. He said: So I lay in wait for him, knowing that he would return because of the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: He came and began scooping up food. I seized him and said: I will take you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He complained of need, so I let him go. Then morning came, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: What did your prisoner do last night? Or the previous night? I said: O Messenger of Allah, he complained of need, so I let him go, and he claimed that he would not return. He said: Indeed, he has lied to you, and he will return. So I lay in wait for him, knowing that he would return because of the words of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He came and began scooping up food. I seized him and said: I will take you to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: Let me go, and I will teach you words by which Allah will benefit you. He said: They were the most eager of people for good. He said: When you go to your bed, recite Ayat al-Kursi: 'Allah - there is no deity except Him, the Ever-Living, the Sustainer of [all] existence' (Qur'an 2:255). For there will remain with you a guardian from Allah, and no devil will come near you until morning. So I let him go. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: What did your prisoner do, O Abu Hurayrah? So I informed him, and he said: He spoke the truth to you, though he is a liar. Do you know whom you have been addressing for three nights? That was the devil."],[2440,"There is no Muslim servant who gives charity from good earnings - and Allah accepts only what is good - except that Allah takes it with His right hand and nurtures it for him as one of you nurtures his foal, or he said: his young camel, until a date becomes like Mount Uhud."],[2441,"Indeed, when a servant gives charity from what is pure, Allah accepts it from him and takes it with His right hand, then nurtures it as one of you nurtures his colt or his young camel. A man may give a morsel in charity, and it grows in the hand of Allah — or he said: in the palm of Allah — until it becomes like a mountain. So give charity."],[2442,"Hisham narrated to us, from al-Qasim, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet."],[2443,"He mentioned the Fire and sought refuge from it, turning his face away twice or three times, then said: Guard yourselves against the Fire even with half a date; if you cannot find that, then with a good word."],[2444,"Guard yourselves against the Fire even with half a date."],[2445,"Ransom yourselves from the Fire even with half a date."],[2446,"Every person will be in the shade of his charity until judgment is passed between people — or he said: until it is decided between people."],[2447,"He was the first of the people of Egypt to go to the mosque, and I never saw him enter the mosque except that in his sleeve there was charity — either small coins, bread, or wheat — until I even saw him carrying onions. He said: I would say: O Abu al-Khayr, this will make your clothes stink. He said: He would say: O son of Habib, indeed I did not find anything in the house other than it to give in charity."],[2448,"It was mentioned to me — he said: he says: The deeds will boast, and charity will say: I am the most excellent of you."],[2449,"She asked the Prophet ﷺ for a servant, and he gave her one. She then freed her. He said: If you had given her to your maternal uncles, it would have been greater in reward for you."],[2450,"The hands are three: the hand of Allah is the highest, the hand of the giver is next, and the hand of the beggar is the lowest until the Day of Resurrection. So refrain from asking as much as you can."],[2451,"The best charity is that which leaves self-sufficiency, and the upper hand is better than the lower hand. Begin with those you support. Your wife says: Spend on me or divorce me. Your slave says: Spend on me or sell me. Your child says: To whom will you entrust us?"],[2452,"The example of the one who spends and the miser is like the example of two men upon whom are two iron coats from their chests to their collarbones. When the one who gives charity and spends wants to spend, the coat of mail becomes ample upon him, or it passes until it reaches his fingertips and covers his trace. When the miser wants to spend, it shrinks and every ring takes its place until it seizes his collarbone or his neck.” Abu Hurayrah said: I bear witness that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying with his hand: “He was widening it and it would not widen.”"],[2453,"Charity does not decrease wealth, Allah does not increase a servant through pardoning except in honor, and no one humbles himself for Allah except that Allah raises him."],[2454,"The best charity is that which is given out of surplus wealth, and begin with those you support."],[2455,"The hands are three: the Hand of Allah is the highest, the hand of the giver is next to it, and the hand of the beggar is the lowest. So give from what is surplus, and do not be helpless with regard to yourself."],[2456,"A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with an egg of gold that he had found in one of the mines. Al-Dawraqi said: “With something like an egg of gold that he had found in one of the mines.” They both said: He said: “O Messenger of Allah, take this from me as charity, for by Allah I have not become such that I own anything other than it.” He turned away from him. Then he came to him from his right side and said the same thing, and he turned away from him. Then he came to him from his left side and said the same thing, and he turned away from him. Then he said it to him a fourth time, and he said: “Give it here,” angrily, and he threw it at him with a throw that, had it hit him, would have wounded him or crippled him. Then he said: “One of you comes with all his wealth and gives it in charity, then he begs from people. Charity is only from surplus wealth.” This is the hadith of Ibn Rafi'. Al-Dawraqi added: “Take your wealth from us, we have no need of it.”"],[2457,"He said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he repented: “O Messenger of Allah, I divest myself of my wealth as charity to Allah and His Messenger.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: “Keep some of your wealth, for it is better for you.”"],[2458,"A dirham has surpassed a hundred thousand. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, how can a dirham surpass a hundred thousand?” He said: “A man who had two dirhams took one of them and gave it in charity, and another man who had much wealth took from its abundance a hundred thousand.”"],[2459,"O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? He said: “The effort of the one with little, and begin with those you support.”"],[2460,"When one of you is poor, let him begin with himself; if there is surplus, then on his dependents; if there is surplus, then on his relatives or those of his kin; if there is surplus, then here and there."],[2461,"Whoever asks while he has what suffices him only consumes embers. Zayd b. Akhzam said: \"Whoever asks without poverty only consumes embers.\""],[2462,"Whoever asks while he has the value of an uqiyyah is importunate."],[2463,"Whoever asks while he has forty dirhams is importunate, and he is like one who eats grit."],[2464,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me and food was brought that had no meat. He said: \"Did I not see a pot for you?\" I said: \"Yes, but that is meat that was given as charity to Barirah.\" He said: \"It is charity for her, and a gift from her to us.\""],[2465,"There is no charity more excellent than charity given to a slave under an evil master."],[2466,"O Messenger of Allah, which charity is best? He said: “The effort of the one with little, and begin with those you support.”"],[2467,"When one of you is poor, let him begin with himself; if there is surplus, then on his dependents; if there is surplus, then on his relatives or kin; if there is surplus, then here and there."],[2468,"We used to support one another, and a man would come with a large charity and it would be said: \"He is showing off.\" And a man would come with half a sa' and it would be said: \"Allah is in no need of this.\" Then it was revealed: Those who disparage the believers who give voluntary charity and those who find nothing but their effort."],[2469,"A man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, which charity is greatest?\" He said: \"That you give charity while you are healthy, miserly, fearing poverty and hoping for long life, and do not delay until it reaches the throat and you say: 'For so-and-so this, and for so-and-so that,' when it already belongs to so-and-so.\""],[2470,"When it was revealed: You will not attain righteousness until you spend from what you love, Abu Talhah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was on the pulpit and said: \"O Messenger of Allah, I have no land more beloved to me than my land Bariha'.\" The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Bariha' is a good returner, or best gainer\" – the Shaykh was uncertain – Abu Talhah said: \"I seek nearness to Allah with it.\" He said: \"Place it among your relatives.\" So he divided it among them as gardens."],[2471,"Three Allah loves, and three Allah hates. As for those He loves: a man who came to a people and asked them by Allah, not by kinship between them and him, and a man lagged behind at their heels and gave him secretly, no one knowing of his gift except Allah and the one he gave, and a people who traveled through their night until sleep became dearer to them than anything equal to it, then they stopped and laid down their heads, and one stood up flattering Me and reciting My verses, and a man who was in a raiding party and met the enemy and they were routed, but he advanced with his chest until he was killed or victory was granted to him. And the three whom Allah hates: the adulterous old man, the arrogant poor person, and the oppressive rich person."],[2472,"No man gives any charity until the jaws of seventy devils are loosened from it."],[2473,"This verse was revealed: “You will not attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love.” He said: “Or who is it that would loan Allah a goodly loan.” Abu Talhah said: “O Messenger of Allah, my garden in such-and-such place is for Allah, and if I could have kept it secret I would not have made it public.” He said: “Give it to the poor of your relatives, or of your household.”"],[2474,"When this verse was revealed, then he mentioned something similar from the Prophet."],[2475,"When this verse was revealed: “You will not attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love,” Abu Talhah said: “I see that our Lord is asking us for our wealth, so I call you to witness, O Messenger of Allah, that I have made my land Bariha' for Allah.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Give it to your relatives.” He said: So he gave it to Hassan b. Thabit and Ubayy b. Ka'b."],[2476,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left after the morning prayer one day and came to the women in the mosque. He stood before them and said: “O assembly of women, I have never seen any deficient in intellect and religion more capable of removing the minds of men of understanding than you. And I have been shown that you are the majority of the people of the Fire on the Day of Resurrection, so draw near to Allah with what you are able.” Among the women was the wife of Abdullah b. Mas'ud. She returned to Abdullah b. Mas'ud and told him what she had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and took some jewelry of hers. Ibn Mas'ud said: “Where are you going with this jewelry?” She said: “I will draw near to Allah and His Messenger with it.” He said: “Woe to you, come and give it in charity to me and my children, for we are more deserving of it.” She said: “No, not until I go to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.” He said: So she went to seek permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said: “O Messenger of Allah, this is Zaynab seeking permission.” He said: “Which Zaynab is she?” They said: “The wife of Ibn Mas'ud.” He said: “Give her permission.” She entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: “O Messenger of Allah, I heard a statement from you, so I returned to Ibn Mas'ud and told him, and I took some jewelry of mine to draw near to Allah and to you, hoping that Allah will not make me from the people of the Fire. Ibn Mas'ud said to me: 'Give it in charity to me and my children, for we are more deserving of it.' I said: 'Not until I ask permission of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “Give it in charity to him and his children, for they are more deserving of it.”"],[2477,"Ibn Mas'ud spoke the truth. Your husband and your child are more deserving of the one to whom you give charity."],[2478,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to give charity and said: “Give charity, O assembly of women, even from your jewelry.” She said: I was supporting Abdullah and my daughters were in my care, so I said to Abdullah: “Go to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him whether it is sufficient for me if I give it to you along with charity?” He said: “No, rather go to him and ask him.” She said: So I went to him and sat at the door, and awe had been cast upon me. I found a woman from the Ansar whose need was like mine. Bilal came out to us, so we said: “Ask him, but do not tell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who we are.” He said: “Two women who support their husbands and orphans in their care, is that sufficient for them as charity?” He said to him: “Who are they?” He said: “Zaynab and a woman from the Ansar.” He said: “Which Zaynab?” He said: “The wife of Abdullah b. Mas'ud and a woman from the Ansar.” He said: “Yes, they will have two rewards: the reward of kinship and the reward of charity.”"],[2479,"The Prophet ﷺ came to us while we were in the mosque and said: “O assembly of women, give charity even from your jewelry.”"],[2480,"A man gave charity to his son with some land, then inheritance returned it to him. He mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said to him: “Your reward is assured, and your property has returned to you.”"],[2481,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that a cluster of dates be given from every garden to the mosque."],[2482,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque while clusters of dates were hung, and one cluster among them was of poor quality. He had a stick with him, so he poked the cluster with the stick and said: “If the owner of this charity had willed, he would have given in charity better than it. The owner of this charity will eat poor-quality dates on the Day of Resurrection.”"],[2483,"The beggar has a right, even if he comes on a horse."],[2484,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ allowed the sale of 'araya for one wasq, two wasqs, three, and four, and said: “In every ten wasqs of dates, there is a cluster to be placed for the poor in the mosque.”"],[2485,"When you pay the zakah of your wealth, you have removed its evil from yourself."],[2486,"When you pay the zakah of your wealth, you have fulfilled what is due upon you. Whoever amasses wealth unlawfully then gives it in charity, there is no reward for him in it, and its burden is upon him."],[2487,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, a beggar comes to me and I have nothing to give him. He said: Do not turn away your beggar, even if it is with a burnt hoof."],[2488,"She said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: By Allah, a poor person stands at my door and I cannot find anything to give him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: If you find nothing to give him except a burnt hoof, then place it in his hand."],[2489,"The example of one who gives charity and then takes back his charity is like a dog that vomits and then eats its vomit."],[2490,"I heard Muhammad b. Ali b. al-Husayn mention from Sa'id b. al-Musayyab, from Ibn Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the like of it."],[2491,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us and urged charity. Some people were slow until anger was seen on his face. Then a man from the Ansar came with a purse and gave it, so people followed one another until joy was seen on the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: Whoever establishes a good practice, he has its reward and the reward of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything of their rewards. And whoever establishes an evil practice, upon him is its burden and the like of the burdens of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything of their burdens."],[2492,"Two types of protective jealousy: one Allah loves and the other Allah detests. Protective jealousy regarding suspicion Allah loves, and protective jealousy regarding something other than suspicion Allah detests. And display: when a man gives charity, Allah loves it; display in arrogance Allah detests. And he said: Three whose supplications are answered: the parent, the traveler, and the wronged. And he said: Allah admits into Paradise by a single arrow three people: its maker, the one who supplies it, and the one who shoots it in the path of Allah."],[2493,"Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: I asked My servant for a loan but he did not lend to Me, and My servant insulted Me while he does not know. He says: Woe to time, woe to time, while I am time."],[2494,"Whoever spends a pair of his wealth in the path of Allah, the keepers of Paradise will call him. Paradise has gates: whoever is from the people of prayer will be called from the gate of prayer, whoever is from the people of charity will be called from the gate of charity, whoever is from the people of jihad will be called from the gate of jihad, and whoever is from the people of fasting will be called from the gate of al-Rayyan. Abu Bakr said to him: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, there is no distress upon anyone from whichever of them he is called, but will anyone be called from all of them? He said: Yes, and I hope that you will be among them."],[2495,"He mentioned that a man came on Friday while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon, in shabby condition. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered the people to give charity, and they threw clothes, so he ordered two garments for him, and ordered him to pray two rak'ahs while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the sermon."],[2496,"He entered Madinah and found a man with people gathered around him. He asked, 'Who is this?' They said, 'Abu Hurayrah.' I approached him until I sat before him while he was narrating to the people. When he fell silent and was alone, I said, 'I adjure you by a right and a right to narrate to me a hadith you heard from the Messenger of God ﷺ, which you understood and knew.' Abu Hurayrah said, 'I will do it; I will surely narrate to you a hadith that the Messenger of God ﷺ narrated to me and that I knew.' Then Abu Hurayrah sobbed once, remained still for a while, then recovered and said, 'I will surely narrate to you a hadith that the Messenger of God ﷺ narrated to me in this house, with no one with us but me and him.' Then Abu Hurayrah sobbed again, remained still, then recovered and wiped his face. He said, 'I will do it; I will surely narrate to you a hadith that the Messenger of God ﷺ narrated to me while I and he were in this house, with no one with us but me and him.' Then Abu Hurayrah sobbed violently, collapsed on his face, and I supported him for a long time. Then he recovered and said."],[2497,"That Umar acquired land at Khaybar. He came to the Prophet ﷺ to consult him about it. He said, 'I acquired land at Khaybar, and I have never acquired property dearer to me than it, so what do you command regarding it?' He said, 'If you wish, you can make its principal inalienable and give it as charity.' He said, 'So Umar gave it as charity, stipulating that its principal should not be sold, bought, gifted, or inherited. He gave it in charity for the poor, relatives, slaves, in the cause of God, the wayfarer, and the guest. There is no blame on the one who administers it if he eats from it in a reasonable manner or feeds a friend, without accumulating wealth from it.'"],[2498,"Muhammad said, 'Without amassing wealth.' He did not mention Ibn Awn's recitation of the document: :, : : : :, : :, :"],[2499,"Umar acquired land at Khaybar. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned the hadith in full. He said, 'So Umar gave it as charity, stipulating that its principal not be sold, bought, gifted, or inherited; for the poor, relatives, slaves, in the cause of God, the guest, and the wayfarer. There is no blame on the one who administers it if he eats from it in a reasonable manner or feeds a friend, without accumulating wealth from it.'"],[2500,"That Umar consulted the Prophet ﷺ about his charity. He said, 'Make its principal inalienable and dedicate its produce as a charitable endowment.' Abdullah said, 'So Umar made it inalienable for the beggar, the deprived, the wayfarer, in the cause of God, for freeing slaves, and for the needy. He stipulated regarding it: he may eat and feed others without amassing wealth.'"],[2501,"Then Ali, al-Zubayr, Talha, and Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas were present, and I too, when Uthman came and said, 'I adjure you by God, besides whom there is no god, do you know that the Messenger of God ﷺ said, \"Whoever buys the well of Rumah, God will forgive him,\" so I bought it for such and such, and I came to him and said, \"I have bought it for such.\" He said, \"Make it a source of drinking water for the Muslims, and its reward is yours\"?' They said, 'O God, yes.'"],[2502,"By Him in whose hand is my soul, my heirs will not divide anything I leave. What we leave is charity."],[2503,"By God, the Messenger of God ﷺ left at his death neither a dinar, nor a dirham, nor a slave, male or female, except his mule, his weapons, and land that he left as charity."],[2504,"Indeed, among what reaches the believer of his deeds and good rewards after his death are a knowledge he taught and spread, a righteous child he left behind, a mosque he built, a house for the wayfarer he built, a canal he dug, or charity he gave from his property while healthy and alive – it reaches him after his death."],[2505,"When Uthman was besieged, he looked out at them from above his house, then said, 'I remind you by God, do you know that Rumah's water was drunk only for a price, so I bought it from my own money and made it for the rich, the poor, and the wayfarer?' They said, 'Yes.'"],[2506,"I witnessed the house on the day Uthman was attacked. He looked out at us and said, 'O people, I remind you by God and Islam: do you know that the Messenger of God ﷺ came to Madinah and there was no well with sweet water except Rumah? He said, \"Whoever buys Rumah and makes his bucket in it like the buckets of the Muslims, he will have better than it in Paradise.\" They said, \"O God, yes.\" He said, \"I bought it from my own pure money, and you are preventing me from breaking my fast on it until I break my fast on seawater.\"'"],[2507,"He looked over them, meaning Uthman b. Affan, and said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know that I bought the well of Rumah from my own wealth so that its water could be made sweet, and I made my share in it like the share of any Muslim man? They said: Yes. He said: Then why do you prevent me from drinking from it until I break my fast with sea water?"],[2508,"When a person dies, his deeds come to an end except for three: a continuous charity, or knowledge from which benefit is derived, or a righteous child who prays for him."],[2509,"The best of what a person leaves behind after him are three: a righteous child who prays for him and whose supplication reaches him, or a charity that continues and whose reward reaches him, or knowledge that is acted upon after him."],[2510,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother has died. Should I give charity on her behalf? He said: Yes. I said: Which charity is best? He said: Giving water to drink."],[2511,"A man said to the Prophet ﷺ: My father died and left wealth, but he did not make a bequest. Will his sins be expiated if I give charity on his behalf? He said: Yes."],[2512,"A man said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died suddenly, and I think that if she had been able to speak she would have made a bequest to give charity. Will she have a reward if I give charity on her behalf? He said: Yes."],[2513,"Sa'd b. Ubadah went out with the Prophet ﷺ on one of his military expeditions. The mother of Sa'd was on her deathbed, and it was said to her: Make a bequest. She said: What should I bequeath? The wealth is Sa'd's wealth. She died before Sa'd returned. When Sa'd arrived, this was mentioned to him, and he said: O Messenger of Allah, will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf? He said: Yes. Sa'd said: The garden of such-and-such is charity on her behalf, referring to a garden he had named."],[2514,"Sa'd b. Ubadah, the brother of Banu Sa'idah, said: O Messenger of Allah, my mother died while I was absent. Will it benefit her if I give something in charity on her behalf? He said: Yes. He said: Then I call you to witness that my garden at al-Mikhraf is charity on her behalf."],[2515,"A man said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: My mother has died. Will it benefit her if I give charity on her behalf?"],[2516,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, guide me to a deed that will admit me to Paradise. He said: Speak the truth, and give the surplus. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what if I cannot? He said: Do you have camels? He said: Yes. He said: Then set out with a camel from your camels and a water skin, and look for a household who drink water only every other day. Your camel will not perish and your water skin will not be torn until Paradise becomes obligatory for you."],[2517,"Kahmas narrated this hadith to us in a similar manner: , : :"],[2519,"Islam is built upon five: bearing witness that there is no god but Allah, establishing the prayer, giving the zakah, performing the pilgrimage to the House, and fasting Ramadan."],[2520,"Enjoy this House, for it has been destroyed twice and will be raised in the third."],[2521,"This House will surely be performed Hajj and Umrah after the emergence of Gog and Magog. Abu Qudamah said: \"after Gog and Magog.\" Abu Musa said: \"The House will surely be performed Hajj.\""],[2522,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people and said: Allah has obligated Hajj upon you. A man said: Every year, O Messenger of Allah? He remained silent until he repeated it three times, then said: \"If I said yes, it would become obligatory, and if it became obligatory you would not be able to do it.\" And he said: \"Leave me with what I have left you, for those before you were destroyed by their excessive questioning and their disagreement with their prophets. So when I command you with something, do it as much as you are able, and when I forbid you from something, abstain from it.\" He said: Then it was revealed: Do not ask about things which, if they are shown to you, will distress you."],[2525,"The delegations of Allah are three: the warrior, the pilgrim, and the one performing Umrah."],[2526,"Perform Hajj and Umrah consecutively, for they remove poverty and sins just as the bellows removes the impurity of iron, gold, and silver. And there is no reward for an accepted Hajj less than Paradise."],[2527,"Umrah to Umrah is expiation for what is between them, and an accepted Hajj has no reward other than Paradise."],[2528,"Whoever performs Hajj and does not have sexual relations or commit wickedness returns as if his mother gave birth to him."],[2529,"We attended Amr b. al-As while he was at the point of death. He wept for a long time and said: When Allah placed Islam in my heart, I came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, extend your right hand so that I may pledge allegiance to you. He extended his hand, but I withdrew my hand. He said: \"What is the matter with you, O Amr?\" I said: I wanted to stipulate a condition. He said: \"What condition do you stipulate?\" I said: That I be forgiven. He said: \"Do you not know, O Amr, that Islam destroys what came before it, that emigration destroys what came before it, and that Hajj destroys what came before it?\""],[2530,"O Allah, forgive the pilgrim and the one for whom the pilgrim seeks forgiveness."],[2531,"Rarely did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out on a journey for jihad or other than it except on Thursday."],[2532,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came —meaning to the house of Abu Bakr— and sought permission, and he was granted permission. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Permission has been granted to me to depart.” Abu Bakr said, “Companionship, may my father be your ransom, O Messenger of Allah?” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Yes.” A'ishah said, “So I prepared them with the swiftest of preparations. I made for them a traveling provision in a leather bag. Asma' bt. Abu Bakr cut a piece from her waist-band and tied the leather bag with it. For that reason she was called Dhat al-Nitaq (the one with the waist-band).”"],[2533,"It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to travel a journey of three days or more unless she has with her a mahram: her father, her son, her brother, her husband, or another mahram of hers. This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Mu'awiyah. In the hadith of the others: “A woman is not to travel a journey of three days or more,” except that in the hadith of Ibn Abi Za'idah it is: “It would be three days.”"],[2534,"Al-A'mash narrated to us, and he mentioned the hadith similarly."],[2535,"He prohibited a woman from traveling for three [days] unless she has with her a mahram."],[2536,"A woman is not to travel for two days except with her husband or a mahram."],[2537,"It is not lawful for a woman who believes in Allah and the Last Day to travel a day and a night except with a mahram."],[2538,"Yunus b. 'Abd al-A'la and 'Isa b. Ibrahim narrated it to us. 'Isa said, “He narrated to us,” and Yunus said, “Ibn Wahb informed us, [he said], 'Malik informed me, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah.'” Abu Bakr said concerning the report: “It is authentic from his father, from Abu Hurayrah. Al-Layth b. Sa'd, Ibn 'Ajlan, and Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated it from Sa'id, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah. I have extracted it in the large book.” Thus in the Dar al-Mayman edition and in the al-Maktab al-Islami edition."],[2539,"A woman is not to travel a night's journey except with a mahram."],[2540,"A woman is not to travel a barid unless she has with her a mahram. And Yusuf said, “Unless she has with her a mahram.”"],[2541,"It is not lawful for a woman to travel for three [days] unless she has with her a mahram for her."],[2542,"I was once with an expedition, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me, “Go and bring me Maymunah.” I said, “O Prophet of Allah, I am with the expedition.” He said, “Go and bring me Maymunah.” I said, “O Messenger of Allah, I am with the expedition.” The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, “Do you not love what I love?” I said, “Yes, O Messenger of Allah.” He said, “Go and bring her to me.” He said, “So I went and brought her to him.”"],[2543,"Indeed, let no man be alone with a woman, and let her not travel unless she has with her a mahram. A man stood up and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have been enlisted in such-and-such expedition, and my wife has set out to perform Hajj.” He said, “Go and perform Hajj with your wife.”"],[2544,"Go, and perform Hajj with your wife."],[2545,"I was with Ibn ʿUmar, and a man came to him and said: I intend to travel. ʿAbd Allāh said: Wait until I bid you farewell as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to bid us farewell: I entrust to Allah your religion, your trust, and the final deeds of your life."],[2546,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, I intend to travel, so provide me with provisions. He said: May Allah provide you with piety. He said: Give me more. He said: And may He forgive your sin. He said: Give me more, by my father and mother. He said: And may He make things easy for you wherever you are."],[2547,"Whenever the Prophet ﷺ traveled, he would say: O Allah, You are the Companion in travel, and the Successor over the family. O Allah, accompany us in our travel, and be the Successor over our family. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the hardships of travel, from a sorrowful return, from loss after gain, from the supplication of the oppressed, and from an evil sight in family and property."],[2548,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Madinah for nine years without performing Hajj. Then the Hajj was announced, and it was said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ is performing Hajj. Many people came to Madinah, all wanting to follow the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out—meaning from the mosque of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah—and rode, and with him were many people, riders and walkers."],[2549,"The Prophet ﷺ performed Hajj with his companions on foot from Madinah to Makkah, and he said: Tie your waists with your izars. And he walked mixing with a quick pace."],[2550,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out in the year of the conquest, then the walkers among his companions gathered to him and formed rows for him, and they said: We seek to be exposed to the supplications of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They said: Travel has become hard for us, and the distance has become long. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: Seek help—ʿAbd al-Wahhāb said: I think he said—by a fast pace, for it cuts through the land for you and makes it light. So we did that, and it became light for us, and what we had been feeling went away."],[2551,"People complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about walking, so he called them and said: You should walk with a quick pace. So we walked quickly and found it lighter for us."],[2552,"The best companionship is four, the best raiding party is four hundred, the best army is four thousand, and twelve thousand will never be defeated due to small numbers."],[2553,"The best companions in the sight of Allah are those who are best to their companion, and the best neighbors in the sight of Allah are those who are best to their neighbor."],[2554,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched an expedition, and they were a small group. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ called them and said: \"How much of the Qur'an do you have with you?\" He examined them one by one until he came to a man among them who was the youngest of them. He said: \"How much do you have with you, O so-and-so?\" He said: \"I have such-and-such and Surah al-Baqarah.\" He said: \"Go, for you are their commander.\""],[2555,"When there are three people traveling, let them appoint one of themselves as leader; that is the commander whom the Messenger of Allah appointed."],[2556,"Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us that 'Ali b. 'Abd Allah al-Azdi informed him that Ibn 'Umar taught him, and he mentioned something similar."],[2557,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted us on light camels from the zakah camels for the Hajj. We said: \"O Messenger of Allah, we do not think these can carry us.\" He said: \"There is no camel but that on its hump there is a devil. So mention Allah when you mount them as He commanded you, then use them for your own service, for it is Allah who carries.\""],[2558,"Ride these animals while they are sound, and leave them sound. Do not take them as chairs."],[2559,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a camel whose belly was clinging to its back (from hunger). He said: \"Fear Allah regarding these mute beasts. Ride them while they are healthy and eat them while they are healthy.\""],[2560,"On the back of every camel there is a devil. When you mount them, mention the name of Allah, and do not hold back from any need."],[2561,"On the hump of every camel there is a devil, so use them for riding, for it is Allah who carries."],[2562,"When you travel in fertile land, give the mounts their share of grazing, and do not pass by the stopping places. When you travel in barren land, hasten, and upon you is the night journey, for the earth is folded up at night. When the ghuls waylay you, hasten to prayer. Beware of stopping to sleep on the main roads and praying on them, for they are the abodes of snakes and beasts, and relieving oneself on them, for they are the places of cursing."],[2563,"When the land is fertile, give the mounts their share, and upon you are the stopping places. When it is barren, hasten on it, and upon you is the night journey, for the earth is folded up at night. Beware of the main roads, for they are the abode of snakes and beasts. When you see the ghuls, give the call to prayer."],[2564,"When you travel in abundant pasture, give the camels their due, and when you travel in a barren year, hasten with their marrow, and when you halt for the night, avoid the road, for it is the path of beasts and the shelter of creeping creatures at night."],[2565,"The Prophet ﷺ forbade branding on the face and striking on the face."],[2566,"He forbade drinking from the mouth of a water-skin, and riding the jalalah (an animal that eats dung), and the mujaththamah (an animal tied up and shot as a target)."],[2567,"The angels do not accompany a group in which there is a bell, or in which there is a dog."],[2568,"The bell is the flute of Satan."],[2569,"You should travel by night, for the earth is folded up by night."],[2570,"When you halt for the night, avoid the road, for it is the path of beasts, and the shelter of creeping creatures at night."],[2571,"When you halt at night, avoid the road, for it is the shelter of creeping creatures at night."],[2572,"When he halted at night, he lay down on his right side, and when he halted just before dawn, he set up his forearms upright and placed his head on his palms."],[2573,"Reduce going out when the feet (of people) become still, for Allah sends forth in His night of His creation whatever He wills."],[2574,"And restrain your livestock and your families from sunset until the glow of the evening twilight departs - Yusuf said to us - the glow of the night prayer."],[2575,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ intending to travel. He said, 'O Messenger of God, advise me.' He said, 'I advise you to fear God, and to say takbir upon every rise.' When he left, he said, 'O God, roll up the earth for him, and make travel easy for him.'"],[2576,"When we ascended, we said takbir, and when we descended, we said tasbih."],[2577,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ on a military expedition. When we looked down upon Madinah, he said one takbir, and they raised their voices with it. The Messenger of God ﷺ said, 'Your Lord is neither deaf nor absent. He is between you and the heads of your mounts.'"],[2578,"Three whom God loves, and three whom God detests. As for those whom God loves: a people who traveled through their night until, when sleep was dearer to one of them than anything equal to it, they dismounted and laid down their heads. Then he arose to ingratiate himself with Me and recite My verses."],[2579,"The Prophet ﷺ never saw a village he intended to enter without saying when he saw it: 'O God, Lord of the seven heavens and what they shade, Lord of the earths and what they carry, Lord of the devils and what they mislead, Lord of the winds and what they scatter, we ask You for the good of this village and the good of its people, and we seek refuge in You from its evil, the evil of its people, and the evil of what is in it.'"],[2580,"Whoever alights at a stopping place, then says: 'I seek refuge in the perfect words of God from the evil of what He has created,' nothing will harm him until he departs from that stopping place of his."],[2581,"Ibn Wahb informed us, [he said]: 'Amr b. al-Harith informed me from Yazid b. Abi Habib. And al-Harith b. Ya'qub from Ya'qub b. 'Abd Allah b. al-Ashajj with this chain of transmission, something similar.'"],[2582,"The Prophet ﷺ did not alight at a stopping place without bidding it farewell with two rak'ahs."],[2583,"If people knew what I know about loneliness, no rider would ever travel alone at night."],[2584,"One is a devil, two are two devils, and three are a caravan."],[2585,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ was on a journey and dawn appeared to him, he said: 'A hearer has heard the praise of God and His favor and His excellent trial upon us. Our Lord, accompany us, and bestow favor upon us, seeking refuge in God from the Fire.' He said that three times, raising his voice."],[2586,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he went on a military expedition or traveled and night overtook him, would say: 'O earth, my Lord and your Lord is Allah. I seek refuge in Allah from your evil and the evil of what is in you, and the evil of what has been created in you, and the evil of what creeps upon you. I seek refuge in Allah from the evil of every lion, and [every] black snake, and [every] serpent, and [every] scorpion, and from the inhabitants of the land, and from the evil of a parent and what he begets.'"],[2587,"I used to twist the garlands of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with these two hands of mine."],[2588,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ garlanded his sacrificial animal and marked it."],[2589,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed the noon prayer and commanded that his sacrificial camels be marked on their right side, and he garlanded them with two sandals, and he wiped the blood from them."],[2590,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ marked the sacrificial animal on the right side of the hump."],[2592,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent sacrificial camels with him, and he said: 'If any of them becomes exhausted ... upon you, slaughter it, and dip its sandal in the blood of its belly, and do not eat from it, neither you nor anyone among your traveling companions.'"],[2593,"Whoever offers a voluntary sacrificial animal and it goes astray, then if he wishes he may replace it, and if he wishes he may leave it. But if it was [obligatory] due to a vow, then he must replace it."],[2594,"Whoever drives a voluntary sacrificial animal and it becomes disabled, he should not eat from it; for if he eats from it, he must replace it. But let him slaughter it, then dip its sandal in its blood, then strike it on its side. If it was an obligatory sacrificial animal, then let him eat if he wishes; for he must still fulfill it."],[2595,"I saw 'A'ishah gesturing with her hands: 'I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for his ihram when he entered ihram, and for his exiting ihram before he circumambulated the House.'"],[2596,"I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with these two hands of mine, for his ihram when he entered ihram, and for his exiting ihram before he circumambulated the House."],[2597,"I perfumed the Prophet ﷺ before he entered ihram and on the Day of Sacrifice before he circumambulated the House, with a perfume containing musk.\" Ibn Hisham said: from Mansur. And Ahmad said: from Aisha, she said: \"I perfumed, meaning the Prophet ﷺ."],[2599,"It is as if I am looking at the glistening of perfume on the head of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in ihram."],[2600,"I certainly saw the glistening of perfume in the partings of the hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was indeed reciting the talbiyah."],[2601,"It is as if I am looking at the glistening of perfume on the parting of the hair of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in ihram."],[2602,"He asked Ibn Umar about perfume at the time of ihram, and he said: 'To perfume myself with tar is more beloved to me than to do that.' He said: Then I mentioned that to Aisha, and she said: 'May Allah have mercy on Abu Abd al-Rahman. I used to perfume the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then he would go around to his wives, and then he would enter the morning in ihram, exuding perfume.'"],[2603,"The Prophet ﷺ designated Dhul-Hulaifah for the people of Madinah, al-Juhfah for the people of Sham, and Qarn for the people of Najd. Abd al-Jabbar said in his narration: He said, and it was mentioned to me, but I did not hear, that he said: and for the people of Yemen, Yalamlam. And al-Makhzumi said: And Abdullah said: It reached me that the Prophet ﷺ said: 'And the people of Yemen enter ihram from Yalamlam.'"],[2604,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ designated Dhul-Hulaifah for the people of Madinah, al-Juhfah for the people of Sham, Yalamlam for the people of Yemen, and Qarn for the people of Najd. These are for them and for those who come upon them from other than their people, among those who intend Hajj and Umrah. Whoever is within them, then from his own place, and likewise even the people of Makkah enter ihram from it."],[2605,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ designated Dhul-Hulaifah for the people of Madinah, al-Juhfah for the people of Sham, Qarn for the people of Najd, and Yalamlam for the people of Yemen. He said: These are for them and for those who come upon them from other than them, among those who intend Hajj and Umrah. Then from where one begins until that reaches the people of Makkah."],[2606,"He was asked about the miqat. He said: I think he meant the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: The miqat of the people of Madinah is Dhul-Hulaifah; the other route is al-Juhfah; the miqat of the people of Iraq is Dhat Irq; the miqat of the people of Najd is Qarn; and the miqat of the people of Yemen is Yalamlam."],[2607,"He commanded the people of Madinah to enter ihram from Dhul-Hulaifah, the people of Sham from al-Juhfah, and the people of Najd from Qarn. Abdullah ibn Umar said: And I was informed that he said: 'And the people of Yemen enter ihram from Yalamlam.'"],[2608,"We came to Jabir ibn Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: Asma bint Umais gave birth to Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, so she sent to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'What should I do?' He said: 'Bathe, use a waist wrap, and then enter ihram.'"],[2609,"The Prophet ﷺ stripped for his ihram and bathed."],[2610,"One does not enter ihram for Hajj except in the months of Hajj; for it is part of the Sunnah of Hajj that you enter ihram for Hajj in the months of Hajj."],[2611,"A man said: O Messenger of God, what clothes should we wear when we enter ihram? He said: \"Do not wear shirts, trousers, burnouses, turbans, caps, or leather socks, except for one who has no sandals; let him wear them below the ankles. And do not wear any garment that has been touched by wars or saffron.\" He said: And Abdullah used to say: \"And a woman should not wear a face veil, nor gloves.\""],[2612,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade the muhrim from wearing shirts, or qaba' cloaks, or leather socks, unless he cannot find sandals, or trousers, or wearing a garment touched by wars or saffron."],[2613,"O Prophet of God, what do you command us to wear of clothing when entering ihram? He said: \"Do not wear shirts, turbans, burnouses, trousers, or leather socks, unless a man has no sandals, in which case let him wear leather socks below the ankles. And do not wear any garment that has been touched by saffron or wars.\" He said: \"And a woman in ihram should not wear a face veil, nor gloves.\""],[2614,"A woman in ihram should not wear a face veil, nor gloves."],[2615,"A man called out and said: O Messenger of God, what clothing should the muhrim avoid? He said: \"Do not wear trousers, shirts, a burnous, a turban, or a garment touched by saffron or wars. Let one of you enter ihram in a waist-wrapper, an upper garment, and sandals. If he cannot find sandals, let him wear leather socks, and let him cut them so that they are below the ankles.\""],[2616,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by Duba'ah while she was ill and said: Do you intend to perform Hajj? She said: Yes. He said: \"Perform Hajj and stipulate a condition, and say: O God, my place of exiting ihram is wherever You detain me.\""],[2617,"The Messenger of God ﷺ stayed in Madinah for nine years without performing Hajj. Then the Hajj was announced, and it was said: The Messenger of God ﷺ is performing Hajj. Many people came to Madinah, all of them wanting to follow the Messenger of God ﷺ and do as he did. The Messenger of God ﷺ set out until he came to the mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah and prayed in it. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ came out and many people rode with him, riders and walkers, all of them wanting to follow the Messenger of God ﷺ. When he appeared at al-Bayda', he entered ihram, and we intended only Hajj, not knowing Umrah. I looked in front of me, to my right, to my left, and behind me, as far as the eye could see, riders and walkers, all of them wanting to follow the Messenger of God ﷺ."],[2618,"We set out coinciding with the new moon of Dhu al-Hijjah. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Whoever wishes to enter ihram for Hajj, let him enter ihram for Hajj; and whoever wishes to enter ihram for Umrah, let him enter ihram for Umrah.\" So some of us entered ihram for Hajj, and some of us entered ihram for Umrah."],[2619,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered ihram for Hajj, and some people entered ihram with him for it, and some people entered ihram for both Hajj and Umrah, and some people entered ihram for Umrah. Abd al-Jabbar did not say: 'and some people entered ihram with him for it,' and he added: She said: I was among those who entered ihram for both Hajj and Umrah."],[2620,"The Prophet ﷺ arrived on the fourth or fifth of Dhu al-Hijjah and came in to me while he was angry. I said: Who has angered you? He said: \"Do you not realize that I commanded the people with a command and they are hesitating?\"—al-Hakam said: hesitating, I think.—\"If I could have started my affair anew, I would not have driven the sacrificial animal with me until I bought it, then I would have exited ihram as they have.\""],[2621,"We set out with the Messenger of God ﷺ in the year of the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered ihram for 'Umrah. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'Whoever has a hadi with him should enter ihram for Hajj [along with the 'Umrah].'"],[2622,"I saw myself twisting garlands for the sheep intended as the hadi of the Messenger of God ﷺ, then he would remain in a state of being out of ihram."],[2623,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed Zuhr and commanded that his camels be marked by making a small cut on their right sides, and he garlanded them with two sandals and wiped the blood from them. Then when his mount stood with him at al-Bayda', he entered ihram."],[2624,"He went out as a pilgrim with the Messenger of God ﷺ at the Farewell Pilgrimage, and with him was his wife Asma' bt. 'Umays from Khath'am. When they were at al-Shajarah, Asma' gave birth to Muhammad b. Abi Bakr at al-Shajarah. Abu Bakr went to the Messenger of God ﷺ and informed him, so the Messenger of God ﷺ ordered him to tell her to bathe, then enter ihram for Hajj, and do what the people do except that she should not circumambulate the House."],[2625,"This is al-Bayda' where you tell lies about the Messenger of God ﷺ. By God, the Messenger of God ﷺ did not enter ihram except at the door of the mosque."],[2626,"The Prophet's ﷺ entering ihram was from Dhul-Hulayfah when his riding camel rose with him."],[2627,"When he placed his foot in the stirrup and his riding camel rose with him, he entered ihram."],[2628,"Ibn 'Umar, when he came to Dhul-Hulayfah, would order his riding camel to be saddled, then prayed the morning prayer, then rode until when he was settled on it, he faced the qiblah and entered ihram. He said: Then he would recite the talbiyah until when he reached the Haram he would stop, until when he came to Dhu Tuwa he would spend the night there. He said: Then he would pray the morning prayer there, then bathe. He claimed that the Prophet ﷺ did that."],[2629,"Ibn 'Umar, when he passed by Dhul-Hulayfah, would spend the night there until morning, and he would report that the Messenger of God ﷺ used to do that."],[2630,"The Prophet ﷺ was visited while he was at his resting place in Dhul-Hulayfah, and it was said: 'You are in a blessed valley.' Musa said: Salim had made our camels kneel at the resting place where 'Abd Allah used to make his camel kneel, seeking the resting place of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and it is lower than the mosque which is in the bottom of the valley, between it and the road in the middle of that."],[2631,"A visitor came to me tonight from my Lord — and he was at al-'Aqiq — saying: 'Pray in this blessed valley and say: an 'Umrah within a Hajj.'"],[2632,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said: Labbayk for Hajj and Umrah."],[2633,"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj, Labbayk for Umrah and Hajj, many times."],[2634,"We set out with the Messenger of God ﷺ, intending nothing but the Hajj, until the Messenger of God ﷺ arrived in Makkah, circumambulated the House seven times, prayed two rak'ahs behind the Maqam, then said: \"We begin with that which God began with.\" So he began with al-Safa, until he completed the seventh round at al-Marwah. Then Ali b. Abi Talib came to him with his sacrificial animals from Yemen. The Messenger of God ﷺ said to him: \"For what did you enter ihram?\" He replied: I said: \"O God, I enter ihram for that for which Your Messenger entered ihram.\" He said: \"Indeed I have entered ihram for Hajj.\""],[2635,"The talbiyah of the Prophet ﷺ was: Labbayk, O God, labbayk; labbayk, You have no partner, labbayk; indeed praise and favor are Yours, and sovereignty; You have no partner. Mu'ammal said in his hadith: Ibn Umar added: Labbayk, labbayk; labbayk and sa'dayk, and all good is in Your hands; [labbayk], and desire is directed to You and deeds."],[2636,"I received the talbiyah from the Messenger of God."],[2637,"The Prophet ﷺ said in his talbiyah: \"Labbayk, God of Truth.\""],[2638,"Among the talbiyah of the Messenger of God ﷺ was: \"Labbayk, God of Truth.\""],[2639,"Gabriel came to me and said: Command your companions to raise their voices in the talbiyah. Ahmad b. Mani' said: \"with the ihram and the talbiyah.\""],[2640,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Prophet's ﷺ Hajj. He said: He set out until, when his she-camel stood upright with him on al-Bayda', he pronounced the talbiyah of tawhid: \"Labbayk, O God, labbayk; labbayk, You have no partner, labbayk; indeed praise and favor are Yours, and sovereignty; You have no partner.\" He said: And the people pronounced the talbiyah, adding \"Dhu al-Ma'arij\" and the like, while the Prophet ﷺ listened and did not say anything."],[2641,"Gabriel came to me and said: Command your companions to raise their voices in the talbiyah. Ahmad b. Mani' said: \"with the ihram and the talbiyah.\""],[2642,"Gabriel came to me and said: O Muhammad, command your companions to raise their shouts with the talbiyah, for it is the emblem of the Hajj."],[2643,"Gabriel came to me and said to me: Proclaim the talbiyah, for it is the emblem of the Hajj."],[2644,"Gabriel commanded me to raise my voice in the talbiyah, for it is among the rites of Hajj."],[2645,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: Which deeds are best? He said: \"The raising of voices in talbiyah (al-ʿajj) and the shedding of sacrificial blood (al-thajj).\""],[2646,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Makkah to Madinah. When we came to Wadi al-Azraq, he said: \"Which valley is this?\" We said: Wadi al-Azraq. He said: \"It is as if I am looking at Musa,\" and he described his height, his hair, and his complexion, placing his two fingers in his ears, making a fervent supplication to Allah with the talbiyah, passing through this valley. Then we continued until we came to— Dawud said: I think it was Thaniyyat Harsha—he said: \"Which mountain pass is this?\" We said: Thaniyyat Harsha. He said: \"It is as if I am looking at Yunus upon a red camel, its reins made of fiber, wearing a woolen jubbah, at this mountain pass, reciting the talbiyah.\""],[2647,"We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ between Makkah and Madinah. We passed by a valley, and he said: \"Which valley is this?\" They said: Wadi al-Azraq. He said: \"It is as if I am looking at Musa,\" and he mentioned something of his complexion and hair that Dawud did not memorize, placing his two fingers in his ears, making a fervent supplication to Allah with the talbiyah, passing through this valley. He said: Then we traveled until we came to a mountain pass, and he said: \"Which mountain pass is this?\" They said: Harsha or such-and-such. He said: \"It is as if I am looking at Yunus upon a red camel, wearing a woolen jubbah, the reins of his camel made of fiber, passing through this valley, reciting the talbiyah.\""],[2649,"They were on a journey, and among them were some who had entered ihram. He said: Abu Qatadah mounted his horse and came upon a wild donkey, and he killed it, and they ate its meat. Then it was as if they were apprehensive about that, so they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: \"Did you participate in the hunt or point it out?\" They said: No. The Prophet ﷺ said: \"Then eat it.\" In the report of Ibn Abi ʿAdi, he said: \"Did you point it out or assist?\" And in the report of Ibn Abi ʿAdi from Shuʿbah there is something similar. He said: \"Did you point it out, or hunt, or assist?\" They said: No. He said: \"Then eat it.\""],[2650,"He killed a wild donkey while he was with a group of people who were in ihram. They mentioned it to the Prophet ﷺ, and he said: \"Did you hunt, or assist, or point it out?\" They said: No. He said: \"Then eat it.\""],[2651,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by me while I was at al-Abwa'—Ibn Maʿmar said: or at Waddan—and I gifted him a wild donkey, but he returned it to me. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw the displeasure on my face, he said: \"It is not that we are returning it to you, but we are in ihram.\""],[2652,"We were with Talha while we were in ihram, and a bird was gifted to him while Talha was asleep. Some of us ate, and some of us refrained out of scrupulousness. When Talha woke up, he agreed with those who ate and said: \"We ate it with the Messenger of Allah.\""],[2653,"When Zayd b. Arqam arrived, Ibn ʿAbbas said: I will remind him of how he narrated to us regarding the meat that was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ. So I reminded him, and he said: Meat of game was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in ihram, and he returned it, saying: \"We are in ihram.\""],[2654,"Zayd b. Arqam arrived in Makkah—Ibn Maʿmar did not say: Makkah—and Ibn ʿAbbas said, reminding him: How did you inform me about the meat that was gifted to the Prophet ﷺ while it was forbidden? He said: Yes, a man gifted him a limb of game meat, and he returned it to him, saying: \"We do not eat it, we are in ihram.\""],[2655,"The meat of land game is lawful for you while you are in the state of ihram, as long as you did not hunt it or it was not hunted for you."],[2656,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the time of al-Hudaybiyyah. My companions entered ihram, but I did not. I saw a wild donkey, so I attacked it and hunted it. I mentioned its matter to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I mentioned that I had not entered ihram and that I had only hunted it for you. The Prophet ﷺ ordered his companions, and they ate, but he did not eat from it when I informed him that I had hunted it for him."],[2657,"He went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while they were in ihram, but he was not in ihram. He saw a wild donkey, so he mounted his horse and asked them to hand him a spear or a whip, but they refused to hand it to him. So he took it himself, then charged at it and hamstrung it. Then he brought it, and they caught up with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He said: \"Do you have any of its meat with you?\" They said: \"Yes.\" So they brought him its leg, and he ate from it."],[2658,"O Zayd b. Arqam, do you know that ostrich eggs were gifted to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was in ihram, and he returned them? He said: \"Yes.\""],[2659,"I met Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the hyena: \"Should we eat it?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Is it game?\" He said: \"Yes.\" I said: \"Did you hear that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?\" He said: \"Yes.\""],[2660,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set for the hyena that a muhrim kills a Najdi ram as compensation, and he considered it game."],[2661,"For the hyena, compensation was judged as a ram."],[2662,"The hyena is game. If a muhrim kills it, the compensation is a mature ram, and it may be eaten."],[2663,"The muhrim does not marry, nor does he give in marriage."],[2664,"Al-Miswar b. Makhramah and Ibn Abbas disagreed while they were at al-Arj about washing the head of a muhrim — and he said once: about the Prophet ﷺ washing his head. So they sent me to Abu Ayyub to ask him. I came to him at al-Arj while he was bathing between the two posts of the well. I greeted him, and when he saw me, he pulled his garment to his chest until it seemed I could see his chest. I said: \"Your nephew Abdullah b. Abbas sent me to ask you: How did you see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wash his head while he was in ihram?\" He ordered a bucket, so it was poured, and he poured it over his head. Then he brought his hands forward and back over his head, and said: \"This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.\" He (the narrator) said: Then I came to Ibn Abbas and informed him, and al-Miswar said to him: \"I will never dispute with you about anything after this.\""],[2665,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had cupping done while he was in ihram."],[2666,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ applied non-perfumed oil while he was in ihram."],[2667,"Hajjaj b. Minhal narrated to us:"],[2668,"When a man complains of his eyes while in ihram, he should bandage them with aloe."],[2669,"The Prophet ﷺ was cupped while in ihram. And did the Prophet ﷺ use the siwak while in ihram? He said: Yes."],[2670,"I heard the Prophet ﷺ raising the talbiyah with his hair matted."],[2671,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped on his head while in ihram."],[2672,"Anas was asked about the fasting person who is cupped. He said: We did not consider that disliked except due to his weakness, and he did not attribute it to the Prophet."],[2673,"The Prophet ﷺ was cupped on the top of his foot while in ihram due to a pain he had."],[2674,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was cupped while in ihram due to a sprain in his back or hip."],[2675,"The Prophet ﷺ came upon a man driving a sacrificial camel and said: \"Ride it.\" The man said: \"It is a sacrificial camel.\" He said: \"Ride it, woe to you—or may God have mercy on you.\""],[2676,"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ being asked about riding a sacrificial camel. He said: \"Ride it until you find another mount.\""],[2677,"He heard Jabir b. Abd Allah being asked about riding a sacrificial animal. He said: I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: \"Ride it in a fair manner if you are compelled to it, until you find another mount.\""],[2678,"Five creatures: no blame on the one who kills them: the scorpion, the crow, the kite, the mouse, and the vicious dog."],[2679,"The snake, the wolf, the leopard, and the vicious dog."],[2680,"Five whose killing is lawful in the Haram: the snake, the scorpion, the mouse, the kite, and the vicious dog."],[2681,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded a person in ihram to kill a snake in the Haram."],[2682,"Five are harmful pests that are to be killed in the Hill and the Haram: the snake, the speckled crow, the mouse, the vicious dog, and the kite."],[2683,"Ya'la b. Umayyah said to Umar: \"How I wish I could see the Prophet ﷺ when revelation descends upon him.\" So when he was at al-Ji'ranah, with a garment shading him and with him some of his companions in it, a man came to him who had smeared himself with perfume. He said: \"O Messenger of God, what do you think of a man who entered ihram in a jubba after smearing himself with perfume?\" He said: He looked at him for a while, then revelation came down upon him. Umar sent to Ya'la: \"Come.\" So he came and put his head in, and there was his face red like that for a while, then it was relieved from him. Then he said: \"Where is the one who asked me about the umrah just now?\" The man was sought, and the Prophet ﷺ gave an order concerning him, saying: \"As for the perfume that is on you, wash it off three times. As for the jubba, take it off. Then do in your umrah what you do in your hajj.\""],[2684,"Take off your jubba."],[2685,"Take off your jubba."],[2686,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade men from using saffron."],[2687,"The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade a man from using saffron."],[2688,"I became pale one day, and a companion of mine said to me, “Let us go to the camp.” He said: So I went and washed, and applied khalooq. The Messenger of God ﷺ used to wipe our faces, but when he came near me he began to keep his hand away from the khalooq. When he finished, he said to me, “O Ya‘la, what made you apply khalooq? Did you get married?” I said, “No.” So the Messenger of God ﷺ said to me, “Then go and wash it off.” He said: I passed by a well and began to plunge into it, then I began to rub myself with dust until it was gone. Then I came back, and when the Messenger of God ﷺ saw me, he said: “And al-‘Ala’ returned with the goodness of his religion; he repented and the sky poured down.”"],[2689,"The Messenger of God ﷺ came to me at the time of al-Hudaybiyyah, when I had abundant hair, and said, “It seems the vermin of your head are troubling you?” I said, “Yes.” He said, “Then shave it, and sacrifice a sheep as a ritual offering, or fast three days, or give charity of three sa‘s among six poor people.”"],[2690,"The Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was lighting a fire under a clay pot – or he said: under a cooking pot – and lice were falling on his face. The Prophet ﷺ said to him, “Are these troubling you?” He said, “Yes, O Messenger of God.” Then was revealed: “a ransom of fasting or charity or ritual sacrifice.” The Prophet ﷺ commanded him while they were at al-Hudaybiyyah, and he had not made clear to them that they would shave their heads there, for they were still hoping to enter Makkah. Then God, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the ransom, and the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to shave and fast three days, or feed a faraq among six poor people, or sacrifice a sheep."],[2691,"The Messenger of God ﷺ saw him while his lice were falling on his face, and said, “Are your vermin troubling you?” He said, “Yes.” So he commanded him to shave while he was at al-Hudaybiyyah, but he had not made clear to them that they could exit ihram there, for they were still hoping to enter Makkah. Then God, Mighty and Majestic, sent down the ransom, and the Messenger of God ﷺ commanded him to feed a faraq among six, or sacrifice a sheep as an offering, or fast three days."],[2692,"We went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ as pilgrims, and the riding camel of the Messenger of God ﷺ and the riding camel of Abu Bakr were one and the same. We stopped at al-‘Arj, and our camel was with the slave of Abu Bakr. She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ sat down, and ‘A’ishah sat beside him, and Abu Bakr sat on the other side of the Messenger of God ﷺ, and I sat beside my father, waiting for his slave and our camel – when would he come to us. Then the slave appeared walking, without his camel. She said: Abu Bakr said to him, “Where is your camel?” He said, “It strayed from me last night.” She said: Abu Bakr got up and began to beat him, saying, “One camel, and you lost it, and you are a man?” The Messenger of God ﷺ did nothing more than smile and say, “Look at this muhrim, and what he is doing.”"],[2693,"The Messenger of God ﷺ entered Makkah to perform ‘umrah before its conquest, and Ibn Rawahah was walking in front of him, reciting: “Make way, O sons of the disbelievers, from his path; today we strike you for its revelation, a blow that removes heads from their resting place and makes a friend forget his friend.” ‘Umar said, “O Ibn Rawahah! In the sanctuary of God, and in front of the Messenger of God ﷺ, you recite this poetry?” The Prophet ﷺ said, “Leave him, O ‘Umar! For by Him in whose hand is my soul, his words are harder on them than the falling of arrows.”"],[2694,"I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was delivering a sermon, saying: 'Trousers are for one who does not find a waist-wrapper, and leather socks are for one who does not find sandals.'"],[2695,"A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at that place: 'O Messenger of Allah, what garments should the muhrim not wear?' He said: 'He should not wear shirts, trousers, turbans, or leather socks unless he cannot find sandals, in which case he may wear them cut below the ankles. Nor should he wear any garment touched by wars or saffron, nor a burnous.'"],[2696,"If the muhrim does not find sandals, let him wear leather socks and cut them below the ankles."],[2697,"A man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what should we wear of garments when we enter ihram?' He said: 'Do not wear shirts, trousers, burnouses, turbans, caps, or leather socks, unless someone has no sandals, in which case he may wear them cut below the ankles.'"],[2698,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted a concession to women regarding leather socks."],[2699,"He ordered—meaning the Prophet ﷺ—for a dome of hair to be made for him, and it was pitched for him at Namirah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then traveled until he came to Arafah and found the dome already pitched for him at Namirah, so he stopped there."],[2700,"I performed Hajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and I saw Usamah b. Zayd and Bilal, one of them leading his riding camel by its halter and the other holding up a garment to shade him from the heat, until he threw the pebbles at Jamrat al-Aqabah."],[2701,"When we entered ihram, we would wear any garment we had not worn when we entered ihram, and we would wear the dyed garment—it is only clay."],[2702,"We used to cover our faces from the men, and we used to comb our hair before that."],[2703,"We were with the Messenger of God ﷺ while we were in ihram, and when a caravan passed by us, we let down the garment over our faces."],[2704,"He spent the night at Dhu Tuwa until morning, then entered Makkah."],[2705,"He used to enter from the upper mountain pass and leave from the lower mountain pass."],[2706,"He once entered ihram from Dhu al-Hulayfah, from near the tree. When the Messenger of God ﷺ came to Dhu Tuwa, he spent the night until he prayed the morning prayer, then performed ghusl, then entered Makkah from the upper part of Makkah, from Kuda, and when he left, he left from Kuda, from the lower part of Makkah."],[2707,"When Ibn Umar came to Dhu al-Hulayfah, he ordered his riding camel to be saddled, then he prayed the morning prayer, then he mounted. When it stood upright with him, he faced the qiblah and entered ihram. He said: Then he would continue the talbiyah until he reached the Haram, then he would stop until he came to Dhu Tuwa and spent the night there. He said: He would pray the morning prayer there, then perform ghusl. He asserted that the Prophet ﷺ did that."],[2708,"I performed Hajj with Abdullah b. Umar b. al-Khattab, between Hajj and Umrah, twelve times. He said: I said to him: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, I have seen four characteristics from you. Then he mentioned the hadith and said: I saw that when you entered ihram and entered the Arsh, you stopped the talbiyah. He said: You are right, O Ibn Jurayj. I went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and when he entered the Arsh, he stopped the talbiyah. My talbiyah will not cease until I die."],[2710,"I saw Abdullah b. Umar stop the talbiyah when he entered the Haram, and he would resume it when he circumambulated the House, and when he finished the tawaf between al-Safa and al-Marwah."],[2711,"A man from the people of Iraq said to him: Ask Urwah b. al-Zubayr for me about a man who enters ihram for Hajj. So I asked him, and he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ performed Hajj, and Aishah informed me that the first thing he began with when he arrived in Makkah was that he performed wudu, then circumambulated the House."],[2712,"When the Messenger of God ﷺ came in the covenant of Quraysh, when he entered Makkah, he entered through this great gate, and Quraysh had sat down near the Black Stone or the Hijr."],[2713,"A woman would circumambulate the House naked, saying: 'Today part of it or all of it appears; whatever appears of it, I do not deem lawful.' Then it was revealed: 'O Children of Adam, take your adornment at every mosque.'"],[2714,"He used to say: 'The Day of Sacrifice is the day of the greater Hajj.'"],[2715,"Hands are raised in seven places, and in the report: 'and upon facing the House.'"],[2716,"Jabir b. Abdullah was asked about a man who sees the House, should he raise his hands? He said: 'I do not think anyone does this except the Jews. We performed Hajj with the Messenger of God ﷺ and we did not do this.'"],[2717,"We asked Jabir b. Abdullah about a man who finishes his prayer and his tawaf, then leaves the Sacred Mosque and faces the House. He said: 'I did not think anyone does this except the Jews.'"],[2718,"When one of you enters the mosque, let him send peace upon the Prophet and say: 'O God, open for me the doors of Your mercy.' And when he leaves, let him send peace upon the Prophet and say: 'O God, protect me from Satan the accursed.'"],[2719,"The Messenger of God ﷺ and his companions performed idtiba' and jogged for three circuits and walked for four."],[2720,"Why the jogging now and the baring of shoulders, when God has established Islam and banished disbelief and its people? Nevertheless, we do not abandon anything we used to do with the Messenger of God."],[2721,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: 'We set out intending only Hajj until we came to the Kaaba. The Messenger of God ﷺ touched the Black Stone, then jogged three and walked four.'"],[2722,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ when he arrived in Makkah touching the Black Corner first when he circumambulated; when he arrived he ran three circuits of the seven."],[2723,"Umar b. al-Khattab kissed the Stone and said: 'By God, I know that you are a stone. Were it not that I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ kissing you, I would not have kissed you.'"],[2724,"The Messenger of God ﷺ faced the Stone and touched it, then placed his lips upon it weeping for a long time. I turned and there was Umar weeping. He said: 'O Umar! Here tears are shed.'"],[2725,"We entered Makkah at mid-morning. He—meaning the Prophet ﷺ—came to the gate of the mosque, made his mount kneel, then entered the mosque. He began with the Stone and touched it, and his eyes overflowed with tears. He mentioned the hadith. He said: He performed ramal for three [circuits] and walked for four, until he finished. When he finished, he kissed the Stone, placed his hands on it, then wiped his face with them."],[2726,"I saw Muhammad b. ʿAbbād b. Jaʿfar kiss the Stone and prostrate on it, then he said: I saw your maternal uncle Ibn ʿAbbās kiss it and prostrate on it. And Ibn ʿAbbās said: I saw ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb kiss it and prostrate on it, then he said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do this, so I did it."],[2727,"I saw Ibn ʿUmar touch the Stone with his hand, kiss his hand, and say: I have not left it since I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do it."],[2728,"When his mount stood straight with him at the mosque of Dhū al-Ḥulayfah for a Hajj or ʿUmrah, the Messenger of God ﷺ entered the state of iḥrām and said: \"Labbayka Allāhumma labbayk, labbayka lā sharīka laka labbayk, inna al-ḥamda wa-al-niʿmata laka wa-al-mulk, lā sharīka lak.\" This was the talbiyah of the Messenger of God ﷺ. Then when he reached the House, the Stone faced him, so he said the takbīr, then faced the Stone, then ran three circuits and walked four circuits, then prayed two rakʿahs."],[2729,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed ramal for three [circuits] and walked for four."],[2730,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed ramal from the Stone to the Stone—ʿAlī added: three [circuits]—and walked four."],[2731,"I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: Ramal is three circuits around the House and four walking; your people claim it is a sunnah. He said: They are right and wrong. The Prophet ﷺ came to Makkah, and when the people of Makkah heard of him, they said: Look at the companions of Muḥammad—they are too weak from emaciation to circumambulate the House. So the Messenger of God ﷺ said: Show them what they dislike."],[2732,"The Quraysh said: Muḥammad and his companions have been weakened by the fever of Yathrib. When the Messenger of God ﷺ came in the year he came, he said to his companions: Perform ramal around the House three times so that the polytheists may see your strength. When they performed ramal, the Quraysh said: They have not been weakened."],[2733,"He heard the Prophet ﷺ say between the corner of Banū Jumaḥ and the Black Stone: \"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.\""],[2734,"The Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulated the House while on a camel. Each time he came to the corner, he pointed to it with something in his hand and said the takbīr."],[2735,"He used to, when he circumambulated the House, wipe, or he said: touch, the Stone and the Corner in every circumambulation."],[2736,"The Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulated the House on a camel, and whenever he came to the Corner he pointed to it."],[2737,"The Messenger of God ﷺ did not touch any of the corners of the House except the Black Corner and the one next to it towards the houses of the Jumahiyyin."],[2738,"\"Did you not see that your people, when they built the Kaaba, fell short of the foundations of Abraham?\" She said: I said, \"O Messenger of God, will you not restore it to the foundations of Abraham?\" He said: \"Were it not that your people are recent in disbelief.\" He said: Ibn Umar said: \"If Aisha heard this from the Messenger of God ﷺ, I do not think the Messenger of God ﷺ left off touching the two corners that are adjacent to the Hijr except that the House was not completed upon the foundations of Abraham.\""],[2739,"The Messenger of God ﷺ kissed the Yemeni Corner and placed his cheek upon it."],[2740,"Ibn Abbas used to say: \"Memorize this hadith,\" and he would trace it back to the Prophet ﷺ, and he would supplicate with it between the two corners: \"My Lord, make me content with what You have provided me, and bless it for me, and replace for me every absent thing with good.\""],[2741,"He heard his father say to Ibn Umar: \"Why do I not see you touching except these two corners: the Black Stone and the Yemeni Corner?\" Ibn Umar said: \"If I do so, I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say: 'Wiping them removes sins.'\""],[2742,"Ubaydah b. Humayd narrated to us, from Ata b. al-Saib, from Abdullah b. Ubayd b. Umayr, from his father, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of it."],[2743,"The Corner and the Station are two rubies from the rubies of Paradise. God obliterated their light, and were it not for that they would illuminate all between the east and the west."],[2744,"Indeed the Stone and the Station..."],[2745,"The Black Stone descended from Paradise whiter than snow, and the sins of the children of Adam made it black."],[2746,"The Black Stone is a white ruby from the rubies of Paradise. It was made black only by the sins of the polytheists. It will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection like Uhud, bearing witness for those who touched it and kissed it among the people of the world."],[2747,"God will surely resurrect this corner on the Day of Resurrection: it will have two eyes with which it sees, and a tongue with which it speaks, testifying for one who touched it in truth."],[2748,"Indeed, this stone has a tongue and two lips, testifying on the Day of Resurrection for one who touched it in truth."],[2749,"The Corner will come on the Day of Resurrection greater than Abu Qubays, with a tongue and two lips, speaking about the one who touched it with intention, and it is the right hand of God with which He shakes the hands of His creation."],[2750,"The throwing of the pebbles and the circumambulation of the House have only been instituted for the establishment of the remembrance of God, not for anything else."],[2751,"Circumambulation of the House is like prayer, except that you may speak during it; so whoever speaks, let him speak only good."],[2752,"The Hijr is part of the House, because the Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulated the House from behind it, and God said: 'Let them circumambulate the Ancient House.'"],[2753,"\"Your people shortened the building of the House. Were it not that their covenant with polytheism was recent, I would restore what they left of it. If your people after me decide to build it, come and I will show you what they left of it.\" So he showed her about seven cubits."],[2754,"Were it not that your people's covenant with disbelief was recent, I would demolish the House and build it on the foundation of Abraham, for Quraysh shortened its building, and I would make for it a rear entrance."],[2755,"Ibn 'Umar intended Hajj and said: 'I will make it 'Umrah; if I am prevented, I will do as the Messenger of God ﷺ did.' When he looked upon al-Bayda', he said: 'I do not see the way of the two except as one. I call you to witness that I have made Hajj obligatory along with my 'Umrah.' When he came to Qudayd, he bought a sacrificial animal and drove it with him until he arrived in Makkah. Then he circumambulated the House, prayed two rak'ahs behind the Maqam, and went between Safa and Marwah—meaning he performed sa'y—and said: 'Thus I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ do.'"],[2756,"The companions of the Prophet ﷺ who combined Hajj and 'Umrah performed a single circumambulation."],[2757,"Whoever enters ihram for Hajj and 'Umrah, a single circumambulation suffices for both, then he does not exit ihram until he completes his Hajj, and then he exits ihram from both together."],[2758,"He pronounced the talbiyah for Hajj and Umrah, and performed a single tawaf for both, and said: \"This is how I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do.\""],[2759,"O Banu Abd Manaf, let no one be prevented from performing tawaf around this House and praying at any hour he wishes, whether by night or by day."],[2760,"There is no prayer after dawn, nor after the afternoon, except in Makkah, except in Makkah, except in Makkah."],[2761,"Al-Miswar b. Makhramah performed eighteen circuits, then prayed two rak'ahs for every seven."],[2762,"The Prophet ﷺ drank water during tawaf."],[2763,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed while performing tawaf around the Ka'bah by a man leading another man by a nose ring in his nose, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cut it with his hand, then ordered him to lead him by his hand. He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed while performing tawaf around the Ka'bah by a man who had tied a man's hand with a strap, or with a thread, or with something else, and the Prophet ﷺ cut it and said: \"Lead him by your hand.\""],[2765,"\"You are indeed crowding for these two corners.\" He said: \"If I do so, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Wiping them removes sins.' And I heard him say: 'Whoever performs tawaf around the House does not raise a foot nor put one down except that Allah writes for him a good deed, removes from him a sin, and writes for him a degree.' And I heard him say: 'Whoever counts a complete circuit, it is like freeing a slave.'\" Yusuf said in his hadith: \"And a degree is raised for him thereby.\""],[2766,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned the hadith in full and said: When he finished — meaning from the tawaf — he went to the Maqam of Ibrahim and prayed two rak'ahs behind it, and recited: 'And take the Maqam of Ibrahim as a place of prayer.' He said: That is, he recited in them Surah al-Tawhid and 'Say: O disbelievers.'"],[2767,"Then he jogged three times and walked four, then came to the Maqam, then recited: 'And take the Maqam of Ibrahim as a place of prayer.' He placed the Maqam between himself and the door. When he finished, he came to the House and touched the corner."],[2768,"When the Prophet ﷺ prayed the two rak'ahs, he returned to the Stone and touched it."],[2769,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then he returned to the Stone and touched it, and went out to Safa. He said: 'I begin with what Allah began with,' and recited: 'Indeed Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.' He climbed Safa until when he could see the House, he said takbir three times — meaning and said: 'There is no god but Allah, alone with no partner, His is the dominion, and His is the praise, and He is over all things capable. There is no god but Allah, He fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone.' Then he repeated this speech three times, then descended. When his feet reached the valley, he ran, and when he ascended, he walked until he came to Marwah and climbed it. When he could see the House, he said upon it as he had said upon Safa."],[2770,"He raised his hands and began to praise Allah and supplicate to Him as Allah willed."],[2771,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then he returned to the Stone and touched it, and went out to Safa. He said: 'I begin with what Allah began with,' and recited: 'Indeed Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.' He climbed Safa until when he could see the House, he said takbir three times — meaning and said: 'There is no god but Allah, alone with no partner, His is the dominion, and His is the praise, and He is over all things capable. There is no god but Allah, He fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone.' Then he repeated this speech three times, then descended. When his feet reached the valley, he ran, and when he ascended, he walked until he came to Marwah and climbed it. When he could see the House, he said upon it as he had said upon Safa."],[2772,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived and circumambulated the House seven times, prayed two rak'ahs behind the Station, and performed Sa'i between Safa and Marwa seven times. And you have in the Messenger of Allah an excellent example."],[2773,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He mentioned part of the hadith and said: Then he returned to the Stone and touched it, and went out to Safa. He said: 'I begin with what Allah began with,' and recited: 'Indeed Safa and Marwah are among the symbols of Allah.' He climbed Safa until when he could see the House, he said takbir three times — meaning and said: 'There is no god but Allah, alone with no partner, His is the dominion, and His is the praise, and He is over all things capable. There is no god but Allah, He fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, and defeated the confederates alone.' Then he repeated this speech three times, then descended. When his feet reached the valley, he ran, and when he ascended, he walked until he came to Marwah and climbed it. When he could see the House, he said upon it as he had said upon Safa."],[2774,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to run in the middle of the valley between Safa and Marwa."],[2775,"When his mount stood upright with him at the mosque of Dhul-Hulayfah in a Hajj or 'Umrah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered ihram. He mentioned the hadith, and said: Then he came to Safa and performed Sa'i between Safa and Marwa seven times, and when he passed through the course, he ran."],[2776,"We had a ring in the Jahiliyyah. She said: I looked out from a small window between Safa and Marwa, and I saw the Prophet ﷺ, and he was running, and he was saying to his companions: Run, for Allah has made the Sa'i obligatory upon you. I saw him, due to the intensity of his running, his lower garment twisting around his stomach until I saw the whiteness of his stomach and his thighs."],[2777,"She heard the Prophet ﷺ between Safa and Marwa saying: Sa'i has been made obligatory upon you, so perform Sa'i."],[2778,"I recited to 'A'ishah: 'Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah' (Quran 2:158). I said: I do not think there is any obligation upon one who does not perform Tawaf between them. She said: How bad is what you have said, O son of my sister! It was only those who entered ihram for Manat, the idol at Mushallal, who used to perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. When Islam came, they said: O Messenger of Allah, our Tawaf between these two stones is from the affairs of the Jahiliyyah. She said: Then the verse 'Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah' was revealed. She said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Tawaf, and it became a Sunnah. Someone other than her said: Allah said: 'So whoever does good voluntarily' (Quran 2:158). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did good voluntarily and performed Tawaf. Al-Zuhri said: I narrated this to Abu Bakr b. 'Abd al-Rahman, and he said: This is indeed knowledge. I have heard men of knowledge say: The people who used to perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying: O Messenger of Allah, we were commanded to perform Tawaf around the House, but we were not commanded to perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah.'"],[2779,"The Ansar, before they accepted Islam, together with Ghassan, used to enter ihram for Manat, and they felt uneasy about performing Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. That was a Sunnah in their days: whoever entered ihram for Manat did not perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. When they accepted Islam, they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah' up to His saying: 'All-Knowing, All-Aware' (Quran 2:158). 'Urwah said: 'A'ishah said: It is a Sunnah established by the Messenger of Allah."],[2781,"I said to 'A'ishah: I do not think there is any sin upon me if I do not perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. She said: Why? I said: Because Allah says: 'So whoever performs Hajj of the House or 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him to perform Tawaf between them' (Quran 2:158). She said: If it were as you say, it would have been: 'there is no blame upon him not to perform Tawaf between them.' Allah revealed this concerning some people of the Ansar who, when they entered ihram in the Jahiliyyah, they entered ihram for Manat, and it was not permissible for them to perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa. When they came with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for Hajj, they mentioned that to him, and Allah revealed this verse. By my life, Allah has not completed the Hajj of one who does not perform Tawaf between Safa and Marwa, because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: 'Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah.' So they are among the symbols of Allah."],[2782,"I saw Ibn 'Umar walking in the course. [He said:] I said to him: You walk in the course between Safa and Marwa? He said: If I run, I have seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ running, and if I walk, I have seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walking, and I am an old man."],[2783,"I saw Ibn 'Umar walking between Safa and Marwa, and I said to him. He said: If I walk, I have seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walking, and if I run, I have seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ running."],[2784,"Al-Dahhak narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Said b. Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, something similar."],[2785,"The Prophet ﷺ ran one year and walked one year."],[2786,"I went out with the Messenger of God ﷺ as a pilgrim, and people used to come to him. Some would say: O Messenger of God, I performed Sa'i before I performed Tawaf, or I delayed something or brought something forward. He would say to them: No harm, no harm, except a man who borrows from the honor of a Muslim man while he is an oppressor. That is the one who is in harm and has perished."],[2787,"The Messenger of God ﷺ performed Umrah and circumambulated the House, then he went out to perform Sa'i between Safa and Marwa. We began to shield him from the people of Makkah, lest any of them throw something at him or hit him with something. I heard him supplicating against the confederates, saying: O God, Sender down of the Book, Swift in reckoning, defeat the confederates. O God, defeat them and shake them."],[2788,"She arrived while she was ill, and she mentioned that to the Prophet ﷺ . He said: Perform Tawaf behind the people while you are riding."],[2789,"The Messenger of God ﷺ and his companions only performed Sa'i by the House and between Safa and Marwa so that the polytheists might see his strength."],[2790,"The Prophet ﷺ circumambulated the House during the Farewell Pilgrimage on his mount, and between Safa and Marwa, so that the people could see him, for the people had crowded around him."],[2791,"I said to Ibn Abbas: What do you think about riding between Safa and Marwa? He said: Your people claim that it is a Sunnah. He said: They have spoken truth and they have spoken falsehood. The Prophet ﷺ came to Makkah and began to perform Sa'i between Safa and Marwa. The people of Makkah came out, even the women came out, and no one would be struck in his presence, nor would they leave him alone. So he called for his mount and rode. If he had been left alone, walking would have been dearer to him."],[2792,"The Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulated during the Farewell Pilgrimage on a camel, touching the corner with his crook."],[2793,"The Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulated on his she-camel al-Qaswa' on the day of the conquest, touching the corner with his crook."],[2794,"I saw the Prophet ﷺ circumambulating the House on his she-camel or on his mount, touching with his crook and kissing the tip of the crook."],[2795,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ circumambulating on his mount around the House, touching the corners with his crook. He said: And I think he was kissing the tip of the crook. Then he went out to Safa and performed Sa'i on his mount."],[2796,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and we entered Ihram for Umrah. Those who had entered Ihram for Umrah circumambulated the House and went between Safa and Marwa, then they exited Ihram."],[2797,"The Prophet ﷺ commanded his companions to make it an Umrah, then to circumambulate, then to shorten their hair or shave, except those who had a sacrificial animal with them."],[2798,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived on the morning of the fourth day that had passed of Dhul-Hijjah. When we arrived, he commanded us to exit Ihram, and said: 'Exit Ihram and have intercourse with women.' Ata said: Jabir said: He did not make it obligatory upon them to have intercourse with women, but he made it permissible for them."],[2799,"Every road of Makkah is a path and a place of sacrifice."],[2800,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the Farewell Pilgrimage, and we entered Ihram for Umrah. She said: I arrived in Makkah while I was menstruating, and I did not circumambulate the House or go between Safa and Marwa. I complained about that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: 'Undo your hair and comb it, enter Ihram for Hajj, and leave the Umrah.' She said: I did that. When we had completed the Hajj, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me with Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr to al-Tanim, and I performed Umrah. He said: 'This is in place of your Umrah.'"],[2801,"Whoever has a sacrificial animal with him should enter Ihram for Hajj along with the Umrah, then he should not exit Ihram until he exits from both of them together."],[2802,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the Hajj in three different ways: some of us entered Ihram for Hajj and Umrah together, some of us entered Ihram for Hajj alone, and some of us entered Ihram for Umrah alone. Whoever entered Ihram for Umrah and Hajj together should not exit from any of the restrictions until he completes the rites of Hajj. Whoever entered Ihram for Umrah alone, then circumambulated the House and went between Safa and Marwa, has completed his Umrah, and may then begin the Hajj."],[2803,"Ibn Abbas became severely ill, so he called his children and gathered them, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever performs Hajj from Makkah on foot until he returns to Makkah, Allah will record for him for every step seven hundred good deeds, each good deed like the good deeds of the Haram.' It was said to him: What are the good deeds of the Haram? He said: 'For every good deed, one hundred thousand thousand good deeds.'"],[2804,"Adam came to the House one thousand times, never riding in any of them, from India on his own two feet."],[2805,"When it was one day before the Day of Tarwiyah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would give a sermon to the people and inform them of their rites."],[2806,"He heard Jabir b. Abdullah reporting about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: He commanded us after we had circumambulated to exit Ihram. The Prophet ﷺ said: 'When you want to depart to Mina, enter Ihram.' He said: So we entered Ihram from al-Batha."],[2807,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until when we had performed tawaf around the House, he said: \"Make it an 'Umrah, except for those who have a sacrificial animal with them.\" He said: So we made it an 'Umrah. Then when it was the Day of Tarwiyah, we proclaimed the talbiyah for Hajj and set out to Mina."],[2808,"Tell me something which you comprehended from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: Where did he pray Zuhr on the Day of Tarwiyah? He said: In Mina. I said: Then where did he pray 'Asr on the Day of Nafr? He said: In al-Abtah. Then he said: Do as your leaders do."],[2809,"I met Anas b. Malik on a donkey heading to Mina on the Day of Tarwiyah. I said to him: Where did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray Zuhr on this day? He said: He prayed where your leaders pray."],[2810,"It is part of the Sunnah of Hajj—and he said once: part of the Sunnah of the imam—to pray Zuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib, 'Isha', and Subh in Mina."],[2811,"The Prophet ﷺ prayed five prayers in Mina."],[2812,"It is part of the Sunnah of Hajj that the imam prays Zuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib, the later 'Isha', and Subh in Mina, then goes forth to Arafah and takes a midday nap wherever it is decreed for him, until when the sun declines, he addresses the people, then prays Zuhr and 'Asr together, then stands at 'Arafat until the sun sets, then departs and prays at al-Muzdalifah or wherever Allah decrees, then stands at Jam' until when it becomes light, he pushes on before sunrise. Then when he stones the major Jamrah, everything that was forbidden to him becomes lawful except women and perfume, until he visits the House."],[2813,"part of the Sunnah"],[2815,"When it was the Day of Tarwiyah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted and prayed in Mina Zuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib, 'Isha', and Subh. Then he stayed a little while until the sun rose. He ordered a dome of hair for him, and it was pitched at Namirah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ traveled until he came to Arafah, and he found the dome had been pitched for him at Namirah, so he alighted there."],[2816,"A man from Quraysh said to 'Abd Allah b. 'Amr: I am weak in family and beasts of burden; our only beasts of burden are these slow-moving donkeys. May I depart from Jam' at night? He said: As for Ibrahim, he spent the night at Mina until morning came and the edge of the sun rose, then he traveled to Arafah until he alighted at his stopping place there—and Mu'ammal said: his stopping place at Arafah. They said: Then he went forth and stood at his standing place there—and Mu'ammal said: at it. They said: Until the sun set, he departed, and came to Jam'. Ziyad said: Then he alighted at his stopping place there, and Mu'ammal said: at it. They said: Then he spent the night there until when it was time for the early Subh prayer, he stood until when it was time for the bright Subh prayer, he departed. That is the way of your father Ibrahim, and your Prophet ﷺ has been commanded to follow him."],[2817,"Jibril came to Ibrahim to show him the rites—and he mentioned the hadith in full—and he said: Then he moved on with him until he stoned the Jamrah, then he said to him: \"Now you know,\" and he showed him all the rites. And he did that with the Prophet."],[2818,"We went forth with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Mina to 'Arafat; among us were those reciting the talbiyah, and among us were those saying the takbir."],[2819,"I went forth with 'Abd Allah from Mina to Arafah. 'Abd Allah was a dark-skinned man with two braids, having the appearance of the people of the desert, and he was reciting the talbiyah. A crowd of the rabble gathered around him: \"O bedouin, this is not a day for talbiyah; it is for takbir.\" He said: At that, he turned to me and said: \"Have the people become ignorant or forgotten? By the One who sent Muhammad with the truth, I went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Mina to Arafah, and he did not abandon the talbiyah until he stoned the Jamrah, except that he would mix it with tahlil or takbir.\""],[2820,"It is part of the Sunnah of Hajj that the imam prays Zuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib, the later 'Isha', and Subh in Mina, then goes forth to Arafah and takes a midday nap wherever it is decreed for him, until when the sun declines, he addresses the people, then prays Zuhr and 'Asr together, then stands at 'Arafat until the sun sets, then departs and prays at al-Muzdalifah or wherever Allah decrees, then stands at Jam' until when it becomes light, he pushes on before sunrise. Then when he stones the major Jamrah, everything that was forbidden to him becomes lawful except women and perfume, until he visits the House."],[2821,"I heard the Messenger of God ﷺ say in his sermon on the Day of Arafah during the Farewell Pilgrimage: Know that your blood, your wealth, and your honor are sacred to you, like the sanctity of this day of yours, like the sanctity of this month of yours, and like the sanctity of this land of yours."],[2822,"He gave a sermon, then Bilal called the adhan, then the iqamah, and he prayed Dhuhr, then he called the iqamah and prayed Asr, and he did not pray anything between them. Then he rode al-Qaswa until he came to the standing place."],[2823,"Abdullah b. Umar came to al-Hajjaj b. Yusuf on the Day of Arafah when the sun had declined, and I was with him, and said: Departure, if you want the Sunnah. He said: At this hour? He said: Yes. Salim said: I said to al-Hajjaj: If you want to follow the Sunnah today, then shorten the sermon and hasten the prayer. Abdullah b. Umar said: He spoke the truth."],[2824,"The Prophet ﷺ combined the two prayers: Dhuhr and Asr at Arafat with one adhan and two iqamahs, and Maghrib and Isha at Muzdalifah with one adhan and two iqamahs."],[2825,"He gave a sermon, then Bilal called the adhan, then the iqamah, and he prayed Dhuhr, then he called the iqamah and prayed Asr, and he did not pray anything between them. Then he rode al-Qaswa until he came to the standing place."],[2826,"Umar b. al-Khattab used to lead the people of Makkah in two rak'ahs, then he would say the salam, then they would stand up and complete their prayer."],[2827,"Abd al-Malik b. Marwan wrote to al-Hajjaj b. Yusuf commanding him not to contradict Ibn Umar in any matter of Hajj. When it was the Day of Arafah, Ibn Umar came to him when the sun had declined, and I was with him, and he called out beside his pavilion: Where is this one? Al-Hajjaj came out to him wearing a yellow-dyed cloak and said to him: What is the matter with you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman? He said: Departure, if you want the Sunnah. He said: Yes. Let me pour water over myself, then I will come out to you. So he waited for him until he came out, and he walked between me and my father. I said to him: If you want to follow the Sunnah, then shorten the sermon and hasten the standing. He began looking at Ibn Umar so that he might hear that from him. When Ibn Umar saw that, he said: He spoke the truth."],[2828,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Farewell Pilgrimage of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: He stood at Muzdalifah and said: \"I have stood here, and all of Muzdalifah is a place of standing.\""],[2829,"Move away from the bottom of Uranah and move away from the bottom of Muhassir."],[2830,"It used to be said: Move away from Muhassir and move away from Uranat."],[2831,"Ibn Mirba' al-Ansari came to us while we were standing at Arafah behind the standing place—a place which Amr considered far from the standing place—and he said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of God to you."],[2832,"We were standing behind the standing place, a place which Amr considered far from the imam, and Ibn Mirba' al-Ansari came to us and said: I am the messenger of the Messenger of God to you. He says to you: Remain at these ritual sites of yours, for you are upon an inheritance from the inheritance of Ibrahim. Except that Abu Ammar said: We were standing in a distant place behind the standing place, and Ibn Mirba' came to us."],[2833,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was at Jamʿ (Muzdalifah). I said, “O Messenger of God, I have come to you from the two mountains of Ṭayyiʾ; I have exhausted my riding camel and worn out myself. By God, I did not leave any hill but I stopped at it. Is there any Hajj for me?” He ﷺ said, “Whoever prays this prayer with us, stands at this standing place with us, and has already departed from ʿArafāt before that, by night or by day, his Hajj is complete and he has fulfilled his rites.”"],[2834,"I was among the first of the pilgrims. I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was at al-Muzdalifah, and he went out for the prayer when dawn broke. I said, “O Messenger of God, I have come to you from the two mountains of Ṭayyiʾ; I have exhausted my riding camel and worn out myself. I did not leave any mountain but I stopped at it.” He said, “Whoever attends the prayer with us, then stands with us until we depart, and has already stood at ʿArafāt before that, by night or by day, has fulfilled his rites and completed his Hajj.”"],[2835,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ at ʿArafah, and some people from the people of Najd came to him while they were at ʿArafah and asked him. He ordered a crier, who called out: “The Hajj is ʿArafah. Whoever comes on the night of Jamʿ before the break of dawn has caught the Hajj. The days of Minā are three; whoever hastens in two days, there is no sin upon him, and whoever delays, there is no sin upon him.” He mounted a man behind him, and he called out."],[2836,"The Quraysh used to depart only from al-Muzdalifah and they would say, “We are the ḥums, so we do not leave the sanctuary,” and they had abandoned the standing at ʿArafah. He said: I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ in the Jāhiliyyah standing with the people at ʿArafah on a camel of his, then he would arrive in the morning with his people at al-Muzdalifah and stand with them, departing when they departed."],[2837,"I was riding behind the Prophet ﷺ at ʿArafāt. He raised his hands, and his she-camel swayed with him, and her nose-rein fell. He took hold of the nose-rein with one of his hands while still raising the other hand."],[2838,"The Messenger of God ﷺ departed from ʿArafāt with Usāmah b. Zayd riding behind him. He said: The she-camel swayed with him while he was raising his hands—they did not pass above his head—until he reached Jamʿ. Then he departed from Jamʿ with al-Faḍl b. ʿAbbās riding behind him. Al-Faḍl said: He did not cease reciting the talbiyah until he threw the pebble."],[2839,"We entered upon Jābir, and I said, “Tell me about the Hajj of the Messenger of God ﷺ.” He mentioned part of the hadith and said: He mounted al-Qaṣwāʾ until he came to the standing place. He made the belly of his she-camel face the rocks, placed the path of the pedestrians before him, and faced the qiblah. He remained standing until the sun set and the yellowness departed a little when the disk had disappeared."],[2840,"There is no day on which God sets free more servants from the Fire than the Day of ʿArafah. He draws near, then boasts to the angels and says, “What do these want?”"],[2841,"Some people disputed in the presence of Umm al-Faḍl on the Day of ʿArafah about the Messenger of God ﷺ. Some of them said, “He is fasting,” and others said, “He is not fasting.” So Umm al-Faḍl sent a bowl of milk while he was standing on his camel, and he drank it—he was that day at ʿArafah."],[2842,"“I shall send him a drink. If he is not fasting, he will not refuse it.” She sent him a bowl of milk, and he ﷺ drank while the people were looking—meaning on the Day of ʿArafah."],[2843,"We were with Ibn 'Abbas at 'Arafah, and he said to me: O Sa'id, why do I not hear the people reciting the talbiyah? So I said: They fear Mu'awiyah. He said: Then Ibn 'Abbas came out of his tent and said: Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, for they have abandoned the Sunnah out of hatred for 'Ali."],[2844,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at 'Arafat, and when he said: Labbayk Allahumma labbayk, he said: Indeed, all good is the good of the Hereafter."],[2845,"I was riding behind the Prophet ﷺ when he departed from Muzdalifah, and a Bedouin was traveling alongside him, with his beautiful daughter riding behind him. Al-Fadl said: I began looking at her, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took hold of my face and turned me away from her. He did not cease reciting the talbiyah until he threw the pebbles at Jamrat al-'Aqabah."],[2847,"Al-Fadl b. 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the Day of 'Arafah, and the young man began glancing at the women, with a similar narration, except that he said: He turned his face, and he did not say: O son of my brother."],[2848,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered a caller during his Hajj, and he called at the time of the sun's decline: Perform ghusl. And he mentioned the hadith in full, and he said: When it was the Day of Tarwiyah, he ordered a caller, and he called: Enter into ihram for Hajj. And he ordered that the sacrificial camels be stopped at 'Arafah, and at all the sacred rites."],[2849,"My two eyes beheld my beloved, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, standing at 'Arafat on a red camel of his, short and sturdy, with a Bolani velvet cloth beneath him, and he was saying: O Allah, make it a Hajj with no showing off, no ostentation, and no seeking of reputation."],[2850,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at 'Arafah, then departed when the sun had set, and he mounted Usamah b. Zayd behind him."],[2851,"The people of the Jahiliyyah used to stand at 'Arafah until when the sun was on the tops of the mountains like turbans on the heads of men, they would move on. And they would stand at Muzdalifah until when the sun rose and was on the tops of the mountains like turbans on the heads of men, they would move on. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the departure from 'Arafah until the sun had set, then he prayed the morning prayer at Muzdalifah when dawn broke, then he departed when everything had become clear in the latter time before the sun rose."],[2852,"Indeed, Allah boasts to the people of heaven about the people of 'Arafat, and says to them: Look at My servants, they have come to Me disheveled and dusty."],[2853,"When it is the Day of 'Arafah, Allah descends to the lowest heaven and boasts about them to the angels, saying: Look at My servants, they have come to Me disheveled and dusty, having come from every deep mountain pass. I call you to witness that I have forgiven them. The angels say: O Lord, among them is so-and-so who is conceited, and so-and-so and so-and-so. He said: Allah says: I have forgiven them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There is no day on which more people are freed from the Fire than the Day of 'Arafah."],[2854,"The most frequent supplication of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the evening of 'Arafah was: O Allah, to You belongs praise, as we say, and better than what we say. O Allah, to You belongs my prayer, my sacrifice, my life, and my death. To You is my return, and to You, O Lord, belongs my inheritance. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the punishment of the grave, from the whispering of the heart, and from the scattering of affairs. O Allah, I ask You for the good of what the wind brings, and I seek refuge in You from the evil of what the wind brings."],[2855,"Jibril came to Ibrahim to show him the rites. He prayed with him Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha, and Subh at Mina. Then he went with him to Arafah and prayed with him Zuhr and Asr at Arafah. He made him stand at the standing place until the sun set. Then he departed with him and prayed with him Maghrib, Isha, and Subh at Muzdalifah. Then he made him spend the night there. Then he departed with him until he threw the pebbles. He said to him: 'Recognize now.' He showed him all the rites. And he did that with the Prophet."],[2856,"The Messenger of God ﷺ said on the evening of Arafah and the morning of Jam', when they departed for the people: 'You must be calm.' He was restraining his she-camel."],[2857,"The Prophet ﷺ took him as a rider behind him when he departed from Arafah. He departed calmly and said: 'O people, you must be calm, for righteousness is not in urging horses and camels.' He said: I did not see his she-camel raise her hand until he reached Jam'. Then he took al-Fadl as a rider behind him and commanded the people to be calm. He departed with calmness and said: 'Righteousness is not in urging horses and camels.' I did not see his she-camel raise her hand until he reached Mina."],[2858,"I heard Usamah, while he was beside me—and he was the rider behind the Prophet ﷺ from Arafah—being asked: How was the Messenger of God ﷺ traveling when he departed from Arafah? He said: He was traveling at a brisk pace, and when he found an opening, he urged his mount."],[2859,"When his mount stood straight at the mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah in a Hajj or Umrah, the Messenger of God ﷺ entered ihram—and he mentioned the hadith—and he said: He stood—meaning at Arafah—until the sun set. Then he proceeded, remembering God, glorifying Him, declaring His oneness, and praising Him until he reached Muzdalifah."],[2860,"When the Prophet ﷺ departed from Arafah, he took him as a rider behind him that evening. When he reached the mountain pass, he dismounted and urinated—he did not say 'poured water'—and I poured water for him from a waterskin, and he performed a light ablution. We said: The prayer. He said: 'The prayer is ahead of you.' When we reached Muzdalifah, he prayed Maghrib. Then they unloaded their baggage, and I helped him with it. Then he prayed Isha."],[2861,"The Messenger of God ﷺ prayed Maghrib and Isha together at Muzdalifah."],[2862,"The Messenger of God ﷺ combined Maghrib and Isha at Jam' with no prayer between them. He prayed Maghrib as three rak'ahs, then prayed Isha as two rak'ahs. And Abdullah used to pray at Jam' likewise until he met God."],[2863,"I departed from Arafat with the Messenger of God ﷺ. When he reached the mountain pass where the commanders dismount, he urinated and performed ablution. I said: O Messenger of God, the prayer. He said: 'The prayer is ahead of you.' When he reached Jam', he gave the adhan and the iqamah, then prayed Maghrib. Then the last of the people had not yet finished unloading before he gave the iqamah and prayed Isha."],[2864,"The Messenger of God ﷺ departed from Arafah and took Usamah as a rider behind him. When he reached the mountain pass, he dismounted and urinated—he did not say 'poured water.' Usamah said: I poured water for him from the waterskin, and he performed a light ablution. I said: The prayer, O Messenger of God. He said: 'The prayer is ahead of you.' Then he came to Muzdalifah and prayed Maghrib. Then he placed his saddle, then prayed Isha."],[2865,"Abdullah b. Mas'ud departed from Arafat at his own pace, not striking his camel, until he reached Jam'. He dismounted, gave the adhan and the iqamah, then prayed Maghrib. Then he ate dinner. Then he stood, gave the adhan and the iqamah, and prayed Isha. Then he spent the night at Jam' until dawn rose. He stood, gave the adhan and the iqamah, then prayed Subh. Then he said: 'These two prayers are delayed from their times. The Messenger of God ﷺ did not pray them on this day except in this place.' Then he stood."],[2866,"Tell me about the Hajj of the Prophet ﷺ. He said: He came to Muzdalifah and combined Maghrib and Isha with one adhan and two iqamahs. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ lay down until dawn rose."],[2867,"I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray any prayer except at its time, apart from these two prayers: I saw him pray Maghrib and Isha together at Muzdalifah, and he prayed Fajr before its time in the dark."],[2868,"He prayed Fajr when dawn became clear to him – meaning at Muzdalifah."],[2869,"When his mount stood upright with him at the mosque of Dhul-Hulayfah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered ihram – and he mentioned the hadith – and he said: He spent the night – meaning at Muzdalifah – until morning, then prayed the morning prayer, then stood at al-Mash'ar al-Haram, and the people stood with him, supplicating Allah, remembering Him, declaring His oneness, glorifying Him, and magnifying Him, until he departed to Mina."],[2870,"We came to Jabir b. Abdullah and asked him about the Farewell Pilgrimage of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He said: He stood at Muzdalifah and said: \"I have stood here, and all of Muzdalifah is a place of standing.\""],[2871,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at Jam', and said: \"All of Jam' is a place of standing.\""],[2872,"The polytheists would not depart from Jam' until the sun had risen on Thabir, so the Prophet ﷺ differed from them and departed before the sun rose."],[2873,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed from Arafah and from Jam' with tranquility until he reached Mina."],[2874,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastened in the valley of Muhassir."],[2875,"The beginning of hastening was from the people of the desert: they would stand at the edges of the people, having hung up wooden bowls, staffs, and leather water-skins. When they departed, these rattled, startling the people. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seen while the forepart of his she-camel's hump was touching the ground, and he was saying: 'O people, keep calm! O people, keep calm!'"],[2876,"Then he took the middle road that brings you out to the great jamrah until he reached the jamrah that is near the tree."],[2877,"There are no days in which righteous deeds are more beloved to Allah than these days – meaning the ten days. They said: 'Not even jihad in the way of Allah?' He said: 'Not even jihad in the way of Allah, except a man who goes out with his own life and wealth, then returns with none of that.'"],[2878,"The greatest day with Allah is the Day of Sacrifice, then the Day of Rest."],[2879,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me on the morning of al-‘Aqabah — Ibn Abī ‘Adī said in his ḥadīth, and so did ‘Awf: “Fetch me pebbles,” that is, small pebbles for throwing. When they were placed in his hand, he said: “With the like of these, with the like of these. And beware of exaggeration in religion, for those before you were destroyed only by exaggeration in religion.”"],[2880,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me on the morning of al-‘Aqabah: “Fetch me some pebbles.”"],[2881,"Sawdah was a large, heavy woman, so she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission to depart from Jam‘ at night, and he permitted her. ‘Ā’ishah said: “Would that I had asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for permission as Sawdah asked.” ‘Ā’ishah would not depart except with the imām."],[2882,"I was among those whom the Prophet ﷺ sent ahead on the night of Muzdalifah among the weak members of his family."],[2883,"He used to send ahead the weak members of his family, and they would stand at al-Mash‘ar al-Ḥarām at night, remembering Allah as long as they wished, then they would depart. Some of them would reach Minā for the dawn prayer, and some would arrive after that. Those were the weak members of his family. He said: “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted that.”"],[2884,"I was among those whom the Prophet ﷺ sent ahead with the luggage."],[2885,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed, and when he descended into the valley of Muḥassir, he said: “O people, use small pebbles for throwing,” and he gestured with his hand like a man throwing small pebbles."],[2886,"I performed Ḥajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When we stood at ‘Arafāt, I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placing one finger over the other, so I said to my uncle: “O uncle, what is he saying?” He said: “He is saying: ‘Throw the jamarāt with the like of small throwing pebbles.’”"],[2887,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stoned the Jamrah with the like of small throwing pebbles."],[2888,"The Prophet ﷺ would throw the pebbles on the Day of Sacrifice in the forenoon. Ibn Ma‘mar added: one — meaning one Jamrah — and they said: As for after that, it is when the sun declines."],[2889,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ throwing pebbles while mounted on his riding camel on the Day of Sacrifice, and he said to us: “Take your rites from me, for I do not know whether I will perform Ḥajj after this Ḥajj of mine.”"],[2890,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ throwing the pebbles on the Day of Sacrifice on a reddish camel, with no striking, no driving away, and no [saying] 'to you, to you'."],[2891,"I heard al-Hajjaj say: Do not say 'Surah al-Baqarah'; say 'the surah in which the cow is mentioned.' I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: 'Abd al-Rahman b. Yazid told me that he was with 'Abd Allah when he threw Jamrat al-'Aqabah. He entered the interior of the valley, then he faced it—meaning the Jamrah—and threw it with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble. I said, 'Some people climb the mountain.' He said, 'Here, by the One besides whom there is no god, I saw the one upon whom Surah al-Baqarah was sent down throw.'"],[2892,"He performed Hajj with 'Abd Allah, and he threw the Jamrah with seven pebbles, placing the House on his left and Mina on his right, and said, 'This is the station of the one upon whom Surah al-Baqarah was sent down.' Al-Za'farani did not say 'he performed Hajj with 'Abd Allah,' and he said, ''Abd Allah threw the Jamrah.'"],[2893,"I was riding behind [the Messenger of Allah] ﷺ, and he did not cease saying the talbiyah until he threw Jamrat al-'Aqabah. He threw it with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble."],[2894,"The tawaf around the House, and between al-Safa and al-Marwah, and the throwing of the pebbles have only been prescribed for the establishment of the remembrance of Allah."],[2895,"He used to send ahead the weak members of his family, and some of them would arrive at Mina for the Fajr prayer, and some would arrive after that. When they arrived, they would throw the Jamrah. Ibn 'Umar used to say, 'The Messenger of Allah gave concession to those ones.'"],[2896,"Asma' stopped on the night of Jam' at the abode of al-Muzdalifah, and she stood to pray. She said, 'O my son, get up and see if the moon has set.' I said, 'No.' She prayed, then she said, 'O my son, see if the moon has set.' I said, 'Yes.' She said, 'Depart.' So we departed and threw the Jamrah, then she prayed the morning prayer at her dwelling. He said: I said to her, 'O dear woman, we threw the Jamrah at night.' She said, 'We used to do this with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' This is the hadith of Bundar. And Ibn Ma'mar said..."],[2897,"The Prophet ﷺ did not cease saying the talbiyah until he threw the Jamrah."],[2898,"I observed the Prophet ﷺ, and he did not cease saying the talbiyah until he threw Jamrat al-'Aqabah with the first pebble."],[2899,"I departed with the Prophet ﷺ from 'Arafat, and he did not cease saying the talbiyah until he threw Jamrat al-'Aqabah, saying takbir with each pebble. Then he discontinued the talbiyah with the last pebble."],[2900,"When his mount stood him upright at the mosque of Dhu al-Hulayfah for a Hajj or 'Umrah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would enter ihram. Then he mentioned the hadith in full, and said: He would come to Jamrat al-'Aqabah and throw it with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, and he would not stop, then he would depart."],[2901,"Then the Prophet ﷺ came to the Jamrah and threw it, then he came to the place of sacrifice and said, 'This is the place of sacrifice, and all of Mina is a place of sacrifice.'"],[2902,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered at Mina. He said: And all of Mina is a place of slaughter."],[2903,"He said - meaning a man - \"O Prophet of Allah, shall we not build a structure at Mina to provide you shade?\" He said: \"No. Mina is the stopping place of whoever arrives first.\""],[2904,"So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered with his own hand sixty-three - meaning camels - then he gave 'Ali the remainder, and he slaughtered what was left."],[2905,"I saw Ibn 'Umar come upon a man who had made his camel kneel at Mina in order to slaughter it, and he said: \"Make it stand, hobbled. That is the Sunnah of Muhammad.\""],[2906,"And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered with his own hand seven camels while they were standing."],[2907,"He used to sacrifice two horned rams with black and white markings, pronouncing the Name of Allah and saying the takbir. I saw him slaughter them with his own hand, placing his foot on their sides."],[2908,"I heard Anas b. Malik say it, and I said to him: \"Did you hear it directly?\" He said: \"Yes. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to sacrifice, with the like of it.\""],[2909,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered as hadi the camel of Abu Jahl among his sacrificial animals in the year of Hudaybiyyah, and on its head was a nose ring of silver. Abu Jahl had been stripped of it at the Battle of Badr."],[2910,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered in the year of Hudaybiyyah among his sacrificial animals a camel that had belonged to Abu Jahl, with a silver nose ring on its head, in order to anger the polytheists by that."],[2911,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered two rams on the day of 'Id, then said when he had turned them toward the qiblah: \"Indeed, I have turned my face toward the One who created the heavens and the earth, as a hanif, and I am not of the polytheists. Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my living, and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds. He has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am the first of the Muslims. In the Name of Allah, and Allah is Greater. O Allah, from You and for You, from Muhammad and his Ummah.\""],[2912,"We shared with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Hajj and 'Umrah, every seven of us in one camel."],[2913,"We slaughtered with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the year of Hudaybiyyah a camel on behalf of seven, and a cow on behalf of seven."],[2914,"We were performing tamattuʿ during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — and Bundār said: He said: We performed tamattuʿ with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — and we would slaughter a cow on behalf of seven, sharing in it."],[2915,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered on behalf of those of his wives who performed ʿumrah during the Farewell Hajj a cow among them."],[2916,"When we were at Mina, I was brought beef. I said, “What is this?” They said, “This is the meat of a cow that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered on behalf of his wives as a sacrifice.”"],[2917,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a cow on behalf of his wives as a sacrifice. This is the wording of ʿAbd al-Jabbār and ʿAlī. As for Abū Mūsā, he said: The Prophet ﷺ said to her — when she menstruated at Sarif before entering Makkah — “Perform what a pilgrim performs, except that you should not circumambulate the House.” She said: When we were at Mina, I was brought beef. I said, “What is this?” They said, “The Messenger of Allah ﷺ slaughtered a cow on behalf of his wives as a sacrifice.”"],[2918,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out in the year of Hudaybiyyah intending to visit the House, not intending to fight, and he drove with him seventy camels as hady. The people were seven hundred men, so each camel was on behalf of ten individuals."],[2919,"That the Prophet ﷺ set out in the year of Hudaybiyyah with some ten-plus hundred of his companions. When he was at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah, he garlanded the hady and marked it, then entered iḥrām from there."],[2920,"We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and the time of sacrifice came, so we shared in a cow: seven, and in a camel: ten."],[2923,"ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb gave as hady a noble she-camel of his for which he was offered three hundred dinars. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, “O Messenger of Allah, I have given a noble she-camel as hady, and I have been offered three hundred dinars for it. Shall I sell it and buy with its price camels, then slaughter them?” He said, “No, slaughter it as it is.”"],[2924,"He said: Tell me what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked or prohibited among sacrificial animals. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, gesturing with his hand like this — and my hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — “Four will not suffice in sacrificial animals: the one-eyed whose blindness is evident, the sick whose sickness is evident, the lame whose lameness is evident, and the emaciated that has no marrow.” He said: I also dislike that there be a defect in the ear or the horn. He said: What you dislike, leave it, but do not prohibit it to others."],[2925,"He prohibited sacrificing one with a broken horn or ear."],[2926,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to examine the eyes and ears."],[2927,"A man asked ʿAli about a cow. He said: 'From seven.' He said: 'The horn?' He said: 'It does not harm you.' He said: 'Lameness?' He said: 'If it can reach the place of sacrifice.' He said: 'The Messenger of God ﷺ commanded us to examine the eyes and ears.'"],[2928,"The Messenger of God ﷺ distributed sacrificial animals among his Companions. ʿUqbah said: A jadhaʿah fell to my lot. I said: O Messenger of God, a jadhaʿah fell to my lot. He said: \"Sacrifice it.\""],[2929,"\"The greatest day in the sight of God is the Day of Sacrifice, then the Day of Rest.\" Five or six camels were brought to the Prophet ﷺ, and they began to draw near to see which of them he would start with. When their sides fell, he said a light word I did not understand. I asked someone near him, and he said: [He said]: \"Whoever wishes may cut off.\""],[2930,"Do not slaughter except a musinnah, unless it is difficult for you, then slaughter a jadhaʿah from sheep."],[2931,"The Prophet ﷺ ordered me to take charge of his sacrificial camels and to give charity with their skins and coverings. I think he said: and their meat."],[2932,"The Prophet ﷺ ordered him to take charge of his sacrificial camels, and ordered him to distribute all of his sacrificial camels—their meat, skins, and coverings—to the poor, and not to give anything from them for the butcher's fee."],[2934,"The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered me to take charge of his sacrificial camels and ordered me not to give the butcher anything from them."],[2935,"The Prophet ﷺ ordered him to take charge of the sacrificial camels and ordered him not to give the butcher anything from their butchering."],[2936,"The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered a piece from every slaughtered camel, so it was put into a pot and cooked, and they ate from the meat and drank from the broth."],[2937,"She drove two sacrificial camels and lost them, so Ibn al-Zubayr sent her two camels and she slaughtered them. Then she found the first two camels and slaughtered them as well. Then she said: This is the sunnah regarding sacrificial camels."],[2938,"People were talking a lot, so we set out intending the Ḥajj until there were only a few nights between us and our exiting the state of iḥrām. We were ordered to exit. One of us would go to ʿArafah while his private part was dripping semen. That reached the Messenger of God ﷺ, so he stood as a speaker and said: \"Do you teach me about God, O people? By God, I am more knowledgeable about God than you and more God-fearing. If I were to face what I have left behind, I would not have driven a sacrificial animal, and I would have exited the state of iḥrām as they did. Whoever does not have a sacrificial animal with him should fast three days and seven when he returns to his family. Whoever finds a sacrificial animal should slaughter it.\" And we would slaughter a camel on behalf of seven."],[2939,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ distributed sheep among his companions that day, and Sa'd b. Abi Waqqas received a young he-goat, which he slaughtered for himself. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at 'Arafah, he commanded Rabi'ah b. Umayyah b. Khalaf, who stood beneath the forelegs of his she-camel, and the Prophet ﷺ said to him: 'Call out: O people, do you know which month this is?' They said: 'The sacred month.' He said: 'Do you know which land this is?' They said: 'The sacred land.' He said: 'Do you know which day this is?' They said: 'The greater Hajj.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Allah has made your blood and your property sacred like the sanctity of this month of yours, like the sanctity of this land of yours, and like the sanctity of this day of yours.' Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed his Hajj, and when he stood at 'Arafah he said: 'This is the standing place, and all of 'Arafah is a standing place.' And when he stood at Quzah he said: 'This is the standing place, and all of Muzdalifah is a standing place.'"],[2940,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw the pebbles and slaughtered his sacrificial animal, he handed the barber his right side, and he shaved it. Then he handed it to Abu Talhah. Then he handed him the left side, and he shaved it, and he handed it to Abu Talhah, and he instructed him to distribute it among the people."],[2941,"O Allah, forgive the shavers. They said: And those who shorten. He said: 'O Allah, forgive the shavers.' They said: And those who shorten. He said it three times, then he said: 'And those who shorten.'"],[2942,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ shaved during the farewell Hajj, and they claimed that the one who shaved the Prophet ﷺ was Ma'mar b. 'Abd Allah b. Nadlah b. 'Awf b. 'Ubayd b. 'Uwayj b. 'Adi b. Ka'b."],[2943,"His father attended the Prophet ﷺ at the slaughtering place, he and a man from the Ansar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ shaved his head in his garment, and gave it to him, and he distributed some of it among men, and he trimmed his nails and gave them to his companion. He said: It is with us, dyed with henna and katam, or with katam and henna."],[2944,"Abu Salamah narrated to us that Muhammad b. 'Abd Allah b. Zayd narrated to him that his father narrated to him. Al-Darimi said: Then he mentioned the story. Abu Bakr said: No one said 'that his father narrated to him' except 'Abd al-Samad."],[2945,"I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with these two hands of mine for his ihram when he entered ihram, and for his exiting ihram before he circumambulated the House."],[2946,"I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Mina before he visited the House."],[2948,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: Then I menstruated, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came in to me while I was weeping. He said: What is the matter with you, have you begun menstruating? I said: Yes. He said: 'This is a matter Allah has decreed for the daughters of Adam, so perform what the pilgrim performs, except that you should not circumambulate the House.'"],[2949,"When you have thrown the pebbles and shaved, perfume and clothing become lawful for you, except for intercourse."],[2950,"I perfumed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ is more deserving of being followed."],[2951,"When a man throws the pebbles at the Jamrah with seven pebbles, sacrifices, and shaves, then everything is permissible for him except women and perfume."],[2952,"That 'Abbad b. 'Abdullah entered upon A'ishah bt. 'Abd al-Rahman while she had a slave girl combing her hair on the Day of Sacrifice. She had menstruated on the day they arrived in Makkah and had not performed tawaf of the House before 'Arafah. She had entered ihram for Hajj, departed from 'Arafat, and thrown the pebbles at the Jamrah. 'Abbad entered upon her while she was combing her hair and applying perfume. 'Abbad said, 'Are you applying perfume when you have not performed tawaf of the House?' A'ishah said, 'She has thrown the Jamrah and cut her hair.' He said, 'Even so, it is not permissible for her.' A'ishah disapproved of that and sent to 'Urwah and asked him about it. He said, 'Perfume is not permissible for anyone who has not performed tawaf before 'Arafat, even if they have shortened their hair and thrown the pebbles.'"],[2953,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the ifadah on the Day of Sacrifice, then returned and prayed Zuhr in Mina. Nafi' said: Ibn 'Umar used to perform the ifadah on the Day of Sacrifice, then return and pray Zuhr - meaning in Mina - and he mentioned that the Prophet ﷺ did that."],[2954,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit the House, circumambulate it seven times, pray two rak'ahs, and then women became permissible for him."],[2955,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not do ramal in the seven circuits of the ifadah. 'Ata' said: There is no ramal in it."],[2956,"Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed the ifadah to the House - meaning on the Day of Sacrifice - and came to the Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib while they were providing water at Zamzam. He said, 'Draw water, Banu 'Abd al-Muttalib. Were it not that people would overpower you in your water provision, I would draw with you.' They handed him a bucket and he drank from it."],[2957,"That the Prophet ﷺ drank a bucket of Zamzam water while standing."],[2958,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the water provision area and asked for water. Al-'Abbas said, 'O Fadl, go to your mother and bring the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a drink from her.' He said, 'Give me to drink.' He said, 'O Messenger of Allah, they put their hands in it.' He said, 'Give me to drink.' So he drank from it. Then he came to Zamzam while they were providing water and working there. He said, 'Work, for you are upon a righteous deed.' Then he said, 'Were it not that you would be overpowered, I would draw until I placed the rope upon this' - meaning his shoulder - and he pointed to his shoulder."],[2959,"A bedouin came to the water provision area and drank some nabidh. He said, 'What is the matter with the people of this house that they serve nabidh, while their cousins serve milk and honey? Is it out of stinginess or need?' Ibn 'Abbas said, and this was after he had lost his sight: 'Bring the man to me.' So he was brought. He said, 'We have no need and no stinginess, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque while he was on his camel, behind him Usamah b. Zayd, and he asked for water, so we gave him nabidh to drink and he drank. Then he handed the remainder to Usamah and said, \"You have done well and acted beautifully. Do likewise.\" So we do not wish to change that.'"],[2960,"We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Farewell Pilgrimage. She said: Those who entered ihram for 'Umrah performed tawaf of the House and between Safa and Marwah, then exited ihram. Then they performed another tawaf after they returned from Mina for their Hajj."],[2961,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ on the Day of Sacrifice and said, 'I shaved before I slaughtered.' He said, 'Slaughter, and there is no harm.' He said, 'And I slaughtered before I threw the pebbles.' He said, 'Throw, and there is no harm.' Al-Makhzumi said in his hadith: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'I shaved before I slaughtered,' and he replied similarly. Then another asked him and said, 'I sacrificed before I threw the pebbles.'"],[2962,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was being asked on the day of Mina and he would say, 'No harm, no harm.' A man asked him and said, 'I shaved before I slaughtered.' He said, 'No harm.' He said, 'I threw after evening came.' He said, 'No harm.'"],[2963,"While the Prophet ﷺ was delivering a sermon on the Day of Sacrifice, a man stood up to him and said: O Messenger of God! I did not think that such-and-such comes before such-and-such. Then another said: O Messenger of God, I did not think that such comes before such — for these three. He said: Do it, and there is no harm."],[2964,"The Messenger of God ﷺ delivered a sermon on the Day of Sacrifice."],[2965,"I saw the Messenger of God ﷺ at Mina addressing the people while he was on his she-camel al-ʿAḍbāʾ, and I was riding behind my father."],[2966,"The Messenger of God ﷺ departed, then he wanted from Ṣafiyya what a man wants from his wife. It was said: She is menstruating. The Messenger of God ﷺ said: Is she holding us back? They said: She has already departed. So the Messenger of God ﷺ departed with her."],[2967,"I witnessed the Prophet ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage while he was delivering a sermon. A man came to him and said: He forgot to stone. He said: Stone, and there is no harm. Then another came to him and said: He forgot to circumambulate. The Prophet ﷺ said: Circumambulate, and there is no harm. Then another came to him and said: I forgot to sacrifice. He said: Sacrifice, and there is no harm. He was not asked about anything that day except that he said: No harm. And he said: God has eliminated harm except for a man who borrowed from a Muslim — that is harm."],[2968,"The Messenger of God ﷺ departed on the last day when he prayed the noon prayer, then he returned and stayed at Mina during the nights of the Days of Tashrīq, stoning the Jamrah when the sun declined, each Jamrah with seven pebbles, saying Allāhu akbar with each pebble. He stood at the first and at the second, prolonging the standing and supplicating humbly, then he stoned the third and did not stand at it."],[2969,"The Prophet ﷺ gave permission to al-ʿAbbās b. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib: he sought permission from the Prophet of God ﷺ to spend the nights of Mina in Mecca because of his providing water, so he granted him permission."],[2970,"When it was my night in which the Messenger of God ﷺ came to me in the evening of the Day of Sacrifice, he came to me. She said: Wahb entered upon me and with him were men from the family of Abū Umayya wearing shirts. She said: The Messenger of God ﷺ said to Wahb: Have you departed yet, O Abū ʿAbd Allāh? He said: No, by God, O Messenger of God. He said: Take off the shirt. So he removed it from his head. He said: And his companion removed his shirt from his head. They said: Why, O Messenger of God? He said: This is a day in which it has been permitted for you, when you have stoned the Jamrah, to exit from everything you were forbidden, except women. But if you reach evening before you circumambulate this House, you become as you were before stoning the Jamrah."],[2971,"I attended the festival with ʿUmar b. al-Khaṭṭāb, and he said: The Messenger of God ﷺ forbade fasting on these two days. As for the Day of Fiṭr, it is your breaking fast from your fasting; and as for the Day of Sacrifice, you eat on it from the meat of your sacrifices."],[2972,"The Messenger of God ﷺ ordered him to proclaim during the Days of Tashrīq — and al-Makhzūmī said: He sent him during the days of Mina to proclaim: None will enter Paradise except a believing soul, and they are days of eating and drinking."],[2973,"I entered with ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr b. al-ʿĀṣ upon his father during the Days of Tashrīq, and he was having lunch. He invited us to food, but ʿAbd Allāh b. ʿAmr said to him: I am fasting. ʿAmr said to him: Do you not know that these are the days in which the Messenger of God ﷺ forbade fasting and commanded breaking the fast? So he ordered them, and they broke their fast."],[2974,"Uthman prayed four (rak'ahs) at Mina. Abdullah said: I prayed two rak'ahs with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and two rak'ahs with Abu Bakr, and two rak'ahs with Umar, then the ways diverged for you. I wish that instead of four rak'ahs I had two accepted rak'ahs."],[2975,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, and Umar prayed two rak'ahs at Mina, and Uthman at the beginning of his rule."],[2978,"The greatest of days in the sight of Allah is the Day of Sacrifice, then the Day of Qarr."],[2979,"Gabriel came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and took him to show him the rites. Thabir opened up for him and he entered Mina and showed him the Jamarat, then he showed him Arafat. Satan appeared to the Prophet ﷺ at the first Jamrah, so he threw seven pebbles at him until he sank, then he appeared to him at the second Jamrah, so he threw seven pebbles at him until he sank, then he appeared to him at Jamrat al-Aqabah, so he threw seven pebbles at him until he sank and went away."],[2980,"The Prophet ﷺ would stone on the Day of Sacrifice in the forenoon, and after that after the sun's decline."],[2981,"Ata' said: I do not stone until the sun has risen."],[2982,"Tawaf around the House, and between Safa and Marwah, and stoning the Jamarat were only made for the establishment of the remembrance of Allah."],[2983,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed at the end of his day when he prayed Zuhr, then returned and stayed at Mina during the nights of the Days of Tashriq, throwing the Jamrah when the sun declined, each Jamrah with seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, and he stood at the first and second and prolonged the standing and supplicated, then threw the third and did not stand at it."],[2984,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw the Jamrah that is next to the mosque of Mina, he threw seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, then he went forward ahead of it and stood facing the House with his hands raised supplicating, and he would prolong the standing. Then he came to the second Jamrah and threw seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, then he descended to the left towards the valley and stood facing the qiblah with his hands raised supplicating. Then he came to the Jamrah at al-Aqabah and threw seven pebbles, saying takbir with each pebble, then he left and did not stand at it."],[2985,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave us a sermon on the Day of Heads and said: \"What land is this?\" We said: \"Allah and His Messenger know best.\" He said: \"Is it not the Sacred Monument?\" We said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"What day is this?\" We said: \"Allah and His Messenger know best.\" He said: \"Is it not the middle of the Days of Tashriq?\" We said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Indeed your blood\"—Ishaq added: \"and your honors,\" and they both said: \"and your wealth are forbidden to you like the sanctity of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours\"—Ishaq added: \"Let the nearest of you convey to the farthest of you. O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed?\""],[2986,"When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the Umrah of al-Ji'ranah, he sent Abu Bakr on the Hajj. We set out with him until when we were at al-Arj, he called for the dawn prayer. When he stood to say takbir, he heard the sound of camels behind him, so he paused from the takbir... [He mentioned the hadith in full]. When it was the first day of departure, Abu Bakr stood and addressed the people, telling them how to depart and how to stone, teaching them their rites. When he finished, Ali stood and recited Bara'ah to the people until he completed it."],[2987,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted a concession to shepherds to stone at night and to combine the stoning."],[2988,"The Prophet ﷺ granted concession to the shepherds to stone one day and leave off one day."],[2989,"Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allah b. Abi Bakr, from ʿAbd al-Malik b. Abi Bakr, from Abu al-Baddah, from his father, a similar hadith."],[2990,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted concession to the shepherds to stone the jamarat one day and leave off one day."],[2991,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ granted concession to camel herders regarding the overnight stay: they stone on the Day of Sacrifice, then they stone the next day or the day after for two days, then they stone on the Day of Departure."],[2992,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha, and slept a short sleep at al-Muhassab, then rode to the House and circumambulated it."],[2993,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us while we were at Mina: \"Tomorrow we will alight at Khayf Bani Kinanah.\""],[2994,"Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ said something similar."],[2995,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, when he wanted to depart from Mina: \"Tomorrow we will alight, if Allah wills, at Khayf Bani Kinanah, where they swore together on disbelief.\""],[2996,"I said: \"O Messenger of Allah, where will you alight tomorrow?\" — that was during his Hajj. He said: \"Has ʿAqil left us any place?\" Then he said: \"Tomorrow we will alight at Khayf Bani Kinanah, where Quraysh swore on disbelief, because Bani Kinanah allied with Quraysh against Bani Hashim not to intermarry with them, not to trade with them, and not to give them refuge.\""],[2997,"I pitched the tent of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at al-Abtah, and he did not command me to alight at al-Abtah. Then he came and alighted."],[2998,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only alighted at al-Muhassab so that it would be easier for his departure. Whoever wished, alighted there, and whoever wished, left it."],[2999,"Stopping at al-Muhassab is not part of the Sunnah; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ only stopped there so that it would be easier for his departure."],[3000,"al-Muhassab is nothing; it is only a stopping place where the Messenger of Allah stopped."],[3001,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abu Bakr, Umar, and Uthman used to stop at al-Abtah."],[3002,"Ma'mar reported to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, something similar."],[3004,"That [the Prophet of Allah] ﷺ stopped at al-Batha' on the evening of departure, and that Abu Bakr and Umar used to do that, and Ibn Umar used to do it until he died, and he prayed there Zuhr, Asr, Maghrib, and Isha."],[3005,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray the Asr prayer at al-Abtah as two rak'ahs."],[3006,"I came to the Prophet ﷺ at al-Abtah while he was in a red dome of his. He said: Then Bilal came out with the leftover water of his ablution, and some people got some of it and others were sprinkled with it. Then Bilal called the adhan, and I began to follow his mouth like this and like this, meaning right and left. He said: Then a short spear was planted for him, and the Prophet ﷺ came out wearing a red jubbah or a red hullah of his, and it is as if I am looking at the gleam of his shins. He prayed facing the short spear, Zuhr or Asr, two rak'ahs, while a woman, a donkey, and a dog passed beyond it without being prevented. Then he continued to pray two rak'ahs until he reached Madinah."],[3007,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from Madinah to Makkah, and he used to pray with us two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until we returned to Madinah. He said: I said to him: Did you stay in Makkah for any length of time? He said: We stayed there for ten days."],[3008,"I met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ setting out in the latter part of the night from al-Abtah, while he was going up and I was coming down, or he was coming down and I was going up."],[3009,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he mentioned the hadith in full, and he said in the report: Then he announced the departure among his companions – meaning from al-Muhassab – and the people departed. He passed by the House before the morning prayer, circumambulated it, then went out, mounted, and then left heading toward Madinah."],[3010,"The people were commanded that their last contact should be with the House."],[3011,"The people used to disperse in every direction, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: None of you should depart until his last contact is with the House."],[3012,"Whoever performs Hajj should make his last covenant with the House, except menstruating women; for the Messenger of God ﷺ granted them a concession."],[3013,"That Safiyyah menstruated, and that was mentioned to the Messenger of God ﷺ, so he said: 'Is she holding us back?' I said: 'She menstruated after she had performed the ifadah.' He said: 'Then no; let her depart.'"],[3014,"You were only commanded to perform tawaf, and you were not commanded to enter it. He said: He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him say..."],[3015,"That he and the Messenger of God ﷺ entered the House, and he ordered Bilal to close the door. At that time the House was on six pillars. He went until he came to the two columns that are next to the door, the door of the Ka'bah. He praised God and extolled Him, asked Him, and sought forgiveness. Then he stood until he came to what faced the back of the Ka'bah, and he placed his face and his body against the Ka'bah. He praised God and extolled Him and sought forgiveness. Then he turned to each corner of the corners of the Ka'bah and faced it with takbir, tahlil, and tasbih; he praised God and extolled Him with petition and seeking forgiveness. Then he came out and prayed two rak'ahs facing the front of the Ka'bah, outside the House, and said: 'This is the qiblah, this is the qiblah.'"],[3016,"Abd al-Malik narrated to us, and they mentioned the hadith in full, and sometimes they differed in a letter or a thing: :, : :, : : :"],[3017,"That he entered with the Messenger of God ﷺ, and he mentioned the hadith, and said: Then he turned to the corners of the House, facing each corner with takbir, tahlil, and tamjid, and he asked God and sought His forgiveness."],[3018,"That when the Messenger of God ﷺ entered it, he fell prostrate between the two pillars, then sat and supplicated, and he did not pray."],[3019,"That the Messenger of God ﷺ prayed inside the Ka'bah."],[3020,"That on the day of the conquest, the Messenger of God ﷺ came on a camel, with Usamah b. Zayd riding behind the Messenger of God ﷺ, and with him were Bilal and 'Uthman b. Talhah. When he came to the House, he sent Ibn Talhah with the key of the House, and he opened it, and the Messenger of God ﷺ, Usamah b. Zayd, 'Uthman b. Talhah, and Bilal entered. They stayed inside for a long time and locked the door behind them. Then the Messenger of God ﷺ came out, and the people rushed to the House. Ibn 'Umar and another with him got there first. Ibn 'Umar asked Bilal, 'Where did the Messenger of God ﷺ pray?' He showed him where he prayed, but he did not ask him how many rak'ahs he prayed."],[3021,"On the day of the conquest, the Messenger of God ﷺ entered while he was on a camel belonging to Usamah, until he made it kneel in the courtyard of the Ka'bah. Then he called 'Uthman b. Talhah for the key. He went to his mother, but she refused to give it to him. He said: 'You will give it to me, or the sword will come out of my loins.' So she handed it to him, and he opened the door, and the Prophet ﷺ entered, and 'Uthman, Bilal, and Usamah entered with him. They closed the door for a long while. Ibn 'Umar said: I was a strong young man, and the people rushed, and I rushed ahead of them. I found Bilal standing at the door. I said: 'O Bilal, where did the Messenger of God ﷺ pray?' He said: 'Between the two front pillars.' I forgot to ask him how many rak'ahs he prayed."],[3022,"I asked Bilal, 'Where did the Messenger of God ﷺ pray?' He said: 'At the front of the House, between him and the wall three cubits, or about three cubits.'"],[3023,"That ʿĀʾishah used to say: It is astonishing for a Muslim man, when he enters the Kaʿbah, how he raises his gaze toward the ceiling, leaving that aside out of reverence for Allah and magnification. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the Kaʿbah, and his gaze did not leave the place of his prostration until he came out of it."],[3024,"Whoever enters the House enters into a good deed and exits from an evil deed, forgiven for him."],[3025,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ left me while he was delighted, at ease, then he returned to me while he was sad. I said: O Messenger of Allah, you left me while you were such and such. He said: I entered the Kaʿbah. I wish I had not done that. I fear that I may have burdened my community after me."],[3026,"You have only been commanded with circumambulation, and you have not been commanded to enter it. He said: He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him say."],[3027,"The Prophet ﷺ entered the House, and I came and found that he had already come out, and Bilāl was standing at the door of the Kaʿbah. He said: I said, O Bilāl, where did the Prophet ﷺ pray? He said: Here. He said: Then he came out and prayed two rakʿahs between the Hijr and the door. He said: Mujāhid used to describe them between the two columns that are near the door of Banū Makhzūm."],[3028,"When the Prophet ﷺ conquered Makkah, he said: I said: I will put on my clothes."],[3029,"I used to love to enter the House and pray in it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took me by the hand and led me into the Hijr, then said: O ʿĀʾishah, your people, when they built the Kaʿbah, deemed it too short, so they took the Hijr out of the House. If you want to pray in the House, pray in the Hijr, for it is a portion of the House."],[3030,"Were it not for your people's recent emergence from disbelief, I would have incorporated the Hijr into the House."],[3031,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: O ʿĀʾishah, were it not that your people are recent in the era of ignorance, I would have demolished the House and included in it what they removed from it in the Hijr, for they were unable to afford its expense. I would have made for it two doors, an eastern door and a western door, attached it to the ground, and placed it on the foundation of Abraham. He said: That is what prompted Ibn al-Zubayr to demolish it and rebuild it. He said: I witnessed it when he demolished it and rebuilt it, and he extracted the foundation of the House like the humps of Bactrian camels, interlocking. My father said: I said to Yazīd b. Rūmān, while I was circumambulating with him that day, show me what they removed from the Hijr of it. He said: I will show you now. When he reached it, he said: This is the place. My father said: I estimated it to be about six cubits."],[3032,"Yazīd said: I witnessed Ibn al-Zubayr when he demolished it."],[3033,"The Kaʿbah in the era of ignorance was built with stone without clay, and it was about the size that a young goat could enter."],[3034,"Your people cut short the building of the House, and were it not for the recency of their covenant with polytheism, I would have restored what they left of it. If it occurs to your people after me to build it, then come, and I will show you what they left of it,” and she showed her close to seven cubits."],[3035,"The Prophet ﷺ shaved his head in the Farewell Pilgrimage. He said: And the people used to shave during Hajj, then perform 'Umrah at the time of departure, so he would say: What is this one shaving? So we would say to one of them: Pass the razor over your head."],[3036,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made A'ishah perform 'Umrah from al-Tan'im in Dhu al-Hijjah."],[3037,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made A'ishah perform 'Umrah from al-Tan'im on the night of al-Hasbah."],[3038,"O Messenger of Allah, do the people depart with two rites while I depart with one rite? He said: Wait, and when you become pure, go out to al-Tan'im and enter ihram from it, then meet us at such and such a mountain. He said: I think he said: Kuda. But it is according to the extent of your effort or the extent of your expenditure."],[3039,"We set out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ coinciding with the new moon of Dhu al-Hijjah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever wishes to enter ihram for 'Umrah, let him do so, and whoever wishes to enter ihram for Hajj, let him do so. Were it not that I have brought the sacrificial animal, I would have entered ihram for 'Umrah. So among them were those who entered ihram for 'Umrah and among them those who entered ihram for Hajj. I menstruated before entering Makkah, and the Day of 'Arafah overtook me while I was menstruating. I complained of that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said: Leave your 'Umrah, undo your hair and comb it, and enter ihram for Hajj. When it was the night of al-Hasbah, he sent 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr with me to al-Tan'im, and he mounted her behind him, and she entered ihram for 'Umrah in place of her 'Umrah. Thus Allah fulfilled her Hajj and her 'Umrah, and there was no sacrificial animal, no fasting, and no charity in any of that."],[3040,"She narrated to them about their 'Umrah after the Hajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She said: I menstruated, so I performed 'Umrah after the Hajj, then I did not fast and I did not offer a sacrifice."],[3041,"A woman from Khath'am said: O Messenger of Allah, my father is an old man upon whom the obligation of Allah for Hajj is due, and he is unable to sit firmly on the back of his camel. The Prophet ﷺ said: Then perform Hajj on his behalf."],[3042,"Al-Fadl b. 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A woman from Khath'am came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking his ruling, and al-Fadl began to look at her and she at him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began to turn al-Fadl's face with his hand to the other side. She said: O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Allah for Hajj has found my father an old man who cannot remain steady on the mount; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes. And that was in the Farewell Pilgrimage, some of them adding more than others."],[3043,"A woman from Khath'am asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the morning of the Day of Sacrifice, while al-Fadl was riding behind him. She said: O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Allah for Hajj upon His servants has found my father an old man who cannot hold himself on the mount; do you think I should perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes. Al-Makhzumi said: On the morning of Jam', and said: That I perform Hajj on his behalf?"],[3044,"Al-Fadl b. 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. A woman from Khath'am came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking his ruling, and al-Fadl began to look at her and she at him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ began to turn al-Fadl's face with his hand to the other side. She said: O Messenger of Allah, the obligation of Allah for Hajj has found my father an old man who cannot remain steady on the mount; shall I perform Hajj on his behalf? He said: Yes. And that was in the Farewell Pilgrimage, some of them adding more than others."],[3045,"I said — meaning to Ibn 'Abbas — 'I have a mother in the city, and I go on these military campaigns. Will it suffice her if I manumit a slave, though she is not with me?' He said, 'Shall I not tell you something more astonishing than that? I ordered the wife of Sinan b. 'Abdullah al-Juhani to ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for me about her mother who had died without performing Hajj: would it suffice her mother if she performed Hajj on her behalf?' He said, 'Yes. If her mother had a debt and she paid it on her behalf, would that not suffice her? So let her perform Hajj on behalf of her mother.'"],[3046,"A man of Juhaynah said, 'O Messenger of Allah, my father has died as an old man who had not performed Hajj — or he could not perform Hajj.' He said, 'Perform Hajj on behalf of your father.'"],[3047,"Al-Fadl b. 'Abbas was riding behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a woman from Khath'am came to ask him for a ruling. Al-Fadl began looking at her and she at him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned al-Fadl's face to the other side. She said, 'O Messenger of Allah, Allah's obligation upon His servants concerning Hajj has overtaken my father, an old man who cannot sit firmly on a mount. May I perform Hajj on his behalf?' He said, 'Yes.' That was during the Farewell Hajj."],[3048,"It reached me that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, 'My father is an old man who has lived to see Islam but has not performed Hajj and cannot keep himself steady on a mount. If I tie him with a rope onto the mount, I fear I might kill him.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'Perform Hajj on behalf of your father.'"],[3049,"Yahya b. Abi al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from 'Awf, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ the like of that, except that he said: the questioner asked about his mother."],[3050,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard a man saying, 'Labbayk on behalf of Shubrumah.' He said, 'Who is Shubrumah?' He said, 'My brother, or a relative of mine.' He said, 'Have you performed Hajj for yourself?' He said, 'No.' He said, 'Make this one for yourself, then perform Hajj on behalf of Shubrumah.'"],[3051,"O Messenger of Allah, my father is an old man who cannot perform Hajj or 'Umrah or travel.' He said, 'Perform Hajj and 'Umrah on behalf of your father.'"],[3052,"A woman vowed to perform Hajj, then she died. Her brother came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about that. He said, 'Do you think that if your sister had a debt, would you pay it?' He said, 'Yes.' He said, 'Then fulfill what is owed to Allah, for He is more deserving of fulfillment.'"],[3053,"A woman from Khath'am asked the Prophet."],[3054,"The Prophet ﷺ came upon an old man being supported between his two sons, leaning on them. The Prophet ﷺ said, 'What is the matter with this old man?' His sons said, 'O Messenger of Allah, he had a vow upon him.' The Prophet ﷺ said, 'Ride, old man, for Allah is in no need of you or your vow.'"],[3055,"Whether I heard it from Anas or it is from Thabit, from Anas."],[3056,"He asked the Prophet ﷺ about his sister who had vowed to walk to the Kaʿbah. He said: \"God is free from need for your sister's vow. Let her ride and offer a sacrificial camel.\""],[3057,"About a man who swears an oath to walk and then becomes unable and rides. He said: Ibn ʿAbbas said: He performs Hajj the following year and may ride as much as he wishes and walk as much as he wishes and ride."],[3058,"A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: \"My sister has imposed upon herself walking to the House of God.\" He said: \"God does not benefit from your sister's hardship. Tell her to perform Hajj riding and to expiate her oath.\""],[3059,"ʿAli b. Abi Talib passed by a madwoman of Banu So-and-so who had committed adultery. ʿUmar had ordered her to be stoned. ʿAli turned her away and said to ʿUmar: \"O Commander of the Faithful, would you stone this one?\" He said: \"Yes.\" He said: \"Do you not remember that the Messenger of God ﷺ said: 'The pen is lifted from three: from the insane person whose mind is overcome, from the sleeping person until he wakes, and from the child until he reaches puberty'?\" He said: \"You have spoken the truth.\" So he released her."],[3060,"The Prophet ﷺ departed from Makkah, and when he was at al-Rawhaʾ, a group of riders met him. He greeted them and said: \"Who are the people?\" They said: \"Muslims. And who are you?\" He said: \"The Messenger of God.\" A woman among them became startled and lifted a child of hers from a carriage and took hold of his arm muscles and said: \"O Messenger of God, does this one have to perform Hajj?\" He said: \"Yes, and you have the reward.\""],[3061,"If a child performs Hajj, it counts as a Hajj for him until he reaches the age of discernment. When he reaches discernment, he owes another Hajj. If a Bedouin performs Hajj, it counts as a Hajj for him. When he migrates, he owes another Hajj."],[3062,"\"We are people who hire out our camels on this route, and my people claim that there is no Hajj for us.\" Ibn ʿUmar said: \"Do you not circumambulate the House? Do you not walk between al-Safa and al-Marwah? Do you not, do you not? Indeed, a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him about the like of what you asked me, and he did not know how to answer him until it was revealed: 'There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord' [2:198]. Then he called him and recited it to him and said: 'You are pilgrims.'\""],[3063,"I said to Ibn 'Umar, and he mentioned something similar."],[3064,"A man came to Ibn 'Abbas and said: 'I hired myself out to some people, and I left for them part of my wages, and they let me perform the rites. Will that suffice for me?' Ibn 'Abbas said: 'Yes, this is among those of whom Allah said: \"Those - they will have a share of what they have earned, and Allah is swift in account\" (Quran 2:202).'"],[3065,"The people, at the beginning of Hajj, used to trade at Mina, 'Arafah, the market of Dhu al-Majaz, and the Hajj gatherings, but they feared trading while in a state of ihram. So Allah revealed: \"There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord during the Hajj gatherings\" (Quran 2:198)."],[3066,"I heard Ibn al-Zubayr reciting it: \"There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord during the Hajj gatherings\" (Quran 2:198)."],[3067,"That the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Hajj three times: two Hajjs before he emigrated, and one Hajj after he emigrated, with which there was an 'Umrah."],[3068,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ before revelation came to him, standing on a camel of his at 'Arafat with the people, departing with them from there. That was only a guidance from Allah."],[3069,"The Quraysh and those who followed their religion used to stand at al-Muzdalifah, and they were called al-Hums, while the rest of the Arabs used to stand at 'Arafah. When Islam came, Allah commanded His Prophet ﷺ to go to 'Arafat and stand there, then depart from it. She said: That is His saying: \"Then depart from where the people depart\" (Quran 2:199)."],[3070,"I lost a camel of mine on the day of 'Arafah, so I went to 'Arafah looking for it, and there I saw Muhammad standing among the people at 'Arafah on his camel in the evening of 'Arafah, and that was after revelation had come to him."],[3071,"I went looking for a camel of mine at 'Arafah, and I saw the Prophet ﷺ standing at 'Arafah with the people. I said: 'By Allah, this man is one of al-Hums, so what is he doing here?' The Prophet ﷺ used to stand at 'Arafah in the years he was there."],[3072,"Then why did he leave the Haram? The Quraysh used not to go beyond the Haram, saying: 'We are the people of Allah; we do not leave the Haram.'"],[3073,"I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the pre-Islamic era while he was standing at 'Arafat with the polytheists, then I saw him in Islam standing at that same place of his, and I knew that Allah had granted him success in that."],[3074,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered Makkah in the year of the conquest, and on his head was a helmet. When he removed it, a man came to him and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, Ibn Khatal is clinging to the curtains of the Ka'bah.' So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, 'Kill him.' Ibn Shihab said, 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was not in ihram that day.'"],[3075,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me and sent with me a man from the Ansar, and said, 'Go to Abu Sufyan b. Harb and kill him.' He mentioned the hadith. And he said: When we entered Makkah, my companion said to me, 'Would you like that we start by circumambulating the House seven times and pray two rak'ahs?' I said, 'I know the people of Makkah better: when it becomes dark, they sprinkle their courtyards and then sit in them, and I am more recognizable in them than a piebald horse.' He kept persuading me until we came to the House and circumambulated it seven times and prayed two rak'ahs, then we left."],[3076,"'Islam is that you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and that you establish the prayer, give the zakah, perform Hajj and 'Umrah, perform ghusl from janabah, complete the wudu, and fast Ramadan.' He said, 'If I do that, then I am a Muslim.' He said, 'Yes.' He said, 'You have spoken the truth.'"],[3077,"There is no one except that upon him is a Hajj and an 'Umrah, both obligatory, there is no escape from them. So whoever adds after that, it is good and voluntary."],[3078,"There is no one from the creation of Allah except that upon him is an obligatory 'Umrah."],[3079,"The Prophet ﷺ was asked about 'Umrah: 'Is it obligatory?' He said, 'No, but if you perform 'Umrah, it is better.'"],[3080,"I was a Bedouin Christian man, then I embraced Islam. I was eager for jihad, and I found that Hajj and 'Umrah were prescribed for me. I went to a man from my tribe called Hudaym b. 'Abdullah and said, 'O so-and-so, I am eager for jihad, and I found that Hajj and 'Umrah are prescribed for me. How can I combine them?' He said, 'Combine them, then slaughter what is easy of the sacrificial animal.' He said: So I entered ihram for both together. When I came to al-'Udhayb, Salman b. Rabi'ah and Zayd b. Suhan met me while I was in ihram for both together. One of them said to the other, 'This one is no more knowledgeable than his camel.' It was as if a mountain was thrown upon me until I came to 'Umar. I said to him, 'O Commander of the Faithful, I was a Bedouin Christian man and I embraced Islam. I am eager for jihad, and I found that Hajj and 'Umrah are prescribed for me. I went to a man from my tribe called Hudaym b. 'Abdullah and said, 'O so-and-so, I am eager for jihad, and I found that Hajj and 'Umrah are prescribed for me. How can I combine them?' He said, 'Combine them, then slaughter what is easy of the sacrificial animal.' And I entered ihram for both together. When I came to al-'Udhayb, Salman b. Rabi'ah and Zayd b. Suhan met me while I was in ihram for both together. One of them said to the other, 'This one is no more knowledgeable than his camel.' He said: So 'Umar said to me, 'You have been guided to the Sunnah of your Prophet.'"],[3081,"I and 'Urwah b. al-Zubayr entered the mosque, and behold, 'Abdullah b. 'Umar was sitting near the room of 'A'ishah. He said: And the people in the mosque were praying the Duha prayer. We asked him about their prayer, and he said, 'An innovation.' Then he said, 'How many times did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform 'Umrah?' He said, 'Four.'"],[3082,"I said to Anas b. Malik, 'How many times did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform 'Umrah?' He said, 'Four 'Umrahs, and he performed Hajj once, and his 'Umrah was with his Hajj.'"],[3083,"'Umrah to 'Umrah is an expiation for what is between them, and the accepted Hajj has no reward other than Paradise."],[3084,"'Umrah to 'Umrah expiates what is between them, and the accepted Hajj has no reward other than Paradise."],[3085,"I said: O Messenger of Allah, is there any jihad upon women? He said: \"Upon them is a jihad without fighting: Hajj and 'Umrah.\""],[3086,"Marwan sent to Umm Ma'qil asking her about this hadith, and she narrated that her husband had assigned a young camel for the sake of Allah, and that she wanted to perform 'Umrah, so she asked her husband for the young camel, but he refused. She then came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that to him, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to give it to her, and he said: \"Indeed Hajj and 'Umrah are among the ways of Allah, and an 'Umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj or suffices for a Hajj.\""],[3087,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon me while I was weeping. He said, \"What is the matter with you?\" She said: I do not pray. He said: \"It will not harm you; you are only one of the daughters of Adam. Allah has written for you what He has written for them.\" Then she mentioned the hadith. And he said: \"Until he alighted at al-Muhassab, and we alighted with him. Then he called 'Abd al-Rahman b. Abi Bakr and said: 'Take your sister out and let her enter ihram for 'Umrah.'\""],[3088,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to perform Hajj, and a woman said to her husband, 'Take me to perform Hajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.' He said, 'I do not have what to take you on.' She said, 'Then take me on your old camel.' He said, 'That one I and your child use in turns.' She said, 'Take me on your such-and-such camel.' He said, 'That one is dedicated for the sake of Allah.' She said, 'Then sell [the dates of your storage].' He said, 'That is my food and yours.' He said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Makkah, she sent her husband to him, and she said, 'Convey my greetings and the mercy of Allah to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and ask him what equals a Hajj with you.' Her husband came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, my wife conveys her greetings and the mercy of Allah to you, and she had asked me to take her to perform Hajj with you. I said to her, \"I do not have what to take you on.\" She said, \"Take me on your such-and-such camel.\" I said to her, \"That one is dedicated for the sake of Allah.\"' He said: \"As for you, if you had taken her, it would have been for the sake of Allah.\" She said, 'Take me on your old camel.' I said, 'That one I and your child use in turns.' She said, 'Then sell [the dates of your storage].' I said, 'That is my food and yours.'\" He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed in wonder at her eagerness for Hajj. \"And she ordered me to ask you what equals a Hajj with you.\" He said: \"Convey my greetings and the mercy of Allah to her, and inform her that an 'Umrah in Ramadan equals a Hajj with me.\""],[3089,"Regarding His saying, 'A declaration of immunity from Allah and His Messenger,' he said: When the Prophet ﷺ returned from Hunayn, he performed 'Umrah from al-Ji'ranah, then appointed Abu Bakr over that Hajj."],[3090,"The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the people in the year of the Farewell Hajj, saying: \"Whoever wishes to return with an 'Umrah before the Hajj, let him do so.\""]]);
